var tafsirData_3 = {
  "1": "\"Alif-Lãm-Mĩm\" opens the Family of Imran with the same three letters that opened the Cow—and the repetition is the first commentary: what those letters signalled there (see the discussion at Surah 2, verse 1), they signal here: the disconnected letters of the Arabs' own alphabet laid on the table before a Book they could never match, the challenge of Surah 2, verse 23 renewed at the threshold of a new surah whose whole occasion would demand it. For this chapter's historical setting, preserved by the commentators from the sirah, is the Quran's most formal doctrinal confrontation: the deputation of Najran—sixty riders of Christian Arabia, bishops and scholars at their head—come to Madinah to dispute with the Prophet, peace be upon him, concerning Isa; the chapter's opening eighty-odd verses, the early authorities transmit, descended around that encounter (Ibn Kathir and at-Tabari recording the reports at the surah's head). A Book about to adjudicate between communities of scripture opens by displaying its raw material: letters—the same letters, the unmatchable arrangement.\n\nThe letters' function at this door carries the two workings noted at their first appearance, each sharpened by the new context. The summons to attention: strange syllables arresting the ear before a discourse whose stakes—the identity of Isa, the standing of the two earlier scriptures, the meaning of Ibrahim's legacy—could not be higher. And the challenge of composition: to an audience now including the trained clergy of Najran, bearers of their own scripture's learning, the letters say what they said to Quraysh's poets: the material is common property; the Book is not—produce its like, or hear it out. The reward-tradition also stands renewed at this door: \"I do not say that Alif-Lam-Mim is one letter, but Alif is a letter, Lam is a letter, and Mim is a letter\"—each at ten rewards (Jami` at-Tirmidhi, Hadith 2910).\n\nAnd the discipline the letters teach stands renewed with them: no certain meaning has been transmitted for them from the Prophet, peace be upon him, and the sound position—stated at Surah 2, verse 1 and held here—is the confession the well-grounded will voice inside this very chapter's seventh verse: \"we believe in it—it is all from our Lord.\" That placement is this opening's finest touch, noted by the careful readers: a surah that will shortly divide the Book into the precise and the elusive (verse 7), and warn against hearts that chase the elusive for discord's sake, opens with three letters that are themselves the elusive in miniature—the reader's first act in the chapter is thus his first test in it: to recite what he cannot decode, trust its Sender, and pass on to the precise, where the chapter's whole business—the creed of the Ever-Living, the confirmed scriptures, the truth about the son of Maryam—waits in language no honest reader can mistake.",
  "2": "\"Allah! There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him—the Ever-Living, All-Sustaining\" plants at the new surah's threshold the exact opening of the Verse of the Footstool (Surah 2, verse 255)—the creed's negation-and-exception, then the two master-names—and the repetition here is not echo but strategy, for this chapter's announced business is the Najran deputation's claim about Isa (the surah's occasion, noted at verse 1), and the two names chosen are, precisely, the two that adjudicate that claim. The Ever-Living: His life original, underived, without beginning, end, or dependency—and therefore categorically unlike every life that begins in a womb (the very next verses will press this: \"He is the One Who shapes you in the wombs,\" verse 6), eats food (Surah 5, verse 75's argument about the Messiah and his mother: \"they both ate\"), and tastes death or its likeness. The All-Sustaining: He maintains all existence while nothing maintains Him—and therefore whoever was himself carried, nursed, sheltered from enemies, and sustained by provision stands on the sustained side of the line, not the Sustainer's: honoured as His servant and messenger, but His servant (Surah 4, verse 172: \"the Messiah would never be too proud to be a servant of Allah\").\n\nThe verse's economy is thus a whole polemic folded into a creed: before a word of the Isa-narratives begins—and this chapter will tell them at length, with more of Maryam and her son than any surah save Maryam's own—the terms of measurement are fixed: divinity is defined by these two names, and by them every candidate is tested. The chapter's later summary-verse will apply the test explicitly: \"the example of Jesus in the sight of Allah is like that of Adam: He created him from dust, then said to him 'Be!' and he was\" (verse 59)—createdness, however miraculous its mode, is the disqualifier; and this verse holds the qualifying attributes up first, so the reader carries the standard into every story that follows.\n\nIts pairing with the surrounding verses completes the architecture the commentators admired: verse 1's elusive letters, then this verse's maximum precision—the Book's two registers (verse 7's coming doctrine) displayed side by side in the chapter's first two breaths; and the Sunnah marks the pairing's rank: the Prophet, peace be upon him, said Allah's greatest Name—the Name by which, when called, He answers—is found in these verses; the transmitted specification points to \"Allah, there is no god except Him, the Ever-Living, All-Sustaining\" as it stands here, in the Footstool-verse, and in Surah 20, verse 111's kindred declaration (Sunan Abi Dawud, Hadith 1496; Jami` at-Tirmidhi, Hadith 3478—graded sound). The chapter of history's greatest dispute about God thus opens with God named at His greatest—and every reader, Najran's bishops first, is met at the door not by an argument but by the One about whom the argument will be settled.",
  "3": "\"He has revealed to you ˹O Prophet˺ the Book in truth\" continues the threshold-creed of verse 2 into the doctrine of scripture, and its verb carries a precision the commentators noted: the sending-down named here for the Quran is the gradual kind—the Book descending piece by piece across twenty-three years, each portion timed to its hour (the abrogation-wisdom of Surah 2, verse 106; the community's staged training); \"in truth\"—the cargo true, the delivery true, the Sender true: the triple warrant this Book claims at every self-description (Surah 2, verse 176).\n\n\"Confirming what came before it\" states, at the door of the chapter that will host Christianity's formal deputation, the Quran's constitutional relationship to the earlier scriptures—the confirmation-doctrine this whole argument has run on since Surah 2, verse 41: the final Book arrives not as the Torah's and Gospel's rival but as their verifier: vindicating their divine origin against every denial, their prophets against every slander (this very chapter will defend Isa's honour and his mother's against both their rejecters and their deifiers), and their central promise—the coming prophet—by being its fulfilment. The confirmation is also the argument's blade, as at Surah 2, verse 91: the communities of the earlier Books cannot reject the confirmer without wounding the confirmed; Najran's bishops, come to defend the Gospel's honour, would be shown that the Gospel's honour stood higher in the visitors' Book than in their own creed's revisions of it.\n\n\"As He revealed the Torah and the Gospel\" completes the verse with the two earlier revelations named—and the naming is itself doctrine, binding on every Muslim (the creed of Surah 2, verse 136): the Torah, sent down to Musa—\"a guide and mercy\" (Surah 6, verse 154); the Gospel—the Injil—sent down to Isa: one revelation given to him, this verse rules, as the Torah was given to Musa: the Quran's quiet correction, in a single phrase, of the whole later architecture in which four human-authored accounts replaced the one sent-down Book of the Messiah. The verb's shift the scholars also marked: for these two, the sending named is the complete kind—each given whole to its prophet, where the Quran descended in portions upon the living history of its community: three Books, one Sender, the modes of delivery fitted to each mission.\n\nThe verse's function at this chapter's threshold gathers all three clauses into one positioning: before the disputation begins, the Quran locates itself on the map of revelation—descended in truth, confirming its predecessors, standing third in the named line of the one God's Books; so that the coming argument with Najran is framed from the first as it would be conducted throughout: not Islam against the scriptures of Isa and Musa, but the scriptures' own claims—the one God, His sent servants, His sent Books—defended by their final confirmation against the alterations of their custodians (Surah 2, verse 79's file, about to be reopened at this chapter's own verse 78). The next verse will name the earlier Books' office—\"as a guide for people\"—and unsheathe, for the third time in the Quran's arrangement, the title that judges all disputes: the Standard.",
  "4": "\"Previously, as a guide for people\" completes verse 3's sentence, fixing the Torah's and Gospel's office in the divine economy: sent before, as guidance for the people of their eras—true Books for their appointed seasons (Surah 5, verse 44: \"We revealed the Torah, containing guidance and light\"), each guiding until the next arrived, the relay this whole argument has traced since Surah 2, verse 87's \"successive messengers.\" The clause honours and bounds in one breath: guidance real, tenure appointed—the reverence every Muslim owes the earlier Books (the creed of Surah 2, verse 136), and the succession no honest reader of a relay can deny.\n\n\"And ˹also˺ revealed the Standard ˹to distinguish between right and wrong˺\" names, for the third time in the Quran's arrangement, the title that adjudicates everything: the Furqan—the Criterion, the separator of truth from falsehood (its grammar unfolded at Surah 2, verse 53, where Musa received it; its supreme instance at Surah 25, verse 1, the Quran itself). Its unsheathing at this chapter's door is tactical, the commentators note: a deputation approaches bearing scriptures and interpretations in conflict—Torah-readings against Gospel-readings against the new Book's claims—and the verse announces in advance what the coming disputation will assume: a Criterion exists; the dispute is not interpretation against interpretation into the void, but claims measured against the Standard sent down for exactly such measuring: the revelation that separates—in the Isa-question as in all questions—what is from Allah and what accreted upon it.\n\n\"Surely those who reject Allah's revelations will suffer a severe torment\" turns the threshold-creed, for the first time in this chapter, to warning—and its timing is the courtesy of full disclosure: before the guests arrive and the narratives begin, the stakes are posted; and the rejection named is precise: Allah's revelations—the signs and verses just catalogued: the confirmed Books, the descended Criterion; to reject them after their confirmation of one's own scripture (the Najran predicament exactly) is the aggravated refusal this argument has priced since Surah 2, verse 89's \"they recognized... they rejected.\"\n\n\"For Allah is Almighty, capable of punishment\" seals the threshold with a name-pairing unique in its second term—not the customary paired name but an attribute-phrase: possessor of retribution—and the scholars read the pairing's exactness: Almighty—the might that no rejecter's community, wealth, or numbers can weigh against (the next verses will say it of the disbelievers' wealth and children, verse 10); and \"capable of punishment\"—retribution named as capacity in active possession: not eagerness (the chapter will display, within a few verses, the door of mercy at its widest: verse 8's prayer, verse 16's forgiveness-seekers) but readiness—the deterrent stated, then the invitation resumed. The threshold thus closes complete: the God named (verses 1 and 2), the Books mapped (verse 3), the Criterion drawn, the stakes posted—and the chapter steps, in the next verse, from the Books to the Author's knowledge, before which nothing in the earth or the heavens hides: the attribute the whole Isa-question will finally rest upon, for only the Knower of the unseen can say what happened in Maryam's chamber and on the day the Messiah was raised.",
  "5": "\"Surely nothing on earth or in the heavens is hidden from Allah\" stands between the threshold's doctrine of scripture (verses 3 and 4) and its doctrine of creation (verse 6), and its placement is the argument: total knowledge named as the hinge on which both doctrines turn. Toward the Books just mapped: revelation is knowledge descending—the Torah, the Gospel, the Criterion are transmissions from the One from whom nothing hides; their accounts of the unseen (the past's buried histories, the Day's unarrived scenes) carry the warrant of this verse: the Narrator was present, and is present, everywhere His narrations reach (Surah 2, verse 255's knowledge-clause: \"He knows what is ahead of them and what is behind them\"). Toward the wombs about to be named: the hidden's most intimate case—the unformed child in its triple darkness—introduced by the universal law of which it is an instance: nothing hidden, in the heavens' farthest reach or the body's deepest chamber.\n\nThe verse's force in the chapter's occasion—the Najran disputation—is the force the commentators drew: the whole controversy about Isa is, at bottom, a dispute about hidden things: what happened in a virgin's chamber in Galilee; what nature joined to what nature in the child she bore; what occurred on the day of the crucifixion's appearance and the raising (this chapter's verse 55 ahead). Human parties to such a dispute hold traditions, texts, and inferences—each at generations' remove from the events, each transmission's custody already impeached in this argument's earlier files (Surah 2, verse 79). One Party holds the events themselves: the verse names Him; and the narratives this chapter is about to deliver—Maryam's birth and nurture, Zakariyya's chamber, the annunciation, the cradle's speech—descend from that unhidden vantage: \"that is from the news of the unseen which We reveal to you\" (this chapter's own verse 44 will say it, over Maryam's story, to the Prophet who was not there: neither were the disputants).\n\nThe verse also carries, beneath its polemical work, the devotional weight it holds wherever the Quran sounds it: nothing hidden—the believer's whole interior life conducted before an audience of One: the concealed charity (Surah 2, verse 271) seen, the entombed testimony (Surah 2, verse 283) seen, the heart's revealed and concealed alike within the account (Surah 2, verse 284—this verse its serene restatement, the storm of that verse's descent now settled into doctrine). And its two named territories—earth and heavens, in that order, the reader's ground named first—walk the mind outward from its own hiding-places to the cosmos's: one Knowledge, no gradient, the sparrow's fall and the throne's secrets equally lit. On this foundation the threshold's final creed-stone is laid in the next verse: the Knower is the Shaper—and what He shapes in the wombs He shapes \"as He wills,\" the clause that will meet the Messiah's miraculous forming exactly where Najran's creed misread it.",
  "6": "\"He is the One Who shapes you in the wombs of your mothers as He wills\" brings the threshold-creed of this chapter to the argument its occasion demanded—and its aim is exact to the breath. The Najran deputation's case for Isa's divinity rested, at bottom, on his forming: conceived with no father, therefore—they reasoned—more than man. This verse relocates the entire question: forming in wombs is Allah's act universally—He shapes you, all of you, every human ever carried; the womb is His workshop without exception, and \"as He wills\" is the clause that holds every mode of the shaping: with two parents (all humanity's ordinary case), with none (Adam, from dust), from a man alone in the sense of no mother's line—or from a mother alone: Isa, shaped in Maryam's womb \"as He wills.\" The miracle of the fatherless conception thus testifies, when the wombs' true Shaper is named, exactly opposite to Najran's inference: not the child's divinity but the Shaper's freedom—the argument this chapter will state as its summary-verdict: \"the example of Jesus in the sight of Allah is like that of Adam: He created him from dust, then said to him, 'Be!' and he was\" (verse 59); and the verse's own address quietly seals it: He shapes you—and among the \"you\" of humanity shaped in a mother's womb is the Messiah himself, the shaped standing on creation's side of the line the chapter's second verse drew.\n\nThe verse's doctrine ranges wider than its polemic, and the scholars gave it the pause it commands: the shaping—taswir, the very word from which the Fashioner's name (al-Musawwir, Surah 59, verse 24) is drawn—is each human's first biography: the drop staged to clot, the clot to the formed frame (Surah 23, verses 13 and 14's stations), the features written—face, print, and temperament individually issued in the triple darkness where no craftsman's eye reaches (verse 5's unhidden chamber); the Prophet, peace be upon him, described the angel commissioned to the womb's stages by Allah's command (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 318; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2646's decree-narration)—instrumentation serving the one Fashioner. \"As He wills\" then covers what every delivery-room confirms and no laboratory legislates: male and female, sound and tested, resembling and unprecedented—\"He grants whoever He wills daughters, and grants whoever He wills sons... and He leaves whoever He wills infertile\" (Surah 42, verses 49 and 50).\n\n\"There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him—the Almighty, All-Wise\" closes the threshold-creed by re-sounding verse 2's negation-and-exception—the chapter's frame locked at both ends of its opening—and the closing pair is chosen for the ground just covered: Almighty—the shaping's power, unshared and unassisted, the wombs' Workman needing no partner and begetting no son (the might that voids the deputation's whole grammar); All-Wise—the shaping's design: each forming, ordinary and miraculous, placed in its purpose—Adam's to found, Isa's to sign (verse 49's \"a sign from your Lord\"), every reader's own to test and be tested. The threshold now complete—God named, Books mapped, knowledge total, creation owned—the chapter turns to the instrument the whole disputation will be conducted with: the Book itself, its precise verses and its elusive ones, and the two kinds of hearts that read them.",
  "7": "\"He is the One Who has revealed to you ˹O Prophet˺ the Book, of which some verses are precise—they are the foundation of the Book—while others are elusive\" delivers, at the disputation-chapter's door, the Quran's own doctrine of its two registers—and the doctrine's terms repay exactness. The precise (muhkam): verses whose meaning stands clear and single—the commands, the creed's plain statements, the boundaries; and their office is named: \"the foundation of the Book\"—the mother-text, the reference to which all else returns. The elusive (mutashabih): verses bearing likeness and layers—the unseen's descriptions, the disconnected letters (verse 1's specimen at this very chapter's head), expressions whose full reality outruns the language's ordinary reach; revelation containing such verses by design: the test they constitute is about to be stated, and the reader's humility they train (Surah 2, verse 26's mosquito-parable sorting hearers) is half their purpose.\n\n\"Those with deviant hearts follow the elusive verses seeking ˹to spread˺ doubt through their ˹false˺ interpretations\" names the method of every heresy in one sentence, and the chapter's occasion supplied the sitting exhibit: the Najran deputation pressing the title \"Word of Allah\" and the term \"Spirit from Him\" (Surah 4, verse 171's honourifics for Isa) as levers for the incarnation—elusive expressions chased past the precise verses standing all around them (\"there is no god except Him,\" this chapter's frame). The anatomy is permanent: the deviant heart's direction is set first—\"deviant\" before the following: the swerve precedes the exegesis—then the elusive is mined for pretexts, the foundation ignored, the fragment enthroned; the Prophet, peace be upon him, recited this verse and said: \"when you see those who pursue the elusive verses, those are the ones Allah has named—so beware of them\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 4547; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2665). The counter-method is the verse's own architecture: the elusive read by the light of the precise—returned to the foundation, as every sound science reads its hard cases by its settled law.\n\n\"But none grasps their ˹full˺ meaning except Allah. As for those well-grounded in knowledge, they say, 'We believe in this ˹Quran˺—it is all from our Lord'\" states the boundary and the posture. The full reality of the elusive—the how of the attributes, the unseen's textures, the letters' secret—rests with Allah alone; and the well-grounded are known precisely by their peace with that boundary: their sentence—\"we believe in it; all of it is from our Lord\"—is knowledge's own voice, for the deepest learning knows where learning ends (the angels' confession, Surah 2, verse 32); the scholars' two readings of the verse's pause—whether the well-grounded share in the knowing of sound interpretation, or halt wholly at \"except Allah\"—converge in practice: what they know, they know by the foundation; where the foundation is silent, they believe and stop.\n\n\"But none will be mindful ˹of this˺ except people of reason\" closes the doctrine at the chapter's recurring address (Surah 2, verse 269)—and the closing performs the verse's last correction: the halt before the elusive is not reason's defeat but its maturity; the mind that demands every verse yield to its categories is the deviant heart's instrument, while the mind that reads the clear, obeys it, and bows before the veiled is reason at its full stature—and it is handed, in the very next verse, the prayer that guards it: \"Our Lord! Do not let our hearts deviate after You have guided us.\"",
  "8": "\"˹They say,˺ 'Our Lord! Do not let our hearts deviate after you have guided us'\" gives the well-grounded of verse 7 their prayer—and the sequence is the teaching: the verse that exposed deviant hearts chasing elusive verses is followed at once by the petition that guards against becoming one; doctrine flowing directly into supplication, the Quran's constant method (Surah 2, verse 286's petitions after its principles). The prayer's anatomy repays care. \"Do not let our hearts deviate\": the askers know where deviation happens—not first in books or arguments but in hearts (verse 7's \"those with deviant hearts\": the swerve preceding the exegesis); and they know hearts are held—guidance is not a summit conquered once but a state maintained by the Guide, withdrawable, renewable, asked-for daily (the Fatihah's whole logic, prayed seventeen times a day by the already-guided). \"After you have guided us\": the petition's most sobering clause—deviation after guidance is possible; the chapter's histories have shown it (Surah 2, verse 109's envious learned), and the well-grounded, precisely because they are well-grounded, fear it: knowledge of the road includes knowledge of its exits. The Prophet, peace be upon him, prayed this prayer's own substance more than any other: Umm Salamah reported his most frequent supplication was \"O Turner of the hearts, keep my heart firm upon Your religion\"—and when she asked why, he answered: there is no heart but that it is between two of the Fingers of the Most Merciful; whom He wills He makes firm, and whom He wills He turns (Jami` at-Tirmidhi, Hadith 3522, graded sound; the hearts-between-Fingers doctrine also in Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2654).\n\n\"Grant us Your mercy. You are indeed the Giver ˹of all bounties˺\" completes the petition with the positive half—for firmness is not merely deviation withheld but mercy granted: the heart kept straight by being kept warm; and the closing name is chosen with the precision this Book's prayers always show: al-Wahhab—the Giver, the Bestower whose giving is pure grant: unearned, unpurchased, flowing from generosity alone. The name's logic seals the prayer's humility: the askers do not plead their grounding as credit (\"we are the well-grounded; maintain our due\")—they ask as beggars of a Bestower, for guidance kept is a gift continued, not a wage owed; the same name will close this chapter's other great firmness-prayer (Sulaiman's, in Surah 38, verse 35's \"grant me... You are the Giver\"). Placed at this chapter's door, the prayer is also the community's equipment for the disputation ahead: about to hear the elusive verses bent by trained advocates (the Najran encounter, verse 7's occasion), the believers are armed not first with counter-arguments but with this asking—for the debater's real vulnerability was never his logic but his heart, and the well-grounded guard it where it is actually held: between the Fingers of the Most Merciful, by the Giver's grace.",
  "9": "\"Our Lord! You will certainly gather all humanity for the ˹promised˺ Day—about which there is no doubt\" completes the well-grounded believers' prayer begun in verse 8, and its movement is the mark of their grounding: from the heart's private peril (deviation after guidance) to the race's public terminus (the gathering)—for the two are one concern: hearts are kept firm precisely by keeping the Day in view; deviation begins where the gathering grows faint (Surah 2, verse 46's definition of the humble: \"certain that they will meet their Lord\"). Their sentence is confession, not petition—\"You will certainly gather\": the Day spoken to Allah as settled fact, the creed recited into the prayer; and its two seals are the chapter's own vocabulary: \"about which there is no doubt\"—the phrase that opened the whole revelation's self-description (Surah 2, verse 2: \"no doubt about it\") now applied to the appointment the Book exists to prepare its readers for; the gathering-doctrine itself this argument's constant horizon (Surah 2, verse 148: \"wherever you are, Allah will bring you all together\").\n\n\"Surely Allah does not break His promise\" grounds the certainty in the attribute that carries all certainty, and the clause's selection is the verse's finest instruction: the Day's sureness rests not on humanity's deserving it, nature's tending toward it, or history's arc—but on a promise made by One whose promises do not fail (Surah 2, verse 80's challenge to the entitlement-theologians ran on exactly this: \"have you taken a pledge from Allah—for Allah never breaks His word?\"); the resurrection is a covenant-item, and the covenant-Keeper's record is the believer's whole security. Note also the prayer's address-shift the reciters feel: \"Our Lord... You will gather\" turning to \"Allah does not break His promise\"—the worshippers stepping back, mid-prayer, from intimate address to public creed, as though turning from the Beloved's face to testify about Him to the world: prayer and proclamation in one breath.\n\nThe two-verse prayer, read whole, is the chapter's issued equipment for everything ahead, and its architecture deserves the summary the scholars gave it: a heart guarded (verse 8's first petition), a mercy sought (its second), and a Day held certain (this verse)—interior, grace, and horizon: the three anchors without which the coming disputation's winds—the elusive verses bent, the deputation's pressures, the battlefield-tests this chapter will shortly narrate (Badr at verse 13, Uhud in its later movement)—would find the community loose. And its placement teaches the order of operations this Book always keeps: before the enemies are answered (verses 10 to 12 turn to them at once), the self is secured; the polemic that follows is conducted by people who have already prayed—the well-grounded entering the argument with their hearts deposited where arguments cannot reach.",
  "10": "\"Indeed, neither the wealth nor children of the disbelievers will be of any benefit to them against Allah\" turns the chapter from the believers' prayer (verses 8 and 9) to the deniers' position, and its opening confiscation is aimed at the two assets every age mistakes for security: wealth and children—property and posterity, the fortune that buys exits and the sons who extend power; the pagan aristocracy's actual creed (Surah 2, verse 212's measures), and the audible boast of the Prophet's opponents: \"we have more wealth and children, and we will not be punished\" (Surah 34, verse 35). The verse's ruling: \"of no benefit against Allah\"—the qualifier the whole sentence turns on: wealth and children benefit richly within Allah's order (provision, joy, the righteous child's prayers); against Him—as ransom at His judgment, as shield against His seizure—they convert to nothing: \"the Day when neither wealth nor children will benefit, except whoever comes to Allah with a sound heart\" (Surah 26, verses 88 and 89); the gathering just confessed (verse 9) arriving with its currency-rules published (Surah 2, verse 254's closed doors).\n\n\"And they will be the fuel for the Fire\" completes the confiscation with the reversal this Book prices at its darkest: the men who trusted assets against Allah become themselves the asset consumed—fuel, the Fire's own kindling (Surah 2, verse 24: \"fuelled with people and stones\"; Surah 21, verse 98); the securities they hoarded left behind, the hoarders themselves fed forward. The wording's economy carries the horror: history's magnates and dynasts, reduced in one clause from the Fire's would-be ransomers to its firewood.\n\nThe verse's placement does the chapter's tactical work, and the commentators read it in the occasion's light: the disputation-and-testing season this surah addresses—Najran's wealthy church-delegation, Madinah's confident Jewish tribes, Makkah's still-unbroken aristocracy between Badr and Uhud—was a landscape of impressive adversaries: institutions funded, lineages long, numbers weighty; and the young community needed this verse's X-ray before the encounters: the imposing fronts run on assets already ruled valueless \"against Allah\"—argue, trade, and if war comes fight, without awe of treasuries and head-counts, for the next verse will read the precedent (Pharaoh's file: assets maximal, seizure total) and the verse after it will predict the outcome aloud (\"soon you will be overpowered\"). And beneath the tactics, the verse's permanent audit turns toward every reader, believer included: whatever is being trusted against Allah—the portfolio against the provision-Owner, the connections against the Day's closed doors (Surah 2, verse 254), the heirs against the grave—stands under this verse's confiscation-order; the sound heart alone crosses (Surah 26, verse 89), and hearts were the previous verses' whole business: guarded by prayer, anchored to the gathering—the equipment this verse's deniers traded for fuel-status, holding assets that burn.",
  "11": "\"Their fate will be like that of the people of Pharaoh and those before them\" answers the imposing front of verse 10—the wealth, the children, the confident institutions—by opening history's file at its maximal case, and the selection is argument by a fortiori: if assets ever availed against Allah, Pharaoh's would have—the granaries of the Nile, the armies, the god-king's apparatus at antiquity's summit; the audience addressed (Makkah's aristocracy, Madinah's fortified tribes, Najran's funded church) held fractions of that portfolio, and the file's verdict is already public (Surah 2, verses 49 and 50): the sea closed, the corpse preserved as exhibit (Surah 10, verse 92). \"And those before them\" widens the file to the whole cabinet—Ad, Thamud, Nuh's people: the Quran's recurring roll-call of maximal fronts seized mid-confidence—so the pattern stands as law, not anecdote: \"consistent with the way of the people of Pharaoh\" is this Book's own formula for it elsewhere (Surah 8, verses 52 and 54).\n\n\"They all rejected Our signs, so Allah seized them for their sins\" states the law's mechanism with the precision of a charge-sheet, and each term is weighed. The crime: rejection of the signs—not ignorance (the signs came: Musa's nine to Pharaoh, each nation's evidences to it) but the denial-after-clarity this argument has prosecuted from its first files (Surah 2, verse 89's \"they recognized... they rejected\"). The seizure: the word is sudden apprehension—the arrest that interrupts; Pharaoh seized between pursuit and triumph, the nations at their prosperity's noon: \"We seized them suddenly while they were unaware\" (Surah 7, verse 95's pattern). And the causation: \"for their sins\"—the verse's justice-clause: no arbitrary doom; the seizure invoiced to the earning (Surah 2, verse 134's ledgers), the destroyed nations' files stamped, every one, with their own signatures.\n\n\"And Allah is severe in punishment\" seals the file with the attribute the evidence just proved—the same clause that sealed the favour-traders' warning at Surah 2, verse 211—and its pairing across this chapter's threshold is the balance the scholars mark: verse 4 closed with \"capable of punishment,\" verses 8 and 9 opened mercy's petitions, and this verse's severity stands between them as one attribute among the Names, real as the mercy is real: the God of the two-verse prayer is the God of the Pharaoh-file, and the mature creed holds both without dissolving either (Surah 15, verses 49 and 50's twin announcement).\n\nThe verse's function for its first hearers was armour and aim at once: the community facing Arabia's fronts is handed history's actuarial tables—the imposing adversary's category has a failure-rate of totality wherever it hardened into sign-rejection; and its function for every later reader is the mirror this Book grinds at each such file (Surah 2, verse 66's \"lesson to the God-fearing\"): the relevant question is never the neighbour's resemblance to Pharaoh's people but one's own—which signs, arriving with clarity, are being rejected on the instalment-plan of neglect; for the law named here indexes to the crime, not the costume, and the next verse will turn from history's precedent to open prophecy: the fronts presently standing will fall.",
  "12": "\"˹O Prophet!˺ Tell the disbelievers, 'Soon you will be overpowered and driven to Hell—what an evil place to rest!'\" converts the historical law of verse 11 into open prophecy—and the conversion's daring must be measured in its hour. The instruction's occasion, as the commentators transmit from the early authorities (Ibn Kathir recording the reports): after Badr, the Jewish tribes of Madinah—and with them every front still confident against the community—weighed the Muslims' victory and discounted it; some of Banu Qaynuqa answered the Prophet's summons with the boast that Quraysh's defeat proved nothing against real men of war. Into that landscape—the community a single town, its enemies Arabia's remainder—this verse commands a public forecast, addressed to \"the disbelievers\" as a class: soon, overpowered; then, driven to Hell. The Book thus staked its credit, again, on checkable prediction (Surah 2, verse 24's \"you will never be able\"; Surah 2, verse 95's \"they will never wish for it\")—and the ledger filled within the speakers' lifetimes: Badr already banked, then the sieges' failures, Makkah's gates opened, the peninsula's fronts folded one by one; the Romans-verse's own vindication (Surah 30, verses 2 to 4) running in parallel. Prophecy of this kind is argument, not taunt: a Book that announces outcomes in advance, before audiences armed and eager to falsify it, and is never once caught wrong, is presenting credentials no forger risks.\n\n\"And driven to Hell\" extends the forecast past the visible ledger to the invisible one, and the pairing is the verse's full anatomy of defeat: overpowered in this world—the fronts broken, the boasts collected; then gathered and driven—the word is the herding of a host—to the Fire: the two-stage fate this argument has priced since Surah 2, verse 85's \"disgrace in this worldly life and... the harshest punishment on the Day of Judgment.\" The clause keeps the verse honest about its true stakes: the worldly overpowering, complete as it proved, was the smaller entry; the driving is the sentence—and its subjects are those who die in the rejection (Surah 2, verse 161's double condition standing), for this class-address, like all of them, released every individual who left the class: the boasting fronts of this verse's occasion yielded, within years, believers by the thousands, each exiting the forecast by the door the whole Book holds open.\n\n\"What an evil place to rest!\" seals the verse with the bitter idiom this argument stamped on the proud at Surah 2, verse 206—the resting-place, the cradle spread—and the echo is placed: there it closed the file of the impressive disputant whom pride carried to sin; here it closes the class of impressive fronts whom assets carried to boasting (verse 10's confiscated securities): one idiom over both, for the disease was one—and over against their evil resting-place, the chapter is about to set the alternative in its next movement: the sign at Badr read rightly (verse 13), and then, at verse 15, \"shall I inform you of what is better than all this?\"—Gardens, purity, and Allah's pleasure: the lodging the forecast's hearers were being invited, even by its severity, to choose while choosing remained.",
  "13": "\"Indeed, there was a sign for you in the two armies that met in battle—one fighting for the cause of Allah and the other in denial\" summons the freshest exhibit in the community's memory—Badr, the seventeenth of Ramadan in the second year, the first full meeting of the two orders—and names its evidentiary status: a sign, in the same register as the parted sea and the revived dead: history arranged as demonstration. The two armies are identified by their banners' realities, not their tribes: one fighting in the cause of Allah—the three-hundred-and-some of the emigrants and Ansar (Talut's river-census, Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3958); the other—kin to them by blood, arrayed against them by denial: the meeting was creed against creed, the first such sorting since the summons began.\n\n\"The believers saw their enemy twice their number\" preserves the field's optics, and the clause's precision engages the scholars: Quraysh stood near a thousand against a third of that, three times over in raw count—yet the seeing named is \"twice\": the readings converge on the battle's staged perceptions, which Surah 8, verses 43 and 44 detail from both sides—the enemy diminished in the believers' eyes at the closing \"that Allah might conclude a matter already destined,\" each host shown what the divine plan required it to see; and the deeper point stands above the arithmetic's mode: the disproportion was real, seen, and overridden—the field itself argued that outcomes do not obey head-counts.\n\n\"But Allah supports with His victory whoever He wills\" names the battle's deciding variable—the clause the whole day was staged to teach: victory is His allocation (the Talut-remnant's creed, Surah 2, verse 249: \"how many times has a small force vanquished a mighty army by the Will of Allah\"), delivered at Badr through instruments the Book itemises elsewhere: the rain that firmed the believers' ground, the drowsiness sent as security, the thousand angels rank on rank (Surah 8, verses 9 to 12), and the steadfast hearts those aids answered—for His willing, as all this argument has taught, moves with His wisdom: the supported were the praying, patient, outnumbered obedient (verse 250's three petitions granted on this very field).\n\n\"Surely in this is a lesson for people of insight\" closes the exhibit with the faculty it examines: insight—the sight behind sight, which reads events as texts; Badr's data reached every eye in Arabia, but as \"lesson\" it opened only to readers (Surah 2, verse 164's signs \"for people of understanding\"—the sorting-clause of every exhibit in this Book). And the lesson's addresses fan out as the chapter's occasion requires: to the boasting fronts of verse 12—the forecast has a down-payment; discount it at your cost; to the People of the Book weighing the new claim—your own scriptures' God still decides fields exactly so; and to the believers—permanently: the community that read Badr rightly carries its insight into every later disproportion: counted, outnumbered, and unafraid, because the deciding variable never appears in the enemy's census. The chapter now pivots, with deliberate suddenness, from the battlefield to the heart's own battlefield: the glittering appetites (verse 14) against which even Badr's victors must be armed—for the enemy of denial meets its defeat in a day, and the enemy of desire besieges every day.",
  "14": "\"The enjoyment of ˹worldly˺ desires—women, children, treasures of gold and silver, fine horses, cattle, and fertile land—has been made appealing to people\" pivots the chapter from Badr's field (verse 13) to the longer war, and its inventory is drawn with a census-taker's completeness: the six magnetisms of human life—spouse and offspring, the two hoarded metals, the mount of prestige, the herd of wealth, the land of harvest—Arabia's entire economy of desire, and beneath its antique dress every economy's since: the same six run the modern city under new names. The verse's key verb repeats the diagnosis of Surah 2, verse 212: \"made appealing\"—the adornment is real and by design: \"We have indeed made whatever is on earth as an adornment for it, in order to test them\" (Surah 18, verse 7); desire's pull is not the reader's private shame but the test's installed apparatus—and the verse's objects are not evils: women and children fill this Book's prayers (Surah 25, verse 74), wealth rightly moved is the charity-passage's whole instrument, the tended land is provision's honour. What the verse audits is the enthronement: the six loved as ends, hoarded against Allah's claims (the confiscated securities of verse 10), loved \"as they should love Allah\" (Surah 2, verse 165's line).\n\n\"These are the pleasures of this worldly life\" prices the inventory with the word this Book reserves for it: mata—the traveller's provision, the temporary kit: usable, needed, and expiring; the phrase neither curses the pleasures nor extends their lease—they are of this life, and this life is the journey's furnished lodging (Surah 2, verse 36's \"residence and provision for your appointed stay\"), its best goods thinning at the grave's door where the confiscation-rule of verse 10 operates.\n\n\"But with Allah is the finest destination\" completes the sentence with the comparison that reorders everything before it: with Allah—the location this argument has taught as the only secure vault (Surah 2, verse 110's \"you will find it with Allah\")—is the finest return: the destination outpricing the entire six-fold inventory at once; and the verse's placement gives the comparison its living force: spoken to a community fresh from Badr's spoils-questions (Surah 8 opened with them), entering years in which every named magnetism—captives, wealth, horses, land—would pour toward it in victory's stream: the warning issued at the flood's own edge, for communities are seldom drowned by their defeats. The Prophet, peace be upon him, stood at the same edge with the same warning: \"By Allah, it is not poverty I fear for you—I fear that the world will be spread out before you as it was spread before those who came before you, and you will compete for it as they competed, and it will destroy you as it destroyed them\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 4015; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2961). The verse leaves its question hanging exactly one verse—\"shall I inform you of what is better than all this?\"—and the answer descends next, item for item, with the six-fold inventory's own currency outbid.",
  "15": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ 'Shall I inform you of what is better than ˹all of˺ this?'\" answers verse 14's six-fold inventory with a merchant's own opening—the offer of the better deal—and the question's form is the passage's courtesy: desire is not scolded but outbid; the heart pulled by the adorned six is invited, not shamed, toward the superior portfolio (Surah 61, verse 10's identical courtship: \"shall I guide you to an exchange that will save you...?\"). The Quran's method with appetite, here at its clearest: acknowledge the goods, price them honestly (verse 14's \"pleasures of this worldly life\"), then present the alternative that wins the comparison outright.\n\n\"Those mindful ˹of Allah˺ will have Gardens with their Lord under which rivers flow, to stay there forever, and pure spouses\" delivers the counter-inventory, and its items answer verse 14's list entry by entry, the commentators tracing the correspondence: against the fertile land and treasures—Gardens with rivers, estate and liquidity in one image, located \"with their Lord\" (the vault-address again); against every acquisition's expiry—\"forever\": the clause that breaks all comparison, for the six magnetisms' deepest flaw was never their substance but their lease (Surah 2, verse 25's eternity-analysis); against women as possession and display—\"pure spouses\": companionship perfected, purity comprehensive (body and heart both, as at Surah 2, verse 25), intimacy without the shadow-sides desire's economy carries. The recipients are named by the chapter's watchword—the mindful, the muttaqin—for the exchange's whole mechanics is taqwa: the discipline that declines the adorned now for the better then is precisely mindfulness of Allah in its economic form.\n\n\"Along with Allah's pleasure\" crowns the counter-inventory with the item beyond category—and the Quran's own parallel passage ranks it explicitly: after listing the Gardens and dwellings, \"but Allah's pleasure is far greater: that is the ultimate triumph\" (Surah 9, verse 72); the Prophet, peace be upon him, unveiled the ranking's scene: Allah will ask the people of Paradise, \"Are you pleased?\"—they will say, how could we not be?—and He will say: \"I grant you My pleasure, and I will never be displeased with you again\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 6549; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2829): the gift above the Garden, from the Giver Himself—for the mindful were never finally trading for real estate but for the Owner's face.\n\n\"And Allah is All-Seeing of ˹His˺ servants\" seals the offer with the name that guarantees its bookkeeping—the same seal the charity-soils received (Surah 2, verse 265)—and its work here is the trade's fine print made mercy: the exchange this verse invites is transacted invisibly, in declined glances, released coins, and desires governed in private; every instalment is seen by the All-Seeing of His servants, priced at its secret cost, and banked toward the inventory just published. The verse thus closes the pivot verse 14 opened: the two portfolios stand printed side by side—the six-fold now and the everlasting with-Him—and the chapter turns immediately to the traders who chose rightly: their prayer (verse 16) and their five-fold portrait ending in the dawn's forgiveness-seeking (verse 17).",
  "16": "\"Who pray, 'Our Lord! We have believed, so forgive our sins and protect us from the torment of the Fire'\" opens the portrait of verse 15's mindful—the holders of the better portfolio—and its first stroke is their speech: they are identified, before their virtues are listed (verse 17), by what they say to their Lord; the chapter's method continuing from verse 8: communities drawn by their prayers, for nothing photographs a heart like its unforced petitions (Surah 2, verses 200 and 201's two pray-ers). And this prayer's anatomy is the believer's whole logic in one sentence. Its ground: \"We have believed\"—faith itself brought as the plea; not works amassed, ranks earned, or lineage (the entitlement-files of the second surah closed forever), but the bare covenant-fact pleaded before the Covenant-Keeper: we responded to You—the same argument the Quran teaches at its most beloved: \"our Lord! We have heard the caller to faith... so we believed: our Lord, forgive us our sins\" (this chapter's own closing movement, verse 193). Its first petition: forgiveness—the believers' instinctive first ask, for faith's immediate discovery is the account's disrepair (Surah 2, verse 285's community pleading pardon in the very breath of its creed). Its second: protection from the Fire—the plea this Book plants in its model prayers (Surah 2, verse 201's third clause), kept vivid precisely in the people furthest from deserving the Fire: the mindful pray against it because mindfulness is the Fire held in view (Surah 2, verse 24's \"fear the Fire\").\n\nThe prayer's placement inside the passage's commerce completes its meaning, and the commentators draw the thread: verse 14 priced the world's portfolio, verse 15 published the better one—Gardens, purity, and the Pleasure—and this verse shows the successful traders' actual posture, and it is not the investor's confidence but the beggar's petition: those holding the finest destination hold it asking—forgive us, protect us; the paradox is this Book's deepest teaching about assurance: the portfolio is secured by the very humility that never presumes it secured (the builders of the Kaaba begging acceptance over their own commissioned work, Surah 2, verse 127). No believer graduates from this prayer; its words remain the same on the first day of faith and the last.\n\nAnd the prayer's two petitions, read against verse 15's two grants, map with the exactness the reciters feel: forgiveness sought—purity granted (the pure spouses, the cleansed abode); the Fire feared—the Gardens given: each ask answered above its measure, for the Asked is the Giver of verse 8's closing name. The portrait now turns from the mindful's speech to their five-fold character (verse 17)—but the order already taught its lesson: in this Book's anatomy, the prayer precedes the virtues, because the virtues grow from the asking, not the asking from the virtues.",
  "17": "\"˹It is they˺ who are patient, sincere, obedient, and charitable, and who pray for forgiveness before dawn\" completes the portrait of the mindful begun at verse 15—the prayer first (verse 16), now the character—and the five strokes are arranged, as the scholars read them, in a deliberate ascent through the whole religious anatomy. Patient: the foundation-virtue (Surah 2, verse 153's equipment), the holding steady under command, against sin, and beneath affliction—named first because every later virtue stands on it. Sincere: the truthful—in speech, and deeper: in the correspondence of interior to exterior, the anti-hypocrisy this argument has audited since Surah 2, verse 8; truthfulness as the creed's load-bearing wall (the Prophet, peace be upon him: \"truthfulness leads to righteousness, and righteousness leads to Paradise\"—Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 6094; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2607). Obedient: the qunut of Surah 2, verse 238—devotion's standing posture, the will bent into the command's shape. Charitable: the spenders—the whole fourteen-verse economy of the second surah's charity-passage compressed to one word, wealth's virtue seated among the soul's. And then the fifth, held for last because the verse's crown:\n\n\"Who pray for forgiveness before dawn\" seals the portrait at a specific hour, and the specification is the verse's genius: the sahar—the last reach of night before first light, the hour of deepest sleep and emptiest streets—chosen as the signature of the completed believer. The hour's warrant stands in the Sunnah's most repeated announcement: \"Our Lord descends every night to the lowest heaven when the last third of the night remains, and says: Who calls upon Me, that I may answer? Who asks of Me, that I may give? Who seeks My forgiveness, that I may forgive?\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 1145; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 758)—the descent as befits His majesty, affirmed without asking how (the discipline of verse 7); and the portrait's people are found awake inside that announcement: risen before obligation, asking pardon at the open window. The stroke's placement after the four virtues carries the teaching the scholars treasured: after the patience, the truthfulness, the obedience, the charity—the day filled with the four—the fifth act is seeking forgiveness, as though the completed record itself were the thing most needing pardon: the logic of Surah 2, verse 199 (istighfar commanded at the rites' summit) and of the Prophet's own seventy-daily askings (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 6307), here made the mindful's daily architecture: virtue by day, contrition by night; Yaqub's sons asking their father to seek forgiveness for them were answered, the commentators note, with delay until this hour: \"I will ask my Lord's forgiveness for you\" (Surah 12, verse 98)—the patriarch banking the petition for the sahar.\n\nThe five-fold portrait, closed at the dawn-window, finishes the passage verse 14 opened: against the six magnetisms of the world's inventory stand the five disciplines of the better portfolio's holders—and the chapter, its believers now fully drawn, rises in the next verse to the summit-testimony of the whole Book: Allah Himself, the angels, and the people of knowledge bearing one witness: that there is no god but Him.",
  "18": "\"Allah ˹Himself˺ is a Witness that there is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him—and so are the angels and people of knowledge\" raises the chapter's creed to the summit-testimony of the whole Book: the oneness of Allah presented not as proposition but as attested fact, and the witness-list is the verse's majesty, read in its descending order. Allah Himself testifies—the greatest conceivable Witness to the greatest conceivable fact: His own oneness, attested by His speech (this Book), His acts (the signs of creation), and His self-disclosure; no truth in existence carries higher attestation. The angels: the unseen realm's entire population—who know their Lord at nearer range than any creature—joined as second signatories (the creed's article about them, Surah 2, verse 285, here reciprocated: the believers testify to angels who testify with believers). And the people of knowledge: the verse's stunning third seat—human scholars ranked into a testimony opened by Allah and the angels; the scholars drew from this seating the honour-doctrine of knowledge itself: no rank in the Book stands higher for the learned than co-signature on the shahadah's cosmic instrument, and the testimony's content defines who the truly knowledgeable are: whoever's learning has not arrived at \"no god but Him\" has, whatever his credentials, missed knowledge's centre (Surah 35, verse 28: \"only those fear Allah, among His servants, who have knowledge\").\n\n\"He is the Maintainer of justice\" stands inside the testimony as its middle jewel—the original: upholding equity—and its placement is doctrine: the One being attested is attested as He maintains all things in balance: the cosmos measured (Surah 55, verse 7's balance), provision apportioned, judgment exact (Surah 21, verse 47's scales that wrong no soul); oneness and justice joined in a single testimonial breath, for the false gods' regimes—idols fronting tyrannies, deified kings, enthroned appetites—are always injustice's architecture, and the true God's oneness is the ground of equity itself: one Lord, one law, no privileged bloodlines (the whole second surah's case), no purchased verdicts (Surah 2, verse 254's closed doors).\n\n\"There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him—the Almighty, All-Wise\" seals the verse by sounding the testimony's content a second time—the creed opening and closing its own attestation, the repetition the emphasis of a document's operative clause—and the two closing names sign it: Almighty—the attested oneness enforced by unconquerable might (no rival ever unseated Him because none ever existed); All-Wise—the oneness displayed in the design the wise read (verse 6's wombs, Surah 2, verse 164's eight signs). The verse became, in the community's devotion, among the most beloved single sentences of the Book—the summit of testimony recited at dawn and dusk; and its function in the chapter's occasion is the disputation's opening exhibit: before Najran's delegation and every counter-creed, the Muslims' case is laid as a witnessed instrument—and the next verse will name the religion the testimony entails: \"certainly, Allah's only Way is Islam.\"",
  "19": "\"Certainly, Allah's only Way is Islam\" draws from verse 18's witnessed testimony its institutional conclusion, and the sentence is among the Book's constitutional lines: the religion with Allah—the din He sent, accepts, and owns—is Islam: the submission (the word's whole meaning, unfolded at Surah 2, verses 112 and 131) that every prophet practised and preached: Nuh's \"I have been commanded to be one of those who submit\" (Surah 10, verse 72), Ibrahim's \"I submit to the Lord of all worlds\" (Surah 2, verse 131), Yaqub's deathbed and his sons' answer (Surah 2, verse 133), the disciples of Isa: \"bear witness that we submit\" (this chapter's verse 52 ahead). The sentence thus names one religion across all revelation—surrender to the one God by each era's sent terms—now bearing, under the final revelation, its proper name as the completed way: \"today I have perfected your religion for you... and am pleased with Islam as your religion\" (Surah 5, verse 3); and its exclusivity (\"Allah's only Way\") is the theology of verse 18 applied: one God witnessed, therefore one submission owed—rival \"ways\" being, at bottom, submissions to something else.\n\n\"Those who were given the Scripture did not dispute ˹among themselves˺ out of mutual envy until knowledge came to them\" answers, in advance, the objection the sentence always meets: if the way is one, why do the scripture-communities disagree—with Islam and each other? The verse's account repeats the finding of Surah 2, verse 213 with its devastating clause-order: the disputing came after knowledge—not from evidence's shortage but from its arrival; the differing born \"out of mutual envy\": the word is the rebellious rivalry between them—position, primacy, the resentments this argument has documented (Surah 2, verse 90's envy at grace's address; verse 109's \"envy from their own souls\"; Surah 42, verse 14's identical diagnosis). Religious division, the Book's settled doctrine: a moral event wearing doctrinal costume—the sects' genealogies trace to ambitions, not to honest readings honestly divided (the deviant hearts of verse 7 chasing pretexts).\n\n\"Whoever denies Allah's signs, then surely Allah is swift in reckoning\" closes the verse over the deniers with the pairing this argument has used at exactly this station before (Surah 2, verse 202's clause, there toward hope, here toward warning): the signs—the witnessed testimony, the confirmed Books, the recognisable Prophet (Surah 2, verse 146)—denied after knowledge invoke the reckoning; and its named speed is the warning's edge: the disputers' calculations assumed long horizons—positions to protect this generation, verdicts deferrable to a distant Day; \"swift in reckoning\" collapses the horizon (Surah 21, verse 1: \"the reckoning of humanity has drawn near\"): He audits all souls at once, His accounting instant when its hour strikes, and the hour nearer than the schemers' schedules. The verse leaves the chapter's disputation-ground fully surveyed: one witnessed God, one named way, division diagnosed at its moral root—and the next verse hands the Prophet the encounter's actual script: \"I have submitted myself to Allah, and so have my followers.\"",
  "20": "\"So if they argue with you ˹O Prophet˺, say, 'I have submitted myself to Allah, and so have my followers'\" hands the Prophet the disputation's script—and its content is the passage's masterstroke: to the arriving arguers (Najran's delegation foremost, the chapter's occasion), the commanded response is not a counter-thesis but a testimony of state: I have submitted my face to Allah—the original's idiom: the face, the whole person's frontage, surrendered (Surah 2, verse 112's wording)—and so have my followers. The move relocates the entire encounter: they came to debate natures and doctrines; he answers with the one religion just named (verse 19: \"Allah's only Way is Islam\") enacted in the first person—the argument, as it were, stood up and living; for the submission-creed's best evidence was always its practitioners (the colouring of Allah, Surah 2, verse 138), and against trained polemic the Book deploys, first, a settled soul.\n\n\"And ask those who were given the Scripture and the illiterate ˹people˺, 'Have you submitted yourselves ˹to Allah˺?'\" turns the encounter's question around, and its address-list is deliberately total: the scriptured (Jews and Christians, the learned communities) and the unlettered (the pagan Arabs, the ummiyyin without a Book—Surah 2, verse 78's word)—all humanity's religious estates served the same single question: have you submitted? The question's genius is its bypass: it does not ask \"do you accept my claims?\"—it asks each estate to audit itself against its own professed God: you who claim the scriptures' Lord, you who claim Ibrahim's shrine—has the claimed allegiance become actual surrender? The question that ends every evasion by beginning at the end.\n\n\"If they submit, they will be ˹rightly˺ guided. But if they turn away, then your duty is only to deliver ˹the message˺\" publishes the encounter's two exits with the Book's constant boundary-clause: submission is guidance—whoever, from whichever estate, surrenders has arrived (Surah 2, verse 112's rule: the door open to all, label irrelevant); and refusal transfers nothing to the deliverer: \"your duty is only to deliver\"—the mission's limit drawn for the Prophet at Surah 2, verse 119 and here re-drawn at the disputation's threshold, releasing him in advance from the outcome of the very debates the chapter is staging: the guest-delegation's hearts were never his portfolio (Surah 2, verse 272: \"you are not responsible for people's guidance\"); clarity delivered is the commission discharged.\n\n\"And Allah is All-Seeing of ˹His˺ servants\" seals the script with the name that adjudicates what the debate-hall cannot: the question just commanded—\"have you submitted?\"—will be answered in words by every party; the answers' truth is read elsewhere: the All-Seeing of His servants watches the submissions professed and the surrenders actually made, the delegations' calculations and the Prophet's faithful delivery alike (verse 15's identical seal over the traders' secret instalments). The script now issued, the chapter turns—before the guests' own file is opened—to the oldest audit on record: the community that answered its prophets' delivery not with arguments but with blades (verse 21).",
  "21": "\"Indeed, those who deny Allah's signs, kill the prophets unjustly, and kill people who stand up for justice\" opens the file the disputation-script (verse 20) made necessary—the record of what delivery has historically met—and its three-count indictment is built as an escalation of the same enmity through three targets. The signs denied: truth's evidences rejected at the mind's door (the chapter's running charge since verse 4). The prophets killed: truth's bearers destroyed when its evidences could not be—the file documented against Israel's factions at Surah 2, verses 61 and 91: Zakariyya and Yahya the tradition's named martyrs, \"unjustly\" marking (as at Surah 2, verse 61) that the crimes stood condemned by the killers' own law. And then the third count, this verse's addition to the earlier files: the killing of \"people who stand up for justice\"—those who command equity: the ordinary righteous, teachers and reformers with no prophetic office, cut down for the mere advocacy of fairness. The escalation's logic is the verse's anatomy of hardened communities: enmity to truth descends from its evidences, to its official bearers, to any private citizen who so much as speaks it—the war on the message become, at last, a war on conscience wherever found; the Prophet, peace be upon him, named the summit of martyrdom in exactly this verse's third category: \"the master of martyrs is Hamzah, and a man who stood before a tyrant ruler, commanded him and forbade him, and was killed\" (recorded in the collections; graded sound by the verifiers—al-Hakim's transmission).\n\n\"Give them good news of a painful punishment\" closes the indictment with the Book's bitterest irony—the herald's word, \"good news\" (bashshir: the gospel-verb itself, verse 39's angels will use it for Yahya's birth), served to the prophet-killers as sentence: for people who converted glad tidings' bearers into corpses, the only annunciation remaining is this one. The irony is measured retribution in language (Surah 2, verse 15's mockery returned upon mockers): every register of mercy they silenced now speaks to them once more, in inversion.\n\nThe verse's placement in the living chapter carried its edge to named addresses, and the commentators state it: the delegation-season's audiences included communities whose ancestors' file this was and whose scholars were, at that hour, re-enacting its first count against the final Prophet (Surah 2, verse 146's recognition suppressed)—the verse warning that the lineage of sign-denial runs, unless repented, toward its historical terminus. And its permanent edge faces every age's third count: regimes and mobs that no longer meet prophets may still meet—and kill—those who command justice; this verse files such blood in the same indictment as the prophets', the advocates of equity ranked, in their deaths, beside the messengers they echoed. The deeds such killers hold—the piety displayed, the charities endowed—are addressed in the very next verse: wasted, in both worlds, with no helpers.",
  "22": "\"They are the ones whose deeds are wasted in this world and the Hereafter\" pronounces upon verse 21's three-count criminals—the sign-deniers, prophet-killers, and slayers of justice's advocates—the sentence this Book reserves for its terminal files: habt, the wasting: deeds voided, the harvest annulled (the whirlwind-parable's word, Surah 2, verse 264's \"do not waste your charity\" from the same root). The clause's force is in what it concedes: they had deeds—these were religious communities: prayer, fasting, charities, scholarship, the whole apparatus of piety maintained around the killings; and the sentence voids the apparatus entire, in both worlds. In the Hereafter: the works unweighed, \"We will turn to whatever deeds they did, and reduce them to scattered dust\" (Surah 25, verse 23)—for deeds are accepted upon faith's ground (Surah 2, verse 217's apostasy-clause), and the ground here was forfeited by war on faith's own bearers. And in this world: the piety purchased nothing—no honour that lasted, no protection when the seizures came (verse 11's file), the religious establishment's centuries of observance remembered by history chiefly for the prophets' blood on its steps; the wasting temporal as well as eternal, the commentators note: their works won neither world.\n\nThe sentence's justice deserves the pause the scholars gave it: why should killing a justice-advocate void a lifetime's prayers? Because deeds are currency drawn on a covenant, and these crimes are the covenant's repudiation: the prayer performed while the Prayer-Sender's messengers are murdered is not obedience but costume (Surah 2, verse 85's selective covenant \"believing in some and rejecting the rest\"—here at its bloodiest); piety that coexists with war on piety's source has revealed its own hollowness, and the void-sentence merely publishes what the deeds already were. The verse thus completes this Book's constant separation of religiosity from religion: the apparatus can survive—flourish—while the substance is dead (the donkey carrying books, Surah 62, verse 5; the bare rock under the soil, Surah 2, verse 264).\n\n\"And they will have no helpers\" closes the file with the clause that has ended each such account (Surah 2, verse 270's wrongdoers; Surah 2, verse 86's traders): at the sentence's execution, no advocate rises—the intercessions they presumed (the entitlement-theologies of Surah 2, verse 48), the institutions they built, the nations that applauded the killings: none present, none permitted; those who cut down the standers-for-justice find, at their own standing, the category of helper abolished (the reciprocity of Surah 2, verse 270's commentary). The file closed, the chapter turns—in the next verse—from the historical indictment to its living exhibit: the same communities' scholars, invited in Madinah to their own Book's arbitration, turning away from it in the act—the ancestral crimes' method (truth refused at the door) performed politely, in the present tense, before the Prophet's own eyes.",
  "23": "\"Have you not seen those who were given a portion of the Scriptures?\" opens the living exhibit the historical file (verses 21 and 22) prepared, and its subjects are described with a qualifier the scholars weighed: given \"a portion\" of the Scripture—a share, not the whole; the phrase auditing its holders before the scene begins: what they held was partial—the Torah's text with its transmission's gaps (Surah 2, verse 79's file), and partial too in the deeper sense: scripture held as possession and profession, not as governing whole (the portion kept, the covenant-clauses shed—Surah 2, verse 85's anatomy). These are Madinah's rabbinic establishment, the Prophet's own contemporaries: the exhibit is not history but reportage.\n\n\"Yet when they are invited to the Book of Allah to settle their disputes, some of them turn away heedlessly\" stages the scene, and its documented occasions give it flesh: the commentators transmit the disputes brought to the Prophet, peace be upon him, for arbitration—most famously the case of the two who had committed adultery, where the Prophet asked the Torah's own ruling and the concealing scholar's hand was lifted from the stoning-verse before the assembly (\"the episode preserved at Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3635; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 1699); the reports also name doctrinal summons—their own Book invited to testify about Ibrahim's religion, about the awaited prophet's description (Surah 2, verse 146). The scene's construction is the exposure: invited to the Book of Allah—their Book, the arbitration they professed to live under—and the response: \"some of them turn away,\" the qualifier keeping this argument's constant census (Surah 2, verse 100's \"a group of them\"; the believing exceptions honoured at verse 199 ahead); and \"heedlessly\"—the original: while they are averse: the turning not ignorance's but aversion's: they knew what the opened Book would say, and the knowing was the reason for the walking away (Surah 2, verse 101's posture: the Book cast behind backs \"as if they did not know\").\n\nThe exhibit's force in the chapter's argument is its timing and its universality at once. Its timing: placed directly after the deed-wasting sentence (verse 22), it shows the sentence's living candidates mid-act—the ancestral escalation (signs denied, prophets killed) here at its polite first stage: truth declined at the committee table; the blade's grandfather is the averted face. And its universality: the verse's charge transfers, with full force, to every community that holds scripture—the Muslims foremost, custodians now of the whole rather than a portion: whoever professes the Book's arbitration and then, invited to a ruling that costs his position, his faction, or his appetite, \"turns away heedlessly,\" is performing this verse in his own person (Surah 24, verse 51 states the believers' counter-portrait: invited to Allah and His Messenger's judgment, they say \"we hear and obey\"). The next verse will name the theology that made such turning feel safe—the counted days of the Fire—the entitlement-doctrine this argument demolished once at Surah 2, verse 80 and here finds still running, a generation later, under the same management.",
  "24": "\"This is because they say, 'The Fire will not touch us except for a few days'\" opens the diagnosis beneath verse 23's averted faces—and the \"because\" is the verse's whole work: conduct traced to creed; the scholars who turned from their own Book's arbitration could afford the turning, in their own minds, because their theology had capped the downside: a few counted days of Fire, the entitlement-doctrine this argument met and demolished at Surah 2, verse 80 (its variants there: the calf's forty days, a token week)—found here, a full argument later, still operational: for false comforts are not killed by refutation alone; they are revenue-bearing assets, and their holders repair them overnight. The doctrine's function is what this verse dissects: a capped Hell is a licence-printing machine—every betrayal of the Book becomes rational once its maximum price is a brief singe; the averted face at the arbitration-table (verse 23), the concealed verse, the resisted Prophet: all covered, in the holders' accounting, by the policy's few-days deductible. Every religion's corrupters require some version of this cap—the indulgence, the automatic intercession, the tribal immunity—and the Quran's war on it (Surah 2, verses 80 to 82; Surah 4, verse 123's \"neither by your wishes nor the wishes of the People of the Book\") is its war for moral seriousness itself.\n\n\"They have been deceived in their faith by their wishful lying\" completes the diagnosis with a sentence whose mechanism deserves its exactness: what they fabricated—the counted-days doctrine, humanly authored (Surah 2, verse 79's pens) and wishfully believed (Surah 2, verse 78's amaniyy: the wish-religion)—returned upon its authors as deception in their religion: the lie, told long enough, deceived the tellers; the forgers became the forgery's final victims (the self-deception spiral of Surah 2, verse 9: \"they deceive none but themselves\"). The clause's word-order is the tragedy compressed: deceived in their din—the deception penetrated the religion itself: worship continued, scripture recited, the whole apparatus running—operated throughout by a counterfeit actuarial table; religion practised inside a lie about its own stakes, the most complete deception a soul can suffer, for it corrupts the very instrument (the din) by which deceptions are detected.\n\nThe verse's permanent audit faces every inheritor of scripture, the Muslims explicitly included, and honesty requires its statement: the counted-days doctrine has Muslim dresses—salvation presumed by the label (Surah 2, verse 111's demolished monopolies), punishment discounted by communal membership, sins licensed against an assumed brief remand before an assumed guaranteed exit; the authentic intercession-texts (real, for the people of tawhid, by His leave—Surah 2, verse 255) converted by wish into exactly the cap this verse condemns. Against every version stands the Book's published actuarial table: \"whoever commits evil will be rewarded accordingly\" (Surah 4, verse 123), the deeds weighed, the mercy real and never mocked—and the next verse strikes the table's gavel: \"how will it be when We gather them on the Day about which there is no doubt?\"—the counted days meeting the uncounted One.",
  "25": "\"But how ˹horrible˺ will it be when We gather them together on the Day about which there is no doubt\" answers the counted-days theology of verse 24 with the Book's simplest and most terrible instrument: the question that stages the meeting between the doctrine and the Day. \"How will it be\"—the interrogative left open, the horror uncompleted by design: the reader's own imagination is conscripted to finish the sentence, standing the few-days claimants in the gathering their arithmetic never priced. The Day is named by the two credentials this argument has stamped on it: the gathering—all humanity convened (Surah 2, verse 148's convergence; verse 9's confession: \"You will certainly gather all humanity\"), no absentee option for the doctrine's holders; and \"no doubt about it\"—the chapter's own sealed phrase (verse 9), the certainty-grade of the Book itself (Surah 2, verse 2) assigned to the appointment: whatever else the wish-religion discounted, the meeting's date stands undiscountable.\n\n\"When every soul will be paid in full for what it has done\" states the Day's operating rule—and its wording is the exact rule that closed the second surah's revelation (Surah 2, verse 281, the near-last verse sent down: \"every soul will be paid in full for what it has done\")—here re-sounded against the precise doctrine it annihilates: payment in full, by the earning (the wafiyya-verb of complete discharge: no instalment-plan, no capped remand, no communal flat-rate); the counted-days table presumed a tariff by membership; the Day's published rule is an audit by deeds—\"whoever commits evil will be rewarded accordingly\" (Surah 4, verse 123's demolition of both communities' wishes), each soul's file its own (Surah 2, verse 134's untransferable ledgers). Between the two accounting-systems—theirs by label, His by earning—the gathering adjudicates, and only one system operates there.\n\n\"And none will be wronged\" closes the verse with the Judge's solvency-clause (Surah 2, verse 281's own ending), and its placement against this audience is the verse's finest justice: the clause is, at once, the wish-theology's final refutation and the tribunal's guarantee to its very holders. Refutation: none wronged means none over-credited—the counted days will not be honoured, for honouring a forged voucher wrongs every honest account (the entitlement-doctrine was always, at bottom, a demand that the Judge participate in the forgery). And guarantee: none wronged means the doctrine's holders themselves receive exact justice—no ancestral crime charged to them (Surah 2, verse 134), no earned good uncounted (Surah 2, verse 62's open door), their repentance to the last breath received (Surah 2, verse 160): the same clause that voids their forged credit protects their genuine account. The question-verse thus completes the three-verse arc (23 to 25) with the argument's constant motion: from the averted face, to the doctrine beneath it, to the Day above it—and the chapter now turns, in the next verse, from the deniers' imagined ledgers to the true Ledger-Owner's throne-room: \"Say: O Allah! Lord over all authorities...\"—the sovereignty-prayer that dissolves every human pretension the passage has been auditing.",
  "26": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ 'O Allah! Lord over all authorities! You give authority to whoever You please and remove it from who You please'\" rises from the audited pretensions of the preceding passage into the Book's great sovereignty-prayer—and its taught wording is itself the doctrine: the address, \"Owner of all dominion\" (the original's Malik al-mulk), names Allah as sovereignty's sole proprietor: every throne, office, mandate, and hegemony a leasehold from His estate; and the two verbs publish the estate's management: given to whom He pleases, stripped from whom He pleases—the lease revocable at the Owner's will, no dynasty's tenure self-secured (Talut's file: \"Allah grants kingship to whoever He wills,\" Surah 2, verse 247) and no people's forfeiture final by pedigree. The prayer's occasion-setting gives its audacity flesh, as the commentators note: taught in Madinah—a town of date-groves between two empires' shadows—to a community promised, amid the sieges, the treasures of Caesar and Khosrau (the promises preserved at Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3618's family); within a generation, the two leases of the age were called in, and the prayer's clauses had become history's headline.\n\n\"You honour whoever You please and disgrace who You please\" extends the estate past thrones to the subtler dominion: honour itself—izzah, standing, weight among creatures—distributed and withdrawn by the same Hand; the clause's audit falling on every ladder humans climb for it: \"whoever desires honour—then all honour belongs to Allah\" (Surah 35, verse 10), sought elsewhere it converts to disgrace (the hypocrites seeking izzah at the disbelievers' tables, Surah 4, verse 139), while His granting reverses every human ranking: the enslaved Bilal raised to the Kaaba's roof, the age's aristocracies beneath his call.\n\n\"All good is in Your Hands\" gathers the prayer's four verbs under its creed-clause, and the wording's selection is the theology this Book keeps exact: the good ascribed to His Hands—wholly, \"and evil is not attributed to You\" (the Prophet's own prayer, Sahih Muslim, Hadith 771): what He decrees of stripping and disgrace is, in His wisdom, justice or medicine—good in its design even when bitter in its taste (Surah 2, verse 216's principle); the clause training the prayer's users to read the departures of dominion—their own losses included—inside goodness's estate, never outside it.\n\n\"Surely You ˹alone˺ are Most Capable of everything\" seals the prayer with the capability-clause this argument stamps on its largest claims (Surah 2, verses 106, 148)—and here it underwrites the prayer's whole politics: the community taught these words is thereby immunised against history's two idolatries: the worship of power's holders (their lease is revocable tonight—\"how many a town We destroyed in its prosperity\") and despair before power's imbalances (the Owner's transfers need no parliament: one night at Badr redrew Arabia's table). The believer prays the verse and rises with the age's ledger re-read: thrones, offices, honours—all instruments in the Hands that hold all good; and the next verse will widen the estate from the political to the cosmic: night and day interleaved, the living and the dead exchanged, and provision without measure—the same Hands, the same ease.",
  "27": "\"You cause the night to pass into the day and the day into the night\" continues the sovereignty-prayer of verse 26 from thrones to the cosmos, and the continuation is the argument: the same Hands that transfer dominion transfer the daylight—the verse's verb is the merging: night's substance threaded into day and day's into night, the lengths exchanging through the year's turning, dawn and dusk the daily visible act of the Owner's administration; and the pairing with verse 26 teaches the scale on which political transfers should be read: the God who slides continents of darkness into light each morning, unresisted, moves empires with the same silence (the commentators reading the two verses as one lesson: the removal of kings is, to Him, the removal of a night).\n\n\"You bring forth the living from the dead and the dead from the living\" names the deeper exchange, and its registers stack as the scholars unfolded them: in nature—the green shoot from the dry seed, the bird from the egg's stillness, life extracted daily from death's materials (Surah 2, verse 164's rain reviving the dead earth); in history—the resurrections this argument has exhibited (Surah 2's five specimens); and in the register the early authorities loved most: the believer brought forth from the disbeliever—the living heart from the dead house: Ibrahim from Azar the idol-carver, and (the chapter's coming narratives making the reading almost explicit) Maryam from Imran's aged line, the prayer's clause about to be enacted in story: the barren womb opened (verse 40), the virgin's chamber visited (verse 47)—for the annunciations ahead are this verse's verbs in biography.\n\n\"And You provide for whoever You will without limit\" closes the prayer with provision—the estate's daily distribution—and the phrase this Book has twice hung over unexpected abundance: \"without limit,\" without count or account (Surah 2, verse 212's ending; and ahead, at verse 37, its narrative enactment: Maryam's chamber stocked out of season, and her answer to Zakariyya word for word this clause: \"Allah provides for whoever He wills without limit\"). The clause completes the prayer's four-fold estate—dominion, honour, the cosmos's turnings, and bread—one Owner across all registers, and its placement arms the praying community at its actual anxieties: politically overshadowed (verse 26's answer), and materially thin (this clause's): the boycott-survivors and siege-eaters taught to address the Provider whose measures ignore all sieges.\n\nThe two-verse prayer, held whole, is among the Book's most complete instruments: sovereignty, honour, time, life, death, and provision confessed into the same two Hands—and the confession's fruit is the believer's signature freedom: unawed by any holder of anything, for every holding is a lease; unafraid of any withholding, for the supply-lines run from \"without limit.\" And the chapter's arrangement now performs its quiet masterstroke: from this prayer's cosmic estate it steps directly into the guardianship-law (verse 28)—for a community that has just confessed where all power and provision reside has been shown exactly why it need never purchase protection at loyalty's expense: the Owner's clients do not pawn their allegiance to the leaseholders.",
  "28": "\"Believers should not take disbelievers as guardians instead of the believers\" legislates the community's alliance-law directly upon the sovereignty-prayer's foundation (verses 26 and 27)—and the sequence is the law's own reasoning: a people that has just confessed all dominion, honour, and provision into Allah's Hands has no rational motive left for the transaction here forbidden: the taking of His enemies as awliya—guardians, patrons, intimate protectors—\"instead of the believers\": the clause that defines the crime precisely; not kindness, trade, neighbourliness, or justice toward disbelievers (all protected elsewhere: \"Allah does not forbid you from dealing kindly and fairly with those who have neither fought you... \" Surah 60, verse 8; the Prophet's own pledged dealings, his armour with a Jewish creditor—Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 2916)—but allegiance transferred: the community's loyalty, counsel, and protective intimacy relocated to the faith's opponents over the faith's own house; the Madinan hazard by name, where hypocrites kept insurance-alliances among the hostile tribes (\"those with sickness in their hearts race toward them, saying: we fear a turn of fortune\"—Surah 5, verse 52).\n\n\"And whoever does so will have nothing to hope for from Allah\" prices the transfer at its true cost, and the wording's totality is the deterrent: nothing from Allah—the original: he is not of Allah in anything: the relationship itself severed by the rival guardianship, for wala is exclusive by nature (Surah 2, verse 257's architecture: one Wali of the believers; the crowd of false patrons for the rest)—whoever pawns the community's allegiance to its enemies has left the Guardian's client-list entirely, whatever his registration papers profess.\n\n\"Unless it is a precaution against their tyranny\" opens the law's measured exception, and the scholars bounded it as the wording does: under coercive power—the believer captive, the minority under persecution's hand—outward accommodation is licensed as shield: the tongue's concession under duress while \"the heart is at rest with faith\" (Surah 16, verse 106's charter, revealed for Ammar under torture); a concealment of necessity, never a career—the exception measured by the tyranny it shields against, expiring with it, and licensing no inward affection nor real betrayal of the believers.\n\n\"And Allah warns you about Himself. And to Allah is the final return\" seals the law with the Book's most solemn deterrent-formula, unique in its directness: He warns you of Himself—not of the enemy's untrustworthiness (though the argument has documented it: Surah 2, verse 120's unappeasable displeasure) but of the Guardian abandoned: the transaction's real peril was never the foreign patron's knife but the true Patron's displeasure; and \"to Him is the final return\" closes the account where all this Book's accounts close: the alliance-portfolios mature at the gathering, where the purchased patrons stand disowning their clients (Surah 2, verse 166) and the Guardian's clients stand in His keeping. The verse's balance—allegiance guarded absolutely, coexistence and kindness untouched, the persecuted sheltered by licence—remains the community's alliance-jurisprudence entire; and the next verse will follow the law inward, to the chest where hidden loyalties are actually kept: \"whether you conceal what is in your hearts or reveal it, Allah knows.\"",
  "29": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ 'Whether you conceal what is in your hearts or reveal it, it is known to Allah'\" follows the alliance-law of verse 28 into its enforcement-chamber—and the sequence is exact jurisprudence, for the crime that verse forbade is precisely the crime committed in concealment: allegiance is a heart-transaction; the hypocrite's foreign guardianships were held as secret policies, the outward community-membership maintained above them (Surah 2, verse 14's two faces); so the law's next clause is this verse's audit: the chest's contents—the hidden loyalty, the hedged commitment, the insurance kept with the enemy—known to Allah in both states, concealed or declared, the two conditions named only to be equalised (Surah 2, verse 284's identical audit, whose descent bowed the Companions' backs). The verse's taught form—\"Say\"—makes the audit public doctrine: the community is to know, and to know that everyone knows, that its interior politics are transacted before an open ledger.\n\n\"For He knows whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth\" widens the audit from chests to cosmos, and the widening is the argument's logic (verse 5's identical structure at the chapter's threshold): the knowledge that misses nothing in the heavens' reaches and the earth's depths does not strain at a heart's closed door—the concealed alliance is, to that Knowledge, as public as the sky; and the clause's pairing with the sovereignty-prayer completes the passage's architecture: verses 26 and 27 confessed all power and provision into His Hands; verse 28 drew the alliance-law from the confession; this verse seals it with all knowledge—power, provision, and information: the three securities for which loyalties are ever sold, all three already His.\n\n\"And Allah is Most Capable of everything\" closes the audit with the capability-clause (verses 26's ending; Surah 2, verse 284's own seal)—and its work here is the enforcement's guarantee: the Knower of the hidden guardianships is Capable over their holders and their patrons alike; the secret policy's entire premise—that the foreign protector can deliver what the true Guardian might withhold—dies before the pairing: what could the leaseholder deliver against the Owner (verse 26), and what could the concealment shelter from the Knower (this verse), and what could any of them effect against the Capable-over-everything?\n\nThe verse's permanent work runs past its first politics into the believer's whole interior economy, as the scholars read it: every double-ledger of the heart—the professed devotion over the hidden appetite, the public creed over the private hedging, the displayed contentment over the concealed resentment of the decree—stands under this verse's equalising clause; and its mercy is the same as its severity: the heart known completely is the heart that need perform nothing (Surah 2, verse 235's tender parallel: \"Allah knows that you will remember them\"—the interior foreknown and provided for). The next verse will convert the audit into its final scene: the Day each soul finds its record present—good and evil laid out—and wishes a great distance from what it hid.",
  "30": "\"˹Watch for˺ the Day when every soul will be presented with whatever good it has done\" converts the heart-audit of verse 29 into its final session, and the scene's mechanics are the doctrine: presented—the original: each soul finds its deeds brought forth, muhdaran: made present, attending in person; the record not read about but met—\"they will find everything they did present before them\" (Surah 18, verse 49), the deeds themselves arrayed as the session's furnishings. And the good named first: whatever good it has done, found present—the clause the believer should not rush past: the half-date (Surah 2, verse 263's kind word, verse 271's secret coin), the dawn-forgiveness (verse 17), the unwitnessed patience—all of it kept, none of it lost (Surah 2, verse 143's \"Allah would never discount your faith\"), attending its doer at the session like witnesses for the defence.\n\n\"And it will wish that its misdeeds were far off\" stages the session's other finding with the Book's most piercing image of regret: the soul, meeting its evil made present, wishes between itself and that day's exhibit \"a great distance\"—the original's measure: an immense span, oceans of separation begged for between the doer and the deed now standing beside him. The image's exactness is the teaching: in the world, the sinner and his sin were intimates—chosen, repeated, defended (Surah 2, verse 206's pride); at the presentation, the intimacy inverts to horror: the deed unownable and undisownable at once, nearer than a shadow and wished farther than the horizon; regret's whole anatomy (Surah 2, verse 167's \"Allah will make them remorseful\") compressed into one wish that cannot be granted—for the session's law is presence: everything attends.\n\n\"And Allah warns you about Himself\" repeats, word for word, verse 28's unique deterrent—the double sounding within three verses the emphasis this Book reserves for its gravest notices: the warning is of Himself—His knowledge (verse 29), His session (this verse), His justice; all other warnings are of instruments; this one is of the Judge.\n\n\"And Allah is Ever Gracious to ˹His˺ servants\" closes the passage with the turn that the scholars treasured as the Book's signature mercy-stroke: the same breath that warned of Him names Him Ra'uf—full of the tenderest kindness (Surah 2, verse 207's closing name over the self-seller to Allah)—and the pairing is the whole passage's theology resolved: the warning itself is the graciousness; the session announced in advance is the session made avoidable: every clause of the presentation-scene was published decades before each reader's own session precisely so the exhibits could be changed while the record still writes—the misdeeds repented off the table (Surah 2, verse 160), the distance wished-for then purchasable now, at the price of turning. The Gracious-to-His-servants warns them of Himself because He would have none of them meet the scene unwarned; and the chapter now turns to the door out of the whole peril—the love of Allah, and its single test: \"Say: if you love Allah, then follow me.\"",
  "31": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ 'If you ˹sincerely˺ love Allah, then follow me; Allah will love you'\" delivers the sentence the scholars named the verse of the test—and its occasion gives the test its edge: the commentators transmit that claimants of the divine love surrounded the revelation on every side—the entitlement-communities' \"we are the children of Allah and His most beloved\" (Surah 5, verse 18), Najran's delegation loving God through doctrines the chapter is correcting, and every age's mystics and nations announcing the same credential—so revelation, meeting the largest claim a creature can make, does what it did to every claim before (Surah 2, verse 111's \"show your proof\"): it publishes the examination. The claim: you love Allah. The test: then follow me—the Messenger's way, the sunnah in its whole breadth: his creed, his worship, his dealings, his priorities; love's evidence located not in intensity of feeling (unfalsifiable, and claimed by all) but in conformity of life—for love, wherever it is real, obeys its beloved's pleasure: \"the sign of love is following,\" the early masters said over this verse, and whoever claims the love while walking another way has been examined and graded by his own biography.\n\n\"Allah will love you\" publishes the test's prize, and the scholars stood long before the exchange's asymmetry: the claimant's question was whether he loves Allah; the verse's answer concerns whether Allah will love him—the greater matter by infinite measure, and the one no devotee can self-award: the servant's love, even genuine, is a creature's affection; Allah's love descending on a servant is election itself—the rank whose issue is the hadith's cascade: \"My servant continues to draw near to Me with voluntary deeds until I love him; and when I love him, I am his hearing with which he hears, his sight with which he sees...\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 6502)—and the verse routes the whole ascent through one gate: the following; the voluntary deeds that draw near are the followed Prophet's own curriculum.\n\n\"And forgive your sins. For Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful\" completes the prize with the lover's first need met—for the claimant examined honestly discovers, first of all, a record (verse 30's presentation standing two verses back): love's road, this clause teaches, does not require arriving clean; it requires arriving following, and the following itself draws the pardon—sins forgiven as the love descends, the two names sealing the exchange in the register this Book keeps for its widest doors (Surah 2, verse 218's hopers).\n\nThe verse's function in the community's life became exactly what its structure built: the permanent criterion between devotion and its counterfeits—every innovation pleading love's licence (\"we honour him beyond his own way\"), every antinomian mysticism pleading love's transcendence of law, every cultural religion pleading love's inheritance, meets this verse's single unyielding clause: then follow me; and the verse's mercy is equally permanent: the follower of modest feelings and faithful steps outranks, on this examination, the ecstatic of grand claims and foreign paths—for the paper never asked how the claimant feels; it asked whom he follows, and the next verse will repeat the command stripped even of the love-clause: \"obey Allah and His Messenger\"—the test's floor beneath its summit.",
  "32": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ 'Obey Allah and His Messenger'\" follows the love-test of verse 31 with its floor—and the sequence of the two verses is a complete education in this Book's method: verse 31 courted the summit (love, and Allah's love returned); this verse states the minimum beneath which no claim survives: obedience—to Allah, and joined to Him by the conjunction that became the religion's constitutional grammar, His Messenger: the pairing this Book sounds in every register (\"whoever obeys the Messenger has truly obeyed Allah,\" Surah 4, verse 80), for the Messenger is obedience's address on earth: the commands of Allah arrive as his teaching, his practice, his rulings (Surah 2, verse 129's \"the Book and wisdom\"), and the theoretical monotheist who bypasses the Messenger has bypassed the only channel monotheism was ever delivered through. The double command's timing, the commentators note, faced the delegation-season's precise evasion: Najran's guests, and the scripture-communities generally, professed obedience to Allah while declining His final Messenger—the profession this verse's conjunction renders incoherent.\n\n\"If they still turn away, then truly Allah does not like the disbelievers\" closes the two-verse examination with its failing grade, and each term is measured. \"If they turn away\": the turning is from the stated pair—the command's channel refused, the claimed devotion to the Sender maintained over the sent one's rejection (Surah 2, verse 285's community-creed the exact inverse: \"we make no distinction... we hear and obey\"). Then the sentence, and its construction carries the passage's whole theology: the verse does not say \"Allah does not like them\" but names the category—\"Allah does not like the disbelievers\": the turning-away itself files the turner under disbelief's heading, for the refusal of the Messenger's authority, maintained knowingly, is not a lover's quarrel within faith but the covenant's rejection (Surah 4, verses 150 and 151's \"we believe in some and reject others... those are the true disbelievers\"); and the love-vocabulary's return is the two-verse unit's deliberate symmetry: verse 31 opened the summit—follow, and be loved; this verse closes the floor—turn away, and stand outside the love entirely: between \"Allah will love you\" and \"Allah does not like the disbelievers\" runs the whole examination, and the single variable across both sentences is the Messenger followed or refused.\n\nThe unit's permanent audit, the scholars pressed, faces inward as searchingly as outward: the community that inherited the Messenger's name inherits the examination's terms—obedience as love's floor: the professed devotion of any Muslim is graded, clause by clause, against the Sunnah's actual claims on his wealth (Surah 2's charity-law), his contracts (its debt-law), his household (its family-law), his allegiances (verse 28); and the claimed love that maintains standing exemptions from the beloved's known commands has failed the floor while reciting the summit. The examination published, the chapter now turns to its great narratives—and the turn is itself the argument's next move: the family of Imran's story will display what the examined love looks like lived: a mother vowing her womb's contents to the Owner's service, a child raised in the sanctuary, and the annunciations that set the whole disputation's subject—Isa, son of Maryam—in his true place: within the chosen families of the obedient.",
  "33": "\"Indeed, Allah chose Adam, Noah, the family of Abraham, and the family of 'Imrân above all people ˹of their time˺\" opens the chapter's great narrative movement—the stories that give the surah its name—and its opening instrument is a genealogy of election: four names, spanning the whole human story, each marked with the chapter's key verb: chose—istafa, the selecting-out, the same election-word this argument stamped on Ibrahim (Surah 2, verse 130: \"We certainly chose him\") and on the faith itself (Surah 2, verse 132: \"Allah has chosen for you this faith\"). The list's construction is the doctrine: Adam—election at the species' head (the honouring of Surah 2, verses 30 to 34: taught, prostrated-to, chosen after the slip: \"then his Lord chose him,\" Surah 20, verse 122); Nuh—election at history's re-founding, the first messenger against the first idolatry (Surah 2, verse 213's fractured unity); the family of Ibrahim—election become a household: the line through Ismail and Ishaq carrying prophethood by covenant (Surah 2, verse 124's terms: \"My covenant is not extended to the wrongdoers\"—election never mere pedigree); and the family of Imran—the list's destination and the chapter's subject: the house of Musa and Harun's line in its first sense, and, by the narrative about to open, the nearer house of Imran, father of Maryam: the family whose vowed daughter and miraculous grandson the whole disputation concerns.\n\nThe verse's placement performs its argument before a story is told, and the commentators read the strategy whole: the Najran delegation's case deified Isa; this verse files his house—by name, at the narrative's head—inside the election-series: chosen \"above all people,\" as Adam was chosen, as Nuh was: election, the category that honours creatures; the family of Imran seated beside the family of Ibrahim as parallel households of the same choosing Lord—the highest available honour, and a created one: the verse's category-work already deciding the debate its stories will document. And the election's stated scope—\"above all people\" of their worlds—carries the doctrine this argument fixed at Surah 2, verse 47: preference real, era-bound, and covenant-conditioned: each chosen line honoured for its bearing of the trust, each line's unworthy members outside the choosing by the Ibrahim-clause.\n\nFor the believer, the verse's genealogy is also his own family-tree of faith, and the scholars drew the reading: the elections named are one continuous project—the same trust carried from Adam's teaching, through Nuh's ark, Ibrahim's house, and Imran's sanctuary-child, to the community now reciting the list (Surah 2, verse 143's commissioning)—and the next verse will name the project's connecting thread in three words: \"descendants of one another\": one lineage of obedience, one Hearer and Knower over it; the door into the chapter's tenderest narrative—a mother, a vow, and a girl named Maryam—now standing open.",
  "34": "\"They are descendants of one another\" threads verse 33's four elections onto a single line, and the sentence's compression holds the passage's two deepest teachings at once. Genealogically: the chosen houses are literally one descent—Nuh from Adam's line, Ibrahim from Nuh's, the families of Imran (Musa's house and Maryam's alike) from Ibrahim's through Ishaq: election running down one unbroken human lineage across millennia, the covenant-project of history carried in a single family tree (Surah 29, verse 27: \"We placed prophethood and the Scripture among his descendants\"). And spiritually—the reading the scholars set beside the literal as its meaning: descendants of one another in faith's substance: each generation of the line reproducing the others' creed, surrender, and office—\"some of them from others\" in the likeness that matters: Adam's repentance in Nuh's patience in Ibrahim's submission in Imran's house's devotion: one religion inherited (Surah 2, verse 132's bequest-scene: the faith handed down as the estate), so that the line's true continuity was never the blood—which carried Kanan, Nuh's drowned son (Surah 11, verses 42 to 46: \"he is not of your family\"), and Azar the idol-maker—but the covenant the blood transmitted where hearts received it: the Ibrahim-clause (Surah 2, verse 124) sorting every generation of the descent.\n\nThe clause's work in the chapter's argument is preparation with a jurist's foresight, the commentators note: the narrative about to open turns entirely on lineage's meanings—a mother from the chosen descent vowing her child (verse 35), a daughter born where a son was expected (verse 36), and at the story's summit a child with no father at all (verse 47): and this verse has already laid the doctrine the miracle will rest on: the election-line is one because its Chooser is one; He who ran the descent through its ordinary begettings for millennia may, at the line's crown, run it through a mother alone—Isa fully \"of\" the chosen descent, \"descendants of one another,\" by Maryam's blood and the line's true substance: the surrender (verse 52's disciples completing it: \"bear witness that we submit\"); the fatherless conception no exit from the family but its final sign.\n\n\"And Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing\" seals the genealogy with the pairing whose selection the next verse instantly justifies: All-Hearing—for the narrative's first scene is a prayer: Imran's wife speaking her vow (\"so accept it from me: You alone are truly the All-Hearing, All-Knowing\"—verse 35 closing with this verse's very names); All-Knowing—for the story's engine is knowledge exceeding its actors': the mother vowing a temple-servant and knowing not she carried the temple's lady (\"Allah fully knew what she had delivered,\" verse 36). The election-doctrine thus closes where election always operates in this Book: not in bloodlines' automatic mechanics but in the Hearing that receives each generation's prayers and the Knowledge that places each soul—the two names under which a barren house and a virgin's chamber are about to become the line's most honoured stations.",
  "35": "\"˹Remember˺ when the wife of 'Imrân said, 'My Lord! I dedicate what is in my womb entirely to Your service'\" opens the chapter's tenderest narrative at its true beginning—not in a sanctuary or an annunciation but inside a pregnancy, with a mother's voice—and the speaker's placement is the election-doctrine (verses 33 and 34) made biography: the wife of Imran, of the chosen descent, transmitting the line's substance in the act the verse records: the vow. Its content, in the original's single word, is total: muharraran—freed, dedicated: the child in her womb released from every worldly claim—her own maternal claims first—to the Temple's service entire: Allah's house to be its house, His worship its occupation; the era's highest conceivable offering, and the verse's adverb (\"entirely\") the vow's whole daring: she reserved nothing. The scene's teaching stands before its story does, the scholars note: the greatest figures of the coming narrative—Maryam, and through her Isa—enter history through a mother's unreserved gift; the line of election runs, at this link, through a woman's devotion: the chapter that will crown Maryam above the world's women (verse 42) opens the file with the mother whose vow made her.\n\n\"So accept it from me\" is the vow's second movement, and its verb carries the spiritual posture this Book stamps on every offering it honours: acceptance begged, not presumed—the identical plea of the Kaaba's builders over history's most honoured construction (\"Our Lord! Accept this from us,\" Surah 2, verse 127): the offerer's eye not on the gift's grandeur but on its reception; for the vow's value, she knows, is not in her womb's contents nor her renunciation's size but in the Receiver's taking—deeds are the condition, acceptance the gift (the doctrine of Surah 2, verse 127's commentary), and the mother of Maryam prays at the same door as Ibrahim.\n\n\"You ˹alone˺ are truly the All-Hearing, All-Knowing\" closes the vow with the two names verse 34 planted in advance, and their selection is the prayer's own logic: All-Hearing—the vow was speech, a woman's voice in a private chamber addressing her Lord over her own body: heard entire; All-Knowing—the vow's object was hidden even from its maker: \"what is in my womb,\" contents unknown, sex unknown, future unknown—dedicated blind by the mother, received seeing by the Known-to-Whom: and the story's next scene will turn exactly on that asymmetry: she will deliver a girl where the Temple's service expected boys, and discover that the Knowledge her prayer invoked had planned past her plan. The verse thus hands every reader the anatomy of the offered life: dedication total, acceptance begged, outcomes surrendered to the Hearing that received the words and the Knowing that holds what the offerer cannot see—the posture in which the narrative's every miracle will now unfold.",
  "36": "\"When she delivered, she said, 'My Lord! I have given birth to a girl'\" records the vow's collision with the decree—and the scene's emotional truth is the Quran's honesty at its most tender: the wife of Imran had dedicated her womb's contents to Temple-service (verse 35), an office the era staffed with boys; the delivery brings a daughter, and her words to her Lord carry the wonder and the ache of a plan overturned: I have delivered a female—the vow, to her eye, landed on an impossibility. The verse's parenthesis answers her before the story does: \"and Allah fully knew what she had delivered\"—the clause the whole narrative turns on: her surprise was never His; the Knowledge her own vow invoked (verse 35's closing name) had received the dedication knowing its object, and the \"impossibility\" was the plan: the commentators state what the chapter will spend twenty verses proving—she asked for a servant of the sanctuary and was given its sovereign lady; the girl outstripping every boy the Temple ever housed.\n\n\"And the male is not like the female\" preserves her reasoning—by its plain sense the mother's own assessment: the male she expected is not like the female she holds, for the service's demands—and the verse leaves her sentence standing while the narrative quietly inverts it: true, and truer than she knew: this female would be unlike every male: chosen over the worlds' women (verse 42), mother of a Word from Him (verse 45); the clause becoming, in the story's light, an unwitting prophecy—the decree's surprises, the scholars draw, are upgrades the offerer's categories could not price (Surah 2, verse 216's principle at its most beautiful: \"Allah knows and you do not know\").\n\n\"I have named her Mary, and I seek Your protection for her and her offspring from Satan, the accursed\" completes the mother's response—and its two movements are the model of received decree: no withdrawal of the vow (the daughter is delivered to the dedication regardless: the gift not repossessed when its form surprised), and the mother's remaining office discharged in two acts: the name given—Maryam, \"the devout worshipper\" in her people's tongue by the commentators' account—and the protection begged: for her, and for her offspring—the prayer's reach astonishing: sheltering, in one clause, the daughter in her arms and the grandson no natural expectation could foresee. And the Sunnah records the prayer's answer as a unique historical fact: the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"No child is born but Satan touches it at its birth, so it begins screaming from his touch—except Maryam and her son\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3431; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2366)—the mother's dawn-of-life petition granted across two generations: of all humanity, the two named in her prayer alone shielded from the toucher at the gate. The verse thus closes the vow-narrative's first movement with its complete teaching: the offering held through the decree's surprise, the child covered by prayer before she was covered by a blanket—and the acceptance the mother begged (verse 35) arrives in the next verse's first words: \"so her Lord accepted her graciously.\"",
  "37": "\"So her Lord accepted her graciously and blessed her with a pleasant upbringing—entrusting her to the care of Zachariah\" answers the mother's two prayers (verses 35 and 36) in the narrative's own voice, and each clause is a petition receipted: she begged acceptance—\"her Lord accepted her\": the girl-child taken into the very service her sex was thought to bar, \"with gracious acceptance\" as the original doubles it: received not by concession but with welcome (the vow's surprise-form embraced, the decree's upgrade confirmed); she surrendered the upbringing—He \"blessed her with a pleasant upbringing\": the original's image is growth: He caused her to grow a goodly growing: body, soul, and station cultivated by the Accepter Himself; and the custody question that verse 44 will show the Temple's men casting lots over is here settled in its result: Zakariyya—prophet, and by the accounts her relative by marriage—made her guardian: the child of the vow placed under prophethood's own roof, the mother's protection-prayer (verse 36) staffed with a prophet.\n\n\"Whenever Zachariah visited her in the sanctuary, he found her supplied with provisions\" opens the scene the whole story pivots on: the mihrab—her sanctuary-chamber, the withdrawn room of worship—entered by her guardian, and each entry meeting the same standing wonder: provision already there; the commentators transmit its form from the early authorities: the fruits of summer in winter and of winter in summer—supply out of season, from no door Zakariyya kept the key to; the girl the Temple almost declined, out-provisioned by the Provider past every steward's schedule.\n\n\"He exclaimed, 'O Mary! Where did this come from?' She replied, 'It is from Allah. Surely Allah provides for whoever He wills without limit'\" records the exchange the scholars treasured as the narrative's creed-lesson, and its two voices teach in both directions. The prophet asks—guardianship's honest question, and wonder's; and the girl answers with the sovereignty-prayer's own closing clause (verse 27: \"You provide for whoever You will without limit\")—doctrine this chapter taught in prayer now spoken as a child's plain report: it is from Allah; the provision-theology of the whole Book (Surah 2, verse 212's identical wording) resting, complete, in Maryam's sentence. And the answer's effect is the next verse's whole content: Zakariyya, aged and childless, standing before out-of-season fruit in a dedicated girl's chamber, hears \"Allah provides for whoever He wills without limit\"—and the words unlock his own locked petition: \"then and there Zachariah prayed to his Lord: grant me—by Your grace—righteous offspring\" (verse 38): the barren house's hope rekindled at the witnessed provision; the scholars drawing the scene's permanent teaching: witnessed grace is contagious—one servant's \"without limit\" answered becomes another's courage to ask; and the chamber that supplied Maryam's fruit was about to supply, through her guardian's reopened prayer, the era's next prophet: Yahya, announced in the sanctuary where the lesson was learned.",
  "38": "\"Then and there Zachariah prayed to his Lord\" fixes the prayer's birthplace with the narrative's own adverb—there, in that place and hour: Maryam's sanctuary-chamber, before the out-of-season provision, her sentence still in the air: \"Allah provides for whoever He wills without limit\" (verse 37)—and the causation is the scene's whole teaching: the aged prophet, decades reconciled to childlessness (his own fuller account: \"my bones have become brittle, and my head crowned with grey,\" Surah 19, verse 4), watches limit-free provision fall on a dedicated child, hears the doctrine from her mouth, and his locked hope turns its key: if winter's chamber can hold summer's fruit, a barren house can hold a son. Witnessed grace re-opens closed petitions—the scholars' drawn lesson: despair is often not theology but forgetfulness, and one displayed \"without limit\" cures it.\n\n\"Saying, 'My Lord! Grant me—by your grace—righteous offspring'\" preserves the petition, and each chosen word carries the man. \"Grant me\": the verb is bestowal's—the gift-word (verse 8's closing name, al-Wahhab, its noun): he asks as beggar of a Giver, no entitlement pleaded; \"by your grace\"—the original: from Yourself, from Your presence directly: a son at his age must come from no ordinary causation, and the prayer names its only possible source (the same phrase Maryam's provision embodied); \"righteous offspring\"—the specification the scholars set beside every parent's prayers: not offspring merely, but goodly, pure descendants: the quality asked before the existence; he wants not an heir but a servant of Allah (the mother of Maryam's vow, verse 35, breathing in her guardian's petition—the chamber's dedications contagious in both directions).\n\n\"You are certainly the Hearer of ˹all˺ prayers\" closes the petition with the name his whole situation leans on, and its selection is the prayer's finest touch: not the Capable (though capability is the need's face) but the Hearer of supplication—for the old man's question was never whether Allah can (Maryam's fruit had just settled it) but whether a petition so long unasked, so naturally hopeless, would be received; and the name answers before the angels do: the Hearer of prayer hears this one. His fuller account adds the certificate every late-life asker treasures: \"and never have I been disappointed in my prayers to You, my Lord\" (Surah 19, verse 4)—the relationship's whole history pleaded as the new petition's ground. The verse thus hands the community its complete anatomy of asking against odds: the hope re-lit at grace's exhibit, the gift begged from grace directly, the quality specified over the quantity, and the Hearer's name held against nature's arithmetic—and the answer arrives, in the next verse, before the prayer's posture has ended: the angels calling to him \"while he stood praying in the sanctuary.\"",
  "39": "\"So the angels called out to him while he stood praying in the sanctuary\" delivers the answer with the narrative's most deliberate timing-details, each one doctrine: the call came while he stood—the petition of verse 38 still on his lips, the answer overtaking the asking (the Hearer of prayer, just named, demonstrating His name's speed: \"I respond to one's prayer when they call upon Me,\" Surah 2, verse 186); it came in the sanctuary—the mihrab, the same chamber-type where Maryam's provision taught him to ask: the place of standing devotion made the place of annunciation; and it came by angels—the honour-channel: the era's next prophet announced to his father by the heavenly court.\n\n\"'Allah gives you good news of ˹the birth of˺ John'\" opens the annunciation with the gospel-verb itself—yubashshiruka, the glad-tidings word—and the gift's first honour is its naming: Yahya, named by heaven before conception (the parallel account adds: \"We have not given this name to anyone before,\" Surah 19, verse 7): the child's identity authored where his existence was: from Allah's presence directly, as the father's prayer had asked (\"by Your grace\").\n\n\"Who will confirm the Word of Allah\" states Yahya's office first—and the title it contains is the chapter's key placed early: he will confirm \"a Word from Allah\": Isa, so titled two annunciations later (verse 45: \"a Word from Him... the Messiah, Jesus\")—the term the disputation-season's deviant readings (verse 7) would bend toward incarnation, here given its function in advance: Isa is called a Word because he came to be by the Word—\"Be!\" without father (verse 47's own explanation; verse 59's Adam-parallel)—and Yahya's whole prophethood would testify to that created wonder: the first believer in his cousin's mission, the herald confirming the sign. \"And will be a great leader, chaste, and a prophet among the righteous\" completes the portrait in four honours: a sayyid—a master: leader in knowledge, worship, and restraint, lordship's true form; hasur—chaste: continent from the appetites that master others (the scholars' preferred reading: not incapacity but governed desire—abstinent by devotion's choice); and a prophet, filed where this Book files all its greatest: \"among the righteous\"—the company-clause (Surah 2, verse 130's identical filing of Ibrahim), heaven's peerage being a fellowship, not a pyramid. The Sunnah completes Yahya's honour-roll at its far end: he met the Prophet, peace be upon him, in the second heaven on the night journey, beside Isa (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3887; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 164), and his death—slain by a king he would not license in sin, by the transmitted accounts—sealed the martyr-file of Surah 2, verse 61's charge.\n\nThe verse's whole scene, held against its predecessor, is the Book's standing exhibit on prayer's economics: verse 38 asked for \"righteous offspring\"; the answer itemises righteousness in four honours and adds prophethood unasked—the Giver's surplus over the petition (Surah 2, verse 261's multiplication operating in annunciations as in alms); and the aged asker's response to such surplus—the honest astonishment of nature's arithmetic—opens the next verse: \"My Lord! How can I have a son when I am very old and my wife is barren?\"",
  "40": "\"Zachariah exclaimed, 'My Lord! How can I have a son when I am very old and my wife is barren?'\" voices the question the annunciation's arithmetic demanded—and its nature must be read as the narrative reads it: this is not doubt's protest (he had prayed for exactly this, one verse earlier, against the same arithmetic) but wonder's request for the mode: the how of a grant already believed—the original's structure the astonished \"anna\" of Maryam's own coming question (verse 47), the mind's honest stagger before a confirmed wonder; the scholars distinguish it cleanly from the demand-for-signs this argument condemns (Surah 2, verse 118): the rebellious ask to evade; the granted ask to comprehend—Ibrahim's \"so my heart can be reassured\" (Surah 2, verse 260) the category's charter. And the question's inventory is the miracle's own measure, stated by its beneficiary: age at its far edge—his parallel account's \"brittle bones and head aflame with grey\" (Surah 19, verse 4)—and a wife barren through their shared decades: both natural doors closed, and the closure now confessed to the Opener.\n\n\"He replied, 'So will it be. Allah does what He wills'\" answers the how with the sentence that is this narrative-cycle's refrain and the chapter's whole theology in four words: kadhalika—\"so will it be\": the matter stands as announced, the mode's mystery left standing with it; and the explanation offered is not mechanism but Agent: \"Allah does what He wills\"—the sovereignty-clause (verse 26's estate, Surah 2, verse 253's seal) applied to a womb: the same answer, word for word in its structure, that Maryam will receive at her greater wonder (verse 47: \"Allah creates what He wills\"), the refrain teaching by its repetition that all such questions have one terminus: the Doer's will, before which \"how\" dissolves—for causation's ordinary doors are His custom, not His constraint (the Originator's \"Be!\", Surah 2, verse 117), and the barren house stands to Him exactly as the fertile one: one word distant.\n\nThe exchange's tenderness should not be lost in its theology, the commentators note: the aged prophet's question is received without rebuke—no \"do you not believe?\" beyond the gentle form Ibrahim himself met (Surah 2, verse 260); the Lord who answers wonder's questions honours the wondering: Zakariyya's stagger is walked through, not stepped over, and his next request—\"grant me a sign\" (verse 41)—will be granted too: the pastoral pattern this Book keeps for its sincere questioners (Surah 2, verse 186's nearness) against every religion of crushed inquiry. And the verse's permanent address, the scholars draw, is every believer's own closed arithmetic: the petitions long-abandoned before life's barren columns—the \"too late,\" \"too far,\" \"too broken\" of every private ledger—stand corrected by the refrain: so will it be; Allah does what He wills: the answer that re-opens, for whoever holds it, all the doors that mere counting closed.",
  "41": "\"Zachariah said, 'My Lord! Grant me a sign'\" continues the granted prophet's exchange with a request the scholars are careful to file rightly: not verification's demand—the annunciation came by angels to a prophet; belief was never the gap—but the asking of nearness: a mark by which to know the promise's commencement, the moment the decades-old barrenness turned; the commentators read it as the joyful watcher's request: when the wonder begins, let me know it has begun—gratitude wants its starting-line; and the request's reception (granted at once, as Ibrahim's reassurance was, Surah 2, verse 260) keeps the narrative's pastoral pattern: the sincere asker honoured in his asking.\n\n\"He said, 'Your sign is that you will not ˹be able to˺ speak to people for three days except through gestures'\" delivers a sign unlike any requested before it, and its design is the verse's studied wonder: the mark is not in the wife's womb but in the husband's tongue—speech toward people withheld three days, gesture their only channel, while (his parallel account confirms: \"so he came out to his people... and signalled to them to glorify Allah morning and evening,\" Surah 19, verse 11) the tongue toward Allah ran free: remembrance unimpeded, conversation impossible. The sign's anatomy, as the scholars unfolded it: proof of the miracle's class—the same power that closes a healthy tongue to men while opening it to praise is the power that opens a barren womb: the sign a miniature of the signified, causation's ordinary doors overridden in the asker's own body; and instruction folded into proof—the three days' design preached what they marked: the gift's season is thanksgiving's season, and the tongue was requisitioned entire for its true work: as though heaven answered \"grant me a sign\" with \"your sign is that, for three days, you will be able to do nothing with your voice but what the gift deserves.\"\n\n\"Remember your Lord often and glorify ˹Him˺ morning and evening\" completes the sign with its commanded content, and the clauses' pairing carries the whole exchange's teaching: remembrance much—the frequency-word this Book attaches to dhikr wherever it prescribes it (Surah 33, verse 41: \"remember Allah often\")—and glorification at the day's two ends: morning and evening, the bracket-hours (the ashiyy and ibkar of his people's signalled worship, Surah 19, verse 11) that consecrate whatever runs between them. The prescription's logic seals the narrative's economics: the prayer of verse 38 asked; the annunciation of verse 39 over-delivered; and the granted man's assignment is neither payment nor project but praise—for the gift \"from Your grace\" can only be answered in gratitude's currency, and the aged father's three mute days toward men, loud toward Heaven, stand as this Book's image of the receiving soul: emptied of talk, filled with thanks. The cycle now turns from the answered house of Zakariyya to the chamber it began in—and to the girl whose provision taught the asking: \"O Mary! Surely Allah has selected you...\"",
  "42": "\"And ˹remember˺ when the angels said, 'O Mary! Surely Allah has selected you, purified you, and chosen you over all women of the world'\" turns the narrative-cycle from the answered house of Zakariyya to the chamber where its lesson began—and the address itself is the first wonder: angels speaking to Maryam: a woman, no prophet's office claimed for her by this Book's majority reading, yet conversed with by the heavenly court as the prophets are—the honour-grade the verse then makes explicit in its three verbs. Selected—istafa, the election-verb of verse 33's genealogy: the choosing that filed Adam, Nuh, and the two households now files a person: Maryam seated by name in the election-line her grandfather's house carried. Purified—the mother's protection-prayer (verse 36) receipted: cleansed, the commentators unfold, from every stain her office required cleanness of: the accusations her people would later hurl (Surah 4, verse 156's file—the purification pre-emptively refuting them), and the states her sanctuary-service was excused; the touch of the toucher-at-birth already barred by the Sunnah's certificate (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3431). And chosen \"over all women of the world\"—election's superlative: the double istafa the scholars read in its two reaches: chosen absolutely among her age's women, and, by the strong reading the Sunnah supports, chosen at the summit of womanhood entire: the Prophet, peace be upon him, ranked her by name: \"the best of its women is Maryam, daughter of Imran; and the best of its women is Khadijah\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3432; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2430), and counted the perfected among women in a sentence that heads with her (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3411): Maryam, Asiyah the wife of Pharaoh—with Khadijah, Fatimah, and Aishah in the honour-texts' company.\n\nThe verse's placement in the chapter's argument does its quiet doctrinal work before the annunciation arrives, the commentators note: the disputation this surah hosts (the Najran occasion) deified Maryam's son and, in some of its streams, exalted her toward worship (Surah 5, verse 116's interrogation: \"did you tell people: take me and my mother as two gods?\"); this verse fixes her true station in the election-vocabulary reserved for creatures: selected, purified, chosen—the highest honours of the created order, every one of them a receiving: the passive of Allah's active, the verse's grammar itself her theology: she is the choosing's object, never its source; above all women, beneath all worship's objects—the seat this Book guards for her against her deniers and her deifiers at once.\n\nAnd for the believing reader, the verse's standing gift is its portrait of what heaven honours, the scholars draw: the chosen-over-the-worlds' résumé, as the narrative has built it: a vowed child, a sanctuary's quiet, provision received with creed on the tongue (verse 37's \"it is from Allah\"), and devotion's long unwitnessed hours—no throne, no lineage-boast, no public work yet performed: election's criteria published in her file for every daughter and son of the community; and the next verse will state her assignment in exactly that register: devotion, prostration, and the bowing company—the chosen one commanded to the ranks.",
  "43": "\"O Mary! Be devout to your Lord, prostrate yourself ˹in prayer˺ and bow along with those who bow down\" completes the angels' address begun in verse 42, and the sequence of the two verses is the passage's whole teaching: election announced, then assignment issued—the honours of \"selected, purified, chosen over the worlds' women\" followed not by privileges but by three commands of worship; heaven's peerage, this Book's constant doctrine, is a commissioning (Surah 2, verse 124's Ibrahim: made a role model and tested; verse 143's community: honoured as witnesses and charged). The three commands themselves, read in their order, build the worshipper entire. \"Be devout to your Lord\": the qunut-word (Surah 2, verse 238's \"stand in true devotion\")—obedience's standing posture: the long, humble, continuous devotion that was already her biography's substance (the sanctuary's years), now made her formal office: uqnuti—remain in devotion; the chosen one's rank maintained by the rank's own duties. \"Prostrate yourself\": the sujud—worship's floor and summit at once: the created forehead at its Creator's ground, the posture in which the servant is nearest his Lord (Sahih Muslim, Hadith 482's doctrine); commanded to the woman chosen over the worlds' women, for no election graduates a creature past prostration—the honour deepens the bowing.\n\n\"And bow along with those who bow down\" closes the commands with the clause whose social grammar the scholars weighed: bow with the bowing—worship joined to the worshipping company: Maryam, the sanctuary's solitary, commanded into congregation's rank (the Temple's praying assembly); the clause repeating, for the chosen individual, what Surah 2, verse 43 commanded the covenant-community in identical words (\"bow down with those who bow down\"): devotion's completion is communal—the elect not exempted from the ranks but planted in them, for the religion of this Book knows no aristocracy of private sanctity above the congregation's floor (the Prophet himself, peace be upon him, in the rows of his mosque). And the commentators note the commands' anticipatory mercy: the assignment issued here—devotion, prostration, congregation—was the regimen building the servant whom the next annunciation would require: the chamber's coming trial (verse 45's Word announced, the people's coming accusations) would land on a soul already long-trained in the three commands' steel; heaven strengthens before it tests (Surah 2, verse 286's calibration), and Maryam's election-narrative places the worship-verses between the honours and the storm.\n\nThe verse's permanent address, the scholars draw, is every honoured soul in the community: whoever discovers grace's selections in his own file—knowledge given, provision eased, station raised—should hear this verse's sequence over his honours: the announcement's next sentence is always the assignment; and the assignment's three clauses have not changed: devotion maintained, prostration deepened, and the congregation kept—the ranks of \"those who bow\" being, until the Hour, the only address at which election safely resides.",
  "44": "\"This is news of the unseen that We reveal to you ˹O Prophet˺\" breaks the narrative's frame at its exact midpoint—between Maryam's election (verses 42 and 43) and the annunciation of her son (verse 45)—to name the story's own credentials, and the interruption is the argument: the account just told and about to continue—the vow, the chamber, the provision, the lots—is filed by its Narrator as anba al-ghayb: reports of the unseen, delivered by revelation to a man who, the verse's next clause proves, possessed no other access. The clause's force in the disputation-season (the Najran occasion) is the commentators' first note: the Prophet, peace be upon him, unlettered, of a people with no scriptural schooling (Surah 29, verse 48; Surah 2, verse 78's ummiyyin), narrates to scripture's own custodians the interior scenes of their sanctuary's history—details their traditions had blurred or lost—with the Witness's own precision: the narration itself the credential (Surah 2, verse 252's identical seal over Talut's account; Surah 11, verse 49 over Nuh's; Surah 12, verse 102 over Yusuf's: the Book's standing formula at its unseen-histories' close).\n\n\"You were not with them when they cast lots to decide who would be Mary's guardian, nor were you there when they argued ˹about it˺\" supplies the proof by its most intimate specimen—and in supplying it, hands the narrative a scene the surface-story had passed over: the custody-contest behind verse 37's quiet \"entrusting her to the care of Zachariah.\" The commentators unfold the transmitted picture: the vowed child delivered to the Temple, and its ministers—the era's scribes and devotees—contending for her guardianship: the daughter of Imran's honoured house, each man's claim pressed until the dispute went to lots: by the accounts, their pens or arrow-shafts cast upon the water, Zakariyya's alone standing or floating against the stream—the lot's verdict crowning the contest Allah had already decided (verse 37's \"her Lord accepted her... entrusting her\"). The scene's selection as the proof-specimen is the verse's finest stroke, the scholars note: of all the story's moments, the demonstration cites the most humanly unrecoverable—not the public miracles but a committee-room's quarrel and its lot-casting: the kind of detail no oral tradition carries whole across six centuries and no forger would think to invent; \"you were not with them\"—twice sounded—measuring the gap between the Narrator's vantage and every human narrator's.\n\nThe verse's doctrine, standing back, is the Book's own epistemology stated mid-story: revelation is the unseen's sole reliable channel (verse 5's foundation: \"nothing hidden from Allah\"—and what He alone sees, He alone can report); the disputation about Isa—conducted by parties generations removed from the events (the commentary at verse 5)—is here answered at the level of sources: one account in the field descends from the scene's only Witness; and the argument-scene the verse preserves (the guardians contending over Maryam) will echo forward, the commentators observe, into the greater contentions her son occasioned (verse 65's arguing over Ibrahim's religion, verse 61's summons to mutual imprecation): the child over whom the pious quarrelled grew into the Word over whom the nations would—and the Narrator was, in the verse's precise sense, present at both quarrels.",
  "45": "\"˹Remember˺ when the angels proclaimed, 'O Mary! Allah gives you good news of a Word from Him'\" delivers the annunciation the whole chapter has been architected around—and its central title must be read with the precision the chapter itself installed: \"a Word from Him\"—the term verse 39 pre-defined at Yahya's announcement (\"who will confirm the Word of Allah\") and verse 47 will immediately explain (\"Be! And it is\"): Isa is called a Word because he came to be by the Word—the creative imperative spoken into Maryam's chamber where, for all other children, ordinary causation carries the decree; the title naming the miracle's mode, not the child's nature: he is min-hu—\"from Him\"—as all creation is from Him (Surah 45, verse 13's grammar), created by His word, not begotten of His essence: the deviant reading (verse 7's method: the elusive term pried from the precise verses around it) answered by the chapter's own glossary before the disputants arrive at verse 59's settlement: \"like Adam: He created him from dust, then said to him 'Be!'\"\n\n\"His name will be the Messiah, Jesus, son of Mary\" completes the naming with three titles, each doctrinally loaded, the commentators note: al-Masih—the Messiah: the anointed, the awaited title of Israel's own hope, conferred here by heaven (the Quran granting Isa the very office the disputing communities contested); Isa—the name itself, heaven-given as Yahya's was (verse 39); and \"son of Mary\"—the patronymic that is the creed compressed: son of his mother, in a genealogical culture where sons were named by fathers: the fatherless conception written into his everyday name, and the divine sonship excluded by the same three words—the title this Book uses for him twenty-three times, honour and boundary in one breath (Surah 5, verse 75: \"the Messiah, son of Mary, was no more than a messenger\").\n\n\"Honoured in this world and the Hereafter, and he will be one of those nearest ˹to Allah˺\" seals the annunciation with the child's two-world portfolio: wajih—of high standing, honoured in both realms: in this world by the office and the signs (verse 49's coming credentials), in the Hereafter by the intercession-scenes' honour and his station; and the closing title files him where verse 39 filed Yahya and Surah 2, verse 130 filed Ibrahim: \"of those nearest\"—al-muqarrabin: the brought-near, creation's innermost circle (Surah 56, verse 11's rank)—the honour-grammar again doing the theology: nearest to Allah, therefore not Allah; the superlative of proximity being the categorical denial of identity, as the chapter's whole vocabulary has insisted: chosen, purified, honoured, brought near—the crown-titles of servants, and the annunciation's every clause, rightly read, is the Najran thesis answered in the language of Isa's own glad tidings: a Word from Him, son of his mother, servant brought near—\"the Messiah would never be too proud to be a servant of Allah\" (Surah 4, verse 172).",
  "46": "\"And he will speak to people in ˹his˺ infancy and adulthood\" continues the annunciation's portrait with the sign that would stand first in time and first in function: speech from the cradle—the infant Isa, days old, addressing his mother's accusers; and the Quran elsewhere records the cradle-speech's exact content, which makes this clause's placement in the chapter's argument its masterstroke: \"I am truly a servant of Allah. He has destined me to be given the Scripture and to be a prophet\" (Surah 19, verse 30)—the miracle's very transcript being the creed the disputants would later invert: the first sentence of the child the Najran thesis deified was the announcement of his servanthood. The sign's design, the commentators unfold, served two mercies at once: Maryam's vindication—the chaste sanctuary-daughter returned to her people with an infant, and the infant himself her defence counsel: the accusation answered by a wonder no slander could survive (Surah 19, verses 27 to 33's scene); and the mission's pre-credential: a prophet whose signs began before his commission, the cradle-speech standing in his people's memory when the adult summons came. The Prophet, peace be upon him, counted Isa's cradle-speech among the three infants who spoke (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3436; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2550—with Jurayj's witness and the child at the mother's breast): the category's most consequential member.\n\n\"And adulthood\" pairs the wonder with the ordinary, and the pairing is itself doctrine, the scholars note: he will speak in maturity—kahlan: the settled adult years—as prophets speak: the revelation delivered, the people summoned; the clause quietly certifying his full human life-course (infancy, growth, maturity—the biography of a creature: \"they both ate food,\" Surah 5, verse 75's argument), and, in the reading many commentators preferred, reaching past the raising (verse 55's coming clause) to the descent at history's end: the adulthood-speech completed when he returns—the Prophet, peace be upon him, announcing: \"by the One in whose Hand is my soul, the son of Maryam will surely descend among you as a just judge\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3448; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 155)—the annunciation's clause, on this reading, still awaiting its final fulfilment.\n\n\"And will be one of the righteous\" closes the portrait with the filing-clause the narrative-cycle has now stamped on each of its three announced servants—Yahya \"a prophet among the righteous\" (verse 39), and here the Messiah himself: of the salihin, the righteous company (Surah 2, verse 130's file where Ibrahim rests)—and the repetition completes the chapter's quiet census: the Word from Him, the cradle-speaker, the sign-worker of the coming verses, is registered in the same company-roll as every honoured servant before him; heaven's honours, sounded now across annunciations, elections, and miracles, have never once broken the ceiling between the brought-near creature and the Creator who brings near—and Maryam, hearing her son so described, asks in the next verse the question whose answer is the chapter's theology entire: \"How can I have a child when no man has ever touched me?\"",
  "47": "\"Mary wondered, 'My Lord! How can I have a child when no man has ever touched me?'\" voices the annunciation's human centre—and her question stands in the narrative's deliberate symmetry with Zakariyya's (verse 40): the same astonished \"how,\" the same honest inventory of closed doors (his: age and barrenness; hers: untouched chastity—the word itself her testimony, guarded even in her astonishment), and the same category: not doubt's protest but wonder's request for the mode—the granted asking to comprehend (the distinction drawn at verse 40; Ibrahim's charter, Surah 2, verse 260). Her address—\"My Lord!\"—keeps the chamber's whole theology in two words: the question about the impossible directed, immediately and only, to the One for whom the word does not exist.\n\n\"An angel replied, 'So will it be. Allah creates what He wills'\" answers with the cycle's refrain—kadhalika, \"so will it be\" (verse 40's identical opening)—and the refrain's one altered word is the passage's precision, which the scholars weighed: to Zakariyya, \"Allah does what He wills\"; to Maryam, \"Allah creates what He wills\"—the verb upgraded to the wonder's grade: the aged couple's son required doing (causation's doors reopened); the virgin's son required creating (causation's doors bypassed): the answer naming, in one verb, exactly what Isa's conception was—a creation-act, khalq: the category of Adam's forming (verse 59's coming parallel: \"He created him from dust\"), not the category of begetting; the chapter's glossary again closing, in the annunciation's own transcript, the door the later disputation would force.\n\n\"When He decrees a matter, He simply tells it, 'Be!' And it is\" completes the answer with the Book's deepest sentence about divine action—the eight-times-sounded formula (Surah 2, verse 117's exposition)—here delivered to the person about to carry its most famous instance: the conception explained to the mother in advance as pure imperative: no father because no instrument; the decree addressed to the matter directly—\"Be!\"—and Maryam's chamber, like the heavens and the earth before it, obeying at the word. The formula's placement is the chapter's whole argument given its seal, the commentators draw: the title \"a Word from Him\" (verse 45) is here cashed into its meaning—Isa is the Word's issue, the \"Be!\" embodied: the miraculous conception testifying, when the wombs' true Shaper is named (verse 6), precisely opposite to the deification it was later made to serve: not the child's divinity but the Creator's freedom—for a God who needs only \"Be!\" needs no son (Surah 2, verse 117's commentary), and the greater the wonder in Maryam's chamber, the greater the Wonder-Worker it points away from itself toward.\n\nThe verse's tenderness, beneath its theology, is the narrative's continuing pastoral pattern: the young woman's staggering assignment—an unwed mother's trial in a community whose accusations the Book records (Surah 4, verse 156)—is opened to her with the refrain of sufficiency: so will it be: the matter stands decreed, carried by the Decree-Maker; and the fuller account adds the provision He sent with it (Surah 19's stream and palm at her labour): the servant handed the impossible is handed, in the same sentence, the Name that makes it light—and her son's coming credentials, itemised in the next verses, will each be stamped with the clause her chamber taught: \"by Allah's Will.\"",
  "48": "\"And Allah will teach him writing and wisdom, the Torah and the Gospel\" continues the annunciation to Maryam with her son's curriculum—and its four items, read in their order, are the verse's quiet theology, for every one is received: taught to him, by Allah; the child just titled \"a Word from Him\" (verse 45) is here enrolled as a student, and the enrolment is the title's safeguard: whatever the Word-title means, it means a person who must be taught—knowledge given, not possessed by nature: the creature's badge (the angels' confession, Surah 2, verse 32: \"we have no knowledge except what You have taught us\"; Adam's honour the same receiving, Surah 2, verse 31). The curriculum's items ascend: writing—al-kitab in its first sense: the scribal instrument itself, letters and the Book's handling; wisdom—the hikmah this Book prices above portfolios (Surah 2, verse 269): sound judgment, the prophetic sense of each moment's due; the Torah—Musa's revelation, mastered by the prophet sent to its people: Isa arriving not as the Torah's stranger but its most learned son (verse 50's coming \"I will confirm the Torah revealed before me\"); and the Gospel—the Injil: his own Book, sent down to him (the doctrine fixed at verse 3: one revelation given him as the Torah was given Musa), the curriculum's summit being his own commissioned scripture.\n\nThe verse's placement in the annunciation carries its pastoral sense, the commentators note: Maryam, staggered by the how of conception (verse 47), is answered past her question into her son's whole future—the mother shown, before the birth, the boy at his lessons: the Teacher who decreed the conception already committed to the education; the impossible child arriving with his upbringing underwritten (her own upbringing's precedent: \"blessed her with a pleasant upbringing,\" verse 37—the family's two generations both grown under direct divine cultivation).\n\nAnd the verse's function in the chapter's argument is its permanent work: the disputation-season's thesis (Najran's deification) is met, clause by clause, with the annunciation's own grammar—conceived by \"Be!\" (verse 47), taught his letters and his Books (this verse), sent as messenger (verse 49 next): the biography of the most honoured of servants, every verb of it transacted between a Lord who gives and a servant who receives; the scholars observing that this verse alone, honestly read, settles the question the delegations crossed deserts to dispute: God is not taught the Torah. The curriculum announced, the next verse opens the commission—\"a messenger to the Children of Israel\"—with the credentials-list that made his mission the age's wonder: the clay bird, the healed blind, the raised dead—each one stamped, in his own announcing voice, with the clause that keeps the wonder's address correct: \"by Allah's Will.\"",
  "49": "\"And ˹make him˺ a messenger to the Children of Israel\" opens Isa's commission, and its address-clause carries the first doctrine: to the Children of Israel—the mission's assigned scope: the Messiah sent to Israel's lost house within the covenant-line (the election-genealogy of verse 33), the universal mission's hour awaiting the final Prophet (Surah 7, verse 158's \"to you all\"); and the commission's voice then shifts to Isa's own proclamation—the credentials announced by their bearer, each one constructed, in his own wording, to point past himself.\n\n\"'I have come to you with a sign from your Lord: I will make for you a bird from clay, breathe into it, and it will become a ˹real˺ bird—by Allah's Will'\" presents the first credential, and its design is the passage's theology in miniature: clay shaped, breath given, life entering—the sequence of Adam's own creation re-staged in miniature before Israel's eyes (Surah 38, verses 71 and 72's clay and breathed spirit), as though the sign were chosen to teach exactly what verse 59 will state: the Messiah's whole category is Adam's—and the clause his announcement stamps on it, \"by Allah's Will\" (bi-idhnillah), is the credential's governing grammar: the deed performed through him, its power never his; the same permission-clause this Book stamps on every delegated wonder (Surah 2, verse 249's \"by the Will of Allah\").\n\n\"'I will heal the blind and the leper and raise the dead to life—by Allah's Will'\" continues the credentials into mercy's register—the born-blind seeing, the leper cleansed, the dead returned—and the clause returns with them: by Allah's Will, sounded a second time in one verse; the repetition is the proclamation's whole defence-in-advance, the commentators note: Isa's signs exceeded in kind what any prophet's people had seen—life given, the grave reversed—precisely the register later generations would mistake for divinity's own; so the announcing voice fastened the permission-clause to each item at the moment of announcement: the miracle's transcript itself refuting the miracle's future misreading (as the cradle-speech's transcript did, verse 46: \"I am truly a servant of Allah\").\n\n\"'And I will prophesize what you eat and store in your houses. Surely in this is a sign for you if you ˹truly˺ believe'\" completes the credentials with knowledge's sign—the unseen of the pantry: what each household ate and laid up, reported by one who never entered: the ghayb-report by revelation's channel (verse 44's doctrine: what Allah alone sees, His messengers alone can tell)—and the proclamation closes with the sorting-clause this Book hangs on every sign since the mosquito (Surah 2, verse 26): \"a sign for you if you truly believe\"—credentials suffice the receptive and convert no settled refusal (Surah 2, verse 118's law); Israel's response to this very list—the healed and the raised among them—would divide, as the signs always divide, by the hearts they landed on (verse 52's \"when Jesus sensed disbelief\"). The wonder-list published with its grammar intact, the next verse adds the mission's legislative office—the Torah confirmed, its burdens eased—and the summons that every credential existed to serve: \"be mindful of Allah, and obey me.\"",
  "50": "\"And I will confirm the Torah revealed before me\" continues Isa's proclamation from credentials (verse 49) to commission, and its first clause seats him in the relay this Book has mapped since Surah 2, verse 87: confirming what came before—the Messiah arriving not to abolish Musa's revelation but to verify it: its divine origin attested, its law upheld as his own base-code, his mission the Torah's own next chapter (the confirmation-doctrine of verse 3, and of Surah 2, verse 41, here in the confirmer's first person); the claim's polemical work standing for both audiences: to Israel's establishment—your rejection of me is rejection of the Torah I confirm; and to the later deifiers—the Messiah's self-description is a prophet in a line, \"confirming... revealed before me\": men do not confirm their own prior decrees.\n\n\"And legalize some of what had been forbidden to you\" states his legislative office, and its measure is the mercy-doctrine this Book teaches about law's history: some of Israel's prohibitions—the burdens laid, in part, as discipline for their transgressions (\"for the wrongdoing of the Jews, We made unlawful for them certain good foods,\" Surah 4, verse 160)—are lifted by his commission: the law lightened by the same Authority that weighted it; abrogation's wisdom (Surah 2, verse 106) operating within the earlier covenants exactly as the later communities would experience it, and Isa's easing a foretaste of the final law's charter: \"he relieves them from their burdens and the shackles that bound them\" (Surah 7, verse 157). The clause's doctrinal yield, the scholars note: law changes, within revelation's one project, by revelation's own hand—the Messiah's modifications of Torah-rules being neither betrayal (as his rejecters charged) nor licence for human editing (as later communities practised, Surah 2, verse 79), but commissioned adjustment: \"I have come to you with a sign from your Lord\"—the repeated credential-clause grounding the legislative right: he eases the law because he is sent, and provably so.\n\n\"So be mindful of Allah, and obey me\" closes the proclamation's commission with the summons every credential existed to serve, and its two clauses are prophethood's whole demand in sequence: taqwa—the chapter's and the Book's watchword (Surah 2, verse 2's threshold), mindfulness of Allah as the response the signs were engineered to produce; and \"obey me\"—the messenger's claim in its exact size: obedience to the sent one as the sent One's channel (the conjunction-grammar of verse 32: \"obey Allah and His Messenger\"), the same two-word summons every prophet in this Book issues (\"so fear Allah and obey me,\" Nuh, Hud, Salih, Lut, and Shuaib's identical refrain through Surah 26)—Isa's version indistinguishable from his brothers': the commentators marking, once more, the transcript's own testimony: the voice the delegations deified asked of its hearers precisely what Musa asked and Muhammad asked: mindfulness of Allah, obedience to His messenger—and the next verse will complete the proclamation with the sentence Isa himself made the mission's summary, which the final Prophet's community recites as its own: \"Surely Allah is my Lord and your Lord. So worship Him alone. This is the Straight Path.\"",
  "51": "\"Surely Allah is my Lord and your Lord. So worship Him ˹alone˺. This is the Straight Path\" closes Isa's proclamation with the sentence he made his mission's summary—and the Quran's insistence on this exact sentence as his is the chapter's whole case resting: it recurs on his tongue across the Book (Surah 19, verse 36 at the cradle-speech's climax; Surah 5, verse 117 in his final disavowal before Allah: \"I only told them what You ordered me: worship Allah—my Lord and your Lord\"; Surah 43, verse 64)—the Messiah's own creed-formula, preserved in his own grammar, and the grammar is the theology: \"my Lord and your Lord\"—one Lordship holding speaker and audience in a single category: the Messiah on the servants' side of the line with those he summons, his relation to Allah in kind (Lord and worshipper) exactly his hearers'; three words that no incarnation-doctrine survives, spoken by the doctrine's own subject. The commentators set the sentence against the disputation's documents: the Najran delegation's case needed Isa claiming worship; the record supplies Isa assigning it—away from himself, wholly, \"so worship Him alone\": the summons of every prophet since the covenant's dawn (Surah 2, verse 21's first command; Surah 16, verse 36; Surah 21, verse 25's universal formula), sounded by the very voice the summoners-to-Isa's-worship invoke.\n\n\"This is the Straight Path\" seals the proclamation with the Book's oldest image—and its sounding here, by Isa, completes a circuit every praying reader walks daily, the scholars note: the sirat mustaqim of the Fatihah's petition (Surah 1, verse 6: \"guide us along the Straight Path\")—defined there by the company of the blessed, defined at Surah 6, verse 153 by the single way against the scattered paths—is here defined by the Messiah himself, in one demonstrative: this—the worship of Allah alone, under the Lord of messenger and people alike—is that path: the definition mattering most from this definer, for history's largest departure from the path was made in his name; and the Muslim reciting the Fatihah's plea is, by this verse's grammar, asking to be kept on the very road Isa pointed down—the path from which \"those who went astray\" (Surah 1, verse 7's closing) wandered by mistaking the pointer for the destination.\n\nThe proclamation now complete—credentials (verse 49), commission (verse 50), creed (this verse)—the narrative turns to its reception, and the turn is the chapter's realism: the next verse opens with \"when Jesus sensed disbelief from his people\"—the perfect proclamation meeting the imperfect hearts, as every delivery in this Book's histories has (Surah 2, verse 87's \"every time a messenger comes to you with something you do not like\"); and the Messiah's response to the sensed rejection—\"who will stand up with me for Allah?\"—will summon history's most famous small company: the disciples, whose answer gives this Book its word for Isa's true followers: \"bear witness that we have submitted.\"",
  "52": "\"When Jesus sensed disbelief from his people, he asked, 'Who will stand up with me for Allah?'\" turns the narrative from proclamation to reception—and the reception is the relay's old story (Surah 2, verse 87's law: the signs land, the hearts divide): Isa perceives the kufr hardening around his mission—the establishment's rejection gathering toward the plot verse 54 will name—and his response is the summons this Book preserves as the prophetic crisis-question: \"who are my helpers toward Allah?\"—the ansar-word itself: who will stand with me, the standing being \"for Allah\" (the cause's true addressee named in the recruiting-call: no personal faction, no dynasty—helpers toward Allah). The question's realism carries the doctrine: prophets require helpers; the mission's divine origin never exempted it from human ranks (Talut's river-remnant, Surah 2, verse 249; Badr's three hundred)—heaven's causes are carried on earth by whoever answers the question this verse records.\n\n\"The disciples replied, 'We will stand up for Allah'\" gives history's most famous small company its charter-scene: the hawariyyun—the disciples, Isa's chosen circle; their title, by the commentators' accounts, from purity's whiteness—and their answer's first clause repeats the summons's own grammar: helpers of Allah—the cause accepted at its true address. The Quran elsewhere makes their answer the community's standing template: \"O believers! Stand up for Allah as Jesus son of Mary asked the disciples: who will stand up with me for Allah? The disciples replied: we will stand up for Allah\" (Surah 61, verse 14)—the Madinan Ansar hearing, in their own title, the disciples' answer inherited; the Prophet, peace be upon him, had his own such circle's pledge: \"every prophet has a disciple, and my disciple is az-Zubair\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3719; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2415—the hawari-word itself).\n\n\"We believe in Allah, so bear witness that we have submitted\" completes the disciples' answer with the declaration that is the chapter's quiet thunderclap, the scholars note: their creed, in their own recorded words—belief in Allah, and then the summons's word: ishhad bi-anna muslimun: \"bear witness that we have submitted\"—muslimun: the submission-word itself, the name of the one religion (verse 19: \"Allah's only Way is Islam\") on the tongues of Isa's own inner circle: the disciples self-identifying, at their charter-moment, by the very term the final community bears; the continuity-doctrine (Surah 2, verse 136's creed; verse 34's \"descendants of one another\") documented from inside the Gospel's own company: Isa's true followers were, by their own witness-request, muslims—submitters to the one Lord their master named in verse 51's creed; and the chapter's argument to Najran completes another circuit: the delegation claimed the disciples' inheritance; the disciples' recorded pledge names the inheritance's condition—standing for Allah (not for the deified messenger), believing in Allah, submitting to Allah: whoever holds that pledge holds their line, and whoever holds another creed in their name has left their charter at this verse. The company now sworn, the narrative moves to the crisis their pledge was sworn for: the plot (verse 54), and heaven's counter-plan—the taking-up that made the Messiah's story the Day's unfinished business.",
  "53": "\"˹They prayed to Allah,˺ 'Our Lord! We believe in Your revelations and follow the messenger'\" turns the disciples' charter-scene (verse 52) from pledge to prayer—and the turn is itself the lesson the narrative-cycle keeps teaching (verse 8's believers, verse 38's Zakariyya): this Book's companies are portrayed praying at their founding-moments; the pledge to stand for Allah is completed, immediately, by the kneeling before Him. Their prayer's two clauses are the whole religion's structure confessed in sequence, the scholars note: \"we believe in Your revelations\"—faith in what was sent down: the Gospel given their master (verse 48's curriculum), the Torah it confirmed (verse 50), the signs performed before their eyes (verse 49's credentials); and \"follow the messenger\"—the belief completed by following: ittaba'na—we have followed: creed become road (the love-test's own verb, verse 31: \"if you love Allah, then follow me\"); the pairing is the examination this chapter set at verses 31 and 32 passed in the disciples' own words: revelation believed, messenger followed—and the messenger followed as messenger: al-rasul, the sent one: their own title for Isa, on the record, being the office-word the later councils would bury beneath natures and persons.\n\n\"So count us among those who bear witness\" completes the prayer with the petition that names their requested rank—and its aspiration must be read at the height the Book assigns it: \"write us with the witnesses\"—the shahidin: those who testify to the truth: the ranks of everyone who has borne witness to the one Lordship across the relay (verse 18's summit-testimony: Allah, the angels, and the people of knowledge attesting the one creed; verse 52's own \"bear witness that we have submitted\"); the disciples ask to be enrolled in that register: their names written among history's attesters—the honour-roll this Book will assign, at Surah 2, verse 143, to the final community as its very commission: \"witnesses over humanity.\" The commentators draw the petition's reach: the witness-rank includes testimony's whole price—the standing-for-truth that the pledge of verse 52 accepted, carried, for some of their number and their successors, to persecution's edge (Surah 85's trench-martyrs among the Gospel's later faithful; Surah 36's city-messengers' helper slain mid-testimony)—the \"count us\" a request, knowingly made, for the register whose entries are paid for.\n\nThe verse's function in the chapter's argument is documentary, and its quiet force accumulates with verse 52's: the disputation-season's contest was over Isa's true followers' identity, and the Quran answers with their prayer-transcript: addressed to \"Our Lord\"—not to their master; believing \"Your revelations\"—not a triune economy; following \"the messenger\"—the office named; and asking enrolment among the witnesses to the creed of verse 18—the same creed the delegation crossed deserts to dispute. Whoever would stand in the disciples' line, this two-verse record rules, stands here: pledged to Allah, praying to Allah, following the sent servant—and the narrative now turns from the praying company to the gathering storm: \"and the disbelievers made a plan against Jesus—but Allah also planned, and Allah is the best of planners.\"",
  "54": "\"And the disbelievers made a plan ˹against Jesus˺\" brings the narrative to its crisis, and the plan's content is history's most consequential conspiracy: the rejecting establishment—the faction of Israel's leadership whose disbelief Isa had sensed (verse 52)—moving from rejection to design: the plot against his person, to seize and crucify: the arrest arranged, the authorities engaged, the informer purchased—makr, the Quran's word: the covert scheme, plotted in councils that presumed themselves the story's authors (the pattern of Surah 2, verse 14's closed-door devils, scaled now to its most fateful instance).\n\n\"But Allah also planned—and Allah is the best of planners\" answers the conspiracy with the counter-plan, and the sentence's grammar is its theology, held with the scholars' care: Allah's makr—His planning—is named in the reciprocity-form this Book uses for all such attributions (Surah 2, verse 15's \"Allah will throw their mockery back at them\"): the requital matching the crime's shape: the schemers out-schemed, their covert design enclosed within a Design they never detected—no defect of deceit ascribed to Allah, but the perfection of wise counter-measure: \"they devised a plan, but We too devised a plan while they were unaware\" (Surah 27, verse 50); and \"the best of planners\" states the mismatch's arithmetic: human conspiracy operates on stolen hours and partial information inside a universe whose every particle obeys the counter-Planner (verse 29's knowledge, verse 26's estate)—the plotters of that week moved pieces on a board that was itself a piece.\n\nThe counter-plan's content, which verse 55 will announce and Surah 4, verses 157 and 158 detail, is the narrative's held breath: the seizure would close on emptiness—\"they did not kill him, nor did they crucify him, but it was made to appear so to them... rather, Allah raised him up to Himself\": the Messiah taken up alive, the cross receiving, by the appearance cast over the event, other than its intended victim; the establishment's triumph-week history's most complete deception—of the deceivers: they published the death of a man who had been lifted past their reach, and the empire of interpretation built on that week's appearance (the commentators note) stands as the counter-plan's continuing lesson: \"it was made to appear so to them.\"\n\nThe verse's permanent doctrine, drawn by the scholars for every generation of the plotted-against: the believers' history is conducted between the two plans—the visible conspiracies of the strong, and the invisible Design enclosing them; and the verse's comfort is its arithmetic: no plot against Allah's cause has ever been the room's only plan (Yusuf's brothers serving the caravan to Egypt's treasury; Makkah's assassination-council convening the migration that conquered it—\"they planned, but Allah also planned,\" Surah 8, verse 30, over that very night); the community reading this verse under its own conspiracies reads its standing assurance—and the next verse will open the counter-plan's announcement to its subject: \"O Jesus! I will take you and raise you up to Myself.\"",
  "55": "\"˹Remember˺ when Allah said, 'O Jesus! I will take you and raise you up to Myself'\" announces the counter-plan of verse 54 to its subject, and its two verbs—held with the scholars' precision—map the event the nations would dispute for millennia: \"I will take you\"—mutawaffika: the taking-in-full: the receiving of his person, read by the strong and majority tradition of the commentators as the taking of him whole, body and soul, from the earth without the plotters' death touching him—the sleep-taking's vocabulary (Surah 6, verse 60's nightly tawaffi) rather than the grave's, with Surah 4, verses 157 and 158 standing as the file's decisive cross-reference: \"they did not kill him, nor did they crucify him... rather, Allah raised him up to Himself\"; and \"raise you up to Myself\"—the elevation itself: the Messiah lifted past the conspiracy's closing hand to the presence of his Lord: alive, honoured, the cross's intended victim absent from its history; the counter-plan's masterstroke being precisely the event's ambiguity to its witnesses (\"it was made to appear so to them\")—the plotters deceived by appearance, the raising concealed within a seeming death: \"the best of planners\" (verse 54) at work.\n\n\"I will deliver you from those who disbelieve, and elevate your followers above the disbelievers until the Day of Judgment\" adds the counter-plan's two continuing clauses: the purification—Isa cleansed of the rejecters: their reach, their slanders (the charges Surah 4, verse 156 files), their claimed verdict over him; and the elevation-promise: those who follow him set above those who disbelieved in him until the Day—the clause the commentators read across its layers: his true followers of every era (the disciples' submission-line, verse 52, flowing into the final community that believes in him rightly: this Book's own people foremost, who alone hold his whole file—servant, messenger, Word, raised, returning) standing above his rejecters through history's length, as the centuries have displayed. And the return completes the file the Sunnah seals: \"by the One in whose Hand is my soul, the son of Maryam will surely descend among you as a just judge: he will break the cross, kill the swine, and abolish the tribute\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3448; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 155)—the raised one's adulthood-speech (verse 46's kahlan) awaiting its final chapter.\n\n\"Then to Me you will ˹all˺ return, and I will settle all your disputes\" closes the announcement at the Book's constant terminus—the return, the settlement—and its placement over this narrative is the chapter's whole disputation-doctrine sealed: the questions the nations warred over—the natures, the persons, the cross's week—are docketed for the one competent Court (Surah 2, verse 113's referral: \"Allah will judge between them on the Day of Judgment regarding their dispute\"); the believers hold what revelation states (the raising, the servanthood, the return), decline what it denies, and leave the residue where this verse files it: with the Judge, at the assembly where Isa himself will testify—\"I was a witness over them as long as I was among them\" (Surah 5, verse 117)—and the two verses following will publish the settlement's terms in advance: severe punishment for the disbelievers, full reward for the believers, \"and Allah does not like the wrongdoers.\"",
  "56": "\"As for those who disbelieve, I will subject them to a severe punishment in this life and the Hereafter\" opens the settlement-terms that verse 55's closing clause promised—the disputes docketed \"to Me\" now receive their published verdict-schedule—and the sentence's two-world structure is this argument's established pricing for rejection-after-clarity (Surah 2, verse 85's \"disgrace in this worldly life and... the harshest punishment on the Day of Judgment\"; verse 114's pairing): the disbelievers in the Messiah's actual mission—his rejecters first (the plotting establishment of verse 54, whose historical file the commentators trace through the sieges and scatterings that followed the generation), and with them, by the verse's open category, every disbelief in him: the deniers of his prophethood and the deifiers of his person alike, for both stand outside the creed his own proclamation fixed (verse 51: \"Allah is my Lord and your Lord\")—subjected to the punishment in both registers: history's humiliations within this life, and the Hereafter's account beyond appeal.\n\n\"And they will have no helpers\" closes the sentence with the clause this Book stamps on every terminal file (Surah 2, verse 270; verse 22 of this chapter)—and its work here carries the disputation's specific irony, the scholars note: the parties sentenced include those whose whole theology was a Helper-doctrine: salvation by the crucified one's ransom, intercession presumed as the system's engine (Surah 2, verse 48's demolished advocacy); and the verdict-schedule's clause answers the doctrine at its root: no helpers—the Day's court recognising no advocate but by the Judge's leave (Surah 2, verse 255), and the Messiah himself, at that court, testifying for the creed and against its inversions: \"I only told them what You ordered me: worship Allah, my Lord and your Lord\" (Surah 5, verse 117)—the presumed Helper standing as witness for the prosecution of the helper-theology.\n\nThe verse's placement in the announcement to Isa deserves the reader's pause, the commentators draw: these terms are spoken to him—the raised one hears, in the same address that promised his rescue and elevation (verse 55), the sentence upon his rejecters: heaven's consolations to its persecuted servants include the docket's assurance: the plotters' file is not closed by their triumph-week but transferred—to a Court whose schedule this verse publishes; and the pattern holds for every wronged bearer of truth after him (the Prophet's own Makkan years read against this verse's structure): deliverance for the servant, elevation for his followers, and the account of the persecutors held—in full, in both worlds—by the Judge who \"does not like the wrongdoers\" (verse 57's coming seal). The settlement's dark page turned, the next verse publishes its bright one: the believers and doers \"rewarded in full\"—the two-page schedule together being the whole disputation's terminus: not the councils' verdicts, nor the delegations', but His.",
  "57": "\"And as for those who believe and do good, they will be rewarded in full\" completes the settlement-schedule announced to Isa (verses 55 and 56) with its bright page, and the qualifying formula is the Book's constant pairing at its most deliberate placement: those who believe—in Allah upon the creed the Messiah himself proclaimed (verse 51), which includes believing in the Messiah rightly: servant, messenger, Word from Him, raised and returning (the full file, held today by this Book's community alone)—and do good: the deeds that faith's tree bears (Surah 2, verse 25's original pairing; verse 277's four-fold portrait); the two-fold gate standing open, as ever, across every era's parties to the disputation: the establishment's children who left the plotting line for faith, the Gospel-communities' sincere who found the confirmed way (Surah 5, verse 83's overflowing eyes), and the final community holding the whole account.\n\n\"They will be rewarded in full\" pays the qualified in the settlement's own accounting-language: yuwaffihim—the discharge-in-full verb: their wages rendered complete, no earning shaved (the identical guarantee at Surah 2, verse 281's near-last revelation and verse 25 of this chapter: \"every soul paid in full\")—and the clause's placement against verse 56's page is the schedule's whole justice displayed: the disbelievers' two-world punishment and the believers' complete wage published in consecutive breaths, the Day's ledger balanced before history reaches it; and the payment \"in full\" carrying, for the settlement's first hearers, its specific comfort: the disciples' line (verses 52 and 53)—persecuted through the generations between the raising and the final revelation (the trench-martyrs of Surah 85 among them)—assured that no testimony, no standing-for-Allah, no era's faithful endurance is discounted at the discharge.\n\n\"And Allah does not like the wrongdoers\" seals the announcement to Isa—and the whole narrative-cycle from the vow of Imran's wife to the raising—with the forfeited-love clause (Surah 2, verse 190's transgressors; verse 32's disbelievers), and its final word files the entire disputation under the Book's oldest category: zulm—wrongdoing: the placing of things where they do not belong (Surah 2, verse 35's definition; Surah 31, verse 13's \"associating others with Him is truly the worst injustice\"): the plotters wronged the Messiah's person; the deifiers wronged his station—the servant seated where only the Lord belongs; and both misplacements stand together outside the love of the Allah whose settlement this three-verse schedule published. The narrative now complete—conception, curriculum, credentials, creed, company, conspiracy, and counter-plan—the chapter steps back, in the next verses, to stamp the account's authority (\"We recite this to you as one of the signs,\" verse 58) and to hand the disputation its final instrument: the Adam-parallel (verse 59), the truth-clause (verse 60), and the summons to mutual imprecation (verse 61) before which the Najran delegation would decline the field.",
  "58": "\"We recite ˹all˺ this to you ˹O Prophet˺ as one of the signs and ˹as˺ a wise reminder\" steps back from the completed narrative—the vow, the chamber, the annunciations, the mission, the plot, and the raising (verses 35 to 57)—to stamp the account's credentials, and the stamping-formula is the Book's established seal at its unseen-histories' close (Surah 2, verse 252 over Talut's account, in near-identical words; verse 44 of this chapter mid-narrative): what was just recited is min al-ayat—\"of the signs\": the narration itself evidence, delivered to an unlettered Prophet with the interior details—the lots cast, the chamber's provision, the disciples' pledge—that no Arabian source held and no human transmission carried whole (verse 44's argument: \"you were not with them\"); the story's existence in this reciter's mouth being, for its first audience of scriptural scholars, a credential of the same class as the wonders the story contains.\n\n\"And ˹as˺ a wise reminder\" adds the second title, and its two words carry the account's double office, the commentators unfold: al-dhikr al-hakim—the Reminder: the Quran's own name for itself (Surah 15, verse 9: \"We have revealed the Reminder, and We will preserve it\")—the narrative not archived history but active reminding: Maryam's chamber summoning every reader's devotion, Zakariyya's reopened prayer summoning every closed hope, the disciples' pledge summoning every generation's helpers; and \"wise\"—muhkam in its resonance: the account precise, fortified, judging: wisdom's own telling of the story against the legends' accretions—each detail included for its work, each silence (the untold years, the unnamed kings) wisdom's editing; the tale told, uniquely among its world's tellings, without one miracle wasted on spectacle or one clause bent toward deification: the wise reminder being exactly what the era's Isa-narratives, swollen with invented wonders and warring genealogies, were not.\n\nThe verse's placement is the chapter's pivot, the scholars note: behind it stands the narrative; ahead of it, the disputation's final instruments—the Adam-parallel (verse 59), the truth-clause (verse 60), the mubahala-summons (verse 61)—and this verse is the hinge that converts story into case: the account just recited is now entered as evidence—\"of the signs\"—before the argument's closing addresses; the Najran delegation, hearing their own tradition's sacred history retold with the Witness's precision and the servant-creed intact, were being shown the exhibit on which the coming summons would rest: this narration, from this source, carries the authority the dispute requires—and whoever would contest it after such recitation contests not a rival school's reading but \"the truth from your Lord\" (verse 60's coming stamp). The evidence entered, the parallel that settles the category-question follows immediately: \"indeed, the example of Jesus in the sight of Allah is like that of Adam.\"",
  "59": "\"Indeed, the example of Jesus in the sight of Allah is like that of Adam\" delivers the sentence the whole chapter's architecture has been building toward—the disputation's settling analogy—and its recorded occasion is the argument's own transcript, the commentators relate: the Najran delegation pressed their case's core—who is Isa's father, if not as we say?—and this verse descended as the answer's instrument: the category-question answered by the category's own founding case. The parallel's logic is exact and unanswerable on the disputants' own premises, the scholars unfold: the argument for Isa's divinity was his fatherless conception; Adam's file exceeds it—no father and no mother: created \"from dust,\" the forming this Book recounts at its own length (Surah 2, verses 30 to 34's announced creation; Surah 38, verses 71 and 72's clay and breathed spirit)—yet no church was ever raised to Adam's godhood; if fatherlessness proved divinity, Adam's double-parentlessness proved it doubly, and the delegation worshipped Adam no more than the Muslims did: the analogy catching the doctrine in its own arithmetic—the miraculous mode of a creature's forming testifies to the Former's freedom (verse 6's wombs-doctrine: \"He shapes you... as He wills\"; verse 47's \"Allah creates what He wills\"), never to the formed one's deity.\n\n\"He created him from dust, then said to him, 'Be!' And he was\" supplies the parallel's mechanics, and its clauses close the case's every joint: \"created him\"—the category-verb: Adam, and by the parallel Isa, filed under creation (the khalq-word verse 47's annunciation already used for the conception); \"from dust\"—the material's humility: the species' origin in the ground's substance (Surah 22, verse 5's stages), the reminder standing against every inflation of the creature; and \"then said to him, 'Be!' And he was\"—the formula this Book has taught as divine action's whole mechanism (Surah 2, verse 117's exposition; verse 47's annunciation-transcript): existence by imperative: Adam stood up from dust at a word, and the Word-title of verse 45 receives here its final gloss: Isa is \"a Word from Him\" as Adam was—the issue of \"Be!\", the imperative's child; the title the deviant readings (verse 7) bent toward incarnation being, by the Book's own glossary, creation-language of the highest register.\n\nThe verse's standing in the community's argument-history is its afterlife, the scholars note: it remains, fourteen centuries on, the discussion's permanent centre—the sentence to which every dialogue about Isa's nature returns, unimproved upon, because its method is proof by the shared premise: it argues from a case both parties already file identically (Adam created, not divine) to the case disputed, and leaves the disputant only the choice of consistency or special pleading; and its manner is the chapter's throughout: the Messiah honoured at every clause—the parallel drawn \"in the sight of Allah\": heaven's own filing—while the honour's ceiling holds: like Adam: wondrous, chosen, formed by the imperative—and created. The analogy delivered, the chapter stamps it (verse 60: \"the truth from your Lord—so do not doubt\") and unsheathes, for whoever disputes past the settled evidence, the summons that ended the Najran encounter: the mubahala.",
  "60": "\"This is the truth from your Lord, so do not be one of those who doubt\" stamps the Adam-parallel of verse 59—and with it the whole narrative-and-argument since verse 33—with the certification-formula this Book reserves for its settled matters, and the formula's two clauses perform their established offices (the identical sentence at Surah 2, verse 147, sealing the qiblah-truth against its manufactured controversy). \"The truth from your Lord\": the warrant named at its source—al-haqq, the truth in the matter of Isa: the conception by \"Be!\", the servanthood, the mission, the raising, the Adam-category—certified not as the winning argument of a debate (though it had won) but as report from the event's only Witness (verse 5's foundation; verse 44's \"news of the unseen\"): the disputation's subject-matter being precisely the kind—natures, chambers, and raisings—on which human parties hold only inherited fragments, and the Lord's report holds the facts.\n\n\"So do not be one of those who doubt\" addresses the Prophet, peace be upon him, with the command whose real audience is the community behind him—the address-strategy this Book deploys at exactly such pressure-points (Surah 2, verse 147's identical construction; verse 120's warning to the protected head so that no lesser head could claim exemption): he did not doubt; the command's descent fortifies every believer who would ever stand where he stood: before confident delegations, trained advocacies, and the sheer social weight of ancient institutions unanimous in a different account. The verse's placement gives the command its specific work, the commentators note: between the settling analogy (verse 59) and the mubahala-summons (verse 61) stands this clause of anchoring—for the summons about to be issued (the mutual imprecation, families staked on the truth) is an instrument only certainty can wield: whoever would call Allah's curse upon the liars must stand doubt-free on which side the lie sits; the sequence—evidence, certification, then the ordeal-summons—being the Book's own model of how conviction escalates lawfully: proof first, certainty stamped, and only then the ultimate stake.\n\nThe verse's permanent teaching, the scholars draw, is the epistemology it enacts one more time: truth's residence is \"from your Lord\"—doubt's cure is never the argument's endless re-litigation but the source's re-recognition (the chapter has argued, demonstrated, and narrated; the certification now closes the file); and the believer's inheritance from the clause is the standing posture it commands: on matters revelation has settled—the creed of verse 18, the servant-Messiah of verse 51's own proclamation, the category of verse 59—the believer is not a party to an open question but a custodian of a closed one: courteous in its presentation (verse 64's coming invitation shows the manner), unshakable in its substance; the \"those who doubt\" of the closing clause being, as the chapter has shown, not the honest questioners (Maryam and Zakariyya asked, and were honoured) but the professional re-openers of what clarity has sealed—the deviant hearts of verse 7, whose method the certified community is here commanded, in its Prophet's person, never to join.",
  "61": "\"Now, whoever disputes with you ˹O Prophet˺ concerning Jesus after full knowledge has come to you\" opens the disputation's final instrument, and its condition-clause is the instrument's licence: \"after full knowledge has come to you\"—the evidence complete (the narrative, verses 35 to 57; the analogy, verse 59; the certification, verse 60): the summons about to be issued is lawful only past that threshold; this Book does not stake families on undecided questions—the mubahala is not argument's substitute but its sequel: what remains when demonstrated truth meets obstinate advocacy (Surah 2, verse 145's proof-proof positions).\n\n\"Say, 'Come! Let us gather our children and your children, our women and your women, ourselves and yourselves—then let us sincerely invoke Allah's curse upon the liars'\" issues the summons—the mubahala: the mutual imprecation: both parties, with their dearest—children, women, selves—assembled in earnest supplication, calling Allah's curse upon whichever side lies in the matter of Isa; and the instrument's design is its power, the scholars unfold: it converts the dispute from rhetoric to stake: advocates maintain positions cheaply; this summons prices the position at one's family—no one brings his children to call down Allah's curse on the liars while privately unsure which side the lie sits; the summons is a sincerity-detector of the same class as the death-wish challenge (Surah 2, verse 94): the confident accept, the privately-knowing decline.\n\nAnd the event is the verse's recorded history, transmitted by the commentators from the sirah's accounts (Ibn Kathir and at-Tabari gathering them; the Sahih preserving the encounter's frame): the Prophet, peace be upon him, issued the summons to the Najran delegation—and came out for the appointment, by the famous transmission, with Hasan and Husain, Fatimah, and Ali: his own dearest staked on the truth of what he carried (Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2404 records his gathering of these under his cloak at this verse's occasion: \"O Allah, these are my family\"); and the delegation, seeing the assembly, withdrew—their bishop, by the transmitted accounts, counselling them that they faced a prophet's imprecation and no people ever invoked such a curse against one and prospered; they declined the mubahala, sought terms, and departed under treaty—the tribute-settlement the Prophet granted them with their worship protected. The encounter's verdict, the scholars draw, was delivered by its declining: the party of trained advocates, offered the stake-test on their own central doctrine, would not bring their children to it—the position maintained for prestige proving unmaintainable at price; while the summoning side arrived with its dearest in hand: certainty's difference displayed in the two parties' feet.\n\nThe verse's permanent bequest is double, the commentators note: the honour-text of the Prophet's house—the beloved four walking to the field enrolled forever in the verse's \"our children... our women... ourselves\"; and the instrument itself, held in the community's law for extremity: truth's advocates, when demonstration is complete and denial merely political, may lawfully move the question to the register where only sincerity survives—and the next verse will pronounce over the departed field the encounter's closing certification: \"certainly, this is the true narrative, and there is no god except Allah.\"",
  "62": "\"Certainly, this is the true narrative\" pronounces over the mubahala's departed field (verse 61) the encounter's closing certification, and its wording gathers the whole preceding movement into one exhibit: \"this\"—the account entire: Imran's wife and her vow, Maryam's chamber, Zakariyya's answered age, the annunciations, the credentials with their permission-clauses, the disciples' submission-pledge, the plot and the raising, and the Adam-parallel that filed it all—is al-qasas al-haqq: the true narration: the story as it happened, from the Witness's own vantage (verse 44's \"news of the unseen\"); the superlative claim made calmly, at the field the opposing advocates had just declined at family-stakes (verse 61's history), where its confidence had been tested in the only court rhetoric cannot rig. The claim's implicit comparison is the verse's quiet work, the commentators note: the era's Isa-narratives were a crowded shelf—the warring genealogies, the invented wonders, the councils' philosophies; against the shelf, this Book sets its account with the definite article: the true narrative: not a contribution to the genre but its adjudication (verse 4's Criterion at work on the genre's own subject).\n\n\"And there is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Allah\" states what the true narrative, truly read, was always about—and the creed's placement here is the chapter's architecture completing its circle, the scholars draw: the surah opened at this sentence (verse 2: \"Allah! There is no god except Him\"), built its summit-testimony on it (verse 18: Allah, the angels, and the people of knowledge attesting it), and now, the narrative told and the analogy delivered, returns to it as the story's own moral: every scene of the account preached this clause—the vowed mother praying to her Lord, the fed girl crediting Allah alone, the annunciated son titled servant and Word-of-\"Be!\", the Messiah's own proclamation (\"Allah is my Lord and your Lord,\" verse 51), the disciples' pledge to Allah, the raising \"to Myself\": one Godhood running unbroken through the very history the deification-doctrines were quarried from; the true narrative and the tawhid being, this verse's two clauses rule, one exhibit.\n\n\"And indeed, Allah ˹alone˺ is the Almighty, All-Wise\" seals the certification with the chapter's threshold-pairing (verse 6's closing names over the wombs' Shaper)—and their selection at this station reads the whole encounter, the commentators unfold: Almighty—the might the mubahala's decliners had just reckoned with (no party invokes the curse of the Omnipotent from a position it doubts); the counter-Planner of verse 54 whose design enclosed the age's greatest conspiracy; All-Wise—the wisdom legible across the entire file: the fatherless conception placed as sign, the raising placed as rescue, the disputation's instruments sequenced (evidence, certification, stake-summons) with a jurist's order, and the delegation dismissed under treaty rather than curse: severity offered, mercy transacted—the two names holding, as they held over Ibrahim's birds (Surah 2, verse 260) and the orphans' law (Surah 2, verse 220), the whole encounter's balance. The certification issued, the next verse closes the Najran file with the Book's constant boundary (the turners-away left to the Knower of corrupters), and the chapter then pivots to its widest summons—past the delegation to all People of the Book: \"come to common terms: that we will worship none but Allah.\"",
  "63": "\"If they turn away, then surely Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of the corruptors\" closes the Najran file with the Book's constant boundary-clause—and each of its terms, placed at this exact station, carries the encounter's completed weight. \"If they turn away\": the turning the record then documented (verse 61's history)—the delegation declining the mubahala's field: the evidence heard (verses 35 to 59), the certification issued (verses 60 and 62), the stake-summons declined; the clause's conditional grammar covering them and every party after them who repeats their sequence: argument exhausted, sincerity-test refused, position maintained—the turning-away this argument has met at every register (Surah 2, verse 137: \"if they turn away, they are simply opposed\"; verse 20 of this chapter: \"your duty is only to deliver\").\n\n\"Then surely Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of the corruptors\" delivers the file's disposition, and its two movements are the Book's whole jurisprudence of the unpersuadable, the scholars unfold. The transfer: the matter passes from the Messenger's hands to Allah's knowledge—the deliverer's commission discharged at clarity's completion (verse 20's boundary; Surah 2, verse 272: \"you are not responsible for people's guidance\"), the community released from the argument's endless re-litigation into its own work (the manner modelled at Surah 2, verse 148: certainty held, life proceeding); no coercion follows the declined summons—the delegation departed under treaty, worship protected: \"no compulsion in religion\" (Surah 2, verse 256) governing even the mubahala's aftermath. And the naming: the turners-away are filed as al-mufsidin—the corruptors: the term's selection the verse's quiet verdict, the commentators note: not \"the mistaken\" nor even \"the disbelievers\" here, but corruption's own word (Surah 2, verse 11's file)—for the delegation's leadership, by the transmitted accounts, declined the field knowing: their counsel to each other conceded the prophet-recognition their public position denied (verse 61's transmitted bishop's-counsel; the pattern of Surah 2, verse 146: \"they recognize him as they recognize their own children\"); and maintaining, for position and revenue, a doctrine one privately knows to fail the stake-test is not error but ifsad: the deliberate corruption of a community's relationship with truth—the scholars' definition standing for every era's professional maintainers of refuted positions.\n\nThe verse's closing comfort is its knowledge-clause's other face, drawn throughout this argument (Surah 2, verse 137's \"Allah will spare you their evil\"): the corruptors are known—their private concessions, their managed communities, their position's real ledger: all filed with the Knower before whom the mubahala would have been argued; the community leaves the encounter with its permanent equipment: deliver with clarity, escalate lawfully, accept the turning-away without despair or pursuit—and trust the file to the Knowledge that reads corrupters entire. The Najran chapter closed, the summons now widens past every delegation to the scripture-communities as such—the Book's most famous interfaith sentence: \"O People of the Book! Let us come to common terms...\"",
  "64": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ 'O People of the Book! Let us come to common terms'\" widens the disputation's close (verse 63) into the Book's most famous interfaith summons—and its instrument's design is the verse's genius: kalimatin sawa—\"a word equitable between us\": common terms: the invitation stepping past the contested territories (the natures, the councils, the delegations' doctrines) to the ground every party's own scripture professes; not compromise's splitting of differences but return to the shared foundation each community's charter already contains—the method of verse 135's Ibrahim-answer (past the branches to the root) now issued as standing diplomatic instrument to \"the People of the Book\" as such: Najran's Christians, Madinah's Jews, and every scripture-community to history's end.\n\n\"That we will worship none but Allah, associate none with Him, nor take one another as lords instead of Allah\" publishes the common terms' three clauses, and their sequence descends through false worship's three depths, the scholars unfold: worship none but Allah—tawhid's positive charter (the creed of verse 2, Isa's own proclamation at verse 51); associate none with Him—shirk's explicit bar: the partners, intercessreory objects, and divinised mediators every community's drift had installed; and the third clause the summons's surgical addition: \"nor take one another as lords instead of Allah\"—the humans-as-lords disease: the scholars, priests, and hierarchies obeyed in Allah's place; and the Sunnah supplies the clause's exact exegesis: when Adi ibn Hatim, the Christian convert, heard \"they have taken their rabbis and monks as lords besides Allah\" (Surah 9, verse 31) and protested \"we did not worship them,\" the Prophet, peace be upon him, answered: \"Did they not forbid what Allah permitted, and you forbade it; and permit what Allah forbade, and you permitted it?\" He said: yes. \"That is your worship of them\" (Jami` at-Tirmidhi, Hadith 3095—graded sound): lordship-taking defined as legislative obedience against Allah's law—the clause thus auditing every community, the summoning one included: whoever's scholars can override the revelation for him has taken a lord.\n\nThe verse's history is its confirmation, the commentators note: the Prophet, peace be upon him, made this verse his instrument of state—inscribed in his letter to Heraclius, emperor of Byzantium: \"O People of the Book, let us come to common terms...\" (the letter preserved entire at Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 7, in Abu Sufyan's audience-account)—the summons carried from the Najran field to the age's greatest scripture-throne: one sentence serving as the community's foreign policy toward the believing nations.\n\n\"But if they turn away, then say, 'Bear witness that we have submitted ˹to Allah alone˺'\" closes the summons with the refused invitation's protocol, and its commanded sentence is the chapter's deepest echo: \"bear witness that we are muslims\"—the disciples' own charter-words (verse 52: \"bear witness that we have submitted\"): the community instructed to answer the scripture-peoples' refusal by declaring itself, in the Gospel-company's exact formula, the standing heirs of what the refusers left; no curse pronounced, no door slammed—a testimony lodged: the common terms remain published, the submitters remain identifiable, and history is left holding the summons still open at this verse's address.",
  "65": "\"O People of the Book! Why do you argue about Abraham, while the Torah and the Gospel were not revealed until long after him?\" opens the disputation's next file—the contested patriarch—and its recorded occasion, transmitted by the commentators, stages the absurdity the verse dissolves: the rabbis and the Najran Christians disputed before the Prophet, peace be upon him, each community claiming Ibrahim as its own—\"Abraham was a Jew,\" \"Abraham was a Christian\"—the ownership-contest this argument met once at Surah 2, verse 140 and here receives its chronological demolition: the Torah descended to Musa, the Gospel to Isa—centuries after Ibrahim's death (the commentators counting the spans: Musa across many generations from Ibrahim, Isa across many more); Judaism as the Torah-community and Christianity as the Gospel-community are, by their own charters' dates, later foundations: enrolling Ibrahim in either is enrolling a man in institutions born long after his funeral—the anachronism so plain that the verse's weapon is a single question about the calendar.\n\n\"Do you not understand?\" closes the demolition with the reason-summons this Book serves on exactly such performances (Surah 2, verse 44's identical clause over the preach-without-practice scholars; verse 76's \"do you not understand\" among the concealers)—and its selection here is the verse's edge, the scholars note: the disputants were the age's learned: chronology was their own science, the scriptures' dates their own curriculum; the contest was maintained not in ignorance of the calendar but in disregard of it—partisanship overriding the party's own knowledge (the diagnosis of verse 19: the differing \"out of mutual envy... after knowledge came to them\"); and the question \"do you not understand?\" is thus not information's request but the mirror's: the learned invited to watch themselves claim, for communal trophy, what their own libraries date as impossible.\n\nThe verse's method is its permanent bequest, the commentators draw: against religious claims, the calendar is admissible evidence—revelation here argues from plain historical sequence, dignifying chronology, documents, and dates as instruments of theological adjudication (the same method verse 59 used with category-logic and Surah 2, verse 111 with the burden of proof): faith's disputes are not exempt from the ordinary disciplines of knowing; and the specific finding stands for every era's version of the contest: whenever later institutions—denominations, schools, movements—claim founding figures who predate them, the question of this verse applies: the founder belongs to what he actually stood upon, not to the structures raised over his name afterward. What Ibrahim actually stood upon, the file's next verses will state in the Quran's own definitive sentence (verse 67: \"neither a Jew nor a Christian; he submitted in all uprightness\")—but first the disputants' epistemology itself is audited (verse 66): a people who argued badly about what they partly knew, examined for arguing at all about what they know nothing of.",
  "66": "\"Here you are! You disputed about what you have ˹little˺ knowledge of, but why do you now argue about what you have no knowledge of?\" audits the Ibrahim-disputants' epistemology itself—and the verse's two-tier structure is the audit's instrument, the scholars unfold. The first tier, conceded: \"you disputed about what you have knowledge of\"—the matters within the communities' actual documents: their own scriptures' contents, their laws, the histories their Books record (the Isa-controversies included, where the Gospel-community held at least its transmitted texts): disputation there was poorly conducted—the chapter has documented the concealments and bendings (verse 71 ahead)—but it operated on some holdings. The second tier, exposed: \"why do you now argue about what you have no knowledge of?\"—the Ibrahim-claim's category: the patriarch's religious identity, dead centuries before either community's founding charter descended (verse 65's calendar), witnessed by no document either party held, transmitted to them by no chain their sciences could certify: the contest conducted, on both sides, from pure communal assertion—zero holdings, maximum confidence; and the verse's astonished form (\"here you are!\") holds the spectacle up: the learned professionally scrupulous about their second-tier traditions, abandoning every evidentiary standard the moment tribal title to a patriarch was at stake.\n\n\"Allah knows and you do not know\" closes the audit with the clause this Book sets over every such boundary (Surah 2, verse 216's ending; verse 232's)—and its work here is double, the commentators draw. The adjudication: the Ibrahim-question has an Answerer—the matter beyond both communities' knowledge sits within the Knowledge from which nothing hides (verse 5); and the very next verse will deliver that Knowledge's verdict on the disputed patriarch: \"Abraham was neither a Jew nor a Christian...\"—the audit of this verse being the verdict's formal preparation: both parties' standing to pronounce removed, the field cleared for the report of the only qualified Witness (verse 44's epistemology: what Allah alone saw, He alone can tell). And the discipline: the clause legislates for every believer the boundary the disputants trampled—speech about the unseen and the undocumented past is licensed only by revelation's report: where the texts speak, knowledge; where they are silent, \"Allah knows and you do not know\" is the learned answer (the well-grounded's own posture at verse 7: belief at the boundary, silence past it)—the confession the scholars called half of knowledge (Surah 2, verse 32's angels' model), and the precise instrument this Book's community was given against ever reproducing the spectacle this verse frames: centuries of confident sectarian assertion about matters on which no party held a single page.\n\nThe audit's permanent reach, the scholars press, extends past ancient patriarch-contests to every age's confident ignorance: the doctrinal wars fought over undisclosed matters—the decrees' secrets, the unseen's furniture, the salvation-status of named souls—all conducted in this verse's second tier; and the believer formed by it carries its two marks: rigour about what his sources actually contain, and rest—not embarrassment—in the boundary-clause: for \"Allah knows and you do not know\" is not the argument lost but the creature's dignity found: the disputants claimed a knowledge that was never theirs, and the next verse shows what the Knowledge they bypassed had to say.",
  "67": "\"Abraham was neither a Jew nor a Christian\" delivers the verdict the two-verse preparation cleared the field for (verse 65's calendar, verse 66's removed standing)—and its negative clauses execute what the audit promised: the Knowledge that alone witnessed Ibrahim (verse 66's closing clause) reports on him, and the report's first movement is double negation: not a Jew, not a Christian—the two communal titles, each the banner of a scripture and a system centuries younger than their claimed founder (verse 65's demolition), stripped from the patriarch by the only competent Witness; the ownership-contest of the delegations dissolved not by splitting the estate but by voiding both claims: the man predates the deeds.\n\n\"He submitted in all uprightness and was not a polytheist\" gives the verdict's positive identification, and its two terms are the Quran's complete portrait of the man, each carrying its established weight (the identical formula at Surah 2, verse 135, there answering the same two communities' recruiting): hanifan—upright: the incliner-away: turned from every false object and crooked path toward the one true direction, the natural monotheism every child is born upon (Surah 2, verse 135's exposition; Surah 30, verse 30's fitrah); and musliman—\"he submitted\": a muslim, in the word's primordial sense this chapter has been assembling witness by witness (verse 19: \"Allah's only Way is Islam\"; verse 52: the disciples' \"bear witness that we have submitted\"): Ibrahim's religion named by the submission-word itself—the identification this Book documents from his own transcript: \"when his Lord ordered him, 'Submit,' he responded, 'I submit to the Lord of all worlds'\" (Surah 2, verse 131). \"And was not a polytheist\" closes the portrait with the clause whose double edge the commentators note (as at Surah 2, verse 135): it bars the pagan Arabs' claim to him—the idol-filled Kaaba's custodians invoking its idol-breaking builder—and it audits the scriptural claimants too: whatever, in any community's system, associates partners with Allah (the deified Messiah of the Najran thesis; the lords-besides-Allah of verse 64's third clause) stands outside the patriarch's religion by the verdict's last word.\n\nThe verdict's function, standing back, is the chapter's whole strategy crowned, the scholars draw: the disputation-season's contests—over Isa, over guidance's monopoly (verse 73), over Ibrahim—were all title-contests: communities claiming exclusive deed to sacred history; and this verse's method with the greatest title of all is the method the argument has taught throughout: the title belongs to the substance, not the institutions—Ibrahim was what he did: inclined, submitted, associated nothing; whoever reproduces the substance holds the succession, whatever his era's labels, and whoever holds only the label holds nothing of the man (Surah 2, verse 134's earnings-rule). The succession's actual heirs are the next verse's business—\"the best claim to Abraham\"—and its award-list will complete this verdict's logic: his followers in his own age, this Prophet, and the believers: the estate of the first muslim awarded, by the Witness's ruling, to the practising muslims of every age.",
  "68": "\"Indeed, those who have the best claim to Abraham are his followers, this Prophet, and the believers\" awards the estate the verdict of verse 67 cleared—and the award's criterion is its doctrine: awla, the best claim, the nearest title: assigned not by blood (the contest's assumed currency) but by following: \"his followers\"—those of his own age and after who walked his actual way: the inclining, the submitting, the association of nothing (verse 67's portrait); then \"this Prophet\"—Muhammad, peace be upon him, named as the succession's centre: the restorer of the patriarch's House and qiblah (Surah 2, verses 125 to 129: the builder's own prayer answered in this very man), his law the hanif-religion's completion (\"follow the faith of Abraham, the upright,\" Surah 2, verse 135's commanded answer; Surah 16, verse 123: \"We revealed to you: follow the way of Abraham\"); and \"the believers\"—his community: the practising muslims of every land enrolled in the patriarch's line by the only registrar the verse recognises: resemblance in religion. The award executes, formally, the rule this argument fixed at its foundation: \"My covenant is not extended to the wrongdoers\" (Surah 2, verse 124)—the covenant travels by conduct; and the Prophet, peace be upon him, stated the award's principle as standing doctrine: \"every prophet has guardians from among the prophets; my guardian among them is my father, the friend of my Lord\"—and he recited this verse (the transmission at Jami` at-Tirmidhi's chapters on this surah; graded by the verifiers)—with the wider rule sounded across the Sunnah: \"whoever is slowed by his deeds is not sped by his lineage\" (Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2699).\n\n\"And Allah is the Guardian of those who believe\" seals the award with the wilayah-clause—and its placement over this estate is the verse's finest stroke, the scholars draw: the contested inheritance of Ibrahim was never, at its heart, a genealogical trophy but a relationship: Ibrahim was the khalil—the intimate friend of Allah (Surah 4, verse 125); to claim his estate is to claim proximity to his Lord; and the closing clause names where that proximity actually resides: Allah is the Wali—Guardian, Patron, intimate Protector (Surah 2, verse 257's covenant)—\"of those who believe\": the believers of the verse's award-list holding, in this clause, the estate's true substance: the Friend's friendship, extended to whoever carries the Friend's religion; while the blood-claimants without the following hold documents to an intimacy they never entered.\n\nThe verse's permanent audit, the commentators press, faces its own award-holders first: the community named in the grant—\"the believers\"—holds the Ibrahim-estate by the grant's own criterion: following; and the title lapses where the following lapses: the Muslim who claims the patriarch while abandoning the patriarch's substance—the tawhid uncompromised, the submission at first command (Surah 2, verse 131), the readiness to stake the dearest (verse 61's field; Surah 37's sacrifice-summit)—has re-entered the very contest this verse closed, on the losing side's terms: claiming by label what is awarded only by likeness. The estate settled, the chapter turns to the disinherited claimants' response—the campaign to mislead (verse 69), the rejected signs (verse 70), the mixed and hidden truth (verse 71): the file of those who, losing the patriarch, moved to unsettle his awarded heirs.",
  "69": "\"Some of the People of the Book wish to mislead you ˹believers˺\" opens the file of the disinherited claimants' response—the estate awarded (verse 68), the campaign begins—and its anatomy repeats the intelligence this argument first served at Surah 2, verse 109 (\"many among the People of the Book wish they could turn you back to disbelief because of their envy\"): the wish—wadda, the settled desire: not casual disagreement but ambition's steady wanting—held by \"some\": the census-qualifier this Book never drops (Surah 2, verse 100's \"a group of them\"; the believing exceptions honoured ahead at verse 199), aimed at the believers' misleading: idlal, the leading-astray: the faith unsettled, the converts reclaimed, the community's certainty eroded by the instruments the chapter has been cataloguing—the elusive verses bent (verse 7), the manufactured controversies (Surah 2, verse 142's qiblah-mockery), and the day-and-evening believing-then-rejecting scheme about to be exposed at verse 72.\n\n\"They mislead none but themselves, yet they fail to perceive it\" delivers the campaign's audit, and its reversal is the Book's established justice at its most exact (Surah 2, verse 9's identical structure over the deceivers: \"they deceive none but themselves, but they fail to perceive it\"): the misleading-project's actual casualty is its operators—and the scholars unfold the reversal's mechanics: the campaign fails outward, for guidance's custody was never within its reach (\"it is Allah Who guides whoever He wills,\" Surah 2, verse 272; the Guardian-clause one verse back: \"Allah is the Guardian of those who believe\"—the campaign filed against the Patron's own clients); and it succeeds inward, catastrophically: every hour spent bending texts, staging doubts, and managing schemes is an hour of the operator's own deepening—the knowing suppression of recognised truth (Surah 2, verse 146) compounding with each operation, the heart's own relationship to truth corroded by the profession of corroding others': the misleader becomes his campaign's most processed product. \"Yet they fail to perceive it\": the campaign's final layer—the self-damage invisible to the self-damager (Surah 2, verse 12's identical clause over the corruptors): the operators book their outward failures as setbacks and their inward ruin not at all; the deceiver's instruments never point at himself.\n\nThe verse's issue to the believers is its placement's work, the commentators draw: served directly after the Guardian-clause (verse 68's closing), the intelligence arrives wrapped in its own antidote: yes, a campaign exists—sober eyes required (Surah 2, verse 109's realism); no, it cannot reach what it targets—for the targeted faith sits in the Wali's keeping, and the verse's audit has already published the campaign's true casualty-list; the believers' response, accordingly, is neither panic nor polemic-obsession but the posture this argument assigned at Surah 2, verse 148: certainty held, good raced toward, and the misleaders left to the self-misleading this verse certifies—while the address turns, in the next verses, from the believers to the campaigners themselves, with the questions that dismantle their operation at its two hinges: \"why do you reject the signs of Allah while you bear witness to their truth? Why do you mix the truth with falsehood and hide the truth knowingly?\"",
  "70": "\"O People of the Book! Why do you reject the signs of Allah while you bear witness ˹to their truth˺\" turns the address from the believers (verse 69's intelligence) to the campaigners themselves—and the turn's form is the summons: \"O People of the Book\": the honour-title itself the indictment's frame (the people defined by scripture's custody, audited on scripture's own terms), and the question's two clauses close like shears: you reject the signs of Allah—the kufr-verb: the denial published, the mission opposed, the misleading-campaign run (verse 69)—\"while you bear witness\": wa-antum tashhadun: the witnessing simultaneous with the rejecting: the scholars' own studies confirming the signs they publicly denied—the Prophet's description in their texts (Surah 2, verse 146's recognition \"as they recognize their own children\"), the confirmations their own sciences certified (verse 3's Torah and Gospel confirmed), the prophecies their traditions carried (Surah 2, verse 89's awaited one). The question's grammar is its power, the commentators note: not \"do you reject?\"—the rejection was public record—but \"why?\": the interrogative that grants no refuge, for every available answer convicts: rejection-despite-witness has no epistemic causes, only motive-causes—the envy (Surah 2, verse 90), the position (Surah 2, verse 174's revenue), the primacy (verse 69's campaign)—and the question stands, deliberately unanswered in the text, as the mirror this Book holds to every knowing rejection: the answer must be supplied by the rejecter, and no answer he can supply survives its own statement.\n\nThe charge's precision deserves the pause the scholars gave it: witnessing-while-rejecting is a different and graver crime than disbelief simple—it is the crime this whole argument has tracked as scripture-custody's occupational disease (Surah 2, verse 42's \"knowingly\"; verse 101's \"as if they did not know\"): the learned rejection: truth held in one hand's documents and denied with the other's declarations; and its gravity is its testimony's value inverted: these witnesses were history's most qualified—the communities trained for centuries to identify the final Prophet (Surah 2, verse 89's pre-announcements); their witness, honestly given, would have carried nations (as it did carry, in the Abdullah ibn Salams and the Najran-readers whose eyes overflowed, Surah 5, verse 83); withheld and inverted, it became the campaign of verse 69—the qualified witness turned professional obscurer: no perjury in history cost more.\n\nThe verse's permanent audit, the commentators press, travels with the title it summons: every \"people of the Book\"—this Book's community now first among them—stands askable of its question: the signs witnessed in one's own sources and rejected in one's own conduct: the Quran's clear commands attested in study and declined in practice (Surah 2, verse 44's mirror; verse 85's selective covenant); the believer formed by this verse keeps its shears from closing on himself by the only available means: the witness and the response held together—what the sources attest, the life enacts; and the address's next question (verse 71) will move from the rejection's fact to its method: the mixing and the hiding by which witnessed truth was processed for public denial.",
  "71": "\"O People of the Book! Why do you mix the truth with falsehood and hide the truth knowingly\" completes the two-question audit begun at verse 70—the first question exposed the rejection's fact (witnessed truth denied); this one names its method—and the two named techniques are the complete toolkit of managed truth, the scholars unfold, each carrying its file from the argument's foundation (Surah 2, verse 42's identical charge: \"do not mix truth with falsehood or hide the truth knowingly\"). The mixing—labs al-haqq bil-batil: truth clothed in falsehood: the adulteration-technique: authentic material—real texts, real prophecies, real law—blended with interpolation, misreading, and invented gloss until the compound defies the ordinary hearer's separation (Surah 2, verse 79's pens; Surah 3, verse 78's coming tongue-twisting: \"you would think it from the Book, but it is not from the Book\"); the technique's power being precisely its truth-content: pure falsehood fails on contact; falsehood alloyed with truth travels on truth's credentials—the counterfeiter's law: the best lies are mostly true. And the hiding—kitman: the suppression-technique: the descriptions unread, the verses skipped, the rulings shelved (the stoning-verse concealed under the scholar's hand until the Prophet had it lifted: Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3635; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 1699): where mixing corrupts the record, hiding removes it—the two together giving their operators complete editorial control over what their communities could know.\n\n\"Knowingly\" closes the question with the adverb this argument has made its gravest (Surah 2, verse 42's; verse 146's \"a group of them hides the truth knowingly\")—and its work here is the audit's point: the techniques' operators were not confused transmitters but informed editors: the mixing done by those who knew which strands were true, the hiding by those who knew what the hidden said; the question's \"why,\" as at verse 70, granting no epistemic refuge—method-crimes of this class have only motive-answers: the revenue (Surah 2, verse 174), the rank, the campaign (verse 69); and the two questions together (70 and 71) thus complete the anatomy of institutionalised untruth: rejection at the top (the witnessed denied), management beneath it (the record mixed and hidden), the community below holding, in consequence, a religion edited by its editors' interests—the condition Surah 2, verse 78 described from the receiving end: the masses knowing \"nothing about the Scripture except lies... wishful speculation.\"\n\nThe verse's permanent legislation, the commentators press, is the purity-standard it implies for all custodians of revelation: truth's two rights, drawn at Surah 2, verse 42's commentary, stand for every generation—to be kept unmixed, and to be made known; and this Book's community holds the standard with the heaviest accountability, custodian now of the final unedited revelation (Surah 15, verse 9's preservation-guarantee): its scholars audited by this verse whenever presentation shades into mixing (the convenient half-quote, the context stripped, the weak report dressed as established) or silence into hiding (the unwelcome ruling shelved, the audience-displeasing text unread—Surah 2, verse 159's curse and its bridle-hadith standing: Jami` at-Tirmidhi, Hadith 2649); while the address moves, in the next verse, from the editors' standing techniques to a specific live operation—the believe-by-morning, reject-by-evening scheme: managed truth deployed as psychological warfare against the young community's confidence.",
  "72": "\"A group among the People of the Book said ˹to one another˺, 'Believe in what has been revealed to the believers in the morning and reject it in the evening, so they may abandon their faith'\" exposes a live operation from the campaign-file (verse 69's wish-to-mislead), and its transcript—quoted from the planners' own councils by the Knowledge no council excludes (verse 5; Surah 2, verse 77)—preserves psychological warfare's ancient design with documentary precision. The scheme: coordinated members enter Islam publicly at the day's opening—the profession loud, the conversion celebrated—and renounce it at the day's close: the exit as public as the entry, staged as discovery: we examined this religion from within and found what decided us; and the mechanism aimed at the believers' interior, the commentators unfold: the new community included fresh converts and watching hearts for whom the scripture-people's verdict carried the expert's weight (the learned neighbours who could read the awaited signs, Surah 2, verse 89)—a stream of credentialed men entering and exiting in one day manufactures the impression their words never could: perhaps those who know the Books best found the flaw we cannot see; the operation weaponising, in effect, the community's own humility before knowledge—apostasy theatre, scheduled for maximum contagion: \"so they may abandon their faith.\"\n\nThe scheme's exposure by revelation was its destruction, the scholars note: psychological operations run on invisibility—the staged exit must read as sincere discovery, or it reads as nothing; this verse, descending into Madinah's streets with the planners' own words (\"said to one another\"), converted every scheduled apostasy in advance from ambush into confirmation: the community, forewarned by the transcript, would meet the morning-believers' evening exits as the fulfilment of a published prophecy—each performance thereafter proving the Book that predicted it (the counter-intelligence pattern of Surah 2, verse 76's exposed councils; the manufactured qiblah-controversy pre-empted at Surah 2, verse 142); revelation's habit of publishing the enemy's playbook being itself among the signs the operators were staging exits to discredit.\n\nThe verse's permanent equipment, the commentators draw, serves the community in every era of the scheme's descendants—for the design outlived its first operators: the celebrated conversion reversed for audiences, the insider's exit dressed as evidence, the testimonial of the disillusioned deployed against the faith's confidence—all of it this verse's operation in later costume; and the believer formed by the exposure holds its two disciplines: the faith's evidences weighed as evidences (verse 62's \"true narrative\"; Surah 2, verse 23's standing challenge)—never as head-counts of the entering and exiting, for the religion's truth was not raised by the morning's professions and is not lowered by the evening's renunciations; and the schemers left to the audit already filed over the campaign entire (verse 69): \"they mislead none but themselves\"—while the next verse completes the councils' transcript: the instruction to trust none but their own, and the bounty-doctrine that dissolves the whole operation's premise: \"all bounty is in the Hands of Allah—He grants it to whoever He wills.\"",
  "73": "\"And only believe those who follow your religion\" continues the exposed council-transcript of verse 72, and this instruction is the scheme's inner wall: the operatives staging morning-belief and evening-rejection for the believers' consumption are instructed, among themselves, where real assent may travel: nowhere outside the community—trust's border drawn at the tribe's edge; the clause exposing the whole operation's epistemology: truth, in the planners' council, was not a matter of evidence to be followed wherever it led (their own scholars' witnessing, verse 70, had led it to Muhammad's door) but a communal asset to be managed—belief itself weaponised abroad and rationed at home. \"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ 'Surely, ˹the only˺ true guidance is Allah's guidance'\" is the commanded interruption—the Book breaking into the quoted council mid-transcript with the sentence that dissolves its premise (the identical instrument at Surah 2, verse 120): guidance is Allah's guidance—not a community's property to issue or withhold, not validated by the scripture-people's admission nor voided by their theatre: the converts the scheme targeted were holding guidance from its Owner, whatever the credentialed exits implied.\n\n\"˹They also said,˺ 'Do not believe that someone will receive ˹revealed˺ knowledge similar to yours or argue against you before your Lord'\" resumes the transcript at its deepest layer, and the disclosed motive is the argument's oldest file: the council's real dread, spoken among its own—that revelation's gift might pass to others: \"that anyone should be given the like of what you were given\"—the prophethood, the Book, the covenant-primacy migrating to the sons of Ismail (Surah 2, verse 90's diagnosis: \"resenting Allah for granting His grace to whoever He wills\"); and its twin: \"or argue against you before your Lord\"—the recipients armed, at the Day's assembly, with testimony against the concealers (Surah 2, verse 76's identical council-fear: \"so that they may use it against you before your Lord\"): the operation of verse 72, beneath its theatre, being envy's campaign against a grant already made.\n\n\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ 'Indeed, all bounty is in the Hands of Allah—He grants it to whoever He wills. And Allah is All-Bountiful, All-Knowing'\" delivers the answer that ends the file, and its doctrine is the bounty-theology this argument has deployed against every monopoly-claim (Surah 2, verse 105's identical structure): the fadl—the bounty: revelation, prophethood, guidance's light—is in His Hands: not in the former custodians' gift, not subject to their councils' embargo; granted \"to whoever He wills\"—the sovereign distribution the envious called scandal and the Book calls lordship (verse 26's estate); and the closing pair seals both fronts: All-Bountiful—wasi: vast: the grant to the new community subtracted nothing from any store (envy's zero-sum premise dissolved, Surah 2, verse 105's commentary); All-Knowing—the placement informed: \"Allah knows best where to place His message\" (Surah 6, verse 124): the bounty's address chosen by the Knowledge the council's dread could not lobby. The next verse completes the answer's mercy-clause—\"He chooses whoever He wills to receive His mercy\"—and the file then turns from the councils' schemes to their commerce: the trusts honoured and betrayed, and the gentile-exploitation doctrine by which betrayal was theologised.",
  "74": "\"He chooses whoever He wills to receive His mercy\" completes the bounty-answer of verse 73 with its widest clause—and the completion's vocabulary is the chapter's own election-language brought full circle, the scholars note: yakhtassu—He singles out, appoints exclusively: the mercy's grants (prophethood at their summit, guidance and revelation in their breadth) distributed by khtisas: sovereign selection—the verb answering, term for term, the exposed council's dread (verse 73's \"that anyone should be given the like of what you were given\"): what the council treated as a communal franchise lapsing scandalously to outsiders was always a selection operating from eternity by no register their genealogies kept: the istifa of verse 33 (Adam, Nuh, and the two households chosen), the \"Allah grants kingship to whoever He wills\" of Talut's file (Surah 2, verse 247), the \"Allah knows best where to place His message\" (Surah 6, verse 124)—the final Prophet's appointment sitting in that unbroken series, and the series' one constant being this verse's clause: whoever He wills; no committee of the previously-graced ever held a veto.\n\nThe clause's naming of the grant as \"His mercy\" deserves the pause the commentators gave it: the disputed items—the Book, the prophethood, the community's commissioning—are filed under rahmah: mercy: the category that reframes the whole quarrel; for mercy, by its nature, indicts no one by its address: the rain's falling on one field wrongs no other (Surah 2, verse 105's commentary: nothing given to the Muslims was taken from Israel's store), and the mercy's former custodians were losing nothing but an exclusivity that was never the grant's terms (Surah 2, verse 124's covenant-clause standing over every era: \"My covenant is not extended to the wrongdoers\")—their true grievance, the transcript showed (verse 73), being not deprivation but the grant's arrival elsewhere: envy's exact definition, the disease this argument diagnosed at Surah 2, verse 90 and the Prophet, peace be upon him, warned had shaved religion itself in the nations before (Jami` at-Tirmidhi, Hadith 2510's warning).\n\n\"And Allah is the Lord of infinite bounty\" seals the two-verse answer with the title that dissolves the council-theology's last premise—the exact seal of Surah 2, verse 105 (dhul-fadlil-azim: possessor of the immense bounty)—and its double work stands as it stood there, the scholars draw: against the enviers, the scarcity-premise voided: bounty without floor or ceiling makes every zero-sum theology of grace a slander on the Treasury (the \"Hands\" of verse 73 holding what no distribution depletes); and toward the granted community, the title is the grant's own weight handed over: the recipients of the immense bounty—the final Book, the witness-commission (Surah 2, verse 143), the mercy singled out to them—stand under the receiving's whole accountability: chosen as their predecessors were chosen, warned by their predecessors' entire file (the chapter's running exhibit), and holding the bounty on the only terms it has ever been held: gratitude and custodianship, or the selection's next address. The bounty-doctrine settled, the address turns from grace's distribution to trust's—the file of the entrusted gold returned and the entrusted coin devoured, and the theology by which the devouring was licensed: \"we are not accountable for the Gentiles.\"",
  "75": "\"There are some among the People of the Book who, if entrusted with a stack of gold, will readily return it\" opens the trusts-file with the Book's characteristic census-justice—and the opening entry is the honourable one, the commentators insist on its weight: among the scripture-people are those of amanah at its summit: the qintar—the hoarded heap of gold—returnable on demand without a dinar's shaving: integrity's full measure attested in the very communities the surrounding verses indict; the Quran's refusal of collective slander standing here as doctrine (the census-clauses of Surah 2, verse 100's \"a group of them,\" and the coming honour-roll of verse 113: \"they are not all alike\")—the argument's targets always conducts, never bloodlines.\n\n\"Yet there are others who, if entrusted with a single coin, will not repay it unless you constantly demand it\" enters the file's second column: the dinar—one coin—unreturnable except under standing siege: the creditor's constant demanding the only instrument the debtor's conscience answers to; the two columns' amounts deliberately crossed, the scholars note: the trustworthy returning heaps, the treacherous devouring units—for integrity was never a function of the sum but of the man: the qintar-returner would return the coin, the coin-devourer would devour the qintar; trust's test is the trustee, and the deliberately inverted amounts strip every excuse of scale.\n\n\"This is because they say, 'We are not accountable for ˹exploiting˺ the Gentiles'\" exposes the devouring's licence, and the exposed doctrine is the file's true subject: \"there is no way against us concerning the ummiyyin\"—the gentiles, the scriptureless: the claim that covenant-obligations run only within the covenant-community: the outsider's wealth fair game, his trust unprotected by any law we answer to—ethics with a tribal border: the exact commercial form of verse 73's council-instruction (\"only believe those who follow your religion\"): trust rationed at home, predation licensed abroad; and its gravity is its theological dress: the exploitation claimed as religion's own ruling—the wrong not merely done but doctrinalised.\n\n\"And ˹so˺ they attribute lies to Allah knowingly\" files the doctrine at its true criminal register, and the filing is the verse's verdict: the gentile-exploitation licence was speech about Allah—His law claimed as its source—and He never legislated it: their own Torah's justice universal (the commentators noting the scripture's own commands against fraud toward any); the licence was thus the manufacture of Surah 2, verse 79 (the written-then-attributed) in commercial deployment, and \"knowingly\" seals it with the argument's gravest adverb (verses 71, 78): the licensers knew their Lord never licensed it. The verse's permanent audit, the scholars press, travels to every community that draws ethics' border at its own edge—the believer's law standing opposite by this Book's explicit charter: trusts returned to their people universally (Surah 4, verse 58), justice owed even to enemies (\"do not let the hatred of a people lead you to injustice,\" Surah 5, verse 8), and the Prophet's own final entry in the file: he entered his migration-night with the Makkans' deposits still in his keeping—the persecutors' valuables returned by Ali's hand as their owners hunted the depositor: the trustee of the hostile, the verse's first column incarnate. The file's ruling follows in the next verse: the criterion neither tribe nor claim, but \"those who honour their trusts and shun evil—surely Allah loves those who are mindful of Him.\"",
  "76": "\"Absolutely! Those who honour their trusts and shun evil\" overturns the gentile-exploitation doctrine of verse 75 with the Book's emphatic reversal-particle—bala: \"no indeed!\": the claimed licence (\"no way against us concerning the Gentiles\") annulled in one syllable—and the annulment's instrument is the criterion that replaces it: not tribe but conduct: \"whoever fulfils his covenant and is mindful\"—the original's singular grammar making the ruling personal and universal at once: man awfa bi-ahdihi: whoever honours his pledge—the trusts of verse 75's file (the entrusted gold, the entrusted coin), the covenants with Allah (Surah 2, verse 40's charter) and with creatures of every community (the treaties, the deposits, the contracts of Surah 2, verse 282's law); and \"shuns evil\"—wattaqa: the taqwa-verb itself: the guarding-mindfulness this whole revelation names as its goal (Surah 2, verse 2): the two clauses together relocating accountability's border from the community's edge (where verse 75's doctrine drew it) to the deed's own quality—ethics de-tribalised by the Lawgiver's explicit reversal.\n\n\"Surely Allah loves those who are mindful ˹of Him˺\" seals the reversal with the love-clause—and its selection over every available alternative is the verse's crowning doctrine, the scholars draw: the verse could have closed with justice's threat (verse 77 will supply it); it closes instead with divine love: Allah loves the muttaqin—the pledge-keepers and evil-shunners of whatever lineage: the love-formula this Book reserves for its honour-rolls (the good-doers, Surah 2, verse 195; the repentant and self-purifying, Surah 2, verse 222; the patient, this chapter's verse 146 ahead)—here conferred on covenant-fidelity itself: the trustee returning the gentile's coin unwatched stands, by this clause, inside the love of Allah, while the licensed devourer's theology (verse 75) had not even kept him inside His law. The clause's polemical exactness completes the file, the commentators note: the exploitation-doctrine's holders claimed beloved-status by membership (\"we are Allah's children and His most beloved,\" Surah 5, verse 18; the Fire's counted days, verse 24)—and this verse publishes love's actual register: entry by taqwa, the criterion their doctrine had waived precisely where taqwa is tested most: the unwatched trust, the demandless debt, the outsider's unprotected wealth.\n\nThe verse's permanent work is the believer's commercial creed, the scholars press: the Muslim trader, employee, and state hold the gentile's coin—every client's, every citizen's, every treaty-partner's—under this verse's reversed border: no counterparty's creed discounts the trust's sanctity (the Prophet's deposits returned to his persecutors; the hypocrite's mark being betrayal of trust regardless of the trustee's prayers: Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 33); and the verse's two clauses supply the audit-order the file taught: fidelity first (the pledge honoured in the transaction's dark), mindfulness beneath it (the taqwa that powers unwatched fidelity), and the love of Allah over both—while the file's other column, the traders of covenant and oaths for the fleeting price, receives its full sentence in the verse that follows: no share, no speech, no look, no purification—the five-fold deprivation of those who sold what this verse's keepers kept.",
  "77": "\"Indeed, those who trade Allah's covenant and their oaths for a fleeting gain will have no share in the Hereafter\" delivers the trusts-file's terminal sentence—and its charge gathers the file's whole commerce into the Book's master-metaphor of ruinous trade (Surah 2, verse 16's franchise): the merchandise sold is double: Allah's covenant—the charter binding scripture's custodians to publish and follow (Surah 2, verse 40; the concealment-files of verses 71 and 75)—and their own oaths: the sworn word between creatures (the false oath seizing the disputed well, the vendor's perjury moving goods); the price taken: thamanan qalilan—the fleeting gain: the argument's constant valuation (Surah 2, verses 41, 79, 174): whatever the sum, small against what was sold; and the settlement's first clause: no khalaq—no share, no portion in the Hereafter: the seller's account in the only lasting currency zeroed by his own signature.\n\n\"Allah will neither speak to them, nor look at them, nor purify them on the Day of Judgment. And they will suffer a painful punishment\" completes the settlement with the four-fold deprivation this Book reserves for its gravest commercial files (Surah 2, verse 174's parallel over the concealment-traders)—no speech: the Judge's address withheld from those who traded His covenant's speech; no look: the turning-away of the Face from those who turned from His charter; no purification: the cleansing refused to those who refused the truth's cleansing office; and the punishment painful beyond the three deprivations—each clause, the scholars note, measure-for-measure against the crime's anatomy: they monetised the Word, and lose the Address; displayed piety for markets, and lose the Look.\n\nAnd the Sunnah supplies the verse's living occasions, transmitted in the Sahih at this very verse: al-Ash'ath ibn Qays said—\"concerning me this verse was revealed\": a dispute over a well between him and a Jewish neighbour; the Prophet, peace be upon him, asked the claimant for evidence or the defendant's oath, and warned: \"whoever swears a false oath to seize a Muslim's wealth will meet Allah while He is angry with him\"—and the verse descended (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 2356 and 2357; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 138); with its market-twin: \"a man who sets up his goods after the afternoon prayer and swears by Allah he was offered such-and-such for them, so a buyer believes him\"—counted by the Prophet among the three Allah will not speak to nor look at (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 2358's context; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 108's three)—the verse's jurisdiction running, by its own occasions, from the scripture-councils' covenant-trading down to one vendor's sworn lie over an afternoon sale: one crime-family, every scale: the sacred word—Allah's covenant or the creature's oath by Him—converted to pricing instrument.\n\nThe sentence's permanent audit, the commentators press, thus guards the two registers every believer trades in daily: the covenant's custody (the knowledge published unmixed, unhidden—the file's standing charge) and the oath's sanctity (the tongue's \"by Allah\" kept priceless: never spent to move goods, win suits, or dress lies—Surah 2, verse 224's law); and its placement seals the file's two columns forever: verse 76's pledge-keepers inside the love of Allah, this verse's word-sellers outside His speech, His look, and His purifying—the same Day, the same Judge, and between the two columns nothing but what each hand did with the trust and each tongue with the Name.",
  "78": "\"There are some among them who distort the Book with their tongues to make you think this ˹distortion˺ is from the Book—but it is not what the Book says\" adds to the editors' toolkit (verse 71's mixing and hiding) its performance-technique, and the verse's anatomy of it is forensic: layy al-lisan—the twisting of the tongue: the recitation itself bent: passages inflected, glosses voiced in scripture's cadence, human matter delivered in the Book's own performance-register \"to make you think it is from the Book\"—the audience's ear deceived not by an altered page but by an altered voice: forgery migrated from the pen (Surah 2, verse 79's written fraud) to the tongue: undetectable by any manuscript-comparison, for the manuscript was never touched; the fraud lived entirely in the delivery—the counterfeit's most refined edition, and the verse's triple denial tracks its triple layer: it is not from the Book; \"they say, 'It is from Allah'—but it is not from Allah\": the performance's claim escalated from textuality to divine origin, and both claims voided in the Book's own voice.\n\n\"And ˹so˺ they attribute lies to Allah knowingly\" files the performance at the register this argument reserves for it—the same sentence that closed the gentile-exploitation doctrine (verse 75) and the same adverb that has tolled through the whole file (verses 71, 75: \"knowingly\"): the tongue-twisters were not confused reciters but informed performers: they knew, at each bent inflection, which words were the Book's and which their own—the lie's address (\"it is from Allah\") chosen with full knowledge of its falsity; and the crime's classification is thus the Book's gravest: speech about Allah without warrant—the category ranked past even shirk in the prohibition-catalogue (Surah 7, verse 33: \"and that you say about Allah what you do not know\")—here committed above the category's floor: not speech without knowledge but speech against it.\n\nThe verse's permanent audit, the scholars press, reaches every performance-register in which revelation is handled: the technique outlives its first practitioners wherever the sacred text's authority is borrowed for unsacred cargo—the quotation shaved to invert its ruling, the paraphrase delivered as text, the \"the religion says\" that fronts the speaker's preference, the translation bent to a school's convenience: each is the tongue's twisting in its measure, and the community of the final Book holds the heaviest custody against it, the guarantee of the text's own preservation (Surah 15, verse 9) leaving the tongue as the last corruptible instrument: the words beyond reach, the voicing and framing still biddable—whence the Prophet's own fearsome fence, peace be upon him: \"whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 110; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 3), and the community's unique sciences—the chains, the verification, the transmitted recitations sealed generation to generation—standing as this verse's institutional answer: a fraud that lives in delivery is met by a tradition that certifies deliverers. The file now rises to its summit-question: whether any prophet—any bearer of Book, wisdom, and office—could himself have asked for the worship the distorters' theologies assigned: \"it is not appropriate for someone whom Allah has blessed with the Scripture... to say to people: worship me instead of Allah.\"",
  "79": "\"It is not appropriate for someone who Allah has blessed with the Scripture, wisdom, and prophethood to say to people, 'Worship me instead of Allah'\" carries the disputation to its summit-question—whether any prophet could himself have solicited the worship later theologies assigned him—and the verse's instrument is the category-impossibility: \"it is not appropriate\"—ma kana: it is not, could not be, for a human being given Allah's three gifts—the Book, the wisdom-judgment, the prophethood-office—to then say \"worship me instead of Allah\": the office's very nature excluding the claim, the scholars unfold: prophethood is servanthood at its summit (Surah 2, verse 23's \"Our servant\" as the Prophet's highest title); its whole commission is worship's redirection to the Sender; a prophet soliciting worship would be the courier consuming the cargo—the contradiction not moral only but constitutional: the gifts listed are precisely what make the claim impossible in their holder. The verse's occasion, the commentators transmit, was the question put directly: men of the delegations asked the Prophet, peace be upon him, whether he sought of them what the communities had given their prophets' memories—\"shall we worship you?\"—and the verse descended with the category's answer: no bearer of the three gifts ever asked it; whatever worship-doctrines stand in prophets' names were built after them, without them, against them (Isa's own disavowal at the assembly: \"I only told them what You ordered me: worship Allah, my Lord and your Lord,\" Surah 5, verse 117).\n\n\"Rather, he would say, 'Be devoted to the worship of your Lord ˹alone˺'—in accordance with what these prophets read in the Scripture and what they taught\" states what the office-holders actually say, and its key term became the community's own honour-word: rabbaniyyin—\"be devoted to your Lord\": be Lord's-people: rabbanis: scholars-and-worshippers whose learning and living alike are attached to the Rabb—the verse deriving the title from its source: the prophet's summons makes people Allah's, never his own; and the clause's two credentials—\"what you read in the Scripture and what you taught\"—ground the summons in the office's daily work, the scholars draw: the teaching and studying of revelation is itself the deification-proof vocation: whoever actually reads what the prophets brought, and teaches it as brought, finds every page assigning worship upward (verse 51's creed on Isa's own tongue); the deification-doctrines required, precisely, the reading's abandonment—the mixing, hiding, and tongue-twisting of the preceding file (verses 71 and 78)—for no honestly-read scripture ever yielded them.\n\nThe verse's permanent legislation, the commentators press, reaches every venerating community, this Book's included: the prohibited claim's soft forms—the teacher exalted past correction, the saint's tomb petitioned, the scholar obeyed against the revelation he was honoured for carrying (verse 64's lords-besides-Allah; Adi ibn Hatim's exegesis: Jami` at-Tirmidhi, Hadith 3095)—all stand under this verse's category-bar: whatever devotion any honoured servant would refuse to solicit, his honourers may not assign; the Prophet, peace be upon him, fencing his own community at the exact point: \"do not extol me as the Christians extolled the son of Maryam; I am only a servant—so say: the servant of Allah and His Messenger\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3445); and the rabbani-title stands as the verse's positive bequest: the community's learned commanded to make Lord's-people, not followings—their success measured, by this verse's grammar, in disciples attached past them to the Rabb they read and taught.",
  "80": "\"And he would never ask you to take angels and prophets as lords\" extends verse 79's category-bar from the prophet's own person to the whole created hierarchy—and the extension closes the deification-file's last exits, the scholars note: the office-holder who could never solicit worship for himself (verse 79's impossibility) equally never brokers it elsewhere: not to the angels—the exit the pagan Arabs took (the daughters-of-Allah cult, Surah 16, verse 57; the intercessory angel-worship Surah 53, verse 26 voids), and not to the prophets—the exit the scripture-communities took (the Messiah enthroned, Uzair entitled, Surah 9, verse 30's file): arbab—lords: the plural naming the whole economy of graded divinities the ancient religions ran: the supreme God acknowledged above a tier of worshipped intermediaries—the system every prophet was sent to dismantle, and which this verse rules no prophet could have installed: the summons's whole content being the tiers' removal (\"worship Him alone,\" verse 51), a prophet assigning lords beneath Allah would be the demolisher building what he was sent against.\n\n\"Would he ask you to disbelieve after you have submitted?\" closes the file with the question that names the assignment's true register—and its naming is the verse's verdict, the commentators draw: taking angels and prophets as lords is kufr: disbelief—not devotion's excess, not veneration's warm overflow, but faith's exit: the question's structure (\"after you have submitted\") measuring the loss: the intermediary-lords' installation undoes the islam itself—the submission of verse 19's one Way, the disciples' pledge (verse 52), the common-terms summons (verse 64's third clause: \"nor take one another as lords instead of Allah\")—for submission's whole grammar is directness: the servant before his Lord without enthroned brokers (Surah 2, verse 186's \"I am truly near\": the nearness that leaves no vacancy for the tiers); and the question's rhetorical form carries the office-holders' honour: would he—the prophet you venerate—ask your disbelief? The venerated themselves are the doctrine's first objectors: Isa at the assembly disavowing (Surah 5, verse 116's interrogation), the angels at theirs (\"it was not us they worshipped,\" Surah 34, verses 40 and 41's scene): every enthroned intermediary, produced in court, testifies against the throne.\n\nThe two-verse unit (79 and 80), standing back, is the Book's complete constitution of religious veneration, the scholars press—and its two lines hold every case: honour without ceiling's breach: the prophets loved past all mankind (the faith's own condition: Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 15), the angels revered, the righteous cherished—the whole register of love this Book commands; and worship without partners: the honour's currency never crossing into the devotion—prayer, prostration, ultimate reliance, legislative obedience (verse 64's exegesis)—that submission reserved; and the unit's permanent audit falls, as the file's always does, on the venerating community's own practices: whatever petition, prostration, or absolute deference any honoured creature would himself refuse—\"would he ask you to disbelieve?\"—his community may not offer; the test of every devotion being the devotee's imagined presence: the Prophet who said \"I am only a servant\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3445) standing, in that sentence, as this verse's living commentary. The constitution fixed, the chapter now unveils its deepest charter: the covenant taken from the prophets themselves—every office-holder sworn, before his sending, to believe in and support the confirming messenger to come.",
  "81": "\"˹Remember˺ when Allah made a covenant with the prophets, ˹saying,˺ 'Now that I have given you the Book and wisdom, if there comes to you a messenger confirming what you have, you must believe in him and support him'\" unveils the chapter's deepest charter—the covenant above all covenants: taken not from communities (Surah 2, verse 83's register) but from the prophets themselves: every office-holder, at the office's conferral (\"now that I have given you the Book and wisdom\"), sworn to a standing clause: should the confirming messenger come in your time, belief in him and support of him are your oath's own terms; and the clause's referent, the commentators transmit from the early authorities (Ali and Ibn Abbas's readings, gathered by at-Tabari and Ibn Kathir): the final Messenger—Muhammad, peace be upon him: the covenant binding every prophet, were he alive at the coming, to follow and aid him—the prophets' own ranks sworn in advance to the seal of their line; whence the Prophet's own statement of the charter's force: \"were Musa alive, he would have no choice but to follow me\" (recorded in Musnad Ahmad and the Sunan's supports; the verse itself the doctrine's ground), and Isa's return-role its standing future instance: the raised prophet descending to judge by this Book's law (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3448)—the covenant of this verse executed, at history's end, in person.\n\n\"He added, 'Do you affirm this covenant and accept this commitment?' They said, 'Yes, we do.' Allah said, 'Then bear witness, and I too am a Witness'\" records the ratification with treaty-formality unmatched in the Book, the scholars note: the terms read, the assent asked—\"do you affirm... and accept My burden upon you?\"—the isr-word: the binding load: the covenant's weight named in the asking; the prophets' unanimous \"we affirm\"; and the witnessing sealed in three registers: the prophets witnesses over one another, and Allah the Witness over all: the instrument executed with the completeness this Book demands of human contracts (Surah 2, verse 282's scribes and witnesses) enacted at the covenant-registry's summit—and the ratification's function in the living disputation is its edge, the commentators draw: the scripture-communities resisted the final Prophet in their prophets' names—loyalty to Musa, devotion to Isa, the banners of verse 65's contest; this verse produces the principals' own signatures: Musa and Isa stand sworn, by recorded oath, to the very belief-and-support their claimed loyalists withheld: the rejection of Muhammad, peace be upon him, thus violating not merely his mission but their own masters' ratified covenant—the loyalists opposing, in their prophets' names, what their prophets swore.\n\nThe charter's permanent doctrine, standing back, completes the Book's architecture of prophethood, the scholars press: the office is one corps under one standing order—no prophet a rival franchise, each sworn to the confirmation-chain's continuity (verse 3's confirming Book, Surah 2, verse 41's charge) and to its seal; whence the community's creed holds the corps whole (\"we make no distinction,\" Surah 2, verse 285) and its love for every prophet routes through obedience to the one they were all sworn to support; and the covenant's shadow-side follows immediately: \"whoever turns back after this\"—the next verse's sentence on all who, informed of the ratified charter, maintain the rejection the principals forswore: \"they will be the rebellious.\"",
  "82": "\"Whoever turns back after this, they will be the rebellious\" attaches to the prophets' ratified charter (verse 81) its enforcement-clause—and each term carries the treaty-register's weight, the scholars unfold. \"Whoever turns back\": tawalla—the turning-away this argument has tracked at every station (Surah 2, verse 137; verse 63 of this chapter): here specifically the covenant's repudiation: the refusal of the belief-and-support the charter swore to the confirming Messenger—committed not by the prophets (their \"we affirm\" stands recorded) but by the claiming communities: whoever, holding the prophets' names, declines the prophets' sworn terms. \"After this\": the aggravation-clause in its most formal instance—after the covenant's own text has been published: the turning-away of this verse is repudiation with the instrument in hand: the ratification recited (verse 81), the signatures produced, the witnesses named—and the rejection maintained; the clause performing, for the covenant-charter, what \"after knowledge\" has performed throughout (verse 19's disputing, Surah 2, verse 145's warning): the removal of every plea of ignorance from the file.\n\n\"They will be the rebellious\" delivers the classification, and its term is the argument's precise instrument: al-fasiqun—the rebellious: those who break out of bounds (the word's root in the breaking-forth; its file at Surah 2, verse 26: \"He leaves none to stray except the rebellious\"; verse 27's covenant-violators): the classification chosen over every available alternative, the commentators note, for its juridical exactness: the turner-back after the charter's publication is not filed first as mistaken (the evidence forecloses it), nor merely as disbelieving (though the state may amount to it), but as covenant-breaker: fisq: the exit from a ratified bond—the community's rejection of the final Prophet re-described, by this clause, from a doctrinal position into a treaty-violation: their own prophets' treaty, their own claimed lineage's signatures; the deepest re-framing the disputation receives: the argument was never Islam against the earlier allegiances but the earlier allegiances' own terms against their claimants.\n\nThe clause's permanent jurisprudence, standing back, serves the Book's whole covenant-architecture, the scholars press: ratified charters bind the downstream—the prophets' oath (verse 81) obligates their communities as the fathers' covenant obligated the children (Surah 2, verse 40's \"fulfil your covenant\"); publication perfects liability—\"after this\" marking the moment excuse dies: the standing rule of every file since Eden's announced tree (Surah 2, verse 35) and the Sabbath's published terms (Surah 2, verse 65); and classification follows conduct—the rebellious are made by the turning, not the lineage: the same communities' faithful (the Abdullah ibn Salams, verse 199's coming honour-roll) standing wholly outside the clause they were born beside. And the believer's own audit closes the file, the commentators draw: the community of the final covenant holds its charter under the same enforcement-grammar—the Book published, the Messenger's terms ratified at every profession of faith (\"we hear and obey,\" Surah 2, verse 285), and the turning-back after this as available to its members as to any before them (Surah 47, verse 38's warning: \"if you turn away, He will replace you with another people\"); the clause's mirror, as ever, facing the reader before the neighbour—while the next verse lifts the whole question to its cosmic register: \"do they desire a way other than Allah's—while all those in the heavens and the earth submit to His Will, willingly or unwillingly?\"",
  "83": "\"Do they desire a way other than Allah's\" lifts the covenant-file's conclusion (verses 81 and 82) to its cosmic register—and the question's astonishment is its argument, the scholars unfold: the turners-back (verse 82) were, in the deepest description, way-shoppers: seeking a din other than Allah's religion (verse 19's \"Allah's only Way is Islam\"; verse 85's coming sentence): and the question holds the project up against the universe it proposes to secede from—\"other than Allah's\"?—the alternative-seeking exposed as the one cosmically impossible ambition.\n\n\"Knowing that all those in the heavens and the earth submit to His Will, willingly or unwillingly\" supplies the exposure's evidence, and its key term is the chapter's own master-word at maximum scope: aslama—submitted: to Him submits everyone in the heavens and the earth: the islam-verb predicated of the cosmos entire; and the two adverbs complete the census: taw'an—willingly: the angels' obedience, the believers' chosen surrender, the creation's worshipful compliance (\"there is not a single thing that does not glorify His praises,\" Surah 17, verse 44); wa-karhan—or unwillingly: the rebel's own biology submitted while his creed rebels: his heartbeat on the Sustainer's schedule (verse 2's al-Qayyum), his birth and death undecreed by him (Surah 2, verse 28's five stations), his very plotting enclosed in the Plan (verse 54); the disbeliever, the scholars draw the verse's point, is not outside islam's jurisdiction but divided within it: a submitted body carrying an unsubmitted heart—the \"way other than Allah's\" he desires being unavailable at every level except the one he is tested on: the will's assent; whence the question's force: the way-shopper seeks exit from a kingdom that holds his own pulse.\n\n\"And to Him they will ˹all˺ be returned\" closes the question with the terminus that ends the shopping absolutely: the return—the Book's constant horizon (Surah 2, verse 285's community-confession; verse 55's settlement-docket)—stated here as the cosmic submission's final act: the willing and unwilling alike carried back to the One from whom the alternative-ways promised distance; every rival din, whatever its architecture, terminating at the same Court as the way it rivalled—the difference between the ways lying, at the return, only in the arriving state: the willing submitter received as the Guardian's client (Surah 2, verse 257), the unwilling delivered as the fugitive retrieved.\n\nThe verse's permanent teaching, standing back, is the believer's deepest orientation, the commentators press: islam—the chosen submission—is not one religious option within a neutral cosmos but the conscious joining of the universe's own standing practice: the believer's prayer aligning his will with what his cells, the stars, and the seasons already perform (Surah 22, verse 18's prostration-census); his surrender thus the creature's only consistency—the unbeliever's the only anomaly: a will out of step with its own body's citizenship; and the question's mirror faces every partial submitter within the community too: whoever holds provinces back from the surrender (Surah 2, verse 208's \"enter into Islam wholeheartedly\") is running, in those provinces, the same impossible secession this verse astonishes at—desiring, in his exempted corners, a way other than Allah's, while his breath, in those very corners, submits on schedule. The cosmic census taken, the chapter answers it with the community's creed recited in full—the next verse's \"we believe in Allah and what has been revealed to us...\"—the willing submission's formal declaration, sounded against the census's background: the creed of those in step with the universe.",
  "84": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ 'We believe in Allah and what has been revealed to us and what was revealed to Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, and his descendants; and what was given to Moses, Jesus, and other prophets from their Lord'\" answers the cosmic submission-census (verse 83) with the willing submitters' formal declaration—the community's creed recited in full, and its text is Surah 2, verse 136's charter re-sounded with the disputation's whole weight now behind it, the scholars note: the same roll—Allah first; the revelation to this community; the patriarchal chain with Ismail seated in it (the seating that voids the line's ethnic monopoly, Surah 2, verse 133's deathbed-census); Musa and Isa named and honoured equally—the two prophets whose banners the delegations flew against the creed that honours them; and the open clause: \"and other prophets from their Lord\"—the whole corps embraced (Surah 16, verse 36's universal sending), the one-Sender grammar (\"from their Lord\") grounding the embrace: the creed's breadth being tawhid's own arithmetic: one Lord, therefore one chain, therefore no exclusions.\n\nThe recitation's placement here—after the prophets' covenant (verse 81), the rebellion-clause (verse 82), and the cosmic census (verse 83)—re-charges each clause with the passage's findings, the commentators draw: \"we make no distinction between any of them\" now reads as the covenant's own compliance: the community declining, in its creed's text, the distinction-making that constituted the turning-back (verse 82's file: belief in some, rejection of the confirmed one—Surah 4, verse 150's \"true disbelievers\")—the Muslims, by their charter's wording, the only community structurally incapable of the crime the prophets' covenant forbade: their creed affirms every signatory of verse 81's instrument, and the Messenger the instrument swore support to; and the closing clause—\"and to Him we ˹fully˺ submit\"—now reads against verse 83's census: nahnu lahu muslimun: we are, to Him, submitters: the willing column of the cosmic register formally joined: the creed's last word being the universe's standing practice (verse 83's willingly-or-unwillingly) chosen, declared, and made the community's name.\n\nThe creed's double sounding in the Book—here and at Surah 2, verse 136, near-verbatim—is itself doctrine, the scholars press: the Quran's most repeated formal declaration is its most inclusive one: the community made to memorise, in its charter's own text, the honour of every prophet the rival communities claimed against it—the polemical seasons (this chapter's whole occasion) never permitted to shrink the creed by one name; the Prophet, peace be upon him, reciting these very charter-verses in the dawn prayer's units (Sahih Muslim, Hadith 727's report on Surah 2, verse 136's twin), the day opened daily on the full roll; and the declaration's function at this exact station is the chapter's gathering finale, the commentators note: the disputation has run its course—the narratives told, the analogies delivered, the summons issued and declined, the covenant unveiled; and the community's closing word is neither triumph nor curse but the creed itself, recited whole: the answer of verse 20's script (\"I have submitted myself to Allah, and so have my followers\") now expanded to its full text—after which the Book states, in the next verse, the sentence the whole architecture has established: \"whoever seeks a way other than Islam, it will never be accepted from them.\"",
  "85": "\"Whoever seeks a way other than Islam, it will never be accepted from them\" delivers the sentence the chapter's whole architecture has established—and its terms must be held with the precision the architecture built, the scholars insist. \"Islam\" here is the word the chapter has defined at every station: the one religion of the one God (verse 19: \"Allah's only Way is Islam\")—the submission of Ibrahim (verse 67), the disciples' pledge (verse 52), the cosmic census's willing column (verse 83), now in its completed, final-covenant form under the Messenger every prophet swore to support (verse 81); and \"whoever seeks a way other\" is the way-shopper of verse 83's question: the deliberate preference of another din after this one's clarity—the sentence thus falling not on the unreached (whose case rests with the justice that \"does not wrong anyone,\" Surah 2, verse 281, and punishes none before the messenger's arrival, Surah 17, verse 15) but on the reached-and-refusing: the turning-back after the covenant's publication (verse 82's file). \"It will never be accepted\": the rejection is of the way, not the person's petitions generally—the alternative din, whatever its devotions' sincerity or its architecture's beauty, is returned undeposited: for acceptance was always the Owner's prerogative (Surah 2, verse 127's builders begging it), and He has named what He accepts (Surah 5, verse 3: \"I am pleased with Islam as your religion\").\n\n\"And in the Hereafter they will be among the losers\" completes the sentence in the Book's terminal accounting-word: al-khasirin—the losers: the chapter's and the argument's category for ruined trade (Surah 2, verse 27's covenant-breakers; verse 16's guidance-sellers): the way-shopper's transaction audited at the return (verse 83's terminus): he traded the accepted way for an unaccepted one—devotion's whole capital spent on a rejected deposit: no loss in the ledgers exceeds it (Surah 39, verse 15's definition: \"the true losers are those who will lose themselves\").\n\nThe verse's relationship to the Book's mercy-texts must be stated as the scholars state it, for its misreading in both directions is perennial: it does not void Surah 2, verse 62's charter—the two verses adjudicate the same rule from two sides: every era's people judged by the revelation binding in their era (the Jew truly following Musa before the next sending, the Christian truly following Isa—muslims of their hours, verse 52's disciples the exhibit); and once the final sending arrives and reaches a person (the Prophet's own boundary: \"no one of this nation hears of me—Jew or Christian—then dies without believing in that with which I was sent, except that he will be among the people of the Fire,\" Sahih Muslim, Hadith 153), the binding revelation is this one: the verse's sentence operating exactly at that boundary, neither before it nor beyond it. Nor is the sentence tribal: \"Islam\" in its clause is the substance—the submission with its creed and covenant—not the demographic label: the label-holder without the substance stands warned by the whole argument (Surah 2, verse 111's demolished monopolies applying to every community, this one included), and the substance-seeker is met by the Way's own openness: \"whoever submits themselves to Allah and does good will have their reward with their Lord\" (Surah 2, verse 112). The sentence published, the chapter turns to its hardest file: those who held the substance and abandoned it—\"how will Allah guide a people who disbelieved after they had believed?\"",
  "86": "\"How will Allah guide a people who chose to disbelieve after they had believed, acknowledged the Messenger to be true, and received clear proofs?\" opens the apostasy-file—the chapter's hardest—and the question's construction is its jurisprudence, the scholars unfold: the case defined by its three completed stages: they believed—the faith real, professed and lived; they \"acknowledged the Messenger to be true\"—shahidu: bore witness: the truthfulness of the Messenger personally attested, not merely inherited; and they \"received clear proofs\"—the evidences delivered in full (the aggravation-clause's final form: not after knowledge merely but after witnessed, professed, proof-established faith); and then the chosen exit: kafaru ba'da imanihim—disbelief after belief: the apostasy this Book files as rejection's most aggravated grade, for every lesser rejecter retains some plea—distance, inheritance, the unexamined life—while the apostate's file contains his own past testimony against him: he is the witness who attested and then denied his own attestation. \"How will Allah guide\" states the case's consequence in the question-form that is its precision: guidance's withholding from such a people is not arbitrary decree but the case's own logic—guidance was given, inhabited, and evicted; the question asks, in effect, by what door guidance should re-enter a house whose owner expelled it from within (Surah 2, verse 17's extinguished light; verse 108's \"whoever trades belief for disbelief has truly strayed\").\n\n\"For Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people\" closes the question with the rule it instantiates—the clause this argument has sounded at every hardened file (verse 58 of the second surah's neighbours; Surah 2, verse 258's dumbstruck king)—and its naming of the apostates as \"the wrongdoing people\" performs the file's classification, the commentators note: apostasy-after-proofs is zulm—the misplacement-crime at its summit (Surah 2, verse 35's definition; Surah 31, verse 13's \"worst injustice\"): truth known, witnessed, and then placed beneath the very falsehood it had been seen to refute; and the rule's grammar preserves the Book's exact justice: guidance is withheld from the wrongdoing as chosen state, not from the wronged-and-returning—the file's own sequel (verse 89: \"except those who repent afterwards\") standing two verses ahead as the rule's published exception.\n\nThe file's occasion, transmitted by the commentators, gives the passage its living stakes—and its mercy: the reports (at-Tabari and Ibn Kathir gathering them) name a man of the Ansar, al-Harith ibn Suwaid among the named, who apostatised and left for Makkah, then repented and sent to his people asking whether return remained possible; and these verses descended—the file's whole arc, question through exception—carried to him, and he returned to Islam and was received: the passage thus revealed, in its own first application, as apostasy's map with the exit marked: the severity of this verse's question real (the coming verses will price the unrepented file at its full weight: the curse, the unaccepted ransom), and the door of verse 89 real beside it—the two together being this Book's constant architecture (Surah 2, verses 159 and 160's paired curse and exit): no crime minimised, no criminal abandoned while breath remains; and the question this verse leaves suspended—how will Allah guide?—receives, in the repenting Harith, its recorded answer: by the returning themselves walking back through the door the next verses hold open.",
  "87": "\"Their reward is that they will be condemned by Allah, the angels, and all of humanity\" prices the unrepented apostasy-file (verse 86's case: disbelief after witnessed faith and delivered proofs)—and each term of the sentence carries the file's exact weight, the scholars unfold. \"Their reward\": jaza'uhum—the wage: the sentence framed in earnings-language (Surah 2, verse 134's ledgers): the curse not arbitrary affliction but the case's own yield—the apostate traded attested truth for chosen denial (verse 86's completed stages), and the trade's market-price is here published: the recompense of those who sold their own testimony. \"Condemned by Allah, the angels, and all of humanity\": the universal curse—la'nah: the banishment from mercy—in the same three-register formula this argument stamped on the sealed disbelievers at Surah 2, verse 161 (the dying-in-denial file) and on the concealers at Surah 2, verse 159: Allah's condemnation the substance (exclusion from the rahmah that encompasses everything, Surah 7, verse 156—forfeited by the only crime that evicts it from within); the angels' the unseen realm's concurrence (the same court that prays for the repentant, Surah 40, verse 7, confirming the verdict on the unrepenting); and humanity's—\"all of humanity\"—the census the commentators unfolded at its parallel (Surah 2, verse 161's commentary): the believers cursing knowingly, and on the Day the whole assembly joining—the apostate's own former community, his misled following, even his fellow-deniers (Surah 7, verse 38's mutual cursing of the damned): no register of existence, seen or unseen, dissenting from the sentence.\n\nThe formula's fit to this file's specific crime deserves the pause the scholars gave it: the apostate-after-witness had himself been a member of all three registers' cause—his faith once joined to Allah's covenant, his testimony once ranked with the angels' and the knowledge-people's own attestation (verse 18's summit-witness: the shahadah he professed enrolled him among its bearers), his profession once counted in believing humanity's census; the universal condemnation is thus his own former memberships inverted: every register he attested from now attests against him—the witness-structure of verse 18 (Allah, the angels, the people of knowledge testifying to the creed) returning upon the man who bore that witness and then denounced it: measure for measure at the cosmic scale (Surah 2, verse 194's law operating in the courts of testimony).\n\nThe sentence's placement holds the file's architecture taut, the commentators press: this verse and the next (the eternal, unlightened, undelayed abiding—Surah 2, verse 162's exact formula returning) price the file at its maximum precisely so that verse 89's exception may operate at its full mercy: \"except those who repent afterwards and mend their ways\"—the door held open in the sentence's very shadow (the Harith ibn Suwaid occasion, verse 86's commentary: the apostate's return received); the Book's constant paired architecture (Surah 2, verses 159 and 160) standing here at its severest instance: the curse of all existence published, and beside it, two verses on, the two names that outlast every curse for the returning: \"then surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\"",
  "88": "\"They will be in Hell forever. Their punishment will not be lightened, nor will they be delayed ˹from it˺\" executes the universal condemnation of verse 87 into its abode and its terms—and the sentence's text is, word for word, the formula this argument sealed on the dying-in-denial file at Surah 2, verse 162: the repetition across the two chapters is the Book's own cross-referencing, the scholars note: the unrepented apostate (verse 86's case, carried to death—for the file's governing condition stands at Surah 2, verse 161: disbelief and death upon it) joins the sealed file at its exact terms: abiding \"in it\"—in the curse and its abode together (the earlier commentary's reading)—forever: the eternity that answers a rejection eternal in resolve (Surah 2, verse 39's doctrine; Surah 6, verse 28's certified reversion).\n\nThe two negations then close the two hopes every sentenced soul reaches for, as the parallel's commentary unfolded and this file sharpens: \"not lightened\"—no amelioration: the pleas for one day's easing refused (Surah 40, verse 49), the adaptation engineered out (Surah 4, verse 56's renewal)—and for this file specifically, the scholars note the fit: the apostate's crime was itself a self-granted lightening: the faith's demands found heavy (the persecution, the discipline, the community's costs) and shed for relief; the relief-seeker's eternal terms contain no relief. \"Nor will they be delayed\": no postponement, no adjournment between verdict and execution—and again the fit: the apostasy was itself a gamble on delay (the exits staged, by verse 72's scheme-transcript, on the calculation that return or ruin could be managed on the apostate's own schedule); the Day's terms confiscate the calendar: all delay was spent in the world, where the door of verse 89 stood open every remaining hour (Surah 35, verse 37's audit: \"did We not give you lives long enough...?\").\n\nThe verse's function in the file's architecture is the pause the commentators mark: between the condemnation (verse 87) and the exception (verse 89), the sentence stands at full, unrelieved length—no softening clause within it, the mercy deliberately withheld from this verse's text so that it may arrive, in the next verse's opening word (\"except...\"), as what mercy actually is: not the sentence's dilution but the sentenced one's exit: the Book's paired architecture (Surah 2, verses 159 and 160; verse 87's commentary) requiring the severity whole precisely so the door beside it is seen for what it is—and the reader formed by the pairing holds the file rightly: the apostasy priced at eternity, unlightened and undelayed; and the price payable by no one who turns before the seal (the Harith ibn Suwaid return, verse 86's occasion): the sentence's whole terror aimed, like every terror in this Book, at the living—for whom \"afterwards\" (verse 89's first word) has not yet closed.",
  "89": "\"As for those who repent afterwards and mend their ways\" opens, in the very shadow of the file's maximum sentence (verses 87 and 88: the universal curse, the eternal unlightened abiding), the door this Book holds open at every severity's side—and its first word is the architecture: illa—\"except\": the exception clause arriving directly upon the condemnation's text, the pairing of Surah 2, verses 159 and 160 (the concealers' curse, then \"except those who repent\") re-enacted for the apostasy-file at its own scale: no crime in the Book's registers, this placement teaches, out-runs the exception—the man condemned by Allah, the angels, and all humanity (verse 87) walks out of the entire sentence through one conditional clause. Its two conditions carry the repentance-jurisprudence this argument has built: \"repent\"—taba: the return: the inward turning: remorse, cessation, resolve (Surah 2, verse 160's first condition)—for this file, the disbelief itself renounced: the attested faith re-attested; \"afterwards\"—min ba'di dhalika: after that: after the apostasy, after the curse's publication: the clause's quiet mercy being its lack of any statute of limitation: the \"afterwards\" running to the last unsealed breath (the death-rattle boundary: Jami` at-Tirmidhi, Hadith 3537); and \"mend their ways\"—aslahu: the outward repair (Surah 2, verse 160's second condition): the conduct reformed, the return lived—and, the scholars note for this file's specific shape, the damage addressed: the apostasy that was public (verse 72's staged exits) repaired publicly: the return declared as the departure was, the misled undeceived (the declaration-condition of Surah 2, verse 160's third clause operating by the crime's own publicity).\n\n\"Then surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful\" completes the exception with the two names—and their arrival at this file's exit is the Book's mercy-architecture at its most deliberate, the commentators press: the names that received Adam at the first repentance (Surah 2, verse 37), Israel after the calf (Surah 2, verse 54), the cursed concealers at their exit (Surah 2, verse 160), and the warring persecutors at theirs (Surah 2, verse 192) here receive the apostate—the criminal whose file carried the universal curse; the pattern's completed census being its doctrine: these names are not reserved for sympathetic sinners (Surah 2, verse 160's commentary): they are who He is, toward whoever meets the conditions, from whatever depth. And the file's recorded occasion is the exception's living exhibit (verse 86's commentary): al-Harith ibn Suwaid, apostatised and departed, sending from Makkah to ask whether return remained—and this passage descending as the answer: carried to him, received by him, his return accepted by the community at revelation's own instruction: the verse's first application being an actual apostate actually walking back through it.\n\nThe exception's placement completes the passage's whole pedagogy, the scholars draw: verses 86 through 89 form a single instrument—the case defined, the curse published, the sentence priced at eternity, the door opened—and the instrument's design serves the living reader at both edges: the believer tempted toward the exits (the staged-apostasy pressures of verse 72's season; every era's costs of remaining) reads the file's price before the step; and the departed reads, before despair completes what apostasy began, that the Book's severest file ends—as every file in this argument has ended for the returning—at the same two names; while the next verse will draw the exception's own boundary: the repentance that never forms—the disbelief increased upon disbelief until the turning itself dies.",
  "90": "\"Indeed, those who disbelieve after having believed then increase in disbelief, their repentance will never be accepted\" draws the boundary of the exception just opened (verse 89)—and the case it fences must be read with the scholars' care, for it seems, at first hearing, to close what verse 89 opened. The file: disbelief after faith—verse 86's apostasy—\"then increase in disbelief\": the departure compounded: the apostate not pausing at his exit but building upon it: rejection deepened, the campaign joined (the commentators connecting the clause to those whose apostasy matured into active war on the faith—the scheme-operators of verse 72, the Makkan apostates arming against the community), each stage hardening the last (Surah 2, verse 10's increased sickness; the black-spots mechanism of the heart-hadith: Jami` at-Tirmidhi, Hadith 3334). \"Their repentance will never be accepted\": the clause the scholars aligned with the Book's whole repentance-law, and the alignment yields its meaning: not that a true return from such men would be refused—for verse 89's door and Surah 39, verse 53's \"Allah forgives all sins\" stand unrepealed, and the law's boundary is death, not depth (Surah 4, verse 18's dying-moment limit)—but that from the increasing, the true return does not form: the repentance this clause voids is the kind such trajectories produce: the deathbed reversal after the increase has spent the life (Pharaoh's drowning confession, Surah 10, verses 90 and 91: the model case), the tactical recantation when the campaign fails, the regret without renunciation—turnings of circumstance, not of heart: unaccepted because they are not repentance; the increase-clause thus describing, the scholars conclude, the state in which the organ of turning itself corrodes: the man who compounds rejection daily is not refused the door: he loses, by his own compounding, the legs that walk to it (Surah 2, verse 18's \"they will never return\").\n\n\"It is they who are astray\" closes the boundary with the classification, and its finality is the file's terminal register: hum ad-dallun—the astray: the Fatihah's own last word (Surah 1, verse 7: \"those who are astray\") assigned as settled identity: not passing through error but resident in it: the wandering-blind state of Surah 2, verse 15 arrived at its address; and the classification's placement between verse 89's open door and verse 91's unaccepted ransom maps the file's whole geography, the commentators draw: three cases in three verses—the apostate who repents (89: received, whatever his file), the apostate who increases (90: the turning dying within him), and the apostate who dies upon it (91: the seal, past all ransom)—the map's mercy being its motion-clause: the cases are not castes but trajectories, and the traveller on the second road remains, until the seal, one true turning from the first (the Harith-precedent standing); while the map's warning is the second case's physics: increase has momentum: every day of compounded rejection prices the return higher and the returner weaker—whence the Book's urgency-doctrine over every open door (Surah 57, verse 16's \"has the time not yet come...?\"): the turning delayed is the turning endangered, and \"it is they who are astray\" is the endangerment's completed form.",
  "91": "\"Indeed, if each of those who disbelieve then die as disbelievers were to offer a ransom of enough gold to fill the whole world, it would never be accepted from them\" completes the apostasy-file's three-case map (verses 89 and 90's commentary) at its terminal case—the seal: disbelief carried to death: the double condition this argument fixed at Surah 2, verse 161 (the crime and the dying upon it) here priced in the Book's most concrete currency-image: \"the earth's fill of gold\"—the planet packed with treasure, offered as fidyah: ransom: and refused; the image's arithmetic being its theology, the scholars unfold: the sum named is the maximum the human imagination can denominate—all wealth that ever was or could be—and the refusal is categorical: \"it would never be accepted\": for the Day's court, as this argument established at its foundations, deals in no currency but the soul's own record (Surah 2, verse 48's closed doors: no ransom, no intercession, no help; verse 254's \"no bargaining\"): wealth was the world's instrument, left at the world's border (Surah 26, verse 88: \"the Day when neither wealth nor children will benefit\"), and the sealed file faces its audit with the one asset it refused to bring: the faith.\n\nAnd the Sunnah stages the verse's scene in the Day's own transcript, the commentators set beside it: the Prophet, peace be upon him, reported that the disbeliever will be brought on the Day of Judgment and asked: \"If you had the earth's fill of gold, would you ransom yourself with it?\" He will say yes—and it will be said to him: \"You were asked for what is far easier than that\"—the tawhid, the association of nothing with Allah, asked of him in the world—and he refused (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 6538; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2805): the hadith the verse's exact commentary: the ransom-offer's tragedy being not its refusal but its redundancy: the price of salvation had been set, in the world, at a sentence—the shahadah's costless word—and the man now offering planets of gold declined, in his lifetime, the easier payment; the scholars drawing the scene's permanent lesson: the Day's exchange-rates are the world's inverted: here, faith is asked and gold is hoarded; there, gold is offered and faith alone is legal tender—and the only hour at which the tender can be acquired is the hour already spent.\n\n\"It is they who will suffer a painful punishment, and they will have no helpers\" closes the file—and the passage—with the sentence's two standing clauses: the punishment painful (the sealed file's terms already published at verse 88: unlightened, undelayed), and \"no helpers\": the no-helpers clause this argument stamps on every terminal account (Surah 2, verse 270; verse 22 of this chapter; verse 56)—here closing the apostasy-passage's whole arc: the man who left the community of helpers (the believers, whose Guardian is Allah: verse 68), increased in the leaving (verse 90), and died outside (this verse), stands at the audit unransomable and unaccompanied: every register that condemned him (verse 87's three-fold curse) present, and none to plead. The file sealed, the chapter turns—with the pivot this Book always executes from warning to works—to the living's own ledger: \"you will never achieve righteousness until you donate some of what you cherish\": the door out of every dark file being, as ever, the deed still available to the reader holding the page.",
  "92": "\"You will never achieve righteousness until you donate some of what you cherish\" pivots the chapter from the sealed files (verses 86 to 91) to the living reader's open ledger—and the pivot's instrument is the Book's most searching single sentence on giving: al-birr—righteousness: the comprehensive piety this argument defined at its full length in Surah 2, verse 177's syllabus (faith, charity from cherished wealth, prayer, fidelity, patience)—is declared unreachable: \"you will never attain it\"—until the giving crosses one specific line: \"some of what you cherish\": mimma tuhibbun: from what you love; the criterion relocating charity's measure from the gift's size to the giver's attachment: the surplus shed painlessly (the worthless picked out for donation, Surah 2, verse 267's audit) purchases no entry here; birr's door is priced in the currency of parting's cost—for the deed's whole function, the scholars unfold, is the heart's ransom: giving what one loves breaks wealth's grip at the exact finger where it holds (Surah 2, verse 177's own clause: \"who give charity out of their cherished wealth\"; the love-audit of verse 14's six magnetisms), and the attainment named is thereby exact: righteousness is reached through the release, because the release is the reaching.\n\nAnd the verse's descent into Madinah's streets produced the scene that remains its living commentary, preserved in the Sahih: Abu Talhah, the Ansar's wealthiest in palms, heard it—and came to the Prophet, peace be upon him: \"Allah says: 'You will never achieve righteousness until you donate some of what you cherish'—and the dearest of my wealth to me is Bayruha\"—his garden facing the mosque, whose sweet water the Prophet loved—\"and it is a charity for Allah: I hope for its birr and its treasure with Allah; so place it, O Messenger of Allah, where Allah shows you.\" The Prophet answered: \"Excellent! That is profitable wealth—that is profitable wealth. I have heard you, and I rule that you place it among your relatives\"—and Abu Talhah divided it among his kin and cousins (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 1461; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 998): the verse's whole jurisprudence enacted in one morning: the criterion applied by the giver to his own inventory (\"the dearest of my wealth\"), the release immediate, and the Prophet's ruling routing the cherished gift to the recipients this Book ranks first (Surah 2, verse 215's map: the relatives)—cost and kinship joined; the commentators adding the season's other exhibits: Umar and his prized Khaibar-share endowed at the same principle's press, Zaid ibn Harithah and his beloved horse.\n\n\"And whatever you give is certainly well known to Allah\" seals the criterion with the audit-clause its economics require (Surah 2, verse 273's identical seal)—and its work here is the criterion's guarantee, the scholars draw: the line this verse prices at—what the giver cherishes—is legible to no observer: the gift's market-value is public; its attachment-cost is interior: the widow's cherished mite outweighing the magnate's painless mountain on a scale only the \"well known to Allah\" can read (Sunan an-Nasa'i, Hadith 2527's pricing); every release, at its true cost, filed by the Knower—and the verse's permanent examination stands, by its own grammar, before each reader at his own inventory: not \"have I given?\" but \"has what I love ever left my hand for Allah?\"—the question birr's door was built to ask, and the chapter now turns from the giving-law to the food-law's polemics: the lawful and the self-forbidden, and the Torah summoned to testify.",
  "93": "\"All food was lawful for the children of Israel, except what Israel made unlawful for himself before the Torah was revealed\" opens a new file in the disputation—the food-laws—and its occasion, the commentators transmit, was a counter-attack: pressed by the chapter's arguments, the scholars of Madinah's Jews challenged the Prophet's community on its diet: how claim Ibrahim's religion (verse 68's award) while eating what we hold forbidden—the camel's flesh and milk foremost—when these, they asserted, were prohibited from Ibrahim's era down? The verse's answer is a two-clause history that dissolves the challenge: all food was lawful to Israel's children—the original breadth (Surah 2, verse 168's charter: the earth's lawful-and-good open)—except one category with a named, human origin: \"what Israel made unlawful for himself\": Yaqub—Israel in person (Surah 2, verse 133's patriarch)—abstaining by his own vow: the transmitted accounts (at-Tabari and Ibn Kathir gathering them; the question-narrations at Jami` at-Tirmidhi's chapters) relating his illness and his vow to forgo the camel's flesh and milk if healed—a personal renunciation, lawful in his law as vows are, which his descendants' tradition then hardened into claimed divine statute; \"before the Torah was revealed\": the clause that seals the chronology: the abstention predated the Torah—so the claim that these foods were God's prohibition from Ibrahim forward is refuted by the claimants' own sequence: Ibrahim ate what Israel later forwent; the broader Torah-prohibitions came later still—and came, this Book elsewhere states, as discipline for transgressions (Surah 4, verse 160: \"for the wrongdoing of the Jews We made unlawful for them certain good foods\"; Surah 6, verse 146's specified list), not as the patriarchal religion's original table.\n\n\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ 'Bring the Torah and read it, if your claims are true'\" issues the challenge that makes this verse a landmark in the Book's method—and its audacity must be measured, the scholars press: an unlettered Prophet, before the era's custodians of the text, stakes the dispute on their own document's public reading: bring it and read—for the Torah in their hands, whatever its transmission's wounds (Surah 2, verse 79's file), contained no Ibrahim-era camel-prohibition: the challenge safe only to one who knew, by revelation, what the scroll would and would not show (verse 44's epistemology; the stoning-verse precedent: the Torah produced, the concealing hand lifted, the text confirming the Prophet's statement—Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3635); and the summons's form—\"if your claims are true\"—is the Book's standing evidentiary rule (Surah 2, verse 111: \"show your proof\") applied at its boldest: the opponents' own scripture subpoenaed as the Prophet's witness. The record preserves no acceptance of the reading-challenge—the silence itself the verdict, as at every staked summons this season (verse 61's declined mubahala; Surah 2, verse 94's unwished death)—and the verse's permanent bequests stand, the commentators draw: the method—religious claims tested against documents, the burden on the claimant, the appeal to sources over slogans; and the doctrine—the lawful is Allah's to bound, and pious human renunciations (the patriarch's own vow included) never harden into His law: the food-charter of Surah 2, verse 168 guarded at both edges: nothing His law forbade admitted, nothing He left lawful forbidden in His name—the next verse pricing exactly that manufacture: \"whoever fabricates lies about Allah after this, they are the true wrongdoers.\"",
  "94": "\"Then whoever still fabricates lies about Allah, they will be the ˹true˺ wrongdoers\" closes the food-law file (verse 93) with its enforcement-clause—and its timing-word carries the whole juridical weight, the scholars note: \"then\"—min ba'di dhalika: after this: after the history's correction (the lawful original table, the patriarch's personal vow, the Torah's later discipline-prohibitions) and after the challenge's issue (the Torah summoned to public reading): whoever, past that clarification, continues to attribute the camel-prohibition to Allah's ancient law—or manufactures any claim in His name—has exhausted the file's last excuse: the fabrication henceforth is not inherited error (the tradition received in good faith, correctable and corrected) but chosen forgery: the \"after this\" performing here what \"after knowledge\" has performed at every station (verse 19's disputing; Surah 2, verse 145's warning): the conversion of mistake into crime by the evidence's arrival; and the crime's name is the argument's oldest register: iftira al-kadhib ala Allah—the fabrication of lies against Allah: the manufacture this Book ranks at prohibition's summit (Surah 7, verse 33's catalogue-peak: \"that you say about Allah what you do not know\"—and worse, against knowledge; the file's entries at verses 75 and 78: the gentile-licence, the twisted tongues; Surah 2, verse 79's pens): religion's supply-chain poisoned at its source-label: human matter shipped under the divine name.\n\n\"They will be the ˹true˺ wrongdoers\" delivers the classification, and its definite construction—hum az-zalimun: they are the wrongdoers—performs the sentence's emphasis, the commentators draw: wrongdoing's very category finds its definition in the post-clarification fabricator: for zulm is misplacement (Surah 2, verse 35's doctrine; Surah 31, verse 13's summit-case), and no misplacement exceeds the forger's: his own speech placed on Allah's throne of authorship—the creature legislating in the Creator's name: the lords-besides-Allah disease (verse 64's third clause; Adi ibn Hatim's exegesis) operated from the inside: the fabricator making himself the lord his followers unknowingly take; and the classification's placement over a food-question measures the Book's seriousness about the register, the scholars press: the dispute's subject was diet—camel-flesh and milk—yet the verdict deploys the argument's heaviest terms: for the item's size never measures the crime: the smallest ruling forged in Allah's name breaches the same wall as the largest doctrine (the wall between revelation and manufacture: the din's whole integrity), and the halal-haram registers are precisely where the breach is oftenest tried (Surah 16, verse 116's direct legislation: \"do not say about what your tongues falsely describe: 'this is lawful and that is unlawful'—fabricating lies against Allah; indeed, those who fabricate lies against Allah will never succeed\").\n\nThe clause's permanent audit, standing back, guards every community's religious speech at the exact joint this file exposed, the commentators draw: the pious inflation (the patriarch's personal vow hardened into divine statute: verse 93's mechanism) being forgery's most respectable production-line—custom sanctified, scruple legislated, the teacher's preference shipped as the Lord's ruling; and the believer's fence is the file's own method: the sources summoned (\"bring the Torah and read it\"), the claims traced to their warrant, and the tongue held at the boundary this Book patrols with its gravest sentences: what Allah said, said; what He did not, never draped in His name—while the next verse turns the file's finding into the summons it always serves: \"Allah has declared the truth—so follow the Way of Abraham, the upright.\"",
  "95": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ 'Allah has declared the truth'\" converts the food-file's verdict (verses 93 and 94) into proclamation—and the commanded sentence is the disputation's whole method compressed, the scholars note: sadaqa Allah—\"Allah has spoken truly\": the declaration issued at the point where the evidence has finished its work: the history corrected (verse 93's chronology), the challenge unanswered (the Torah summoned, the reading declined), the fabrication classified (verse 94)—and the field's verdict pronounced not as the winning side's boast but as the standing fact every file in the chapter has confirmed: His account true (verse 62's \"true narrative\"), their manufacture exposed; the formula's economy being its force: two words closing a controversy the schools had run for generations—and the community's inherited usage of the phrase, the commentators observe, carries this verse's own posture: truth's confession as worship.\n\n\"So follow the Way of Abraham, the upright—who was not a polytheist\" draws the proclamation's practical conclusion—and its wording re-issues, at the food-file's close, the chapter's central summons in its exact earlier form (verse 67's verdict-portrait; Surah 2, verse 135's commanded answer): the millah of Ibrahim: his way, his religion-in-practice—hanifan: the upright incliner (the fitrah-word, Surah 2, verse 135's exposition)—\"who was not a polytheist\": the double-edged clause standing guard as at every sounding (the pagan claimants barred; the association-doctrines audited); and the summons's placement here is the file's finishing logic, the scholars unfold: the food-dispute was, at bottom, a way-dispute: the challengers had claimed Ibrahim's table against the community (verse 93's occasion: \"how eat what the patriarch's law forbade?\"); the file having proven the claimed prohibitions post-patriarchal and human-vowed, the conclusion follows in the summons's own terms: Ibrahim's actual way—the original lawful breadth (verse 93's \"all food was lawful\"), the worship unassociated, the submission at first command (Surah 2, verse 131)—is the very way this community walks: the follow-command thus simultaneously vindication (the Muslims' diet is the patriarch's), invitation (the challengers summoned back to their claimed ancestor's real table and real creed), and the award-verse's enforcement (verse 68: \"the best claim to Abraham... this Prophet and the believers\").\n\nThe verse's function in the chapter's architecture is the gathering-signal, the commentators draw: the disputation's files now closed—the natures (verse 59), the patriarch (verse 67), the covenant (verse 81), the way (verse 85), the trusts (verse 77), the food (verse 94)—the argument turns homeward: the next verses raise the House itself—\"the first House established for humanity is the one at Bakkah\"—Ibrahim's actual monument summoned as the way's standing address: for the millah just commanded has a qiblah, a sanctuary, and a pilgrimage (verse 97's coming obligation), and the summons \"follow the Way of Abraham\" walks, in the chapter's own next steps, straight to the door he built: the proclamation's two words—\"Allah has declared the truth\"—henceforth sounding over the House whose builder begged, at those foundations, for exactly this community and its reciting Messenger (Surah 2, verses 127 to 129).",
  "96": "\"Surely the first House ˹of worship˺ established for humanity is the one at Bakkah\" answers the summons to Ibrahim's way (verse 95) with the way's standing address—and the verse's superlative is its doctrine, the scholars unfold: the first House: the Kaaba at Bakkah—Makkah under its ancient name, the narrowed valley's own word—established for humanity before every temple and sanctuary on earth: the primacy the Sunnah dates in the Prophet's own answer, peace be upon him, to Abu Dharr's question—\"which mosque was set on earth first?\" \"The Sacred Mosque.\" \"Then which?\" \"Al-Aqsa.\" \"How long between them?\" \"Forty years\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3366; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 520)—the House's foundations, by the transmitted accounts, older than Ibrahim's raising of them (Surah 2, verse 127's \"raised the foundations\": the site appointed before him, Surah 22, verse 26's \"We assigned to Abraham the site of the House\"); and the primacy's polemical work at this station is its placement, the commentators draw: the challengers' counter-claims (the food-file's pattern, verse 93) extended to sanctuaries: Jerusalem's temple urged as the elder and worthier qiblah (the controversy of Surah 2, verses 142 to 150 still echoing); this verse settles the seniority by the register the claimants honoured—chronology (verse 65's own method): the House at Bakkah precedes: the qiblah-turn was not innovation's departure from the ancient shrine but return to the most ancient of all—\"for humanity\": the dedication-clause completing the answer: established not for a tribe or a covenant-line but for the species: the universal House for the universal way (verse 95's millah), no nation's franchise.\n\n\"A blessed sanctuary and a guide for ˹all˺ people\" states the House's two standing offices—and each carries its documented file, the scholars note. \"Blessed\"—mubarakan: barakah's residence: the increase-beyond-measure this Book prices as its invisible economics (Surah 2, verse 276's commentary): blessing in its provision (the barren valley fed from every land: Ibrahim's answered prayer, Surah 2, verse 126; Surah 28, verse 57's \"fruits of all kinds\"), in its worship's multiplication (the prayer at the Sacred Mosque outweighing, by the Prophet's report, a hundred thousand elsewhere: the transmission at Ibn Majah, Hadith 1406, graded sound), in its drawing of hearts across fourteen centuries without interruption (Surah 14, verse 37's \"make the hearts of some people incline to them\"). \"And a guide for all people\"—hudan lil-alamin: the House itself guidance: the qiblah aligning the praying world's faces (Surah 2, verse 144), the pilgrimage assembling the nations at one door (verse 97's coming obligation), and the standing sign arguing its Founder to every honest eye: a shrine in a fruitless valley, idol-cleansed by its builder's creed, outlasting every empire that ringed it—the guide-office being the blessed-office made visible: the House teaching, by its own history, the tawhid it was raised for (Surah 2, verse 125's charter: \"purify My House for those who circle it\").\n\nThe verse's function at the chapter's turn is the homecoming-signal, the commentators draw: the disputation's long arc—natures, patriarchs, covenants, tables—closes at the monument where the claimed ancestor actually worshipped: the way of Ibrahim (verse 95) given its address, the award of his estate (verse 68) given its title-deed's physical seat; and the next verse will read the deed's contents—the clear signs, the standing-place, the safety-grant—and attach to the House the obligation that makes every able believer, once in a life, walk the summons home: \"pilgrimage to this House is an obligation by Allah upon whoever is able.\"",
  "97": "\"In it are clear signs and the standing-place of Abraham\" opens the House's title-deed (verse 96's charter) at its contents—and the named exhibit is the deed's signature, the scholars note: ayat bayyinat—clear signs: the House's evidences legible to every honest eye: the sanctuary's peace held through the ages of surrounding anarchy (Surah 29, verse 67's standing wonder), the barren valley's provisioning (Surah 2, verse 126's answered prayer), the elephant-army's ruin in living memory of the first audience (Surah 105's file), the hearts inclined across the centuries (Surah 14, verse 37)—and, named from among them: maqam Ibrahim: the standing-place: the stone that bore the builder at the walls' raising (Surah 2, verse 125's commentary; the prayer-station commanded there: \"take the standing-place of Abraham as a site of prayer\"): the House's founder attested by his own preserved footing—the monument's deed carrying, as it were, the mason's mark: Ibrahim's way (verse 95's summons) given tangible address at the very stone.\n\n\"Whoever enters it should be safe\" states the deed's second clause—the aman-grant: the sanctuary's entrant secured: the ancient inviolability (Surah 2, verse 125's \"sanctuary for the people\") standing as law and as marvel: the fearful entering the precinct beyond pursuit, the ages' feuds checked at its boundary—the grant, the scholars note, both report (the historical fact the pagans themselves honoured) and ruling (the sanctity legislated for the community's law: the Haram's protections codified from this clause and its Sunnah).\n\n\"Pilgrimage to this House is an obligation by Allah upon whoever is able among the people\" attaches to the deed the obligation that made it every believer's document: the hajj—the fifth pillar (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 8's five)—stated in the Book's most solemn obligation-grammar: lillahi ala an-nas: owed to Allah, upon the people: the duty's creditor named in the phrase itself; \"whoever is able\"—man istata'a ilayhi sabilan: the capacity-clause: the way's provision and mount (the Prophet's own gloss transmitted at the Sunan), the law's ease-architecture (Surah 2, verse 286's ceiling) built into the pillar's very text: the summons universal, the liability sized to means; and the Sunnah supplies the obligation's terms: once in the lifetime—\"the hajj is once; whoever adds, it is voluntary\" (Sunan Abi Dawud, Hadith 1721, graded sound)—and its prize: \"whoever performs hajj and does not commit obscenity or sin returns as the day his mother bore him\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 1521), the accepted hajj having \"no reward but Paradise\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 1773; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 1349).\n\n\"And whoever disbelieves, then surely Allah is not in need of ˹any of His˺ creation\" closes the deed with the clause whose severity the scholars weighed: the hajj-refusal—the able who decline the owed journey, and at the refusal's limit the duty's outright denial—filed under kufr's register: the word chosen to price the abandonment's gravity (the transmitted statements of Umar and the early authorities pressing the able-but-never-going with this very clause); and the closing name resolves the severity into the Book's deepest theology of worship: \"Allah is not in need of creation\"—ghaniyyun anil-alamin: the House's Lord unenriched by its pilgrims, unimpoverished by its deserters (Surah 2, verse 263's Self-Sufficient): the obligation, like every obligation in this argument, the debtor's own treasure (Surah 2, verse 272's \"for your own good\")—the summons home to Ibrahim's House being, at the last accounting, the traveller's need, not the Host's; and the chapter, its homecoming complete, turns in the next verses to the final addresses: the People of the Book asked their two last questions, and the believers commanded to the rope of Allah, all together.",
  "98": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ 'O People of the Book! Why do you deny the revelations of Allah, when Allah is a Witness to what you do?'\" opens the chapter's closing addresses to the scripture-communities with the first of two final questions—and its structure re-issues, at the argument's end, the audit-question of verse 70 with one decisive substitution, the scholars note: there, the clause was \"while you bear witness\"—their own witnessing summoned against their denial; here, at the file's close, the witness named is greater: \"Allah is a Witness to what you do\"—the audit transferred from their suppressed testimony to His unsuppressible one: for the argument has run its course: their witnessing was documented (verse 70), managed (verse 71's mixing and hiding), performed against (verse 72's theatre), and twisted (verse 78's tongues)—the human witness-stand, in short, corrupted at every station; and the closing question therefore stands the denial before the Witness no management reaches: shahid ala ma ta'malun: Witness over what you do—the deeds' own observer (verse 5's \"nothing hidden\"; Surah 2, verse 77's \"aware of what they conceal and what they reveal\"): the kufr-verb of the question (\"why do you deny\") thus operating, in this last asking, under the courtroom's true light: rejection maintained not merely against one's own knowledge but in the Knower's sight.\n\nThe question's \"why,\" as at its earlier soundings (verses 70 and 71), grants no epistemic refuge—and its placement at the chapter's close perfects the design, the commentators draw: the whole disputation has answered, in advance, every \"because\" the deniers could offer: because the signs are unclear?—the true narrative certified (verse 62), the analogy delivered (verse 59), the Torah summoned to reading (verse 93); because the claim lacks pedigree?—the covenant of the prophets unveiled (verse 81), the first House deeded (verse 96); because our way suffices?—the way named and awarded (verses 19, 85, 68); the closing question thus arrives with the file's every exit sealed: the denial's remaining causes are the motive-register alone (the envy, the revenue, the rank: Surah 2, verse 90's diagnosis; verse 69's campaign)—and motives, before \"Allah is a Witness to what you do,\" are precisely what cannot be pleaded.\n\nThe verse's permanent function, standing back, is the caller's closing manner taught, the scholars press: the argument's end is not curse but question—the summons held open in interrogative form to the last (the address still \"O People of the Book\": the honour-title kept; the door of verse 64's common terms unshut); and the believer inherits both the question and its restraint: truth's case pressed to completion, the refuser's account left with the Witness (verse 63's transfer: \"Allah has perfect knowledge of the corruptors\")—while the second closing question, in the next verse, turns from their own denial to its export: the active barring of others from the Way.",
  "99": "\"Say, 'O People of the Book! Why do you turn the believers away from the Way of Allah—striving to make it ˹appear˺ crooked, while you are witnesses ˹to its truth˺?'\" issues the second closing question—and its charge advances past the first's (verse 98: their own denial) to the denial's export: sadd an sabil Allah: the blocking of the Way: the campaign-file (verse 69's wish-to-mislead, verse 72's staged exits, verse 100's coming yield-warning) indicted in its full method: \"striving to make it appear crooked\"—tabghunaha iwajan: seeking for it crookedness: the Way not blocked by walls (impossible: the road is Allah's) but by optics: the straight path re-described until it looks bent—the manufactured controversies (the qiblah-mockery, Surah 2, verse 142; the abrogation-taunts, Surah 2, verse 106's occasion), the elusive verses bent to cast doubt (verse 7's method), the Prophet's descriptions edited (Surah 2, verse 79), the morning-belief evening-rejection theatre (verse 72): each operation an application of one technique: crookedness projected onto straightness so that seekers turn back at the distorted image; and the charge's third clause seals its gravity as at every station: \"while you are witnesses\"—the distortion performed by the qualified: those whose own sources certified the Way's straightness (verse 70's file; Surah 2, verse 146's recognition): the surveyors, holding the true map, painting bends on the public copy.\n\n\"And Allah is never unaware of what you do\" closes the second question with the audit-clause in its litotes-form—the understatement whose force this argument has deployed at the concealment-files throughout (Surah 2, verses 74, 85, 140, 144): \"never unaware\": the double-negative that leaves the awareness total: every re-description operation, every projected bend, every seeker turned back at the painted image—filed, with its operators and its casualties, by the Aware; and the clause's pairing with verse 98's \"Allah is a Witness\" completes the two questions' escalating seals, the scholars note: the Witness over their denial, the never-unaware over their campaign: the interior crime and its exported form both docketed.\n\nThe two-question unit (98 and 99), standing back, is the chapter's formal closing of the People-of-the-Book file—and its design bequeaths the community the anatomy of anti-guidance work in every era, the commentators draw: stage one, the internal denial-despite-witness (98); stage two, the external crookedness-projection (99): the pattern's later editions recognisable by this verse's own terms wherever the Way is fought—not by refuting its evidences (the qualified know better) but by distorting its image: the faith re-painted as bent (its justice as harshness, its clarity as simplism, its mercy as weakness) by describers whose own study knows the lines are straight; and the believers' standing equipment against the technique is the argument's whole yield: the Way's actual map held firm (verse 101's coming anchor: \"whoever holds firmly to Allah is surely guided\"), the projected bends tested against the sources (verse 93's method: bring the text and read it), and the painters left to the never-unaware—while the address now turns, in the next verse, from the painters to their targets: \"O believers! If you were to yield to a group of those who were given the Scripture, they would turn you back from belief to disbelief.\"",
  "100": "\"O believers! If you were to yield to a group of those who were given the Scripture, they would turn you back from belief to disbelief\" turns the address from the campaign's operators (verses 98 and 99) to its targets—and the turn's occasion, transmitted by the commentators from the sirah (Zaid ibn Aslam's account, gathered by at-Tabari and Ibn Kathir), stages the verse in a Madinan afternoon: Shas ibn Qays, an aged partisan of the old order, passing a gathering of Aws and Khazraj—the two tribes whose generations of war Islam's brotherhood had ended—and, grieved by their concord, dispatching a youth to sit among them and recite the poetry of Bu'ath: the old battle's verses, each tribe's boasts and wounds re-sounded—until the gathering kindled: the ancient names invoked, weapons called for, the two tribes moving toward the harrah to resume, under Islam's own sky, the war their faith had buried; and the Prophet, peace be upon him, came out to them with the companions and called: \"O Muslims! Allah, Allah! The call of ignorance while I am among you—after Allah guided you to Islam, honoured you, and severed the old order's bond?\"—and the people wept, embraced, and this passage descended (the account standing at these verses' occasion in the transmitted tafsir): the verse's \"if you were to yield\" thus documentary: the yielding had been minutes away—one poem's operation nearly converting brothers back to battlefield enemies.\n\nThe verse's warning-anatomy carries the file's permanent intelligence, the scholars draw: \"a group of those who were given the Scripture\"—the census-clause as ever (not the communities entire: verse 75's trustworthy, verse 199's coming believers exempted by name); \"if you obey them\"—the yielding named as obedience: the operation's mechanism being never open summons to apostasy (unofferable) but incremental compliance: the ear lent to the grievance-poetry, the narrative absorbed, the old identities re-inhabited—obedience by instalments (Surah 2, verse 168's footsteps-doctrine in social form); and the destination published: \"they would turn you back from belief to disbelief\"—raddukum: the reversion: the campaign's full objective (Surah 2, verse 109's wish documented; verse 217's intelligence: \"they will not stop fighting you until they turn you away from your faith—if they can\"): for the Bu'ath-operation's genius, the commentators note, was precisely that it attacked the faith through the flank: no doctrine disputed, no verse denied—only the old tribal selves re-summoned; and the reversion-warning names the stakes truly: the brotherhood was the belief's own fabric (the next verses will say it: the hearts united by His grace, verse 103), and its unravelling toward the harrah was disbelief approaching in war-dress.\n\nThe verse's standing equipment, the scholars press, serves the community at every era's version of the operation: the outside voices cultivating the inside divisions—the old identities (tribal, ethnic, factional, national) re-supplied with their poetry, the grievances curated, the brotherhood's seams worked by describers who know exactly which verses of which Bu'ath to recite; and the believers' defence is the passage's own sequence: the operation named (this verse), the astonishment sounded (verse 101: \"how can you disbelieve while the revelations are recited to you and His Messenger is in your midst?\"), and the rope commanded (verse 103: \"hold firmly, all together—and do not be divided\").",
  "101": "\"How can you disbelieve when Allah's revelations are recited to you and His Messenger is in your midst?\" sounds the astonishment that answers verse 100's near-reversion—and its two named securities are the verse's whole argument, the scholars unfold: the community's immunity-system itemised: \"Allah's revelations recited to you\"—the Book descending and rehearsed among them: the daily recitation renewing the covenant's text (the operation of verse 100 had worked, precisely, in the recitation's absence: the gathering's ears lent to Bu'ath's poetry, the other recital); \"and His Messenger in your midst\"—the living guidance present: the correction one summons away (as the harrah-march proved: his single call dissolved the kindled war, verse 100's occasion)—and the question's grammar prices the securities: anna—\"how?\": disbelief with these two present is anomaly requiring explanation: the reversion-campaign's whole hope (verse 100) resting on distance from exactly these—the poetry recited where the Book was not, the old selves summoned where the Messenger was not looked to; whence the verse's implicit standing counsel, the commentators draw, for every generation after the Messenger's passing: the two securities remain in their forms—the Book recited as then, and the Messenger present in his Sunnah preserved (the \"I have left among you what will never let you stray if you hold to it: the Book of Allah\"—the Farewell-sermon's bequest, Sahih Muslim, Hadith 1218's transcript)—the immunity-system intact wherever the two are kept close, and the anomaly-question standing over every community that lets them drift to arm's length while the rival recitals fill the gathering.\n\n\"Whoever holds firmly to Allah is surely guided to the Straight Path\" completes the verse with the anchor-clause—and its verb is the passage's key handed over, the scholars note: i'tisam: the holding-fast: the grasping of Allah—His covenant, His Book, His protection—as the drowning grasp the rope (the firmest hand-hold of Surah 2, verse 256: \"whoever renounces false gods and believes in Allah has certainly grasped the firmest, unfailing hand-hold\"): the clause stating the immunity-system's activation: the securities named (the recitation, the Messenger) protect the holder, not the vicinity—presence at the gathering saved no one at Bu'ath's poem until the holding-fast re-engaged (the weeping and embracing at the Prophet's call: the grip retaken); and the promise's grammar is the Book's guarantee-form: \"is surely guided\"—qad hudiya: guided already, in the holding itself: the Straight Path (the Fatihah's daily petition; Isa's own definition at verse 51) not a destination the holder travels toward but the state the holding constitutes: whoever grips is on it—the verse thus closing the reversion-file (verses 98 to 101) with the counter-operation stated in one clause: against the campaign's instalment-obedience (verse 100's yielding), the instalment-grip: renewed daily at the recitation, tightened at every summons of the old poetries; and the next verse will name the grip's inner substance—\"be mindful of Allah in the way He deserves, and do not die except in submission\"—before the rope itself is unrolled for the community entire: \"hold firmly to the rope of Allah, all together.\"",
  "102": "\"O believers! Be mindful of Allah in the way He deserves\" issues, at the closing addresses' head, the taqwa-command at its absolute measure—and the measure is the verse's whole weight, the scholars unfold: haqqa tuqatihi: His mindfulness as it truly deserves to be: not the believer's customary carefulness but the guarding proportioned to the Guarded-against's majesty—the early authorities' exposition transmitted from Ibn Mas'ud: \"that He be obeyed and not disobeyed, remembered and not forgotten, thanked and not shown ingratitude\" (the reading at at-Tabari and the transmitted tafsir): obedience, remembrance, gratitude—each at the unfailing register; and the measure's apparent impossibility, the commentators note, is resolved by the Book's own pairing: \"so be mindful of Allah as much as you are able\" (Surah 64, verse 16)—the two verses read together by the scholars as summit and path: this verse fixing the standard (no self-satisfied plateau: His right never fully paid, the striving never complete), Surah 64 fixing the liability (the capacity-ceiling of Surah 2, verse 286): the believer commanded to climb toward a peak whose height keeps him climbing, charged only for the climbing his legs can do—the standard's function being precisely its unreachability: taqwa kept aspirational at every attainment, the muttaqi's rank (the chapter's watchword since Surah 2, verse 2) defined as motion, not arrival.\n\n\"And do not die except in ˹a state of full˺ submission ˹to Him˺\" completes the command with the deathbed-clause—and its text is the patriarchs' bequest re-issued verbatim, the scholars mark: Ibrahim's and Yaqub's charge to their sons (Surah 2, verse 132: \"do not die except in a state of full submission\") now commanded to the whole believing community: the family-counsel of the election-line become the ummah's standing order (verse 68's award enacted: the estate's heirs receiving the estate's dying-instruction); and the clause's jurisprudence stands as at its first sounding (Surah 2, verse 132's commentary): the command over the uncommandable hour—no one schedules his death—is the command over every hour: the state must be held continuously that it may be held at the unannounced moment: \"deeds are only by their endings\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 6607), and the ending is reached by the road; the clause thus converting the summit-standard of the verse's first half into the life-schedule of its second: taqwa at its deserved measure, maintained so that whenever the appointment falls, it falls on a muslim—the Prophet's own dying-counsel binding the two clauses, peace be upon him: \"let none of you die except thinking well of Allah\" (Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2877): the guarded life closing in the good opinion the guarding earned.\n\nThe verse's placement is the passage's architecture, the commentators draw: between the reversion-file's close (verse 101's anchor) and the rope-verse's community-command (verse 103), this verse stands as the individual's charge—for the united community about to be commanded is built of persons, and the rope's collective grip is the sum of single hands: each commanded here to its own taqwa at full measure and its own submission held to death; the order of the two verses being the Book's constant sociology: the ummah's unity (103) rises from the members' taqwa (102), never the reverse—and the rope now unrolls: \"hold firmly, all together, to the rope of Allah, and do not be divided.\"",
  "103": "\"And hold firmly to the rope of Allah and do not be divided\" issues the community-charter—and its central image, the scholars transmit, is defined in the Sunnah's own words: the rope of Allah is the Book: \"it is the strong rope of Allah, and it is the wise reminder, and it is the straight path\" (the description transmitted at Jami` at-Tirmidhi, Hadith 2906's family; the Prophet at Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2408: \"the Book of Allah—it is the rope of Allah: whoever follows it is upon guidance\"): the revelation as the cord lowered from heaven—one end in His Hand, the other in the community's grip (verse 101's i'tisam-verb here made collective: i'tasimu—hold fast, all of you); and the command's two clauses are one legislation, the commentators press: \"all together... and do not be divided\": the rope gripped jami'an—as one body: unity not commanded beside the holding but as its mode: the cord is one, so the holders' oneness is the grip's own geometry—division beginning, always, where hands leave the common rope for party-cords (the sects' history at Surah 2, verse 213's file; verse 105's coming warning).\n\n\"Remember Allah's favour upon you when you were enemies, then He united your hearts, so you—by His grace—became brothers\" grounds the charter in the community's own biography—and the remembered scene is verse 100's occasion still warm, the scholars note: the Aws and Khazraj: the generations of Bu'ath's war (the mutual slaughter whose poetry had just re-kindled at Shas ibn Qays's operation), and the transformation: \"He united your hearts\"—allafa bayna qulubikum: the joining of hearts: named as His act, beyond all treasuries' purchase (Surah 8, verse 63: \"had you spent all that is in the earth, you could not have united their hearts, but Allah united them\")—the brotherhood \"by His grace\": the Ansar's title itself the miracle's monument: yesterday's battlefield enemies sharing houses, wealth, and ranks (the muakhah-pairings of the migration's first days); the favour-remembrance commanded as the unity's maintenance, the commentators draw: communities that forget what their concord cost—and Who paid it—re-price it cheaply at every faction's offer.\n\n\"And you were at the brink of a fiery pit and He saved you from it\" completes the biography at its true depth: the shafa hufratin min an-nar—the pit's very lip: the pre-Islamic condition named at its stakes: not merely the tribal wars' mutual bleeding but their terminus—dying in the old order meant the Fire, and the generation stood at its edge when the rescue reached them: the verse's image, the scholars note, fusing the two salvations in one brink: saved from the war's pit by the brotherhood, from the Fire's by the faith—one rescue, two depths; \"this is how Allah makes His revelations clear to you, so that you may be ˹rightly˺ guided\" sealing the charter with the method-clause (Surah 2, verse 242's formula): the community shown its own before-and-after as the exhibit—the divided past, the united present, the rope in hand—and the guidance named as the showing's purpose: whoever holds the memory holds the rope's reason. The charter now commissions its guardians: the next verse's standing party—\"let there be a group among you who call to goodness, encourage what is good, and forbid what is evil\"—the unity's immune-system, staffed and charged.",
  "104": "\"Let there be a group among you who call ˹others˺ to goodness, encourage what is good, and forbid what is evil\" commissions the unity-charter's guardians (verse 103's rope)—and the commission's grammar carries its jurisprudence, the scholars unfold: waltakun minkum ummah: let there be from among you a community: the duty communal in its discharge—a standing party equipped for the work—while binding the whole in its ensurance: the fard kifayah architecture the jurists drew from this very clause: if the sufficient party stands, the rest are covered; if none stands, all bear the default; and the commissioned work is itemised in three offices: \"call to goodness\"—ad-du'a ila al-khayr: the summons to the good entire: the faith, the Book, the way (the mission's positive voice: Surah 16, verse 125's \"invite to the way of your Lord with wisdom\"); \"encourage what is good\"—al-amr bil-ma'ruf: the known-good commanded: the community's shared standard actively upheld; \"and forbid what is evil\"—an-nahy an al-munkar: the recognised-wrong opposed: the three offices together being the community's immune-system, the commentators draw: the rope of verse 103 holds only where the offices operate—division, corruption, and the reversion-campaigns (verse 100's file) all entering, historically, through the immune-system's lapse: the Sabbath-town's census standing as the Book's own case-study (Surah 2, verse 65's file; Surah 7, verse 165: \"We saved those who forbade evil\"—the forbidders alone named among the saved), and the Prophet's own gradation staffing the duty at every capacity, peace be upon him: \"whoever of you sees an evil, let him change it with his hand; if he cannot, then with his tongue; if he cannot, then with his heart—and that is the weakest of faith\" (Sahih Muslim, Hadith 49)—with the abandonment's price posted in his warning: \"you will surely command the good and forbid the evil, or Allah will soon send upon you a punishment from Him—then you will call upon Him and not be answered\" (Jami` at-Tirmidhi, Hadith 2169, graded sound).\n\n\"It is they who will be successful\" seals the commission with the Book's success-formula at its exclusive register—hum al-muflihun: they are the successful: the falah-word of the chapter's opening portrait (Surah 2, verse 5's \"it is they who will be successful\") here assigned to the commissioned party: the harvest-success this argument defined at its first sounding (the goal actually reached, Surah 2, verse 5's commentary) declared the callers' and commanders' own—and the assignment's logic, the scholars press, completes the passage's architecture: the community was saved from the pit (verse 103's brink), and the saved who then staff the rescue of others (the three offices being rescue's daily forms) are the success-roll's named holders: salvation received become salvation transmitted—the ummah's whole commission (Surah 2, verse 143's witnesses over humanity; the coming verse 110: \"the best community ever raised for humanity—you encourage good and forbid evil\") operating through this verse's standing party; while the charter's dark twin follows immediately: the communities that split after the clarity—\"do not be like those who divided and differed after clear proofs had come to them\"—the immune-system's failure priced, in the next verse, at the tremendous punishment.",
  "105": "\"And do not be like those who split ˹into sects˺ and differed after clear proofs had come to them\" attaches to the unity-charter and its commissioned guardians (verses 103 and 104) the prohibition drawn from history's file—and the named precedent is the argument's documented case, the scholars unfold: \"those who split and differed\": the scripture-communities' sectarian history: the one way fractured into warring parties (Surah 2, verse 213's file: the disputing \"out of jealousy\"; verse 19's identical diagnosis: \"did not dispute until knowledge came to them, out of mutual envy\"), the Jews' and Christians' mutual excommunications (Surah 2, verse 113's \"nothing to stand on\" exchanged), the councils and schisms the delegations carried into Madinah; and the prohibition's aggravation-clause stands as at every sounding: \"after clear proofs had come to them\"—the splitting post-clarity: not the honest divergence of seekers before evidence (the excused striving of the sincere: Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 7352's two-rewards-and-one) but the partisan fracture after it: division as identity, the party-cords gripped over the common rope (verse 103's geometry violated by the very communities whose Books were its earlier editions)—whence the command's form, the commentators press: \"do not be like them\": the Muslims addressed as candidates for the precedent: the prohibition meaningful only because the path is open—and the Prophet, peace be upon him, forecast the candidacy's realisation with the sobriety this verse demands: the handspan-by-handspan following of the earlier communities' ways (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3456), and the community's splitting into sects as those before had split (the seventy-odd-sects transmissions at Jami` at-Tirmidhi, Hadith 2640's family, graded in its supports)—the verse and the forecasts together being the ummah's standing mirror: sectarianism never an outside enemy's operation only (verse 100's file) but the inside's own available disease.\n\n\"It is they who will suffer a tremendous punishment\" prices the precedent—and the sentence's register measures the crime's true class, the scholars draw: adhabun azim: the tremendous punishment: the scale this argument reserves for its gravest files (Surah 2, verse 7's sealed rejecters)—assigned here to the splitters-after-clarity: for the fracture is not an organisational misfortune but the covenant's own breach: the rope commanded (verse 103), the guardians commissioned (verse 104), and the division chosen against both: the united body's tearing being, in this Book's accounting, an assault on the favour itself (verse 103's remembered uniting of hearts: His grace undone by the parties' hands)—each sect's zeal, however pious its banners, working the pit's edge the rescue had pulled the community from; and the sentence's placement completes the passage's balanced architecture, the commentators note: verse 104 sealed the commissioned with success (al-muflihun), this verse seals the splitters with the tremendous punishment: the two endings facing each other across the charter as the Day's own two faces will—for the next verse opens directly upon them: \"on that Day some faces will be bright while others gloomy\": the unity kept and the unity torn, read out at the resurrection in the community's own countenances.",
  "106": "\"On that Day some faces will be bright while others gloomy\" opens the charter-passage's final scene—the Day's reading of the unity kept and torn (verses 103 to 105)—and its register is the countenance: yawma tabyaddu wujuhun wa taswaddu wujuh: the Day faces whiten and faces blacken: the interior states of the world's long test published, at the resurrection, on the body's most public page: the brightness the light of faith held and rope gripped (the radiance-texts' family: \"their light will run before them,\" Surah 57, verse 12; \"faces that Day will be radiant, looking at their Lord,\" Surah 75, verses 22 and 23), the gloom the darkness chosen and increased (Surah 2, verse 257's false-guardians' extractions completed; the darkened faces of \"those who earned evil,\" Surah 10, verse 27)—and the scene's placement is the passage's own verdict-form, the scholars note: directly upon the splitting-prohibition (verse 105), the two countenances read as the two endings' publication: the commentators—Ibn Abbas's transmitted reading among them—expounding the bright faces as the community of the rope: the unity's people, the Sunnah's holders; the gloomy as the splitters and innovators: the sect-makers whose fracture the prohibition priced—the Day's complexions being, in this exposition, the charter's compliance-report.\n\n\"To the gloomy-faced it will be said, 'Did you disbelieve after having believed? So taste the punishment for your disbelief'\" turns the scene to its interrogation—and the question's charge fixes the gloomy file's identity at its widest and most warning breadth, the scholars unfold: \"did you disbelieve after having believed?\"—the kufr-after-iman formula: the apostasy-file's own terms (verse 86's case; verse 90's increase)—read by the commentators across its registers: the outright apostates foremost; the scripture-communities who believed their prophets and disbelieved the confirmed successor (verse 81's covenant turned from); and, in the exposition the passage's placement presses, the community's own splitters whose sectarian tearing carried them past the boundary (the harrah-march's near-reversion, verse 100: brotherhood's unravelling as disbelief approaching in war-dress)—the question's form, as at every Day-interrogation, being conviction's instrument: the charge stated to the convicted whose own biography is the evidence (Surah 2, verse 167's deeds made present); \"so taste the punishment\": the sentence's tasting-verb: the chosen thing finally sampled—the gloom worn outward being the first course of the darkness long fed within.\n\nThe verse's function, standing back, is the passage's summons made visual, the commentators draw: the community commanded to the rope (103), staffed with guardians (104), and warned from the precedent (105) is here shown the stakes at their final legibility: every gathering, faction-choice, and grip-or-release of the common cord travelling toward one of two complexions—and the believer formed by the scene carries its audit into the week's own assemblies: which of my belongings, loyalties, and quarrels is whitening, and which darkening, the face I will wear at the reading?—while the bright column's full portion, held one verse in reserve, closes the passage at mercy's register: \"as for the bright-faced, they will be in Allah's mercy, where they will remain forever.\"",
  "107": "\"As for the bright-faced, they will be in Allah's mercy, where they will remain forever\" completes the Day's two-countenance scene (verse 106)—and its economy of description is the verse's deepest doctrine, the scholars unfold: the bright column's whole portion stated in one term: \"in Allah's mercy\"—fi rahmatillah: within His mercy: the abode itself named not as Paradise first but as the mercy—the commentators reading the usage as the Book's own metonym (the Garden called by its cause: the Prophet's report of the Fire and the Garden disputing, and Allah's word to the Garden: \"you are My mercy, by which I show mercy to whom I will\"—Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 4850; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2846) and as its exactness: the destination's substance being the Merciful's own embrace: the gardens, rivers, and pure companions (Surah 2, verse 25's inventory) the mercy's furnishings, the mercy itself the dwelling—whence the preposition's force: in it: immersed, resident, enclosed (the extraction-motion of Surah 2, verse 257—out of darkness into light—arrived at its terminus: the light's own house); and the residence eternal: \"where they will remain forever\": the khulud-clause at mercy's register: the permanence that sealed the gloomy file's punishment (verse 88's unlightened abiding) sealing the bright file's bliss—the two eternities facing each other across the Day as the two faces did (verse 106), the Book's symmetry complete.\n\nThe bright faces' identity gathers the whole passage's rolls, the commentators draw: the rope's holders all together (verse 103), the commissioned callers and commanders (verse 104's success-roll: \"it is they who will be successful\"—this verse the success cashed), the un-split community (verse 105's compliance), the taqwa-bearers at their deserved measure dying in submission (verse 102's charge kept)—and behind them the chapter's every honour-column: the dawn-forgiveness-seekers (verse 17), the knowledge-people of the summit-testimony (verse 18), the disciples' submission-line (verse 52), the estate-heirs of Ibrahim (verse 68): one register across the surah, published at the Day in the whitened countenance and housed, by this verse, in the mercy itself.\n\nAnd the verse's closing function, standing at this passage's end, is the Book's constant last word given its residence, the scholars press: the chapter's severest files—the curses universal (verse 87), the ransoms refused (verse 91), the tremendous punishment of the splitters (verse 105), the gloomy interrogation (verse 106)—all close, as the whole argument from Adam's slip onward has closed, at mercy's open door: the final scene's final clause being not the punishment tasted but the mercy dwelt in forever; the reader formed by the two-verse diptych holds, accordingly, the Book's own balance (Surah 15, verses 49 and 50's twin announcement): the warning real, the hope realer—\"My mercy prevails over My wrath\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3194)—and the road between the reader and the bright column published at every station of the passage now closed: the rope held, the good commanded, the division refused, the taqwa kept to the last breath: the community of the whitened faces being recruited, by these very verses, until the Day they describe.",
  "108": "\"These are Allah's revelations We recite to you ˹O Prophet˺ in truth\" seals the two-countenance scene just closed (verses 106 and 107)—and with it the whole passage from the rope-charter onward—with the certification-formula this Book stamps at its great movements' ends (Surah 2, verse 252 over Talut's account; verse 58 of this chapter over the Maryam-narratives): what was recited—the unity commanded, the guardians commissioned, the splitters priced, the Day's faces published—is ayat Allah: His revelations, recited \"in truth\": true in their histories, true in their laws, true in their forecasts of the Day; the seal's function at this station being the passage's own authority-transfer, the scholars note: the community about to carry the charter (the best-community commission arriving two verses ahead) is first shown its charter's signature.\n\n\"And Allah desires no injustice to ˹His˺ creation\" completes the seal with a clause whose placement is its doctrine—and the commentators paused at its exactness: directly after the Day's severest scene (verse 106's gloomy interrogation, verse 88's unlightened abiding still echoing), revelation states the Judge's own disposition: He wills no zulm for the worlds—the punishment-scenes just recited are not the appetite of a wrathful sovereign but the operation of a justice that wrongs no one (Surah 2, verse 281's solvency-clause: \"none will be wronged\"); the verse's negation reaching, the scholars unfold, both registers of the word: He does not wrong—\"your Lord does not wrong anyone\" (Surah 18, verse 49): every gloomy face earned its gloom by its own biography (verse 106's \"did you disbelieve after having believed?\": the charge answered by the convicted's record); and He does not desire wrong between His creatures: the revelations just recited—the rope, the commanded good, the forbidden evil—being themselves the anti-injustice architecture: His law the guard He set over His creation's mutual dealings.\n\nThe clause's theology, standing back, is the Book's answer to the oldest slander against divine judgment, the commentators draw: the two eternities of verses 106 and 107 proceed from a Lord who \"has prescribed mercy upon Himself\" (Surah 6, verse 54) and forbidden injustice to Himself—the Prophet, peace be upon him, conveying the decree in His own voice: \"O My servants, I have forbidden injustice for Myself and made it forbidden among you, so do not wrong one another\" (Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2577): the hadith this verse's exact commentary: the same sentence that clears the Judge binds the judged—injustice barred at the throne and outlawed in the street; and the believer formed by the pairing carries its double yield: rest, before every hard verse—the severities are justice's operations, never its excesses (Surah 2, verse 286's capacity-ceiling; verse 25's exact payment)—and duty, in every dealing: the creation toward whom He desires no injustice includes each neighbour the reader will meet before nightfall; while the next verse grounds the whole in the estate that makes His justice inevitable: \"to Allah belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth.\"",
  "109": "\"To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth\" grounds the no-injustice clause just issued (verse 108) in the ownership-doctrine this argument has laid at every foundation (Surah 2, verses 107, 255, 284; verse 26 of this chapter's estate)—and the grounding's logic is the verse's work, the scholars unfold: injustice, in every register the creatures know it, is the creature's disease of limitation: the ruler wrongs to gain what he lacks, the witness lies to protect what he might lose, the judge tilts for the bribe he needs—every zulm a grasping born of need; and the Owner of the heavens' and earth's totality stands beyond the disease's very possibility: what could He gain by wronging whom, when all things in both registers are already His? (the identical reasoning the commentators drew at Surah 2, verse 255's estate-clause): the previous verse's \"Allah desires no injustice\" thus receives here its metaphysical warrant: His justice is not a policy He maintains but a perfection His self-sufficiency entails—\"Allah is not in need of any of His creation\" (verse 97's closing), and the un-needing cannot be corrupted.\n\n\"And to Allah ˹all˺ matters will be returned ˹for judgment˺\" completes the estate with its terminus—the return-clause this Book sounds at every movement's close (Surah 2, verse 210's ending; verse 83 of this chapter's cosmic census)—and its pairing with the ownership-clause is the verse's full architecture, the commentators draw: to Him belongs everything now, and to Him returns everything then: the estate's two tenses: present possession and final convergence—all affairs, umur: the disputes the chapter has hosted (the natures, the patriarchs, the ways), the campaigns and counter-plans (verses 54, 99, 120's coming schemes), the community's own unity kept or torn (verses 103 to 105): every open file travelling, by this clause, toward the one Desk; and the return's placement directly after the justice-clause completes the syllogism the two verses build: He wrongs no one (108), owns everything (this verse's first clause), and will receive every matter back (its second)—the three clauses together being the Day's whole guarantee: a Judge incorruptible by need, a jurisdiction total, and a docket from which nothing escapes.\n\nThe verse's function at this exact station is the passage's hinge, the scholars note: behind it, the charter-passage sealed (verse 108's certification); ahead, the community's commissioning at its most famous sentence (verse 110: \"you are the best community ever raised for humanity\")—and the hinge's teaching is the commission's preparation: the community about to be named humanity's best is first shown the estate it serves in: not its own franchise (the entitlement-disease this argument buried at every station: Surah 2, verse 111; verse 24 of this chapter) but His property—the best-community office held within the heavens' and earth's one ownership, its every matter returning, like all matters, to the Owner for audit; the reader crossing this hinge thus arrives at the great commission already inoculated: whatever \"best\" is about to mean, it will mean best at serving the estate's Owner—and the next verse defines it in exactly those terms: \"you encourage good, forbid evil, and believe in Allah.\"",
  "110": "\"You are the best community ever raised for humanity\" delivers the commissioning-sentence the whole passage prepared (verses 108 and 109's hinge)—and its grammar must be read with the scholars' care, for every word carries load: kuntum khayra ummatin ukhrijat lin-nas: the best community brought forth—ukhrijat: raised, extracted, produced: the passive of divine action: the community not self-made but brought out (the same election-grammar as verse 33's chosen households: honour by the Chooser's act); and \"for humanity\"—lin-nas: the dative that converts the honour into office: best for the peoples: the community produced not above humanity as its aristocracy but toward humanity as its servant-corps (Surah 2, verse 143's commissioning: \"witnesses over humanity\"): the superlative naming a function, not a race—whence the verse's own next clause, the commentators press, states the ranking's entire basis before any pride can form.\n\n\"You encourage good, forbid evil, and believe in Allah\" publishes the best-ness's three credentials—and their order is the verse's most studied feature, the scholars unfold: the two offices of verse 104's commissioned party (the ma'ruf commanded, the munkar forbidden) here precede even the belief-clause: not because works outrank faith (belief is the ground of all: Surah 2, verse 25's pairing) but because the offices are the community's distinguishing mark among believing communities: others believed; this one was raised to carry the commanding-and-forbidding to the nations (the immune-system of verse 104 made a world-mission)—the verse thus being, in the scholars' settled reading, conditional in its very structure: the best-ness holds while the credentials hold: Umar ibn al-Khattab's transmitted ruling on this verse—whoever would join the honour must meet its condition—standing as the community's own audit-clause; and the Prophet, peace be upon him, gave the ranking its census: \"you are the completion of seventy nations; you are the best of them and the most honoured of them with Allah\" (Jami` at-Tirmidhi, Hadith 3001, graded sound).\n\n\"Had the People of the Book believed, it would have been better for them. Some of them are faithful, but most are rebellious\" completes the verse with the standing comparison—and its two clauses keep this argument's constant justice, the commentators note: the invitation still open (\"had they believed... better for them\": the door of verse 64's common terms unshut even in the commissioning's text: their believing would profit them, not diminish the new office); and the census exact: \"some of them are faithful\"—the mu'minun among them honoured by name (the Abdullah ibn Salams; verse 113's coming portrait: \"they are not all alike\")—\"but most are rebellious\": the fasiqun-classification (verse 82's covenant-breakers) for the majority's turning: the community's commission thus framed, at its very grant, by the predecessor's file: the office transferred from a community whose most broke the covenant to one warned, in the same breath, that the office runs on credentials—and the believer formed by the verse holds its two edges as one: the honour real (the best community, by the Raiser's own sentence), and the honour conditional: best while commanding, forbidding, and believing; the label without the offices being exactly the entitlement this whole argument was revealed to bury.",
  "111": "\"They can never inflict harm on you, except a little annoyance\" issues, directly upon the commissioning (verse 110), the commissioned community's threat-assessment—and its intelligence must be read against its hour, the scholars note: the verse descended with Madinah ringed: the scripture-communities' leadership campaigning (verse 99's crookedness-projection), the reversion-operations running (verses 72 and 100), the alliances forming toward the sieges—and revelation's assessment of the entire apparatus: lan yadurrukum illa adhan: they will never harm you except hurt's surface: adha—the annoyance-word: the tongue's campaigns, the mockery and manufactured doubt, the slanders and taunts (the word's own usage at verse 186 ahead: \"you will certainly hear many hurtful words\")—real pain, the Book does not deny it, but categorically bounded: harm's surface, never its substance: the community's faith, mission, and existence beyond the campaigns' reach (Surah 2, verse 105's blocked blessings-envy; the Guardian-clause of verse 68 standing over the assessment).\n\n\"But if they meet you in battle, they will flee and they will have no helpers\" extends the assessment from the war of words to the war itself—and the forecast is unhedged: should the campaigning leadership escalate to the field, \"they will turn their backs\": flight, not conquest; \"and they will have no helpers\": the alliance-apparatus dissolving at the test (the no-helpers clause, Surah 2, verse 270's file, here forecast in this world's register)—and history filed the verification within the first hearers' sight, the commentators record: the confrontations that followed—Qaynuqa's siege, Nadir's expulsion, the later engagements—ran, each one, on this verse's script: the walls yielded, the allies failed to march (Surah 59, verses 11 and 12's documented desertion: the hypocrites' promised aid never leaving their houses), the fighting-forecast \"they will flee\" executed to the letter: the verse standing thereafter among the Book's public predictions banked (Surah 2, verse 24's class: the unmatched challenge; verse 12 of this chapter's \"soon you will be overpowered\").\n\nThe assessment's function in the commission's architecture is the scholars' drawn lesson: the best-community office (verse 110) required exactly this intelligence at its grant—for the office's work (the commanding, forbidding, and calling among the nations) is paralysed wherever its bearers overprice their opponents' reach: communities that mistake annoyance for existential threat trade their mission for their nerves (the yielding-warning of verse 100); and the verse's calibration teaches the permanent posture: the adha priced honestly (it will come, it will sting: verse 186 will command the patience for it), the harm-ceiling held certainly (the campaigns cannot touch the essence), and the field left unfeared (the forecast stands)—the commissioned community walking into its work, by this verse's equipment, neither naive about the hostility nor governed by it; while the next verse will read the assessment's other side: the campaigners' own condition—the disgrace-decree, its two lifelines, and the earned wrath their history invoiced.",
  "112": "\"They will be stricken with disgrace wherever they go, unless they are protected by a covenant with Allah or a treaty with the people\" turns the threat-assessment (verse 111) to the campaigners' own condition—and the decree's structure carries its exact justice, the scholars unfold: duribat alayhim adh-dhillah: the disgrace struck upon them: the humiliation-decree this argument first filed at Surah 2, verse 61 (\"stricken with disgrace and misery\") over the same charges, here re-issued with its geography (\"wherever they go\": the condition travelling with its bearers) and—the verse's studied mercy—its two published exceptions: \"a covenant with Allah or a treaty with the people\": the rope from Allah and the rope from men: protection available, always, by covenant: the dhimmah-architecture the community's law would build from this clause (the protected peoples' security under treaty: their worship, wealth, and blood guarded—the Najran settlement of verse 61's aftermath the standing model); the decree thus never racial nor total: a condition attached to a course, exited by covenant as the course itself is exited by faith (verse 110's open door).\n\n\"They have invited the displeasure of Allah and have been branded with misery for rejecting Allah's revelations and murdering ˹His˺ prophets unjustly\" files the decree's causes—and the file is the argument's oldest, re-entered verbatim, the commentators note: the two charges of Surah 2, verse 61's identical sentence: the signs rejected (the recognition suppressed: verse 70's witnessing-while-denying) and the prophets slain (the file of Surah 2, verse 91; verse 21 of this chapter's three-count indictment: the killed messengers, the killed justice-advocates)—\"unjustly\" marking, as at every sounding, crimes condemned by the killers' own law; the earned wrath (the ghadab of the Fatihah's warning-column: Surah 1, verse 7's \"those You are displeased with\") and the branded misery being, by the verse's own grammar, invoiced: ba'u—they returned with, brought back upon themselves: the wrath carried home as earning (Surah 2, verse 90's \"wrath upon wrath\"), never as arbitrary affliction.\n\n\"This is ˹a fair reward˺ for their disobedience and violations\" closes the decree with the causation-clause that completes Surah 2, verse 61's sentence word for word—\"that is because they disobeyed and used to transgress\"—and the verbatim return is the Book's own cross-referencing, the scholars draw: the same file, opened in the second surah's historical review, closed here in the third's living assessment: the descent traced there (from bored ingratitude to prophet-blood: the commentary at Surah 2, verse 61) certified here as the standing condition its terminus produced; and the verse's permanent jurisprudence holds the file's every boundary against misuse: the decree tracks conduct, not blood (the exceptions by covenant; the faithful of the same communities honoured in the very next verse's \"they are not all alike\"); its executor is Allah, not the mob (the community's hand bound by the treaty-clause and the law); and its mirror faces the successor-community too, the commentators press: the charges that invoiced the wrath—signs rejected, truth's advocates attacked, disobedience habituated—are conducts, available to any people, this one included (Surah 2, verse 61's own closing warning): the decree's lesson being never the neighbour's condition but the course that produces it—while the census-verse now arrives to guard the file's justice in full: \"yet they are not all alike.\"",
  "113": "\"Yet they are not all alike\" arrives directly upon the disgrace-decree (verse 112) as its guarding clause—and the arrival's timing is the Book's justice performing itself, the scholars press: no sooner has the file's severest sentence been read than the census-verse interrupts: laysu sawa'an: they are not one mass: the People of the Book not a bloc but a spectrum—the clause this argument has kept at every indictment (Surah 2, verse 100's \"a group of them\"; verse 75 of this chapter's gold-returning trustworthy; verse 110's \"some of them are faithful\") here given its own verse and its own portrait: the Quran refusing, at the polemic's very peak, the collective slander every polemic breeds; and the occasion the commentators transmit fixes the portrait's names: when Abdullah ibn Salam and his companions embraced Islam, the rabbis declared \"none believed in Muhammad but our worst\"—and this passage descended in the converts' defence: the census-verse revealed, by its own occasion, as heaven's testimonial for named individuals against their former community's spite.\n\n\"There are some among the People of the Book who are upright, who recite Allah's revelations throughout the night, prostrating ˹in prayer˺\" paints the honoured column—and each stroke is chosen, the commentators unfold: ummatun qa'imah: an upright community—standing: the word of steadfast uprightness (the qiyam of both posture and principle): a community within the community, standing where their people bent; \"recite Allah's revelations throughout the night\"—ana'a al-layl: through the night's hours: the portrait's centre being the night-recitation: the sincere of the Book-people known, as the sincere are always known in this argument, by their unwitnessed hours (verse 17's dawn-forgiveness-seekers; Surah 2, verse 274's night-givers)—the revelations recited being, in their season, their own Book truly read (the following-as-it-should-be-followed of Surah 2, verse 121: the reading that carried its readers to the confirmed Messenger), and, upon their believing, this Book joined to it; \"prostrating\": the sujud completing the portrait at worship's floor (verse 43's command to Maryam: the same three-fold regimen—devotion, prostration, company).\n\nThe portrait's placement completes the passage's architecture, the scholars draw: verses 110 through 115 form one balanced instrument—the commission (110), the assessment (111), the decree (112), and now the census with its honoured column (113 to 115): the community handed its office with the predecessor's file whole: the majority's rebellion priced, the minority's uprightness honoured in the same breath—and the instrument's permanent training is the believer's twin discipline: the course-verdicts held without the bloc-verdicts (the decree of 112 never flattened over the standing worshippers of 113), and the honoured column's criteria noted for one's own audit: uprightness, night-recitation, prostration—the credentials that transferred their holders, from inside the indicted community, onto the honour-roll the next verse completes: \"they believe in Allah and the Last Day... and race with one another in doing good: they are truly among the righteous.\"",
  "114": "\"They believe in Allah and the Last Day, encourage good and forbid evil, and race with one another in doing good\" continues the honoured column's portrait (verse 113's night-reciters)—and its credential-list is the verse's quiet thunderclap, the scholars note: the credentials are, item for item, the best-community's own (verse 110's three: belief in Allah, the good commanded, the evil forbidden—here with the Last Day named and the racing added): the upright of the People of the Book certified, by the commission-verse's exact criteria, as holders of the commissioned qualities: the passage's architecture thus completing its justice: verse 110 granted the office by credentials, not lineage—and verse 114 demonstrates the grant's honesty: wherever the credentials appear, the honour attaches, the former community's label no bar (as the label \"Muslim\" without them is no pass: the conditional best-ness of verse 110's commentary); these believers being, by their believing, of the community itself now—their portrait kept under the People-of-the-Book heading, the commentators draw, precisely to complete the census-argument: the door of verse 110 (\"had the People of the Book believed\") shown walked-through by the walkers.\n\n\"And race with one another in doing good\" adds the credential beyond the commission's floor—yusari'una fil-khayrat: they hasten in good works: the racing-verb of Surah 2, verse 148's command (\"compete with one another in doing good\") and verse 133's coming summons (\"hasten towards forgiveness\"): the converts portrayed not as arrivals resting at the threshold but as competitors already outstripping—the historical exhibit standing in the occasion's own names (verse 113's commentary): Abdullah ibn Salam, whose testimony the Book itself cites (Surah 46, verse 10), racing from recognition to declaration to the community's front ranks; the racing-credential's placement teaching, the scholars press, the convert-doctrine this community was formed on: entry's lateness bars no rank (the earnings-rule of Surah 2, verse 134: each soul's own ledger)—the last to enter may outrun the first, for the track is deeds, not tenure.\n\n\"They are ˹truly˺ among the righteous\" seals the portrait with the filing-clause this Book reserves for its honour-registry: min as-salihin—among the righteous: the company-roll where Ibrahim rests (Surah 2, verse 130), where Yahya and Isa were filed at their annunciations (verses 39 and 46): the night-reciters of the former community seated, by name of category, in the same registry as the prophets their recitation followed—the seal's weight being its company, the commentators note: no higher shelf exists in the Book's honours than the salihin's, and the verse assigns it to converts the rabbis had called \"our worst\" (verse 113's occasion): heaven's registry overruling the community's spite by one clause; and the believer formed by the three-verse portrait (113 to 115) carries its permanent yields: the census-discipline (no bloc-verdicts: the standing worshippers honoured inside the indicted people), the credential-doctrine (the honours attach to qualities, wherever found), and the racing-summons turned homeward: the portrait's competitors, entering late and running hard, standing as the standing rebuke of every born-believer's amble—while the next verse banks the column's every deed: \"they will never be denied the reward for any good they have done.\"",
  "115": "\"They will never be denied the reward for any good they have done\" banks the honoured column's account (verses 113 and 114's portrait)—and the banking-clause's construction is its mercy, the scholars unfold: falan yukfaruh: it will never be denied them—the kufr-verb in its rejection-sense turned to accounting: their good will never be met with denial: no deed of the night-reciters and racers refused at the deposit, none shaved at the payment (the full-discharge doctrine of Surah 2, verse 281; verse 57's \"rewarded in full\")—and the clause's specific comfort is its occasion's, the commentators draw: the converts of verse 113's setting (Abdullah ibn Salam's circle) had crossed communities: their former people declaring their ruin, their new deeds beginning past a long former life—and the verse settles both anxieties in one clause: whatever good, whenever done: the pre-conversion uprightness (the night-recitation of their Torah truly read: the following that carried them across, Surah 2, verse 121) and the post-conversion racing banked together with the Knower—the double-reward doctrine the Prophet, peace be upon him, published for exactly this column: \"three will be given their reward twice\"—among them the man of the People of the Book \"who believed in his prophet and then believed in me\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 97; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 154): the crossing not a ledger's reset but its multiplication.\n\n\"And Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of those mindful ˹of Him˺\" seals the banking with the audit-clause at its warmest register—wallahu alimun bil-muttaqin: Knowing of the God-fearing—and the seal's selection completes the census-passage's whole design, the scholars press: the muttaqin-word: the chapter's and the Book's watchword (Surah 2, verse 2's audience) here naming the honoured column by the quality that transferred them: taqwa—the guarding mindfulness that read their own Book honestly, priced their community's spite below their Lord's pleasure, and crossed; and the knowledge-clause performing, for them, the protection every crossing minority needs: known to Allah—their sincerity beyond their slanderers' reach (the rabbis' \"our worst\" answered by the Knower's registry: verse 114's salihin-seal), their unwitnessed night-hours filed (Surah 2, verse 273's banking for the unreceipted), their standing secured at the only Desk that assigns standings.\n\nThe verse's closing function, standing back, completes the six-verse instrument (110 to 115) at its balance-point, the commentators draw: the commission granted on credentials (110), the threat bounded (111), the course-decree filed with its exits (112), the census guarded (113), the credentials demonstrated portable (114), and the account banked with the Knower (115)—the whole teaching one jurisprudence of communities: honours and decrees track qualities and courses, never bloods and blocs; and the passage's mirror, as ever, faces its holders last: the community commissioned at 110 reads, at 115's seal, the registry it too is filed in—bil-muttaqin: by mindfulness, not membership; while the argument now turns, in the next verses, to the column the census's other side: the disbelievers' unavailing wealth and children, and the bitter-wind parable over their harvest.",
  "116": "\"Indeed, neither the wealth nor children of the disbelievers will be of any benefit to them against Allah\" turns the census-passage from its honoured column (verses 113 to 115) to its other side—and the turning-verse's text is the chapter's own earlier assessment re-issued verbatim, the scholars note: verse 10's confiscation-clause word for word: the two securities (wealth and children: property and posterity, the world's whole portfolio of verse 14's magnetisms) ruled valueless \"against Allah\"—and the verbatim return at this station is deliberate architecture, the commentators draw: verse 10 opened the chapter's first assessment of the imposing fronts (the funded delegations, the confident tribes); this verse re-enters the clause after the census has done its work: the fronts now precisely divided—the upright column banked with the Knower (verse 115), the remainder holding, against the audit, exactly what verse 10 priced: assets that do not cross (Surah 2, verse 254's closed doors; the earth's fill of gold refused, verse 91): the repetition teaching by its placement that the confiscation-rule brackets the census: whoever stands outside the credentials (verses 113 and 114's) stands, whatever his holdings, inside this clause.\n\n\"It is they who will be the residents of the Fire. They will be there forever\" completes the re-issued assessment with the residence-formula (Surah 2, verse 39's seal; verse 88's terms)—and the pairing of the two clauses is the verse's whole economics, the scholars unfold: the assets that availed nothing, and the abode their holders purchased anyway: the portfolio's final conversion (verse 10's fuel-clause: \"they will be the fuel for the Fire\" there; the residence-clause here): wealth and children—the two extensions by which mortality consoles itself: the fortune outlasting its maker, the sons carrying the name—exposed at the audit as the disbeliever's two great mis-investments: neither ransoming (Surah 2, verse 48), neither interceding (the presumed advocacies of Surah 2, verse 255's court), both left at the border while their holder crosses alone (Surah 6, verse 94's arrival: \"you have come to Us all alone... and We do not see with you your intercessors\").\n\nThe verse's function at its station, standing back, is the passage's justice completed in both directions, the commentators press: the census forbade the bloc-slander (verse 113's guard)—and equally forbids the bloc-comfort: the same communities' faithful were honoured by credentials, and the same communities' rejecters are priced by conduct: the two verdicts one jurisprudence (qualities and courses, never bloods): and the confiscation-clause's mirror, as at verse 10's commentary, faces every reader's own portfolio last: whatever is being held \"against Allah\"—the wealth trusted past the Owner, the heirs trusted past the grave, the standing trusted past the audit—stands under this verse's assessment whatever the holder's label; while the parable now follows (verse 117) to price the rejecters' remaining asset—their good deeds themselves—at the bitter wind's arithmetic: the harvest of a people who wronged themselves, destroyed at the season's edge.",
  "117": "\"The good they do in this worldly life is like the harvest of an evil people struck by a bitter wind, destroying it ˹completely˺\" prices the rejecters' remaining asset (verse 116 confiscated their wealth and children; this verse audits their deeds)—and the parable's construction answers the question the census-passage leaves standing, the scholars unfold: the disbelievers of the file were not without expenditures in goodness: the charities endowed, the kin maintained, the guests honoured (the pagan lords' famed munificence; the scripture-establishment's alms and offices)—what becomes of such spending at the audit? The parable's answer: it is as a harth—a tillage: a crop raised through the season—\"of a people who wronged themselves\" (the zulm-clause carrying the file's diagnosis: the self-wronging of shirk and rejection, Surah 31, verse 13's worst injustice)—struck, at the season's edge, by a wind \"with sirr in it\": the bitter frost-wind: the burning cold that blackens a standing crop in one night: the year's labour real, the growth real, the destruction total—the deeds performed but unbanked: for the expenditure lacked the one condition that converts works to harvest: the faith that addresses them to Allah (Surah 2, verse 264's bare-rock parallel: the display-soil washed to stone; verse 22 of this chapter's wasted deeds; Surah 25, verse 23's scattered dust)—the parable's justice being its exactness, the commentators press: the wind destroys the crop, not the field's history: the works happened, benefited their worldly recipients, earned their worldly returns (the reputation, the reciprocity: \"whoever does good, it is repaid him in the world's register\"—and the Prophet, peace be upon him, answered Aishah's question about the generous pagan judge Ibn Jud'an: his feeding and freeing avail him nothing, \"for he never said: my Lord, forgive me my sins on the Day of Judgment\"—Sahih Muslim, Hadith 214: the hadith this parable's own case-file: the deed's eternity requiring the Day held in view).\n\n\"Allah never wronged them, but they wronged themselves\" closes the parable with the solvency-clause the passage opened on (verse 108's \"Allah desires no injustice\")—and the closing's placement is the audit's signature, the scholars draw: the frost-wind's arithmetic could read, to the destroyed, as heaven's harshness: a lifetime's goodness voided; the clause files the destruction's cause where the parable's own text placed it: \"a people who wronged themselves\"—the self-wronging preceding the wind: the deeds were sown in the wronged soil of rejection, and soil decides harvests (Surah 2, verses 264 and 265's two grounds under one rain): He wronged them nothing—their works' worldly wages were paid in full where addressed (the world), and their eternal voidness follows their own addressing; the parable thus completing the census-passage's two-column economics at perfect symmetry, the commentators note: the honoured column's every good banked undeniable (verse 115's \"never be denied\"), the rejecting column's every good frost-struck at the border—one Auditor, two soils, and the difference never the deeds' size but their address; while the argument now turns from the columns to the community's own guarding: the intimates not to be taken from those whose rage the next verse unveils—\"they would not miss a chance to harm you.\"",
  "118": "\"O believers! Do not associate closely with others who would not miss a chance to harm you\" legislates the community's inner-circle law—and its key term must be read at its original depth, the scholars unfold: bitanah—the garment's lining: the word for the confidant worn against the skin: the counsellor admitted to secrets, the adviser inside the deliberations, the intimate before whom guards drop—the verse forbidding not acquaintance, trade, or kindness across communities (all protected: Surah 60, verse 8's charter; the treaty-peoples' rights, verse 112) but the lining-intimacy granted to those the clause then diagnoses: \"min dunikum\"—from outside yourselves: outside the faith's bond—whose disqualifying mark is not their creed alone but their documented disposition: \"they would not miss a chance to harm you\": la ya'lunakum khabalan: they spare you no ruin: the corruption-effort unstinting; \"their only desire is to see you suffer\": waddu ma anittum: they wish your distress—the wish-verb of the campaign-file (Surah 2, verse 109; verse 69 of this chapter): the verse's occasion, the commentators transmit, being believers who kept their old confidencies with the hypocrites' circles and the hostile factions' men—the lining worn from before Islam, unexamined after it.\n\n\"Their prejudice has become evident from what they say—and what their hearts hide is far worse\" supplies the law's evidence-clause—and its intelligence-doctrine is the verse's gift, the scholars note: qad badati l-baghda'u min afwahihim: the hatred has shown from their mouths: the tongue leaks—the slips of rage, the mockeries half-swallowed, the \"annoyance\" of verse 111's campaigns: enough surfaces to read the current; \"and what their chests conceal is greater\": the visible leakage certified as the lesser fraction—the believer taught to price the iceberg by its tip, never to excuse the tip by the submerged mass's invisibility; \"We have made Our revelations clear to you, if only you understood\" closing the verse at the reason-summons (Surah 2, verse 242's formula): the law's clarity delivered; the understanding—the reading of linings by their leaks—the hearer's remaining share.\n\nThe law's permanent jurisprudence, standing back, holds its boundaries as the scholars drew them: the verse governs the lining, not the neighbourhood: the community's life with other communities remains open at every lawful register (the Prophet's own dealings, pledged armour and honoured treaties: Surah 2, verse 283's commentary)—closed only at the counsel-chamber's door against the documented ill-wisher; and its mirror-clause, the commentators press, is the diagnostic the believer carries: the disqualifier was never the outsider's label but the harm-wish's evidence—whence the law reads inward too: the labelled insider whose tongue leaks the same baghda' (the hypocrites' file: Surah 2, verse 14's private rage) stands equally outside the lining's trust; the garment's lining chosen, in every era, by one criterion: those who wish the wearer whole—while the next verse will hold the mirror to the believers' own unreciprocated warmth: \"here you are! You love them but they do not love you.\"",
  "119": "\"Here you are! You love them but they do not love you, and you believe in all Scriptures\" holds the mirror the confidants-law (verse 118) required—and its exposed asymmetry is the verse's whole diagnosis, the scholars unfold: ha antum ula'i: here you are, you people—the Book's finger pointed at its own community's warmth: tuhibbunahum wa la yuhibbunakum: you love them, they love you not: the believers' affection real (the old ties, the kinships, the hoped conversions: the love this argument nowhere condemns in itself) and unreturned—and the asymmetry's root named in the next clause with quiet devastation: \"and you believe in all Scriptures\": the believers' creed embraces the others' Books entire (Surah 2, verse 136's charter; verse 84's recitation)—while the others' creeds reject the believers' one: the affection-gap thus structural, not circumstantial: the party of the wider creed loves across a line the party of the narrower creed cannot love back across (Surah 2, verse 120's ceiling: \"never will they be pleased with you until you follow their faith\")—the verse teaching, the commentators press, the sobriety verse 118's law runs on: linings are chosen by reciprocity's evidence, and here the evidence is read aloud.\n\n\"When they meet you they say, 'We believe.' But when alone, they bite their fingertips in rage\" documents the asymmetry's concealed half—and the scene is the hypocrites' file re-entered with its most visceral image, the scholars note: the two-face protocol of Surah 2, verse 14 (\"when they meet the believers they say: we believe\") completed here by the private chamber's posture: addu alaykumu l-anamila mina l-ghayz: they bite their fingertips at you from rage: the gnawed knuckle: fury's oldest gesture—the community's growth, concord, and victories (verse 120's grief-schedule) chewed on in the closed rooms; and the commanded answer follows: \"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ '˹May you˺ die of your rage!'\"—qul mutu bi-ghayzikum: the Book's rarest register: the imprecation-imperative: not a curse flung in temper but a sentence published as forecast, the commentators draw: the rage will not be relieved—its objects will grow, its wishes will fail (verse 111's ceiling), and the raging will consume its containers: die in it; \"Surely Allah knows best what is ˹hidden˺ in the heart\" sealing the scene at the audit-clause (Surah 2, verse 77): the bitten knuckles of the private chambers filed with the chest-Knower.\n\nThe verse's permanent equipment, standing back, is the community's emotional jurisprudence, the scholars press: the believers are not commanded out of their love (the affection for kin and neighbour stands; the hoped guidance stands: Surah 2, verse 272's open charity across the line) but out of their naivety: love's warmth never again mistaken for love's return, the lining-trust (verse 118) never again extended on the strength of one's own feelings toward the other—the asymmetry read, priced, and lived with: warmth outward, wits intact; and the rage-sentence's comfort completes the equipment: the concealed fury that so unsettles the discovering believer (\"they smiled at us and gnawed their fists at us\") is, by this verse, already known, already sentenced, and already working on its own containers—the community's part being only the patience and taqwa the next verse names as the schemes' complete insulation: \"if you are patient and mindful of Allah, their schemes will not harm you in the least.\"",
  "120": "\"When you ˹believers˺ are touched with good, they grieve; but when you are afflicted with evil, they rejoice\" completes the concealed-enmity file (verses 118 and 119) with its diagnostic instrument—and the instrument's precision is the scholars' noted gift: the two-clause schedule: your good their grief, your harm their joy: envy's exact fingerprint (the diagnosis of Surah 2, verse 109; verse 105's blocked-blessing wish)—and its diagnostic power being its involuntariness, the commentators draw: speeches are managed, greetings staged (verse 119's \"we believe\"), but the flinch at the believers' good news and the light in the eye at their setback escape management: the schedule reads hearts through their reflexes—the verse thus handing the community the lining-law's (verse 118) field-test: the confidant-candidate is read not at his professions but at the arrival of your good and your evil: whom your blessing quietly pains, the lining excludes.\n\n\"˹Yet,˺ if you are patient and mindful ˹of Allah˺, their schemes will not harm you in the least\" delivers the file's closing equipment—and its two conditions are the argument's oldest pair at their most operational, the scholars unfold: in tasbiru wa tattaqu: if you are patient and God-fearing: sabr and taqwa—the endurance that absorbs the campaigns' annoyance (verse 111's adha priced) without breaking formation, and the mindfulness that keeps the community's own conduct inside Allah's bounds under provocation (the retaliation-governor of Surah 2, verse 194)—and the conditions' yield stated as absolute insulation: la yadurrukum kayduhum shay'an: their scheming will not harm you at all: the kayd—the whole apparatus: the reversion-operations (verse 72), the crookedness-projection (verse 99), the Bu'ath-poems (verse 100), the bitten-knuckle councils (verse 119)—rendered harmless not by the community's counter-scheming but by its character: patience and taqwa as armour-plate: the doctrine this Book states as physics, the commentators press: the schemes' only entry-points into a believing community are its impatience (the provoked overreaction, the formation broken at the taunt) and its taqwa-lapses (the sins that forfeit the Guardian's shielding: Uhud's coming lesson at verse 152)—seal the two doors, and the apparatus spends itself on the armour (Surah 2, verse 137's promise: \"Allah will spare you their evil\").\n\n\"Surely Allah is Fully Aware of what they do\" seals the file with the encirclement-clause—wallaha bima ya'maluna muhit: Allah encompasses what they do: the muhit-word of Surah 2, verse 19's storm-scene (\"Allah encompasses the disbelievers\"): the schemers' whole theatre—the councils, the schedules of grief and joy, the operations planned and pending—contained inside the Awareness they plot beneath; and the verse's placement closes the pre-Uhud file at the exact seam, the scholars note: the next verse steps directly onto Uhud's morning (\"when you left your home in the early morning to position the believers\")—the battle in which every clause of this file would be field-tested: the enemy's rejoicing at the believers' wound, the hypocrites' desertion, and the insulation-conditions' proof in both directions: harmed where patience and taqwa lapsed (the archers' hill, verse 152), and preserved, through the wound itself, wherever the two held (Hamra al-Asad's response, verse 172)—the file thus standing as Uhud's issued briefing: read before the march, and graded on the mountain.",
  "121": "\"˹Remember, O Prophet,˺ when you left your home in the early morning to position the believers in the battlefield\" opens the Quran's longest battle-commentary—the Uhud passage that will run to verse 179—and its opening frame is the morning of the third year's Shawwal, the scholars set from the sirah: Quraysh had marched three thousand strong to avenge Badr (Surah 2, verse 50's commentary; verse 13's sign-reading), camping by Mount Uhud north of Madinah; the Prophet, peace be upon him, had inclined to defend from within the city—strengthened by a dream he read as loss touching his companions and household—but the eager (many who had missed Badr's honour) pressed for the open field, and he armed; when their eagerness cooled to regret, he answered with the prophethood's own finality: \"it is not for a prophet, once he has put on his armour, to take it off until Allah judges\" (the sirah's transmitted scene at Ibn Ishaq, cited by the commentators): and so the morning this verse frames: the leaving of the household at dawn, the seven hundred marched out (three hundred deserting under Abdullah ibn Ubayy at the road—verse 167's coming file), and the verse's named work: tubawwi'u l-mu'minina maqa'ida lil-qital: positioning the believers in battle-stations: the Messenger as field-commander: the ranks dressed, the fifty archers set on the hill with the order that would decide the day—\"guard our backs; do not leave your post whether you see us win or see us slain\" (the order's transcript at Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3039, from al-Bara).\n\n\"And Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing\" seals the morning-frame with the audit-pairing—and its selection over the battle's threshold is the passage's issued key, the commentators draw: All-Hearing—of the night's deliberations just concluded: the counsel taken and given (the shura the Prophet honoured though his own reading differed: the consultation-doctrine verse 159 will canonise from this very campaign), the eager voices, the deserters' murmurs forming; All-Knowing—of the day about to unfold as no participant knew it: the victory's first hour, the hill's emptying, the reversal, the wound, and the lessons the passage will spend sixty verses extracting: the battle entered, by this seal, under complete audit—every order heard, every heart known—so that the commentary to come carries the authority of the only Witness who missed nothing on the mountain.\n\nThe opening verse's function, standing back, is the passage's method announced, the scholars press: Uhud will be taught not as chronicle but as curriculum—the Book's treatment beginning at the commander's morning (leadership's burdens: the dream, the counsel, the armour's finality) because the day's every lesson hangs from its stations: the positions assigned (this verse) and abandoned (verse 152), the trust commanded (verse 122's coming reassurance) and tested; and the believer reading the passage is enrolled, by its first frame, in its standing course: the community's setbacks examined under All-Hearing, All-Knowing—causes owned, mercies counted, and the formation re-dressed for the next morning.",
  "122": "\"˹Remember˺ when two groups among you ˹believers˺ were about to cower, then Allah reassured them\" preserves the march's first crisis—and its case is fixed in the Sahih by its own subjects, the scholars note: the two groups—Banu Salimah of the Khazraj and Banu Harithah of the Aws, the army's two wings—wavered when Abdullah ibn Ubayy broke away at the road with his three hundred (verse 121's commentary): the desertion's contagion touching the wings with the thought of turning back—an tafshala: to lose heart, to cower—\"then Allah reassured them\": wallahu waliyyuhuma: and Allah was the Guardian of the two: the wavering arrested not by argument or shame but by the wilayah itself: the Guardian's hand on the two hearts (Surah 2, verse 257's covenant operating on a road to battle); and Jabir ibn Abdillah—himself of Banu Salimah—published the verse's reception among its subjects: \"it was revealed concerning us, and I would not wish it unrevealed, for Allah says: 'and Allah is the Guardian of the two'\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 4558; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2505): the named tribes treasuring the verse that recorded their near-fall—because it recorded, in the same clause, their Patron.\n\nThe case's jurisprudence is the passage's first lesson, the commentators unfold: the hamm—the inclination entertained: the two wings did not turn back; they weighed it—and revelation's treatment of the weighing teaches the community its whole psychology of wavering: the inclination recorded without concealment (the Book's honesty about its best: Surah 2, verse 214's crying Messenger), attributed to its trigger (the desertion's pull: the fitnah of numbers this argument prices at Talut's river, Surah 2, verse 249), arrested by grace (the reassurance His act, not their achievement: verse 8's prayer-doctrine: hearts held, not self-holding)—and converted, by the verse's own publication, into honour: Jabir's delight being the case's deepest teaching: the community formed by this Book does not bury its near-failures; it recites them—for the near-failure's record carries the Guardian's name, and shame is a small price for that clause.\n\n\"So in Allah let the believers put their trust\" closes the case with its extracted law—wa ala llahi falyatawakkali l-mu'minun: upon Allah let the believers rely—and the tawakkul-command's placement is its precision, the scholars draw: issued at the exact seam the crisis exposed: the wings had leaned, unawares, on the army's numbers—and numbers deserted; the reassurance re-seated their weight on the only load-bearing point (the Guardian who does not desert), and the closing command generalises the re-seating for every believer's every formation: reliance placed where desertions cannot reach it—the doctrine the whole Uhud-passage will hammer at its stations (verse 160's coming charter: \"if Allah helps you, none can defeat you\") and the Prophet's community carried as its marching-creed: \"sufficient for us is Allah, and He is the best Disposer of affairs\" (verse 173's coming sentence, banked at Hamra al-Asad)—while the passage now turns, before the day's wound, to the memory issued as its medicine: \"indeed, Allah made you victorious at Badr when you were vastly outnumbered.\"",
  "123": "\"Indeed, Allah made you victorious at Badr when you were ˹vastly˺ outnumbered\" issues, at the Uhud-commentary's threshold, the memory the day's wound would need—and the issued exhibit is the community's own founding proof, the scholars unfold: Badr—the two armies' meeting the chapter already entered as evidence (verse 13's sign)—here named by name with its condition attached: wa antum adhillah: while you were lowly: the outnumbered, under-mounted three hundred and some (Talut's census, Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3958's count) against the thousand: the victory delivered at strength's minimum precisely so its Deliverer could never be misread (verse 13's deciding variable: \"Allah supports with His victory whoever He wills\")—and the exhibit's timing is the passage's pastoral art, the commentators draw: placed before the reversal's narration (verses 152 onward), the Badr-memory stands as the wound's pre-issued medicine: the community about to study its defeat is first re-anchored in its victory—the same Lord, the same field-mathematics, the day's difference to be sought (the passage will locate it at verse 152) in the community's own conduct, never in the Helper's capacity.\n\n\"So be mindful of Allah, perhaps you will be grateful\" attaches to the memory its two commanded responses—and their selection is the exhibit's whole conversion into curriculum, the scholars note: fattaqu llaha: so fear Allah—taqwa as Badr's lesson-in-chief: the victory's condition read backward: the three hundred prevailed as the obedient, praying, steadfast (Surah 2, verse 250's three petitions granted); the taqwa-command thus being the victory's maintenance-clause: the community keeps Badr's Helper by keeping Badr's character (the insulation-doctrine of verse 120: patience and taqwa as the schemes' armour—and, the passage will teach, as the field's); \"perhaps you will be grateful\": la'allakum tashkurun—gratitude as the memory's owed currency (Surah 2, verse 52's clause; verse 103's remembered favour): the Badr-grace acknowledged, credited, and lived—for ingratitude toward rescues, this argument's whole historical file testifies (Surah 2, verse 61's descent), is the road by which rescued communities walk back to their perils.\n\nThe verse's function in the passage's architecture, standing back, is the teaching-sequence's first station, the commentators press: the Uhud-curriculum will proceed by paired days—Badr recalled (verses 123 to 127), Uhud examined (152 onward), the two set side by side at verse 140's coming law (\"We alternate these days among people\")—and this verse fixes the pairing's baseline: the community's memory-discipline: victories filed as His deliverances (never the ranks' prowess: the adhillah-clause forbidding it), defeats filed as its own audits (verse 165's coming ruling: \"it is from yourselves\")—the double bookkeeping that keeps a community teachable in both weathers; and the believer formed by the verse carries its personal edition: his own Badrs remembered at his own Uhuds—the past rescues issued, by deliberate recall, as the present wound's medicine, with taqwa and gratitude as the two clauses that keep the Rescuer's pattern running.",
  "124": "\"˹Remember, O Prophet,˺ when you said to the believers, 'Is it not enough that your Lord will send down a reinforcement of three thousand angels for your aid?'\" continues the Badr-memory (verse 123) into its unseen order of battle—and the remembered sentence is the commander's own morale-instrument, the scholars note: the Prophet, peace be upon him, before the outnumbered ranks, publishing the reinforcement-promise as a question: alan yakfiyakum: is it not enough for you?—the interrogative form enlisting the believers' own assent: the promise not merely announced but subscribed: three thousand of the angels, munzalin: sent down: the unseen column committed to the field (Surah 8, verse 9's parallel transcript from Badr's own night: \"I will reinforce you with a thousand angels, rank upon rank\"—the counts read together by the commentators as the promise's stages: the thousand pledged at the crisis, the three and five thousand as the escalation-schedule this verse and the next publish).\n\nThe angelic reinforcement's jurisprudence is the passage's careful doctrine, the scholars unfold: the angels fought at Badr by the texts' testimony (Surah 8, verse 12's \"strike above the necks\"; the Sahih's report of the Companion hearing the whip-crack and the unseen rider's advance before him—Sahih Muslim, Hadith 1763's family)—and the aid's whole theology is fixed by the next verse's coming seal (\"victory comes only from Allah\"): the angels not an independent power but the Power's instruments: their commitment a kindness to the believers' eyes and hearts (verse 126's coming clause: \"only as good news for you and reassurance\"), never a necessity of the Almighty's (who needs no armies: verse 97's un-needing Lord)—the believer taught to read all such means, seen and unseen, as the one Helper's gloves.\n\nThe verse's placement in the Uhud-curriculum carries its teaching-edge, the commentators draw: the Badr-reinforcement remembered at Uhud's threshold poses, silently, the day's hardest question in advance: where were the angels at Uhud?—and the passage's answer is already loading in the next verse's conditions: \"if you are firm and mindful... Allah will reinforce you\": the unseen column's commitment conditional on the ranks' sabr and taqwa (the insulation-pair of verse 120 now revealed as the reinforcement-pair): Badr's ranks held and the heavens marched; Uhud's hill emptied (verse 152's coming audit) and the schedule paused—the doctrine sparing the community every false reading of its wound (the Helper neither absent nor overpowered) and charging it with the true one: the conditions were the community's side of the order of battle; and the believer formed by the pairing carries the standing rule of all divine aid: the reinforcements are real, scheduled, and conditional—firmness and mindfulness being the signals the unseen columns march on.",
  "125": "\"Most certainly, if you ˹believers˺ are firm and mindful ˹of Allah˺ and the enemy launches a sudden attack on you, Allah will reinforce you with five thousand angels designated ˹for battle˺\" publishes the reinforcement-schedule's full terms (verse 124's three thousand escalated)—and the verse's construction is the doctrine, the scholars unfold: the escalation conditional: bala in tasbiru wa tattaqu: yes indeed—if you are steadfast and mindful: the two conditions of verse 120's insulation-law here revealed as the unseen column's marching-orders: sabr—the ranks holding under the shock (the firmness of Surah 2, verse 250's petition: \"make our steps firm\"); taqwa—the conduct inside Allah's bounds under battle's provocations (the governor of Surah 2, verse 194): meet the two, and the enemy's own escalation (\"if they come upon you in their fury\": min fawrihim hadha: in this their rush—Quraysh's vengeance-charge the living case) is met by heaven's: five thousand, musawwimin: designated, marked: the angels distinguished by battle-tokens (the commentators transmitting the marks' descriptions from the early authorities: the turbans' distinguishing bands)—the count's escalations (one, three, five thousand across the texts) being, the scholars draw, the promise's published elasticity: the aid scaled to the crisis, always conditional, never capped.\n\nThe conditional's jurisprudence is the Uhud-curriculum's load-bearing beam, the commentators press: the verse's terms, read against the day's record, become the passage's whole audit in advance: at Badr the conditions held—the ranks firm, the night in prayer (Sahih Muslim, Hadith 1763)—and the columns marched (Surah 8, verse 9's fulfilment); at Uhud the first hour held and victory ran with it—then the hill's fifty broke formation for the spoils (verse 152's coming ruling: \"you disobeyed after He had shown you what you love\"), and the schedule's conditions lapsed with the discipline: the wound following not from the promise's failure but from its terms'—the doctrine sparing the community, at its bleeding, both despairs at once: the Helper unchanged, the schedule intact, the conditions repairable by the repairers (Hamra al-Asad's next-morning march, verse 172, being the repair's first instalment and its immediate crediting).\n\nThe verse's permanent equipment, standing back, serves every generation's outnumbered, the scholars draw: the five-thousand-clause is the community's standing order of battle—the unseen reinforcement neither mythologised into presumption (the conditions forgotten, the aid assumed for every banner: the label-entitlement this argument buries everywhere) nor rationalised into absence (the aid real, the texts' testimony standing): held, instead, at the verse's own grammar: promised, scaled, and conditional—sabr and taqwa the two signals the columns march on; and the believer's personal edition follows the passage's constant translation: his own sudden attacks (the temptation's rush, the crisis's fury) met under the same schedule: firmness and mindfulness at the shock's first instant, and the reinforcement—the steadied heart, the opened way (Surah 65, verse 3's \"whoever is mindful of Allah, He will make a way out for them\")—dispatched by the terms this verse published; while the next verse seals the whole schedule's theology: the angels themselves \"only as good news... and victory comes only from Allah.\"",
  "126": "\"Allah ordained this ˹reinforcement˺ only as good news for you and reassurance for your hearts\" seals the angel-schedule (verses 124 and 125) with its theology—and the seal's restrictive grammar is the doctrine, the scholars unfold: wa ma ja'alahu llahu illa bushra lakum: He did not ordain it except as good news: the three and five thousand, the ranked columns, the battle-marked riders—all of it assigned one function: bushra—glad tidings: the believers' eyes given a promise to hold, \"and reassurance for your hearts\": wa litatma'inna qulubukum: the itminan-word: the heart's settling (Ibrahim's own asking, Surah 2, verse 260: \"so my heart can be reassured\"): the reinforcement ordained, that is, for the ranks' interior—the courage steadied, the outnumbering un-feared—not for the Almighty's arithmetic: the angels not required by the Helper (whose \"Be!\" needs no cavalry: Surah 2, verse 117) but granted to the helped: means dispensed as mercies to human sight, the commentators draw: the doctrine that guards all means-theology in this Book (the same grammar at Surah 8, verse 10 over Badr's thousand, word for word): the instruments honoured, the Instrument-Maker alone leaned on.\n\n\"And victory comes only from Allah—the Almighty, All-Wise\" states the seal's positive half—and its restrictive form completes the guard, the scholars press: wa ma n-nasru illa min indi llah: there is no victory except from Allah's own presence: nasr un-sourced from anything beneath Him—not the angels (His instruments), not the numbers (Badr's were absent: verse 123's adhillah), not the valour (real, and His gift: verse 122's held hearts), not the positions (assigned, and effective only kept: the hill's coming lesson): the clause the whole Uhud-curriculum will grade on—for the day's first hour ran with victory while the conditions held, and the verse's doctrine explains both halves of the day with one source-clause: victory from Him, granted on the schedule's terms (verse 125's conditions), withheld at their lapse (verse 152's audit)—the community thus armed in advance against the field's two idolatries: the victory credited to the ranks (pride's misfiling) or despaired of at the odds (fear's): both dissolved where the source is one.\n\n\"The Almighty, All-Wise\" closes the seal with the chapter's threshold-pairing (verses 4, 6, 62)—and its selection over the battle-passage is the pairing's most operational deployment, the commentators note: al-Aziz—the Almighty: victory's source unconquerable: the nasr drawn from a might no coalition outweighs (the three thousand of Quraysh, the confederates to come: all light against the clause); al-Hakim—the All-Wise: victory's timing governed: granted, delayed, and dosed by wisdom—the delays themselves curricular (the alternated days of verse 140's coming law; the wound's extractions of sincerity, verse 141's)—the pairing teaching the community its complete victory-creed: certain of the source, surrendered on the schedule; and the believer formed by the three-verse seal (124 to 126) carries its standing order into every field: the means welcomed as good news, the heart settled on the Sender, and the outcome's whole account held at one address—\"there is no victory except from Allah\"—while the next verse states what the granted victory is for: the disbelievers' edge cut and their schemes turned back in disappointment.",
  "127": "\"To destroy a group of the disbelievers and humble the rest, causing them to withdraw in disappointment\" states the granted victory's purposes (verse 126's source-clause here given its objects)—and the verse's three verbs map the nasr's complete work, the scholars unfold: liyaqta'a tarafan mina lladhina kafaru: to cut off a flank of the disbelievers: the war-apparatus's edge severed—Badr's execution the standing exhibit: the seventy of Quraysh's leadership fallen (Abu Jahl at their head: the Pharaoh of this community, by the Prophet's own naming, peace be upon him), the persecution-machine's operating tier cut in one morning; aw yakbitahum: or to humble them: the kabt-word: the suppression that turns strength's confidence into confusion—the surviving apparatus disgraced, its awe broken among the tribes (the taraf cut and the remainder unnerved: the two effects by which one field-day re-drew Arabia's map); fayanqalibu kha'ibin: so they turn back disappointed: the khaybah-word: hope's collapse—the campaigns launched to extinguish the community (verse 217's intelligence, Surah 2's file: \"they will not stop fighting you\") returning empty-handed: the enemy's whole strategic ledger, by this clause, written in advance as a series of disappointed withdrawals—Uhud itself included, the commentators note: for all the day's wound, Quraysh withdrew without their objective (the community unbroken, the Prophet alive, Madinah untaken), Abu Sufyan's own field-exchange conceding the account's balance (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 4043's transcript: his boasts answered, his \"day for a day\" a vengeance-cry, not a victory's), and the next morning's Hamra al-Asad pursuit (verse 172) sealing the withdrawal's disappointment.\n\nThe purpose-clause's theology completes the victory-seal's doctrine (verse 126), the scholars press: the nasr's objects are named as Allah's own acts—He cuts, He humbles, He turns them back: the believers' swords the instruments (the means-doctrine of verse 126: honoured, never sourced)—and the objects' selection teaches the war's whole divine economy: the fighting's sanctioned ends being the aggression's dismantling (the flank cut), the persecution's awe broken (the humbling), and the campaigns' hope extinguished (the disappointed return)—the exact terminus-architecture of Surah 2, verses 192 and 193: the fighting until the fitnah ends, ceasing when they cease: no clause of annihilation, no object of conquest's vanity: the enemy's options held open at every verb (the cut flank's survivors entering Islam by the thousands within the decade: Khalid and Ikrimah from the humbled apparatus itself—Surah 2, verse 192's commentary).\n\nThe verse's placement, standing back, closes the Badr-memory's issued medicine (verses 123 to 127) at its forward-looking edge, the commentators draw: the community about to study its wound (the passage turns, in the next verse, to the day's hardest sentence—the Prophet himself told \"you have no say in the matter\") is first shown the war's settled trajectory: the enemy's ledger a schedule of disappointments, the victory's source one, its purposes published—so that Uhud's chapter is read, from its first verse, inside a war already won on paper; and the believer carries the clause's permanent comfort: the campaigns against the faith, in every era's edition, run under this verse's forecast: cut, humbled, or turned back disappointed—the three verbs being the only endings the file has ever recorded.",
  "128": "\"You ˹O Prophet˺ have no say in the matter\" delivers, mid-passage, the Uhud-commentary's hardest sentence—addressed to the community's most beloved—and its occasion is fixed in the Sahih, the scholars set: at Uhud the Prophet, peace be upon him, was wounded: his tooth broken, his face gashed, the blood running as he wiped it, saying: \"How can a people prosper who wound their prophet's face while he calls them to their Lord?\"—and the verse descended (Sahih Muslim, Hadith 1791; al-Bukhari's parallel at Hadith 4069's family); the transmitted reports adding his season of qunut-supplication against the day's named persecutors—Safwan, Suhail, al-Harith by name—until this verse arrested the naming (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 4070's transcript): laysa laka mina l-amri shay: not for you is any of the matter: the amr—the file of hearts and endings: whose repentance will be received, whose punishment sealed—withdrawn, in one clause, from even the Messenger's hands: the day's wounded imprecations answered not with endorsement but with the office's boundary re-drawn (Surah 2, verse 272's \"you are not responsible for people's guidance\" at its severest application: the guidance-file closed to him even concerning the hands that bloodied him).\n\n\"It is up to Allah to turn to them in mercy or punish them, for indeed they are wrongdoers\" states the withdrawn file's true Desk—and the two options' order is the verse's buried mercy, the commentators press: aw yatuba alayhim: or He may relent toward them—the tawbah-option named first, over the very men of the wound: the verse, at the Prophet's bleeding, holding open the repentance-door for his woundersand history cashed the clause with the Book's own dramaturgy, the scholars marvel: the named of the qunut-season and the day's fiercest—Khalid ibn al-Walid who turned the hill's flank, Ikrimah son of Abu Jahl, Safwan and Suhail themselves—entered Islam within the decade and served it to their deaths (Surah 2, verse 192's commentary: the amnesty walked): the verse's first function thus revealed as protective: had the imprecations sealed them, the community would have cursed away its future commanders—the amr belonged to the Knower of endings precisely because only He read Khalid inside the enemy cavalry; \"for indeed they are wrongdoers\" closing the clause with the present classification (the zulm real, the sentence live: the options are mercy or punishment, not mercy or acquittal)—the file open, not empty.\n\nThe verse's permanent jurisprudence, standing back, is the community's inherited boundary, the scholars draw: if the amr was not the Messenger's, it is no preacher's, no wounded party's, no era's zealots': the sentencing of named souls—this one damned, that one beyond return—stands withdrawn from every tongue (the Day's dockets at Surah 2, verse 113's referral); the believer's licensed registers being the deed's condemnation, the general warning, and the wounded prayer for guidance the Prophet himself modelled after this verse's schooling—\"O Allah, guide my people, for they do not know\" (the transmitted plea of his persecution-seasons): the imprecation converted to intercession; and the verse's tenderness beneath its severity completes the passage's reading: the sentence that seems to diminish the Prophet in fact unburdens him (Surah 2, verse 119's release)—the day's blood answered, the file lifted from his wounded shoulders to the Desk of the two beautiful options, \"and Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful\" standing one verse ahead as the estate-clause's seal.",
  "129": "\"To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth\" grounds the hard boundary just drawn (verse 128's withdrawn file) in the estate-doctrine—the ownership-clause this argument lays beneath its every severity (verse 109's identical grounding; Surah 2, verse 284)—and the grounding's logic serves the wounded passage, the scholars unfold: the amr of hearts and endings belongs to Allah because everything belongs to Allah: the wounders and the wounded, the qunut's named men and the naming tongue, the day's field and its every ending—one estate, one Desk: the Prophet's file-transfer (verse 128) thus no diminishment but plain title: the matter returned to its Owner; and the estate-clause's placement between the boundary (128) and the options' seal (this verse's close) completes the three-verse instrument the commentators read as one: the file withdrawn, the ownership stated, the disposition published.\n\n\"He forgives whoever He wills, and punishes whoever He wills\" publishes the disposition—the double-will clause of Surah 2, verse 284's ending, here deployed over Uhud's live cases—and its jurisprudence holds the two guards this argument always posts on it, the scholars press: against presumption—the forgiveness by His will: no wounder's repentance-door forced (the Khalids and Ikrimahs entered by grace, not entitlement), no community's imprecation-list binding on the Desk (verse 128's lesson); and against despair—the punishment by His will: no case sealed by human sentencing, the day's bloodiest hands standing, while breath remained, inside the forgivable (Surah 39, verse 53's widest door: \"Allah forgives all sins\")—the two wills being one Will operating by knowledge and wisdom (never caprice: the estate's Owner the All-Wise of verse 126), the clause thus teaching the community its permanent posture before every open case: hope extended, verdicts withheld, the Desk trusted with both options.\n\n\"And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful\" seals the three-verse instrument (127 to 129's arc; 128's file-transfer at its centre) with the pairing—and the selection's placement is the passage's crowning tenderness, the scholars draw: the sequence ran: the Prophet's wound, the imprecations, the boundary's severity (\"you have no say\")—and the sequence's last words are Ghafurun Rahim: All-Forgiving, Most Merciful: the estate-clause's Owner self-named, at the file's transfer, by the disposition He loves (the mercy prevailing over the wrath: Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3194): the verse's final teaching being the transfer's whole comfort: the file left the wounded Messenger's hands for hands gentler still—the wounders' best hope, and the community's, being precisely that the amr rests where these two names govern it; and history's verification stands filed (verse 128's commentary): the named men forgiven, gathered, and honoured—the seal's names cashed in the qunut-list's own conversions—while the passage now pivots, with the Book's characteristic suddenness, from the battlefield's theology to the market's: \"O believers! Do not consume interest, multiplying it many times over\"—the Uhud-curriculum turning to the community's inner economy before resuming the field at verse 137.",
  "130": "\"O believers! Do not consume interest, multiplying it many times over\" interrupts the Uhud-commentary with the market's law—and the interruption's placement is the passage's studied teaching, the scholars ponder: between the battle's theology (verses 121 to 129) and its resumed narration (verse 137 onward), revelation legislates against riba: the community bleeding from the field is audited at the purse—for the two files are one curriculum, the commentators draw: the day's wound traced (verse 152's coming ruling) to the spoils' pull on the archers' hill—the world's glitter breaking a formation; and riba being that same pull institutionalised: wealth's increase chased past Allah's bounds: the hill's minute-long lapse and the usurer's career-long one, one disease at two durations—whence the interruption: the community is taught, mid-battle-lesson, that Uhuds are prepared in markets: the discipline that holds hills is grown or dissolved in the daily handling of gain.\n\n\"Multiplying it many times over\"—ad'afan muda'afah: doubled and redoubled—names the practice at its Jahili shape, the scholars unfold: the pre-Islamic compounding: the debt unpayable at term, rolled over at increase upon increase until the principal vanished beneath its own multiplications (the borrower's servitude the product): the clause describing the practice's historical extremity, never limiting the prohibition to it—for the law's completed form stands in Surah 2's legislation (verses 275 to 279: the war-declaration, the principal-only settlement: \"neither inflicting nor suffering harm\"), this verse being, in the revelation's staging, among the prohibition's earlier descents (the stages-doctrine of Surah 2, verse 219's wine): the extremity named first, the root extracted after—the gradualism that uproots entrenched appetites (Surah 2, verse 275's commentary) operating here on wealth's oldest one.\n\n\"And be mindful of Allah, so you may prosper\" closes the command with the pairing that converts the market-law into the battle-lesson's own vocabulary, the commentators press: wattaqu llaha la'allakum tuflihun: taqwa commanded—the Uhud-schedule's own condition (verse 125's reinforcement-terms: \"if you are firm and mindful\")—and prosperity promised: al-falah: the success-word (Surah 2, verse 5; verse 104's commissioned party): the verse thus reading, in its station, as the passage's bridge: the community that would hold hills and receive columns must first hold purses—the riba renounced being taqwa's market-edition, and the falah promised being one prosperity across both fields: the trader's clean gain and the soldier's held post drawing on the same guarded heart; while the next verses complete the bridge's span: the Fire to be guarded against (verse 131), the obedience that draws mercy (verse 132), and then the counter-race flung open at Paradise's width—\"hasten towards forgiveness from your Lord\"—the market's multiplication answered by mercy's.",
  "131": "\"Guard yourselves against the Fire prepared for the disbelievers\" attaches to the riba-prohibition (verse 130) its enforcement-horizon—and the attachment's severity is the verse's studied jurisprudence, the scholars weigh: the interest-consumers, addressed as believers one verse back (\"O believers! Do not consume interest\"), are commanded to fear \"the Fire prepared for the disbelievers\": u'iddat lil-kafirin: readied for the rejecters (Surah 2, verse 24's identical clause over the challenge's deniers)—the believer's sin set, by the warning's address, at the border of the rejecters' abode: not as a sentence of disbelief upon the sinner (the community's creed guards the boundary: the monotheist sinner under the will-clause of Surah 2, verse 48's commentary, never eternalised with the deniers) but as the sin's trajectory published: riba being the practice this Book alone confronts with war from Allah and His Messenger (Surah 2, verse 279)—the believer who walks its road walks toward the abode's border, and the verse posts the border's name; the commentators adding the address's mercy inside its severity: the Fire is named \"prepared for the disbelievers\"—its designed occupants the rejecters—so the believing consumer is warned off another people's abode: turn back; this address was never built for you.\n\nThe verse's placement completes the bridge-passage's descent, the scholars draw: verse 130 named the practice, this verse its terminus—the two-step the Book's constant warning-architecture (the deed, then the destination: Surah 2, verse 275's staggering riser)—and the Fire-clause's deployment against a market-sin, between two battle-passages, carries the curriculum's deliberate proportion: the community learning Uhud's lessons is shown that the ledger's sins burn as the field's cowardices wound: no compartment of life exempt from the two abodes' pull (the whole-entry command of Surah 2, verse 208), the purse's daily choices routing, as surely as the battle-hill's, toward one of the two prepared houses (Surah 2, verse 24's Fire, verse 133's coming Paradise: both \"prepared\"—u'iddat in each clause: the symmetric readiness the passage is about to complete).\n\nThe guarding-command itself—wattaqu n-nar: fear the Fire—carries the believer's operational discipline, the commentators press: the ittiqa of the Fire being taqwa's most concrete form: the shield-word's original picture (the guarded man placing a barrier between himself and harm) deployed at its ultimate object—and the Sunnah supplies the shield's material at every purse's scale: \"protect yourself from the Fire even with half a date\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 1417): the riba-consumer's exact counter-programme: the wealth that multiplied by devouring un-multiplied into shield by giving (Surah 2, verse 276's two verbs: interest effaced, charity grown); and the passage's next verse names the shield's other half—obedience to Allah and the Messenger, \"so you may be shown mercy\"—before the counter-race opens at verse 133: the community commanded off the Fire's road and onto forgiveness's, where the racing-verbs of mercy replace the multiplying-verbs of gain.",
  "132": "\"Obey Allah and the Messenger, so you may be shown mercy\" completes the bridge-passage's positive turn (verse 131's Fire guarded against; this verse's mercy sought)—and the command's pairing is the religion's constitutional grammar re-issued at the exact station its lapse had wounded, the scholars unfold: ati'u llaha war-rasul: obey Allah and the Messenger—the conjunction-doctrine of verse 32 and Surah 4, verse 80's coming seal (\"whoever obeys the Messenger has truly obeyed Allah\"): one obedience through the appointed channel—and its deployment here, mid-Uhud-curriculum, reads as the day's own extracted law, the commentators press: the battle's wound traced, at every station of the coming audit, to one lapse: the Messenger's order abandoned on the archers' hill (verse 121's transcript: \"do not leave your post whether you see us win or see us slain\"—Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3039; verse 152's coming ruling: \"you disobeyed after He had shown you what you love\"): the command \"obey the Messenger,\" issued between the wound's narration and its audit, thus carrying the field's blood as its commentary: the community shown, in its own bandages, what the conjunction costs when broken.\n\n\"So you may be shown mercy\" attaches the obedience's yield—la'allakum turhamun: that you may receive mercy—and the clause's selection over every available alternative is the verse's theology, the scholars draw: not \"so you may conquer\" (though victory ran on the schedule's terms: verse 125), not \"so you may prosper\" (verse 130's falah already posted), but mercy: the obedience-mercy circuit this Book wires throughout (Surah 6, verse 155; Surah 24, verse 56's identical clause over prayer, alms, and the Messenger obeyed)—for the command's whole nature is mercy's channel, the commentators unfold: the orders that pinched at the hill (the post held while spoils glittered) were the ranks' protection (the day's reversal proving it within the hour); obedience to the Messenger being never the Lawgiver's tribute-extraction but the community's own shielding walked (Surah 2, verse 286's capacity-sized charges; the ease-intent of Surah 2, verse 185)—the turhamun-clause thus reading, at this station, almost as the day's sigh: obey, and be spared what disobedience just cost you.\n\nThe verse's brevity is its function, standing back, the scholars note: between the Fire's warning (131) and the Paradise-race (133), this one-line command stands as the passage's hinge and the whole curriculum's compression: the two abodes prepared (both verses' u'iddat), and the road between them named in four words—obedience, through the channel, unto mercy; the believer formed by the bridge-passage (130 to 132) carrying its three-step descent-and-ascent as one lesson: the market's multiplication renounced (130), the Fire's border turned from (131), the conjunction held (132)—and the next verse fires the starting-command of the counter-race: \"hasten towards forgiveness from your Lord and a Paradise as vast as the heavens and the earth.\"",
  "133": "\"And hasten towards forgiveness from your Lord and a Paradise as vast as the heavens and the earth\" fires the counter-race the bridge-passage built toward (verses 130 to 132)—and the command's verb is the passage's whole inversion, the scholars unfold: wa sari'u: and race—the hastening-verb of Surah 2, verse 148's charter (\"compete with one another in doing good\") and verse 114's honoured racers: deployed here against the just-prohibited multiplication (verse 130's riba): the community commanded out of wealth's compounding and into mercy's: the racing's first object being maghfirah—\"forgiveness from your Lord\": pardon set before Paradise in the race's own order, the commentators press, by the believer's true sequence (verse 16's prayer-anatomy: forgiveness first, the Fire's protection after): the racer's first need the record's clearing—the repentance-hastening the Sunnah schedules at the sin's own heels (\"follow the bad deed with the good one: it will erase it\"—Jami` at-Tirmidhi, Hadith 1987, graded sound); and the second object's measure being the verse's famous immensity: \"a Paradise whose width is the heavens and the earth\": the ard—the breadth: if the width so measures, the commentators marvel, what of its length?—the image retiring, in one clause, every scarcity-instinct the market's multiplications fed on (Surah 2, verse 268's whispered poverty): the racers compete for an estate whose breadth is the cosmos: no rival's arrival crowds the track (Surah 2, verse 148's commentary: one runner's gain subtracts nothing).\n\n\"Prepared for those mindful ˹of Allah˺\" completes the race-announcement with the estate's readiness and its named heirs—and the clause's symmetry is the passage's sealed architecture, the scholars draw: u'iddat lil-muttaqin: prepared for the God-fearing—the same readiness-verb, word for word, as verse 131's Fire \"prepared for the disbelievers\": the two abodes standing built, doors open, each with its designed occupants published (Surah 2, verses 24 and 25's identical pairing at the Book's threshold): the reader between them holding, in the two u'iddat-clauses, the whole stakes of every hastening and every delay; and the heirs' title—the muttaqin: the watchword's people (Surah 2, verse 2)—being the race's entry-criterion and the next verses' whole business, the commentators note: for the announcement's audience will immediately ask the racing's practical shape, and verses 134 to 136 answer with the muttaqin's four-fold portrait: the givers in ease and hardship, the anger-swallowers, the pardoners, and the sinners who remember and return—the estate's heirs drawn, with this Book's constant realism, not as the sinless but as the racing: those whose falls are followed by faster returns; while the believer formed by the verse carries its permanent tempo-correction: the religion's gait is the race's—the forgiveness sought at speed (the delay-danger of Surah 2, verse 160's commentary: turnings postponed are turnings endangered), the good hastened before its hindrances (the Prophet's counsel: \"take advantage of five before five\"—the transmitted urgency), and the width of the heavens and the earth held before the eyes at every market where smaller multiplications glitter.",
  "134": "\"˹They are˺ those who donate in prosperity and adversity\" opens the estate-heirs' portrait (verse 133's muttaqin now itemised)—and the first credential's two conditions are its calibration, the scholars unfold: alladhina yunfiquna fis-sarra'i wad-darra'i: who spend in ease and in hardship: giving as constant, not as surplus's overflow: the prosperous hand open (the ease-test: wealth's abundance breeding the clutch: Surah 2, verse 268's whisper) and the pressed hand open still (the hardship-test: the little shared from the little: the pressed giver's dirham outweighing the easy fortune—Sunan an-Nasa'i, Hadith 2527's pricing)—the two-weather clause converting charity from transaction to character: the muttaqi identified not by his gifts' size but by their independence from his seasons (Surah 2, verse 274's four-adverb giver: day and night, secretly and openly—the same constancy mapped across time as this clause maps it across fortune).\n\n\"Control their anger, and pardon others\" continues the portrait into the tempers—and the two credentials' sequence is the verse's studied ascent, the commentators draw: wal-kazimina l-ghayz: and the swallowers of rage: the kazm-word: the waterskin's mouth tied on its fullness: anger present, felt, and contained—not the passionless (no virtue in a temper never tested) but the governed: the Prophet's own definition, peace be upon him: \"the strong is not the one who overcomes in wrestling; the strong is the one who controls himself at anger\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 6114; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2609), with the swallowing's price posted: \"whoever swallows rage while able to enact it, Allah will call him before all creation to choose of the maidens what he wills\" (Jami` at-Tirmidhi, Hadith 2021, graded sound); then the ascent's next stair: wal-afina ani n-nas: and the pardoners of people: past containment to release—the offence not merely unavenged but unheld (Surah 2, verse 237's \"graciousness is closer to righteousness\"): the rage swallowed, then the account itself torn up; and the portrait's placement in the Uhud-curriculum carries its living edge, the scholars press: revealed over a community nursing the day's rages—the dead unavenged, the mutilations seen, the deserters known by name—the credentials command the war-wounded themselves to the swallowing and the pardon: the estate's heirs formed, precisely, in the tempers the field inflamed.\n\n\"And Allah loves the good-doers\" seals the portrait's first panel with the love-clause at its highest register—wallahu yuhibbu l-muhsinin: and Allah loves the excellent—and the sealing's grammar completes the ascent, the commentators note: the three credentials climbed from giving, through containment, to pardon—and the seal names the summit's rank: ihsan: excellence (Surah 2, verse 195's crowning term; the worship-as-seeing of Sahih Muslim, Hadith 8)—the pardoner ranked among the muhsinin because pardon is excellence's social form: the beauty done beyond the owed (justice permits the equal answer: Surah 2, verse 194; excellence releases it); and the love-clause itself being the portrait's true wage, the scholars draw: the estate was announced (verse 133's cosmic width), but the panel closes not at the property's grant but at the Owner's love—the racing's real prize (verse 31's circuit: the followed Beloved loving back), toward which the portrait's next panel turns its most merciful credential: the sinners who remember, seek forgiveness, and refuse to persist.",
  "135": "\"˹They are˺ those who, upon committing an evil deed or wronging themselves, remember Allah and seek forgiveness\" completes the estate-heirs' portrait (verses 133 and 134) with its most merciful panel—and the panel's very existence is the doctrine, the scholars press: the muttaqin, drawn for the cosmic estate's inheritance, include sinners by the portrait's own text: idha fa'alu fahishatan aw zalamu anfusahum: when they commit an indecency or wrong themselves—the two registers spanning sin's scale (the grave fahishah and the general self-wronging): the heirs not the fall-less but the described: whose falls trigger the portrait's named sequence: dhakaru llaha: they remember Allah—the remembrance at the sin's own scene: the fall interrupting into recollection (the taqwa-reflex this Book wires: \"when an impulse from Satan touches them, they remember, and at once they see\"—Surah 7, verse 201); fastaghfaru li-dhunubihim: and seek forgiveness for their sins: the remembrance converted, at once, to asking—the racing-tempo of verse 133 applied at the fall (the forgiveness hastened toward, not filed for later)—and the panel's parenthesis lifts the asking's whole warrant into praise: \"and who forgives sins except Allah?\": wa man yaghfiru dh-dhunuba illa llah: the rhetorical exclusivity that is at once the sinner's comfort (one Desk, always open: Surah 39, verse 53) and the creed's guard (no confessor, priest, or intermediary holding the pardon-keys: the forgiveness-monopoly His—the clause the scholars deploy against every mediated absolution).\n\n\"And do not knowingly persist in sin\" closes the panel's sequence with its boundary-credential—wa lam yusirru ala ma fa'alu wa hum ya'lamun: and they do not persist in what they did, knowingly—and the israr-clause is the panel's whole jurisprudence, the commentators unfold: the heir's mark is never sinlessness but non-persistence: the fall not settled into (the sin unrationalised, undefended, unscheduled for repetition)—the knowing persistence being what converts slips into character (the black-spots' spreading: Jami` at-Tirmidhi, Hadith 3334) and what the panel's people refuse: their biography a rhythm of falls and returns, never a residence in the fallen state—the Prophet's own filing, peace be upon him: \"all the children of Adam constantly err, and the best of those who err are those who constantly repent\" (Jami` at-Tirmidhi, Hadith 2499), with the rhythm's divine reception published in the Sahih: the servant who sins and says \"my Lord, forgive me\" again and again, and Allah's answer at each return: \"My servant knew he has a Lord who forgives sin and takes for it—I have forgiven My servant\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 7507; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2758).\n\nThe panel's placement, standing back, is the portrait's completed mercy-architecture, the scholars draw: the estate-heirs' credentials ran: giving in both weathers, rage swallowed, pardon extended (verse 134)—the outward excellences—and the portrait's final panel turns inward to the heirs' relationship with their own failures: the community shown, at the cosmic estate's gate, that the inheriting character is defined at both fronts: excellent toward others' wrongs (the pardon), honest toward one's own (the return)—and the two fronts one taqwa: for the pardoner of people learned pardon's worth at his own asking, and the persistent self-excuser hardens, in time, at both; while the wage-verse now follows to pay the whole portrait: \"their reward is forgiveness from their Lord and Gardens under which rivers flow.\"",
  "136": "\"Their reward is forgiveness from their Lord and Gardens under which rivers flow, staying there forever\" pays the portrait just completed (verses 134 and 135)—and the payment's order is its jurisprudence, the scholars unfold: ula'ika jaza'uhum maghfiratun min rabbihim: those—their reward is forgiveness from their Lord: the pardon paid first, before the estate: the sequence matching the race's own order (verse 133: \"hasten towards forgiveness... and a Paradise\") and the heirs' own need (verse 135's panel: the falling-and-returning sinners whose first requirement was always the record's clearing)—the maghfirah's sourcing \"from their Lord\" carrying the relationship's warmth (the rabb-word of nurture: the pardon issued not by a tribunal's clemency but by the Sustainer's own hand); then the estate: \"Gardens under which rivers flow\"—the inheritance announced at verse 133's cosmic width now deeded in the Book's constant imagery (Surah 2, verse 25's furnishing), with the tenure-clause that converts all of it from visit to home: khalidina fiha: abiding forever—the eternity that answers the heirs' deepest anxiety, the commentators note: the portrait's people, whose biography was falls and returns (verse 135), receive a residence from which no fall exiles: the permanence itself the pardon's completion.\n\n\"How excellent is the reward for those who work ˹righteousness˺\" seals the payment with the exclamation—wa ni'ma ajru l-amilin: and how excellent the wage of the workers—and the seal's chosen noun is the verse's last teaching, the scholars press: al-amilin: the workers: the portrait's people named, at their payment, by their labour: the givers in both weathers, the rage-swallowers, the pardoners, the returners—all of it filed as amal: work: the wage-grammar (ajr: the earned payment) completing this argument's constant economics (Surah 2, verse 134's earnings-ledgers; the full-discharge clauses of Surah 2, verse 281): Paradise deeded by grace and priced in labour at once—the grace sourcing (no worker's deeds purchase the cosmic estate: the Prophet's own clause, peace be upon him: \"none of you will enter Paradise by his deeds... not even me, unless Allah covers me with His mercy\"—Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 5673; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2816) and the labour routing (the mercy reaching, by His own published terms, the working: the two truths held as the scholars hold them: the wage real, the Wage-Payer generous beyond all earning).\n\nThe three-verse unit (134 to 136), standing back, completes the bridge-passage's whole ascent, the commentators draw: from the riba-prohibition's descent (verse 130: wealth multiplied by devouring) through the Fire's border (131) and the obedience-hinge (132) to the race (133), the portrait (134 and 135), and the payment (this verse): the community's inner economy re-founded, mid-battle-curriculum, on the counter-multiplication: gains compounded by giving, strength by swallowing, honour by pardoning, and records by returning—the ni'ma-exclamation's excellence being the whole exchange's audited verdict; and the passage now returns, its economic interlude complete, to the field's resumed lessons: \"similar situations came to pass before you, so travel throughout the land\"—Uhud re-entered through history's own precedents.",
  "137": "\"Similar situations came to pass before you\" resumes the Uhud-curriculum (the economic interlude of verses 130 to 136 complete) with the day's first consolation—and the consolation's instrument is historical law, the scholars unfold: qad khalat min qablikum sunan: there have passed before you sunan: ways, patterns, precedents: the sunan-word naming history's lawfulness—the rise and testing of communities, the alternation of their days (verse 140's coming statement), the endings of their deniers: all of it patterned, repeated, on file—the verse's first work at the community's bandaged hour being the wound's re-framing: Uhud not an anomaly breaking the faith's promises but an instance inside the oldest pattern: the believers of every era struck, shaken, and sifted (Surah 2, verse 214's law: \"they were afflicted with suffering and adversity and were so violently shaken\"), their prophets wounded (the file of the slain and persecuted messengers: verse 21's indictment read now from the other side—as the Prophet's own company), and the deniers' triumphs granted their hour before the pattern's turn (Surah 2, verse 178's respite-doctrine; verse 178 of this chapter ahead): the community, reading its blood inside the sunan, is thereby lifted from the wound's two temptations at once, the commentators press: the despair that reads the setback as abandonment (the pattern says otherwise: the shaking is the road's documented terrain) and the shock that reads it as unprecedented (nothing on the field was new: even the cry \"when will Allah's help come?\" stands filed—Surah 2, verse 214's transcript).\n\n\"So travel throughout the land and see the fate of the deniers\" converts the consolation into commanded method—and the command's empiricism is the Book's own signature, the scholars draw: fasiru fil-ardi fanzuru: so journey in the earth, then look: kayfa kana aqibatu l-mukadhdhibin: how was the end of the deniers: history assigned as fieldwork: the ruins on the caravan-routes—Thamud's carved valleys, the overturned cities' traces (the Quraysh's own trade-roads running past them: Surah 37, verses 137 and 138's \"you pass by them in the morning and at night\")—commissioned as the pattern's open archive: the community's wounded eyes directed from the fresh graves at Uhud to the old ones of the deniers: for the sunan's full reading requires both columns, the commentators note: the believers' shakings temporary (the pattern's testing-phase: the alternated days), the deniers' endings terminal (the aqibah-column: Pharaoh's file at verse 11; the seized nations of Surah 2's histories)—and the travel-command's permanent yield being the believer's historical literacy: the earth itself held as scripture's exhibit-hall (Surah 2, verse 164's signs extended into archaeology), every ruin a verse of the pattern, every era's confident tyranny read against the aqibah-column it will join; while the next verse names what this whole passage is—\"a clear lesson for humanity, and a guide and a lesson to the God-fearing\"—before the alternated-days law (verse 140) states the pattern's mechanics over Uhud's own account.",
  "138": "\"This is an insight to humanity—a guide and a lesson to the God-fearing\" names what the resumed passage is (verse 137's sunan-command; the whole Uhud-curriculum behind it)—and the naming's three titles are graded by audience, the scholars unfold: hadha bayanun lin-nas: this is a clarification for humanity: the widest circle first—the passage's histories and laws (the precedents commanded travelled, the deniers' endings displayed) standing as public clarification: evidence laid before the species as such (the same universal address as verse 96's House \"for humanity\" and verse 110's commission \"for humanity\"); then the narrowing: \"a guide and a lesson to the God-fearing\"—hudan wa maw'izatun lil-muttaqin: guidance and admonition for the watchword's people: the identical sorting this Book performed at its own threshold (Surah 2, verse 2: \"a guide for those mindful\") and at every exhibit since (Surah 2, verse 66's Sabbath-example: \"a lesson to the God-fearing\"; verse 26's parable-sorting): one text, graded receptions—clarification reaching all eyes, guidance entering only the prepared heart: the rain-and-soils doctrine (Surah 2, verse 264 and 265's two grounds) applied to revelation's own reading.\n\nThe three titles' distribution carries the verse's permanent epistemology, the commentators draw: the passage's function shifts with its receiver's state, not its own content: to the resistant, the sunan-histories are information—accurate, received, inert (the flock's heard calls: Surah 2, verse 171); to the muttaqi, the same verses operate—the hudan-title: direction actually taken: Uhud's audit steering the next formation; and the maw'izah-title: the admonition that softens: the histories landing as address, not archive (the God-fearing reading every ruin's aqibah, verse 137's fieldwork, as addressed mail—Surah 2, verse 66's commentary)—the verse thus posting, mid-curriculum, the curriculum's own terms: what the reader takes from the Uhud-passage is a function of what he brings to it: the taqwa named in the title being both the reading's prerequisite and its product (the circle this Book draws at Surah 2, verse 2's commentary: mindfulness unlocking guidance, guidance deepening mindfulness).\n\nThe verse's placement, standing back, is the passage's breath before its hardest teachings, the scholars note: behind it, the wound re-framed inside history's law (verse 137); ahead, the passage's steepest verses—the grief forbidden (139), the days alternated (140), the sifting named (141 and 142), and the community's most shattering sentence prepared (verse 144's \"Muhammad is no more than a messenger\")—and this verse stands between as the reader's re-registration: whoever would take the coming verses as guidance and admonition, not mere clarification, is summoned to the title's terms: the God-fearing reading, of a God-fearing community's bleeding—while the next verse issues the curriculum's first command to the bandaged ranks: \"do not falter or grieve, for you will have the upper hand, if you are true believers.\"",
  "139": "\"Do not falter or grieve\" issues the bandaged community its double prohibition—and the two verbs' selection covers collapse's whole anatomy, the scholars unfold: wa la tahinu: do not weaken: the wahn-word: the inner slackening—resolve's fibre loosening after the blow (the disease the Prophet, peace be upon him, would name in the end-times hadith: \"wahn: love of this world and hatred of death\"—Sunan Abi Dawud, Hadith 4297: the same word: Uhud's temptation and the ages' one disease); wa la tahzanu: nor grieve: the sorrow over the day's losses—the seventy martyrs, the Prophet's wound, the reversal's shame—forbidden not as feeling (the tears lawful: the Prophet wept at loss—Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 1303) but as governor: grief not to steer the community (the distinction this Book keeps at Surah 2, verse 156's formula: sorrow felt, ownership confessed, the march resumed)—the double prohibition thus addressing the wound's two exits into ruin, the commentators press: forward-collapse (the weakening that quits the field) and backward-collapse (the grieving that lives at the graves): both barred in five words.\n\n\"For you will have the upper hand\" grounds the prohibition in the standing fact—wa antumu l-a'lawna: and you are the higher: the elevation-clause: the community's position stated not as consolation's wish but as present-tense reality, the scholars draw: higher in creed (the tawhid over every rival way: verse 85), higher in cause (the fighting's sanctioned ends against the aggressors': Surah 2, verse 193), higher in trajectory (the war's settled ledger: verse 127's scheduled disappointments; verse 12's banked forecast)—the day's score notwithstanding: for Uhud's field, read whole, had already displayed the clause (the enemy withdrawing without objective: verse 127's commentary), and the decade would cash it entire (Makkah's gates, the peninsula's folding): the elevation real at the very hour it was least visible—the verse teaching the community its permanent altitude-reading: position measured by the cause's standing, never the season's score.\n\n\"If you are ˹true˺ believers\" closes the command with the conditional that is the verse's engine, the commentators press: in kuntum mu'minin: if you are believers: the elevation indexed to the iman—the clause working both directions at once: as assurance (the belief present, the upper hand certain: the promise unconditional upon anything outside the community's own faith—no enemy's strength, no season's wound named among the variables) and as summons (the faltering and grieving diagnosed, by the same clause, as faith-gaps: the community that weakens has mislaid, for that hour, the creed's altitude—whence the cure is never morale's manufacture but iman's renewal: the Badr-memory re-issued, verse 123; the reliance re-seated, verse 122); and the verse's permanent equipment, standing back, serves every generation's bandaged hours, the scholars draw: the double prohibition and the conditional carried as one instrument: at each setback—communal or private—the two exits barred (no weakening forward, no grieving backward), the altitude re-read (the cause's standing unmoved by the score), and the conditional turned to audit: the sagging spirit sent not to despair but to the faith's own maintenance—while the next verse explains the score itself: \"if you have suffered injuries, they suffered similarly: We alternate these days among people.\"",
  "140": "\"If you have suffered injuries ˹at Uḥud˺, they suffered similarly ˹at Badr˺\" opens the alternated-days law with the ledger equalised—and the equalisation's realism is its first medicine, the scholars unfold: in yamsaskum qarhun faqad massa l-qawma qarhun mithluh: if a wound has touched you, a like wound touched that people: the qarh-word doubled: your seventy at Uhud, their seventy at Badr (the counts' near-symmetry the commentators note from the records: the fallen and captured of Badr answering Uhud's martyrs almost man for man—Abu Sufyan's own field-cry \"a day for a day\" conceding the arithmetic: Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 4043's exchange): the community's wound thus stripped, at one stroke, of its imagined uniqueness: the enemy bleeds too, has bled first, and bled equally—the war's pain distributed by the same clause that will now name the distribution's law.\n\n\"We alternate these days ˹of victory and defeat˺ among people\" states the law itself—wa tilka l-ayyamu nudawiluha bayna n-nas: and those days: We rotate them among the peoples—and the mudawalah-verb is history's mechanics published, the scholars draw: the days—victory's and defeat's—circulated by divine administration: no community holding triumph as freehold, none leased defeat forever (the estate-doctrine of verse 26: the dominion given and stripped by the Owner's hand; verse 137's sunan as the rotation's archive)—the law's wisdom then itemised in the verse's own purpose-clauses: \"so that Allah may reveal the ˹true˺ believers\": wa liya'lama llahu lladhina amanu: the knowing-clause (His knowledge eternal; the revealing being the manifestation: the believers' reality displayed in the record, to themselves and the worlds—the test-doctrine of Surah 2, verse 143's qiblah-filter): defeat as diagnostic: the day's turning exposing what victory's weather never could (the ranks' constancy, the hypocrites' seams: verse 167's coming exposure); \"choose martyrs from among you\": wa yattakhidha minkum shuhada: and take from you witnesses—the martyrdom-clause at its full height, the commentators press: the fallen seventy not the day's waste but its selection: shuhada—witnesses: taken: the verb of choosing (the martyrs elected through the day's turning to the rank verse 169 will unveil: alive, provided for)—the community taught to read its graves as heaven's own drafting.\n\n\"And Allah does not like the wrongdoers\" closes the law with the forfeited-love clause (Surah 2, verse 190's boundary)—and its placement inside the alternation-doctrine is the law's guard, the scholars note: the days rotate, but the rotation is not moral neutrality: the enemy's granted day at Uhud was administration, never endorsement (the respite-doctrine of Surah 2, verse 178's commentary; verse 178 of this chapter ahead: \"We only give them respite so they may increase in sin\")—the wrongdoers unloved at their triumph's very noon; and the believer formed by the law carries its complete calibration: the wound expected (the rotation's terms), the ledger read whole (their qarh and yours), the purposes trusted (the revealing, the drafting)—and the days' turning never misread in either direction: victory no verdict of love, defeat none of abandonment: the verdicts residing, as this whole argument insists, in the two columns the next verse completes: the believers purified, the disbelievers effaced.",
  "141": "\"And distinguish the ˹true˺ believers and destroy the disbelievers\" completes the alternated-days law (verse 140) with its two remaining purposes—and the pairing's two verbs are the rotation's full metallurgy, the scholars unfold: wa liyumahhisa llahu lladhina amanu: and that Allah may purify the believers: the tamhis-word: the assayer's term: the ore fired until the dross runs off—the community's defeat-day as refinery: the wound burning out the ranks' admixtures (the spoils-love that broke the hill: verse 152's audit; the untested assumptions of easy victory: Badr's habit; the hypocrites' cover: verse 167's coming stripping)—the purification-doctrine this Book applies to every believing affliction (Surah 2, verse 155's syllabus; the sins shed at each pain: Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 5641) here scaled to the community's body: Uhud as the ummah's assay: what emerged from the day—the pardoned ranks re-formed (verse 152's \"He has pardoned you\"), the next morning's wounded march (verse 172's Hamra al-Asad)—being the fired metal the pre-Uhud community only appeared to be; wa yamhaqa l-kafirin: and efface the disbelievers: the mahq-verb: the gradual blotting-out (the same word over riba's effacement: Surah 2, verse 276): the enemy's granted day working, by the rotation's design, toward his own erasure—the Uhud-triumph swelling Quraysh's confidence toward the confederate campaign that broke it (the Trench's exhaustion), the apparatus effaced within the decade (Makkah's opening): the two verbs thus running simultaneously in one day's aftermath, the commentators marvel: the same rotation purifying one column and effacing the other—defeat refining the believers, victory rotting the deniers.\n\nThe metallurgy's placement completes the law's whole consolation-architecture (verses 139 to 141), the scholars draw: the community's protest-question—how could the day turn against the truth's own people?—is answered by the purposes' census: the revealing (140), the drafting of witnesses (140), the purifying and the effacing (this verse): four operations, all of them advancing the believers' file, run through one apparent setback: the day \"against\" them working for them at every named register—the doctrine of Surah 2, verse 216 (\"perhaps you dislike something which is good for you\") documented on the community's own field; and the believer formed by the two-verse law carries its permanent assay-reading: his own defeat-days audited by the four purposes—what was revealed (the reality displayed), what was drafted (the losses elevated), what was purified (the admixture burned), and what, in the adversary's swelling, was already being effaced—the rotation trusted as the Assayer's schedule, while the next verse turns the law's edge into its standing question: \"do you think you will enter Paradise without Allah proving which of you truly struggled and patiently endured?\"",
  "142": "\"Do you think you will enter Paradise without Allah proving which of you ˹truly˺ struggled ˹for His cause˺ and patiently endured?\" turns the alternated-days law (verses 140 and 141) into its standing examination-question—and the question's text is the Book's oldest audit re-issued at the bandaged hour, the scholars note: am hasibtum an tadkhulu l-jannata: or did you reckon you would enter Paradise—the identical opening as Surah 2, verse 214's \"do you think you will be admitted into Paradise without being tested like those before you?\": the assumption named to be demolished: the entry presumed on profession's strength, the estate (verse 133's cosmic width) expected without the assay (verse 141's tamhis)—\"without Allah proving which of you truly struggled\": wa lamma ya'lami llahu lladhina jahadu minkum: while Allah has not yet made known those of you who strove: the knowing-clause as at verse 140's commentary (His knowledge eternal; the ya'lam-verb naming the manifestation: the striving displayed in the record, existent in deed and not merely in claim)—the jihad-word at its full breadth (the striving with self and wealth: Surah 2, verse 218's commentary): the examination's first paper; \"and patiently endured\": wa ya'lama s-sabirin: and make known the patient: the second paper—sabr: the holding (Surah 2, verse 153's equipment; the reinforcement-condition of verse 125): the two papers together being Paradise's published entry-examination, the commentators draw: struggle and endurance—the active and the absorbing: what the cause demands done, and what it demands borne.\n\nThe question's jurisprudence is the doctrine this argument has fixed at every testing-station, the scholars press: the examination is not information-gathering (the Knower needs no data: Surah 2, verse 284) but record-making: claims converted to files (Surah 2, verse 214's commentary: \"faith untested is faith unproven; the test is how the claim becomes a record\")—for the Day's court proceeds by records (the deeds presented: verse 30; the full discharge: verse 25), and mercy's own justice requires the ranks assigned on demonstrated substance (the entered Paradise being the strugglers' and endurers' by the estate's published terms: verse 136's workers' wage)—whence the question's tense at Uhud's hour carries its specific medicine, the commentators note: the community's wound is re-read, one more time, as the examination in session: the day that \"went against\" them was the paper being set: the striving and the patience, unprovable in Badr's easy weather, given at Uhud their documenting field (verse 140's revealing-clause executed).\n\nThe verse's permanent function, standing back, is the presumption-breaker this Book stations before every believer's hopes, the scholars draw: the entry-assumption it demolishes wears every era's dress—salvation by label (Surah 2, verse 111's demolished monopolies), by lineage (verse 10's confiscations), by profession unexamined (the \"we believe\" the testing-law answers: Surah 29, verse 2: \"do people think once they say 'we believe' that they will be left without being put to the test?\")—and the question stands, in its interrogative permanence, over each reader's own reckoning: the papers named (the struggle, the endurance), the sessions scheduled by the days' rotation (verse 140), and the estate's gate opening, by the examination's published terms, to demonstrated substance—while the next verse holds the community's own pre-war professions to the light: \"you certainly wished for martyrdom before encountering it—now you have seen it with your own eyes.\"",
  "143": "\"You certainly wished ˹for the opportunity˺ for martyrdom before encountering it\" holds the community's pre-war professions to the examination-light (verse 142's papers)—and the audited wish is fixed in the record, the scholars set: wa laqad kuntum tamannawna l-mawta min qabli an talqawh: you used to wish for death before meeting it: the wishers named by the occasion the commentators transmit: the eager of Uhud's war-council (verse 121's commentary)—the men who had missed Badr and its honours, who pressed the Prophet, peace be upon him, toward the open field against his own reading, professing hunger for the martyrdom their brothers had won: the wish sincere in its hour, warm in the assembly's safety—and the verse's audit neither mocks nor voids it (the martyrdom-wish itself lawful and honoured: the ranks of verse 169's living martyrs; the Prophet's own thrice-repeated wish to be slain in Allah's cause—Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 2797), but files it at the examination's law: professions are claims awaiting their documenting field (verse 142's commentary): the assembly's wish a paper unsat.\n\n\"Now you have seen it with your own eyes\" completes the audit with the field's arrival—faqad ra'aytumuhu wa antum tanzurun: now you have seen it while you were looking on: death encountered at Uhud in its unedited form: the seventy fallen, the Prophet bleeding, the ranks broken and the swords at work—the wish's object met \"with your own eyes\": and the day's record wrote the examination's results across the community's honest spectrum, the commentators draw: some cashed the profession entire—Anas ibn an-Nadr, who had missed Badr and vowed Allah would see what he would do if given a field, found at Uhud with eighty wounds, recognised only by his sister from his fingertips, the community reading verse 23 of Surah 33 as his citation (\"among the believers are men who have been true to their covenant with Allah\"—Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 2805; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 1903); others, at the same sight, weakened toward the passes (verse 153's flight; the pardon at 155)—the one wish, sat at the one field, sorting its professors: the examination-doctrine's living census.\n\nThe verse's permanent jurisprudence, standing back, is the profession-audit every believer carries, the scholars press: the community's religious speech teems with assembly-wishes—the martyrdom longed for in safety, the sacrifices pledged in sermons, the \"had we been there\" of every heroic tale (the identical audit the Prophet's generation itself faced over the earlier communities' trials)—and the verse files them all at its law: the wish unexamined is a deposit unbanked: honoured as intention (the sincere wisher credited even in his bed: the Prophet's ruling that whoever sincerely asks Allah for martyrdom is granted its rank though he die on his mattress—Sahih Muslim, Hadith 1909), but distinguished, by the day's own grammar, from the substance the field documents—whence the believer's schooled posture toward his own professions: made humbly (the conditional \"if Allah wills\" over every pledge: Surah 18, verses 23 and 24), maintained toward their fields (the preparation that honest wishing funds), and held, at the field's arrival, by the grace this passage's next verses will beg (verse 147's coming prayer: \"make our steps firm\")—while the audit now ascends to the community's severest paper: the Messenger's own mortality, and the sentence that steadied the ummah at his death: \"Muhammad is no more than a messenger.\"",
  "144": "\"Muḥammad is no more than a messenger; other messengers have gone before him\" delivers the Uhud-curriculum's severest paper—and its occasion on the field is the verse's first frame, the scholars set: at the reversal's chaos the cry went up that Muhammad was slain (the Prophet wounded, peace be upon him, and the rumour running the ranks): some arms dropped—if he is gone, for what do we fight?—and the verse descended into that exact collapse with its category-sentence: wa ma muhammadun illa rasul: Muhammad is only a messenger: the office named at its boundary (the same restrictive grammar as Surah 5, verse 75's \"the Messiah, son of Mary, was no more than a messenger\"—the Book applying to its own bearer the clause it applied against the deified): qad khalat min qablihi r-rusul: the messengers before him have passed: the office's whole history one succession of mortal bearers—the message outliving each; \"if he were to die or to be killed, would you regress into disbelief?\": a-fa-in mata aw qutila nqalabtum ala a'qabikum: would you turn on your heels?—the question's severity measuring the confusion it corrects, the commentators press: the faith attached to the man rather than through him to his Sender: the inversion this whole argument guards against (verse 79's rabbaniyyin: the prophet's own summons making people Allah's, never his)—the community schooled, at its beloved's rumoured death, in worship's permanent address: the Ever-Living who does not die (Surah 25, verse 58).\n\n\"Those who do so will not harm Allah whatsoever. And Allah will reward those who are grateful\" completes the paper with its two columns—the heel-turner's turning harming Allah nothing (the estate un-needing: verse 97's closing; the religion's fate never hostage to any member's exit), and the gratitude-column opened: the shakirin—the grateful: those whose faith, sourced past the person, holds at the person's loss: gratitude here being, the scholars unfold, the creed's stability-form: the blessing (the Messenger's sending: verse 164's coming favour) thanked toward its Sender, so that the gift's recall finds the thanks still addressed.\n\nAnd the verse's greatest hour struck eight years after Uhud, the commentators record from the Sahih: at the Prophet's actual death, peace be upon him, the community staggered—Umar himself, sword-sworn, denying the death could be—until Abu Bakr entered, kissed the Prophet's face, and stood before the people: \"whoever worshipped Muhammad—Muhammad has died; and whoever worships Allah—Allah is Ever-Living and does not die\"—and recited this verse: and the hearers, Umar first among them, collapsed as though hearing it newly revealed: \"it was as if the people did not know Allah had sent it down until Abu Bakr recited it; the people took it from him, and it was on every tongue\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3667 and 4452 to 4454's accounts): the Uhud-paper, set at the rumoured death, graded at the real one—the community passing, by this verse's schooling, history's most dangerous succession-hour without a heel turned; and the verse's permanent doctrine stands as the community's anchor at every beloved's loss, the scholars draw: the persons pass—prophets, scholars, parents, guides—and the faith they carried holds, wherever it was banked at this verse's address: the Sender, not the sent.",
  "145": "\"No soul can ever die without Allah's Will at the destined time\" grounds the mortality-paper just set (verse 144's dying Messenger) in the decree-doctrine—and the clause's construction is the battlefield's whole actuarial law, the scholars unfold: wa ma kana li-nafsin an tamuta illa bi-idhni llah: it is not for any soul to die except by Allah's leave: the idhn-clause (the permission-grammar of verse 49's miracles: nothing operating outside His leave)—kitaban mu'ajjala: as a writing, term-fixed: the appointed-time doctrine (the ajal of Surah 2, verse 243's fleeing thousands; verse 154's coming sentence to the hypocrites: \"those destined to be killed would have gone forth to the places of their deaths\"): death neither hastened by courage nor delayed by caution: the term written, sealed, and travelling with its soul—the doctrine's deployment at this station serving the passage's two open wounds at once, the commentators press: the rumoured death of the Messenger (verse 144: his term, too, written—the community's panic mis-filed as protection of the unprotectable) and the ranks' surviving fear (the flight of verse 153: legs that ran from a term no running outruns): the clause disarming, in one sentence, both the grief that thinks death premature and the cowardice that thinks it preventable.\n\n\"Those who desire worldly gain, We will let them have it, and those who desire heavenly reward, We will grant it to them\" turns the decree-doctrine to the day's audited intentions—and the two-desire clause is Uhud's own ledger read back, the scholars draw: wa man yurid thawaba d-dunya nu'tihi minha: whoever desires the world's reward, We give him of it: the hill's fifty (verse 152's coming audit: \"some of you were after worldly gain\") named in category: the spoils desired, and granted—of it: the portion clause: the world's seekers paid in world's coin, the payment itself their whole account (Surah 2, verse 200's world-only prayers; Surah 17, verse 18's hastened portion); wa man yurid thawaba l-akhirati nu'tihi minha: and whoever desires the Hereafter's reward, We give him of it: the Anas-ibn-an-Nadr column (verse 143's commentary): the day's same field paying two currencies by the fighters' own addressed desires—one battle, two payrolls, the intention the sorting-clause (the actions-by-intentions foundation: Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 1).\n\n\"And We will reward those who are grateful\" closes the verse with the gratitude-column's third sounding in two verses (verse 144's ending; here the reward pledged: wa sanajzi sh-shakirin)—and the repetition's insistence is the passage's studied teaching, the commentators note: at the mortality-and-decree station, gratitude named twice as the rewarded quality: for the shakir is precisely the believer the two doctrines produce: the term accepted (no bitterness at the written hour: the grateful reading even the graves inside the favour's frame: verse 140's drafted witnesses) and the currency chosen (the Hereafter's payroll elected over the spoils': gratitude being, at its root, the valuation that prices the Giver above His gifts)—the believer formed by the verse carrying its three-clause equipment into every field and every loss: the term trusted (written, unoutrunnable), the intention audited (which payroll does this deed address?), and the thanks held steady at both weathers—while the passage now summons history's whole company to witness the standard: \"how many devotees fought along with their prophets and never faltered.\"",
  "146": "\"˹Imagine˺ how many devotees fought along with their prophets and never faltered despite whatever ˹losses˺ they suffered in the cause of Allah\" summons history's company to witness the standard the passage has been teaching—and the summons's census-word sets its scale, the scholars unfold: wa ka'ayyin min nabiyyin qatala ma'ahu ribbiyyuna kathir: how many a prophet has fought, with him ribbiyyun in multitudes: the ribbiyyun-word—the Lord's-men: the rabbani-root (verse 79's title: the Lord's-people made by the prophets' own summons): whole companies of the devoted, era upon era, fighting beside their prophets—the earlier communities' faithful (Talut's river-remnant the Book's own exhibit: Surah 2, verses 249 to 251) paraded before Uhud's bandaged ranks as their own lineage; fama wahanu lima asabahum fi sabili llah: and they did not weaken for what struck them in Allah's cause: the wahn-verb (verse 139's first prohibition: \"do not falter\") predicated negatively of the whole company: struck—prophets wounded, ranks thinned, days turned—and unweakened; wa ma da'ufu wa ma stakanu: nor did they soften, nor did they submit: the three negations descending collapse's whole staircase, the commentators draw: wahn—the inner slackening; da'f—the visible weakening; istikanah—the surrender's abjection: the historical company certified at every stair unfallen—the verse thus answering, by census, Uhud's whispered singularity: your prophet wounded? theirs were; your ranks bled? theirs did; the standard is old, and it held.\n\n\"Allah loves those who persevere\" seals the census with the love-clause at the quality it certified—wallahu yuhibbu s-sabirin: and Allah loves the patient—and the seal's selection completes the passage's love-registry, the scholars note: the muhsinin loved at verse 134 (the pardoners' excellence), the mutawakkilin ahead at verse 159 (the reliant), and here the sabirin: patience under the cause's blows named among the qualities that draw the divine love itself (the registry this Book keeps deliberately short: the loved columns few, each a summit)—the clause converting the historical census into the reader's own recruitment, the commentators press: the company paraded was not sealed at its era: the ribbiyyun's rank stands open, its entry-criterion published in the seal (the persevering loved—whoever perseveres), and Uhud's community, holding through the day's staircase (the wahn resisted at 139's command, the ranks re-formed at 172's morning), was writing itself into the very census the verse recited to it.\n\nThe verse's permanent equipment, standing back, is the believer's historical spine, the scholars draw: at every era's blows—the causes bleeding, the guides struck, the days turned—the census stands as issued: how many before you, struck harder, holding longer (Surah 2, verse 214's sawn-and-combed endurers: Khabbab's schooling at Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3612); the three negations carried as the collapse-staircase's posted guard (the slackening named at its first stair, and refused there); and the love-clause held as the blows' own conversion-table: each endured strike drawing not merely reward but the Beloved's love—while the next verse opens the company's mouth: their whole speech under the blows recorded in one sentence, and it is a prayer: \"Our Lord! Forgive our sins and excesses, make our steps firm, and grant us victory.\"",
  "147": "\"And all they said was, 'Our Lord! Forgive our sins and excesses, make our steps firm, and grant us victory over the disbelieving people'\" opens the historical company's mouth (verse 146's census of the unfaltering ribbiyyun)—and the opening's restrictive grammar is the verse's first teaching, the scholars unfold: wa ma kana qawlahum illa an qalu: their speech was nothing but that they said: the illa-clause: under the blows—prophets wounded, ranks thinned—the whole recorded output of history's steadfast companies was one sentence, and the sentence is a prayer: no recriminations (the blame-councils Uhud's hypocrites convened: verse 154's \"if we had any say\"; verse 168's \"if they had obeyed us\"), no despair-speeches, no bravado—the unfaltering identified, at their hardest hours, by their speech's economy: the tongue's whole traffic routed upward (the sorting-instrument of Surah 2, verse 156's commentary: the first blow's sentence as the heart's X-ray).\n\nThe prayer's own anatomy is the passage's studied inheritance, the commentators draw—for its text is Talut's field-liturgy (Surah 2, verse 250's three petitions) expanded by one clause, and the expansion is the depth: rabbana ghfir lana dhunubana wa israfana fi amrina: our Lord, forgive us our sins and our excesses in our affair: the forgiveness-petition set first—before the firmness, before the victory: history's steadfast, at their causes' bleeding edge, begging pardon as the battle-need's first item: the israf-word (the excesses: the overreaches and derelictions in the cause's own conduct—the archers' hill being Uhud's exact instance) confessed by companies whose record the census just certified unfaltering: the theology the scholars press at this clause: defeats trace to the ranks' sins before the enemy's strengths (verse 165's coming ruling: \"it is from yourselves\")—whence the steadfast attack the true variable first: the account cleared, then the feet, then the field; wa thabbit aqdamana: and make our steps firm: the firmness begged, not self-credited (verse 8's held hearts; the two wings' arrest at verse 122); wansurna alal-qawmi l-kafirin: and grant us victory over the disbelieving people: the nasr asked plainly, at its one source (verse 126's restrictive clause: \"victory comes only from Allah\").\n\nThe verse's placement completes the census's conversion into curriculum, the scholars note: verse 146 certified the company's endurance and sealed it with the love of the patient; this verse publishes the endurance's engine—the prayer: the three-petition liturgy being, by the passage's own arrangement, what the loved perseverance runs on: pardon, firmness, victory: in that order, at every era's Uhud; and the believer formed by the pair carries the liturgy as issued equipment (the community's own battle-transcripts already cashing it: Badr's night at Sahih Muslim, Hadith 1763), with the restrictive clause as its discipline: at the blows, the speech audited—\"all they said was\"—the tongue's traffic under pressure being the census's own entry-test; while the payment-verse now follows to close the company's file at both worlds: \"so Allah gave them the reward of this world and the excellent reward of the Hereafter.\"",
  "148": "\"So Allah gave them the reward of this world and the excellent reward of the Hereafter\" pays the historical company's file (verses 146 and 147: the unfaltering ribbiyyun and their three-petition prayer)—and the payment's two-world structure is the verse's studied doctrine, the scholars unfold: fa'atahumu llahu thawaba d-dunya: so Allah gave them the world's reward: the steadfast companies paid in this life's own coin—the victory their prayer's third petition asked (Talut's field: Surah 2, verse 251's \"they defeated them by Allah's Will\"), the establishment after fear, the honour among the nations (the commentators itemising the world-column from the histories: the causes prevailing, the enemies' effacement: verse 141's mahq)—the clause correcting, in passing, the false asceticism that reads faith's payroll as Hereafter-only: the world's reward real, lawful, and granted (Surah 2, verse 201's balanced prayer: the good of both worlds); wa husna thawabi l-akhirah: and the excellent reward of the Hereafter: the second column named with the comparative-superlative the first lacked, the scholars press: husn—the beauty, the excellence: the Hereafter's payment alone receiving the quality-word: both rewards given, one graded: the world's coin genuine but plain, the Hereafter's excellent (the pricing of verse 15's \"better than all this\"; Surah 87, verses 16 and 17's \"better and more lasting\")—the two-desire ledger of verse 145 thus completed at its answer: the company that desired the Hereafter's reward received both, the world's added unasked (the economics the Prophet, peace be upon him, published: whoever makes the Hereafter his concern, Allah places his richness in his heart and the world comes to him compelled—Jami` at-Tirmidhi, Hadith 2465, graded sound).\n\n\"For Allah loves the good-doers\" seals the payment with the love-registry's third entry in the passage—wallahu yuhibbu l-muhsinin: and Allah loves the excellent-doers—and the seal's selection completes the file's ascent, the commentators draw: the company entered the census as fighters (146), was certified as the patient (146's seal: the sabirin loved), opened its mouth as the praying (147), and is paid, at the file's close, as the muhsinin: excellence's people (verse 134's summit-rank: the ihsan of worship-as-seeing: Sahih Muslim, Hadith 8)—the titles' progression being the passage's whole formation-course: endurance ripened into excellence: the blows absorbed (sabr) until the conduct under them turned beautiful (ihsan): the wounded company pardoning, praying, and holding form—and the love-clause standing over both payments as their true content, the scholars press: the two-world reward's crown being neither column's coin but the Payer's love (verse 31's circuit; verse 134's commentary: the portrait's real wage): the file thus closed at the registry where this Book banks its highest accounts—while the passage now pivots from history's paid company to the living community's standing peril: \"O believers! If you yield to the disbelievers, they will drag you back\"—the Uhud-aftermath's whisper-campaign met head-on before the day's full audit opens at verse 152.",
  "149": "\"O believers! If you yield to the disbelievers, they will drag you back to disbelief—and you will become losers\" meets the Uhud-aftermath's whisper-campaign head-on—and the warning's mechanics are the passage's documented intelligence, the scholars unfold: in tuti'u lladhina kafaru: if you obey those who disbelieve: the obedience-verb as at verse 100's identical warning (\"if you were to yield to a group of those who were given the Scripture\")—the yielding named as incremental compliance (the instalment-mechanics of that verse's commentary): and the aftermath-hour's specific whisperers fixed by the setting, the commentators draw: the wound's season crawling with counsel—the hypocrites' told-you-so circles (verse 154's \"if we had any say in the matter, none of us would have come to die here\"; verse 168's \"if they had obeyed us, they would not have been killed\"), the Madinan factions urging re-accommodation with Quraysh, the old-order voices reading the day's score as the new religion's audit: every channel carrying one cargo: step back from this costly way—and the warning pricing the first step's true terminus: yaruddukum ala a'qabikum: they will turn you back on your heels: the heel-turning of verse 144's question (the apostasy-image: the march reversed), the whisperers' counsel walked to its end being not moderation but the old disbelief re-entered: fatanqalibu khasirin: and you will turn about as losers: the khasirin-column (verse 85's terminal accounting; Surah 2, verse 27's category): the trade audited in advance—the yielded faith priced against the purchased relief, and the whole transaction filed at loss.\n\nThe warning's placement is the passage's studied sequence, the scholars press: issued directly after the historical company's two-world payment (verse 148)—the contrast deliberate: the file just closed showed the unfaltering paid in both worlds and loved; this verse shows the faltering's counter-file: the yielders dragged back and bankrupted: the two roads posted side by side at the aftermath's crossroads, where the community's bandaged ranks stood choosing their counsel—and the warning's address-form carries its mercy, the commentators note: \"O believers\": the endangered named by their treasure: the title itself the argument (what the whisperers' road costs is exactly what the address confers)—while the answer to the whole campaign follows in the next verse's two clauses, the passage refusing to leave the warning unaccompanied: \"But no! Allah is your Guardian, and He is the best Helper\": the yielding's premise (protection sought at the enemy's terms) dissolved at its root: the community's security never having resided where the whisperers priced it—the Wali's clientage (Surah 2, verse 257's covenant; verse 68's clause) standing un-forfeited through the day's wound, and the best of all helpers un-replaced by any counsel's cheaper patrons.",
  "150": "\"But no! Allah is your Guardian, and He is the best Helper\" answers the whisper-campaign's whole premise (verse 149's warned yielding)—and the answer's opening particle is its sword, the scholars unfold: bali: but no!—the reversal-word (Surah 2, verse 81's commentary; verse 76's overturning): the aftermath's counsel-market swept in one syllable: the whisperers priced protection at accommodation's fee, and the particle cancels the market itself: bali llahu mawlakum: rather, Allah is your Mawla: your Guardian-Patron: the wilayah-clause (Surah 2, verse 257's covenant: the Wali of the believers; verse 68's \"Allah is the Guardian of those who believe\") re-issued at the exact hour its forfeiture was being rumoured—for the day's wound had been read, by the campaign's theologians, as the clientage lapsed (the old order's score-reading: their gods' day at Uhud answering Badr's)—and the verse re-states the standing title against the score: the Guardian un-changed by the day's turning (the alternation-law's own teaching: verse 140's rotation administered by Him, never against His clients' file: the four purposes all advancing it, verse 141's commentary): the community's patronage never having been a fair-weather contract.\n\n\"And He is the best Helper\" completes the answer with the comparative that ends the shopping—wa huwa khayru n-nasirin: and He is the best of helpers—and the clause's superlative grammar is its jurisprudence, the commentators press: the aftermath's market offered helpers in the plural (Quraysh's terms, the factions' alliances, the hypocrites' patrons: the nasirin-column every pressured community is offered)—and the verse concedes the column's existence to void its ranking: helpers there are; the best is He: the comparison the whole argument has run at every patronage-station (Surah 2, verse 107's \"you have no guardian or helper besides Allah\"; verse 173's coming sentence at Hamra al-Asad: \"sufficient for us is Allah, and He is the best Disposer of affairs\"—the community's own cashing of this verse's clause within days of its descent): the best-helper doctrine holding, the scholars draw, at both registers the wound had shaken: capacity (the Helper of Badr's columns un-depleted: verse 126's source-clause) and commitment (the pardon already issued mid-audit: verse 152's \"He has pardoned you\"—the Helper's disposition toward the very ranks whose lapse cost the day).\n\nThe verse's function at its station, standing back, is the aftermath-passage's hinge, the scholars note: between the yielding-warning (149) and the audit's opening (152), this one-line answer stands as the community's re-anchoring: the counsel-market closed (bali), the patronage re-titled (mawlakum), the help re-ranked (khayru n-nasirin)—so that the coming audit's severities (the disobedience named, the flight narrated) are read from inside the clientage, never as its termination: the Guardian auditing His own clients being the file's whole frame; and the believer formed by the verse carries its permanent market-discipline: at every pressured season's counsel-stalls—the accommodations priced, the patronages offered—the particle first (bali: the premise refused), the title second (the Wali un-forfeited by the season's score), and the superlative held: help shopped nowhere below the best; while the next verse turns the answer outward: the enemy's own interior published—\"We will cast horror into the hearts of the disbelievers.\"",
  "151": "\"We will cast horror into the hearts of the disbelievers for associating ˹false gods˺ with Allah—a practice He has never authorized\" turns the aftermath-answer outward (verse 150's re-anchored patronage) to the enemy's own interior—and the published forecast is the war's hidden ledger, the scholars unfold: sanulqi fi qulubi lladhina kafaru r-ru'b: We will cast terror into the disbelievers' hearts: the ru'b—the horror: the unreasoned dread that unmans strength (the forecast's first cashing already on file, the commentators note from the record: Quraysh's own withdrawal from Uhud unexploited—the victorious army, Madinah open before it, turning for Makkah under an unaccountable unease, then declining the field again at Hamra al-Asad's pursuit, verse 172's coming scene: the terror operating precisely where the score-sheet said confidence should reign); and the casting's stated cause being the verse's theology: bima ashraku billahi ma lam yunazzil bihi sultana: for their associating with Allah what He never sent down authority for: shirk as terror's root—the sultan-clause (the authority-word: no warrant, proof, or sanction ever descended for the partners: Surah 53, verse 23's names without authority)—the causation the scholars draw at its depth: the mushrik's courage is structurally hollow: his securities plural and powerless (the crowd of patrons: Surah 2, verse 257's false guardians; the un-authorised idols whose day at Uhud he credited), his interior unanchored where the believer's is anchored (verse 150's one Mawla): whence the hearts' asymmetry this Book publishes as standing law (the parallel decree at Surah 8, verse 12; Surah 59, verse 2's \"He cast terror into their hearts\"): the believing community, rightly anchored, fields a weapon no armoury holds—the enemy's own unanchored heart; and the Prophet, peace be upon him, counted the ru'b among his mission's unique grants: \"I have been aided with terror at a month's journey\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 335; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 521).\n\n\"The Fire will be their home—what an evil place for the wrongdoers to stay\" completes the forecast at its terminus—wa ma'wahumu n-nar: and their abode is the Fire: the cast terror being the abode's advance shadow, the commentators press: the interior dread of this world and the housing of the next one continuum (the horror's deepest source being the unowned intuition of the destination); wa bi'sa mathwa z-zalimin: and evil is the wrongdoers' lodging: the bitter-lodging idiom (Surah 2, verse 206's closing; verse 12's forecast to the boasting fronts)—the zalimin-title filing the enemy's whole case, one last time, at this argument's master-category: shirk as the worst injustice (Surah 31, verse 13), the day's field-score irrelevant to the file's standing.\n\nThe verse's function in the aftermath-architecture, standing back, is the community's completed intelligence-picture, the scholars note: the wounded ranks, whisper-besieged (149), re-anchored (150), are here shown the enemy's true dossier: his heart scheduled for terror, his practice un-authorised at its root, his lodging assigned—the day's apparent victor revealed, at every register that matters, as the war's structural loser (verse 127's disappointed withdrawals; verse 141's effacement running): and the believer carries the dossier's permanent reading: the confident fronts of every era audited past their scores to their anchoring—what has no sultan from heaven has, in the end, no steadiness in the chest—while the passage now opens the file the whole curriculum has prepared: the day itself, hour by hour: \"Indeed, Allah fulfilled His promise to you when you initially swept them away by His Will...\"",
  "152": "\"Indeed, Allah fulfilled His promise to you when you ˹initially˺ swept them away by His Will\" opens the Uhud-audit proper—and its first finding is the verse's studied justice, the scholars unfold: wa laqad sadaqakumu llahu wa'dah: Allah was true to you in His promise: the audit's opening entry not the ranks' failure but the Promise-Keeper's fidelity: the day's first hour ran on the victory-schedule's published terms (verse 125's conditions held; the promise of nasr operating): idh tahussunahum bi-idhnih: when you were mowing them down by His leave—the hass-verb: the cutting-through: Quraysh's line broken, their standards fallen (the record: the enemy's banner-men cut down in succession), the field turning—the finding pre-empting, at the audit's threshold, the wound's worst misreading: the promise never failed; its terms did (the community must enter the audit knowing which).\n\n\"Then your courage weakened and you disputed about the command and disobeyed, after Allah had brought victory within your reach\" files the day's turning in three verbs—and the three are the collapse-staircase walked, the commentators trace on the hill: hatta idha fashiltum: until you lost heart: the fifty archers watching the spoils-chase below (verse 121's posted order: \"do not leave your post whether you see us win or see us slain\"—Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3039); wa tanaza'tum fil-amr: and you disputed about the command: the hill's recorded quarrel—the post's majority reading the victory complete, their leader Abdullah ibn Jubair holding the order's letter; wa asaytum: and you disobeyed: the descent: forty of the fifty down for the spoils—min ba'di ma arakum ma tuhibbun: after He had shown you what you love: the clause's exactness the audit's sting: the disobedience committed inside the granted victory: the loved thing shown, and the showing itself the test's instrument (Khalid's cavalry, watching the emptied hill, wheeling through the pass: the day reversed by one position's abandonment).\n\n\"Some of you were after worldly gain while others desired a heavenly reward\" enters the ranks' sorted intentions (verse 145's two-desire ledger applied at its documenting field): minkum man yuridu d-dunya wa minkum man yuridu l-akhirah—the audit's census honest about its own: the community's body, on the hill's minute, containing both columns; \"He denied you victory over them as a test, yet He has pardoned you\" delivers the audit's two closing entries in one breath, the scholars press: thumma sarafakum anhum liyabtaliyakum: then He turned you from them to test you: the reversal filed as ibtila (the examination of verse 142: the day the paper, the hill the question); wa laqad afa ankum: and He has certainly pardoned you: the afw—published in the audit's own text: the community convicted and pardoned in the same verse: the discipline named without euphemism (fashiltum, tanaza'tum, asaytum: three counts), and the file closed at mercy before the narration even ends—\"and Allah is Gracious to the believers\": wallahu dhu fadlin alal-mu'minin: the fadl-clause sealing the audit's whole temper: the Auditor's grace toward the audited: the believer formed by the verse carrying its permanent two-sided discipline: his own defeats audited at their true variable (the commands disputed, the posts left: never the Promise-Keeper's failure), and the audit conducted, always, under the pardon-clause's roof—conviction without despair, the file cleared for the next morning's march.",
  "153": "\"˹Remember˺ when you were running far away ˹in panic˺—not looking at anyone—while the Messenger was calling to you from behind!\" narrates the audit's hardest frame (verse 152's reversal now shown at ground level)—and the frame's two figures are its whole anatomy, the scholars unfold: idh tus'iduna wa la talwuna ala ahad: when you were climbing away, turning to no one: the is'ad-verb: the scrambling ascent—the ranks broken through the passes and up the mountain's flanks, each man's world contracted to his own escape (the panic's signature: \"not looking at anyone\": the community's mutual regard, its ranks' whole fabric, dissolved in the running); war-rasulu yad'ukum fi ukhrakum: while the Messenger was calling you from your rear: the frame's fixed point: the Prophet, peace be upon him, wounded and standing, calling the runners by name—\"to me, servants of Allah! to me, servants of Allah!\" (the call's transcript in the sirah's accounts; the Sahih preserving the day's remnant around him: the twelve, and Talhah's shielding hand maimed at his side—Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3724; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 1789's scenes)—the two figures composing the verse's unforgettable inversion, the commentators press: the called flee-ing the caller: the community's whole meaning (the following: verse 31's test) running, for that hour, in reverse.\n\n\"So Allah rewarded your disobedience with distress upon distress\" files the hour's sentence—fa'athabakum ghamman bi-ghamm: so He recompensed you with grief upon grief—and the doubled ghamm's readings the commentators gather from the record: the grief of the reversal upon the grief of the spoils lost; the anguish at the Prophet's rumoured death (verse 144's cry) upon the anguish of the slain; the distress of the shame upon the distress of the wounds: the layers' very multiplicity the sentence's point—the day's compounding sorrows filed as one recompense, athaba's wage-grammar (the ranks paid, for the hill's minute, in grief's currency: the earnings-ledger operating in the day's own coin); \"now, do not grieve over the victory you were denied or the injury you suffered\": likayla tahzanu ala ma fatakum wa la ma asabakum: the sentence's stated purpose its buried mercy, the scholars draw: the grief administered so that grief would end: the doubled ghamm as inoculation—the community, having tasted loss's floor, schooled past the two griefs that cripple (the escaped gain mourned, the arrived harm mourned: the two backward-facing sorrows verse 139 barred), the distress itself the training that made the next morning's march possible.\n\n\"And Allah is All-Aware of what you do\" seals the frame with the audit-clause—wallahu khabirun bima ta'malun: and Allah is Aware of what you do—and its selection over the flight-narration completes the passage's justice, the commentators note: the Khabir's awareness holding the hour's whole spectrum: the runners' panic and its degrees (the pardoned slipping of verse 155's coming clause: Satan's purchase on their prior faults), the standers' ransom (the twelve at the call; Anas ibn an-Nadr's eighty wounds: verse 143's citation), the Messenger's own bleeding fidelity—every figure in the frame filed at his exact conduct: the community reading its worst hour under the clause that guarantees the reading's precision—no runner over-condemned, no stander un-credited; while the passage now descends into the night after: the drowsiness sent down on the sincere, and the hypocrites' whispered arithmetic—\"if we had any say in the matter, none of us would have come to die here.\"",
  "154": "\"Then after distress, He sent down serenity in the form of drowsiness overcoming some of you\" opens the night after the flight (verse 153's doubled grief)—and the sent-down gift is the passage's tenderest wonder, the scholars unfold: thumma anzala alaykum min ba'di l-ghammi amanatan nu'asa: then He sent down upon you, after the grief, security—a drowsiness: the nu'as: the light sleep descending on the battle-line's survivors at the day's exhausted edge: amanah—security itself, in sleep's form: the sign's physiology its proof, the commentators press: terror forbids sleep (the hunted mind races); a drowsiness overcoming the terrified is grace overriding nature—and the Sahih preserves the gift's own witness: Abu Talhah reported: \"drowsiness overcame us while we were in our ranks on Uhud's day, so that my sword kept falling from my hand—I would pick it up, and it would fall again\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 4562): the sword falling from the guarded hand: the community's serenity documented at its own grip—the sakinah-doctrine (Surah 2, verse 248's Ark-cargo) delivered, this time, without the box: straight down onto the believing eyelids.\n\n\"While others were disturbed by evil thoughts about Allah—the thoughts of ˹pre-Islamic˺ ignorance\" turns the night's census to its other column—wa ta'ifatun qad ahammat'hum anfusuhum: and a party whose own souls had made them anxious: the hypocrites' sleepless circle: their care their own skins (anfusuhum: the souls' whole horizon), their theology the night's true exposure: yazunnuna billahi ghayra l-haqqi zanna l-jahiliyyah: thinking of Allah other than the truth—the thought of Jahiliyyah: the old order's providence-reading (the cause's setback proving its falsity; the divine help owed on demand and defaulted): \"they ask, 'Do we have a say in the matter?'\"—their grievance's polite form: the war-council re-litigated (the field-choice they had opposed: verse 121's commentary)—\"Say: All matters are destined by Allah\": qul inna l-amra kullahu lillah: the amr-clause (verse 128's grammar) closing the litigation; \"they conceal in their hearts what they do not reveal to you: they say, 'If we had any say in the matter, none of us would have come to die here'\": the concealed sentence published (the Knower's transcript-service: Surah 2, verse 77)—the hypocrites' whole creed in one arithmetic: counsel prevents death.\n\n\"Say: Even if you were to remain in your homes, those among you who were destined to be killed would have gone forth to the places of their deaths\" answers the arithmetic with the decree-doctrine at its most vivid—law kuntum fi buyutikum labaraza lladhina kutiba alayhimu l-qatlu ila madaji'ihim: the written-for-killing would have come out to their own lying-places: the madaji'—the death-beds: each written soul walking, by its own feet, to its appointed square of earth (verse 145's fixed term; Surah 2, verse 243's fleeing thousands): the homes no fortress against the writing, the field no accelerant of it; \"through this, Allah tests what is within you and purifies what is in your hearts\": wa liyabtaliya... wa liyumahhisa: the test-and-assay pairing (verses 141 and 142's verbs) naming the night's whole function: the drowsy column and the whispering one sorted by one darkness—\"and Allah knows best what is hidden in the heart\" sealing the census at the chest-Knower's desk: the night after Uhud filed, in one verse, as the community's most complete interior X-ray: serenity and suspicion, side by side in the same ranks, distinguished forever by what each thought of Allah when the day went dark.",
  "155": "\"Indeed, those ˹believers˺ who fled on the day when the two armies met were made to slip by Satan because of their misdeeds\" closes the flight-file (verse 153's frame) with its causation-verdict—and the verdict's anatomy is the passage's most careful jurisprudence, the scholars unfold: inna lladhina tawallaw minkum yawma ltaqa l-jam'an: those of you who turned away on the day the two hosts met: the file's subjects named as minkum—of you: believers: the runners of the passes (among them, the records note, men of standing: the file's honesty sparing no rank—Uthman among those named in the transmitted accounts of the pardoned: Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 4066's exchange preserving Ibn Umar's defence of him by this very verse's pardon-clause); innama stazallahumu sh-shaytan bi-ba'di ma kasabu: Satan only caused them to slip by some of what they had earned: the causation-chain the verdict's doctrine, the commentators press: the flight not filed as hypocrisy (the night-census of verse 154 kept its columns separate: these were the drowsy column's men, not the whisperers'), nor as decree's raw imposition, but as istizlal: the enemy's engineered slip—purchased, the bi-clause insists, by prior earnings: \"some of what they had earned\": the day's collapse rooted in the season's accumulated faults (the spoils-love the hill documented; the small disobediences that thin the heart's garrison before the great hour tests it): the sin-to-sin mechanics this Book publishes as standing law (the heart's black spots: Jami` at-Tirmidhi, Hadith 3334; the taqwa-lapses as the schemes' entry-doors: verse 120's insulation-doctrine)—Satan's purchase on the believer being never arbitrary: the footholds sold to him, transaction by prior transaction, in the quiet seasons before the fields.\n\n\"But Allah has pardoned them. Surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Forbearing\" delivers the file's closing entries—wa laqad afa llahu anhum: and Allah has certainly pardoned them: the afw's second publication (verse 152's mid-audit pardon here re-issued over the flight's own file): the runners' amnesty stated as accomplished fact in the eternal text—the community's worst hour closed, by revelation's own hand, at clemency (the pardoned never to be re-prosecuted by the community's tongues: Ibn Umar's cited usage of this clause standing as the jurisprudence); inna llaha ghafurun halim: surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Forbearing: the seal's pairing the file's exact medicine, the scholars draw: al-Ghafur over the flight's guilt, and al-Halim—the Forbearing (Surah 2, verse 225's tender name: the un-hasty)—over its process: the Lord who did not seize the runners at the passes, nor the earners at their earlier earnings, but bore, scheduled, and pardoned: the hilm-name being the verse's deepest teaching about the whole causation-chain: the same forbearance that let the faults accumulate unpunished held the punishment off the collapse they purchased—mercy operating at both ends of the mechanism.\n\nThe verdict's permanent equipment, standing back, serves the believer at his own slips' autopsy, the commentators press: the verse's chain read backward as self-audit: the great collapse traced to the small earnings (the garrison thinned before the wall broke)—whence the discipline: the minor faults serviced at their season (verse 135's returning rhythm) precisely because they are the enemy's future footholds; and the chain read forward as the community's mercy-manual: the fallen brother's file processed at the verse's own order—the causation understood (the slip engineered, the purchase prior), the pardon published, the names guarded—while the passage now turns to the whisperers' counter-theology of death: \"do not be like those who say of their brothers, 'If they had stayed with us, they would not have died.'\"",
  "156": "\"O believers! Do not be like the unfaithful who say about their brothers who travel throughout the land or engage in battle, 'If they had stayed with us, they would not have died or been killed'\" legislates against the aftermath's contagious theology (verse 154's whispered arithmetic now published at its social form)—and the prohibition's target is a sentence, the scholars unfold: la takunu ka-lladhina kafaru: do not be like those who disbelieved: the hypocrites' mourning-formula quoted verbatim: law kanu indana ma matu wa ma qutilu: had they been with us, they would not have died nor been killed: the counterfactual's two theatres named (the travel and the battle: the trade-journeys and the campaigns—the community's whole outward life), and the formula's poison identified at its root, the commentators press: the sentence is creed, not condolence: the \"if\" that re-writes the decree (verse 145's fixed term; verse 154's death-beds walked to) into circumstance's gift: death re-filed under management's failures—the old order's providence (the Jahiliyyah-thought of verse 154) speaking through grief's mouth; and the prohibition's form carries its precision: the believers barred not from the grief but from the likeness: the formula itself the contagion (spoken at funerals, it converts mourners; installed in a community, it closes the roads and the fields: no one travels, no one marches, whom the \"if\"-theology governs).\n\n\"Allah makes such thinking a cause of agony in their hearts\" files the formula's recoil—liyaj'ala llahu dhalika hasratan fi qulubihim: that Allah may make that a regret in their hearts—and the recoil's mechanics are the verse's quiet justice, the scholars draw: the \"if\"-theology purchases no protection and delivers double grief: the loss suffered as all mortals suffer it, plus the invented court of counterfactuals in permanent session (every death re-tried: the journey un-taken, the counsel un-followed, the \"had we only\" compounding the wound forever)—the hasrah being the formula's own product: the believers' creed sparing them precisely this chamber (Surah 2, verse 156's formula the counter-sentence: \"to Allah we belong and to Him we will return\": the loss filed at ownership and appointment, the heart released from the counterfactual court); \"It is Allah who gives life and causes death\" states the file's settling fact—wallahu yuhyi wa yumit: the two verbs at their one Desk (Surah 2, verse 258's credential; verse 27's exchanges): the journeys and battles never having held the keys the formula assigned them; \"and Allah is All-Seeing of what you do\" sealing the legislation at the conduct-audit: the mourning-formulas themselves among the \"what you do\"—the tongue's theology at the graves filed with the Seer.\n\nThe verse's permanent equipment, standing back, is the community's grief-jurisprudence, the commentators note: the believer's losses processed at the creed's own desk—the term written (145), the places appointed (154), the ownership confessed (Surah 2, verse 156)—and the \"if\"-door bolted by the Prophet's own prescription, peace be upon him: \"the strong believer is better than the weak believer... if something befalls you, do not say 'if only I had done such-and-such,' but say: Allah decreed, and what He willed He did—for 'if only' opens the door to Satan's work\" (Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2664): the hadith this verse's exact operating-manual: the counterfactual court dissolved at its first convening—while the next verse completes the re-pricing: the deaths the formula dreads audited against the forgiveness they purchase: \"His forgiveness and mercy are far better than whatever wealth those who stay behind accumulate.\"",
  "157": "\"Should you be martyred or die in the cause of Allah, then His forgiveness and mercy are far better than whatever ˹wealth˺ those ˹who stay behind˺ accumulate\" completes the grief-jurisprudence (verse 156's bolted counterfactual court) with the re-pricing that dissolves the fear beneath it—and the verse's audit is a straight comparison, the scholars unfold: wa la'in qutiltum fi sabili llahi aw muttum: and if you are killed in Allah's cause or die: the two exits named together (the sword's and the bed's: the cause's road covering both—the traveller of verse 156's theatres dying on the journey filed with the fighter: the fi-sabilillah clause the common title); la-maghfiratun mina llahi wa rahmatun khayrun mimma yajma'un: surely forgiveness from Allah and mercy are better than what they amass: the comparison's two sides laid bare, the commentators press: on one side, the cause-death's actual price—forgiveness and mercy: the record cleared at the exit itself (the martyr's known settlement: his sins effaced at the first drop, by the transmitted texts' family) and the mercy entered (verse 107's abode: the mercy as residence); on the other, the stay-behind formula's actual purchase—\"what they amass\": the jam'-verb: the accumulations the \"if\"-theology guards: the wealth, the years, the safety hoarded by the closed roads (verse 156's grounded community)—and the khayr-verdict decisive: the exit with pardon out-prices the lifetime's pile: for the pile is left at the border (Surah 2, verse 254's closed doors; verse 91's refused ransoms), and the pardon crosses.\n\nThe comparison's depth is the passage's whole death-theology gathered, the scholars draw: the verse does not romanticise the exit (death remains death: the griefs of verse 153 were real and doubled) but re-prices it at the true ledger: the believer's two anxieties about the cause's road—the life shortened, the accumulations foregone—audited against the road's actual terminus: and the audit's result re-stated, one verse ahead, at its universal floor (verse 158: \"whether you die or are martyred—all of you will be gathered before Allah\"): the gathering common to every exit, the roads differing only in what they deliver to it—whence the verse's liberating arithmetic, the commentators note: since dying is the one certainty (Surah 3, verse 185's coming universal: \"every soul will taste death\") and the amassing the one certain forfeiture, the only variable under the traveller's hand is the road's title: fi sabilillah or otherwise: the clause, not the exit, being the whole difference the Day reads.\n\nThe verse's permanent function, standing back, is the community's fear-audit at every era's grounded seasons, the scholars press: the formula-theology (verse 156) grounds communities by mis-pricing: the roads' risks counted, the staying's costs hidden (the amassing priced as gain, its border-forfeiture unbilled; the safety priced as life, its gathered account unread)—and this verse re-opens the roads by publishing the full sheet: the cause's travellers and fighters walking, at worst, into forgiveness and mercy; the stay-behind's pile walking, at best, to the border: the believer formed by the audit carrying its one governing question over every venture weighed—what does this road deliver at the gathering?—with the next verse's floor beneath every answer: die or be slain, to Him is the mustering.",
  "158": "\"Whether you die or are martyred—all of you will be gathered before Allah\" lays the floor beneath the re-pricing just published (verse 157's audit)—and the floor's universality is its function, the scholars unfold: wa la'in muttum aw qutiltum la'ila llahi tuhsharun: and if you die or are killed, surely to Allah you will be gathered: the two exits of verse 157 (the bed's and the sword's) repeated with their order reversed—the reversal itself noted by the careful readers as the pairing's completion: verse 157 led with the killing (the feared exit re-priced first), this verse leads with the dying (the universal exit universalised first)—and both verses' two doors opening, by this floor-clause, onto one hall: the hashr: the gathering (Surah 2, verse 148's convergence; verse 9's confessed Day): no exit-door exempting its user from the hall, no road—the cause's or the couch's—terminating anywhere else: the la-ila-llahi construction's emphasis (surely to Allah) sealing the destination against every alternative the aftermath's theologies had traded in (verse 156's counterfactual courts pricing exits as if some led nowhere).\n\nThe floor's placement completes the death-passage's three-verse architecture (156 to 158), the commentators draw: the counterfactual court dissolved (156: the \"if\"-theology's agony), the roads re-priced (157: forgiveness and mercy against the amassing), and now the accounting-hall named where all pricing is settled (this verse): the sequence being the community's complete grief-and-fear curriculum: what not to say at the graves, what the cause's road actually costs, and where every road's travellers reconvene—the hall-clause's specific mercy at the bandaged hour, the scholars press, being its reunion-face: the seventy of Uhud, the passage's whole occasion, not departed but preceded: the gathering re-joining the ranks the passes scattered (the martyrs' interim already unveiled at verse 169's coming curtain: alive, provided for)—death re-filed, by the floor's grammar, from separation's category to sequence's: the community's members arriving at one hall by staggered doors.\n\nThe verse's permanent equipment, standing back, is the believer's exit-theology compressed to one clause, the scholars note: the two doors held with equal hand (the bed not feared as failure, the sword not courted as spectacle: the intention's payroll sorting both—verse 145's ledger; the sincere asker granted the martyr's rank on his mattress: Sahih Muslim, Hadith 1909), and the hall held certain past both: whence the life's whole planning-question re-addressed (verse 157's commentary: what does this road deliver at the gathering?)—the believer walking his roads as the hall's expected guest: the preparation the only luggage the doors admit (Surah 2, verse 197's best provision), and the Gatherer the only host the exits serve; while the passage now turns from the ranks to their commander: the leniency that held the community through its worst hour—\"it is out of Allah's mercy that you have been lenient with them.\"",
  "159": "\"It is out of Allah's mercy that you ˹O Prophet˺ have been lenient with them\" turns the Uhud-audit to its commander—and the turning's finding is the passage's crowning tenderness, the scholars unfold: fa-bima rahmatin mina llahi linta lahum: by mercy from Allah you were gentle with them: the Prophet's post-Uhud conduct audited and sourced: the ranks had disputed his counsel (the field-choice), abandoned his order (the hill), fled his call (the passes' climbing while he bled)—and his response, the record shows, was lin: gentleness: no recriminations-season, no purge, the runners received back (verse 155's pardon administered in his manner before its verse descended)—and the verse files the gentleness at its true source: bima rahmatin mina llah: the leniency itself Allah's mercy, operating through His Messenger's temper: the community's survival-variable named (wa law kunta fazzan ghaliza l-qalbi lanfaddu min hawlik: \"had you been cruel or hard-hearted, they would have certainly abandoned you\"): the counterfactual the Book itself supplies: the cause's truth alone would not have held the ranks around a harsh bearer—the gathering-power of the mission residing, by this verse's audit, in the bearer's mercy as truly as in the message's proof (the Prophet's standing description: \"We have sent you only as a mercy for the worlds\"—Surah 21, verse 107).\n\n\"So pardon them, ask Allah's forgiveness for them, and consult with them in ˹conducting˺ matters\" converts the finding into the leadership-charter—and the three commands' escalation is its studied jurisprudence, the commentators press: fa'fu anhum: so pardon them: the offence's interpersonal debt released (the day's failures owed to him waived); wastaghfir lahum: and seek forgiveness for them: the released debt escalated to advocacy: the wronged commander turned intercessor at the higher Desk (the offence's divine register petitioned on the offenders' behalf); wa shawirhum fil-amr: and consult them in the matter: the charter's astonishing summit, the scholars marvel: the counsel whose result was Uhud—the shura that chose the field against his reading (verse 121's commentary)—re-commanded after its costliest outcome: consultation not suspended at its failure but institutionalised past it: the community's participatory principle priced above any single day's score (the Prophet's own practice thereafter the charter walked: his councils at every juncture, Abu Hurairah's testimony that none consulted companions more—the transmitted observation the commentators cite).\n\n\"Once you make a decision, put your trust in Allah. Surely Allah loves those who trust in Him\" completes the charter with its sequence-clause—fa'idha azamta fatawakkal ala llah: when you have resolved, then rely upon Allah—and the sequence is the whole decision-theology, the scholars draw: the counsel taken (the means honoured), the resolve formed (azm: the deliberation closed—no infinite re-litigation), and the reliance then placed: not on the counsel's quality nor the resolve's firmness but on Allah (verse 122's re-seated weight): tawakkul as the decision's roof, never its substitute; and the love-registry's fourth entry sealing it: inna llaha yuhibbu l-mutawakkilin: Allah loves the reliant—the charter thus closing at the register where the passage banks everything: the lenient commander, the pardoned ranks, the consulted community, and the resolved-and-reliant decision, all held inside the loves this chapter has been listing (the muhsinin, the sabirin, the muttaqin, now the mutawakkilin): leadership's whole manual in one verse, written over the community's worst week.",
  "160": "\"If Allah helps you, none can defeat you. But if He denies you help, then who else can help you?\" states the help-charter beneath the leadership-manual just issued (verse 159)—and the charter's two conditionals are the war's whole arithmetic finalised, the scholars unfold: in yansurkumu llahu fala ghaliba lakum: if Allah helps you, there is no overcomer of you: the first conditional Badr's documented file (verse 123's adhillah-victory: the three hundred unbeatable while the Help ran); wa in yadhulkum: but if He forsakes you: the khidhlan-word: the help withheld, the ranks left to their own weights—Uhud's documented hour (verse 152's audit: the conditions lapsed at the hill, the schedule paused)—faman dha lladhi yansurukum min ba'dih: then who is it that can help you after Him?: the question's sweep emptying the field of every alternative patron (the aftermath's counsel-market: verse 149's whisperers, verse 150's re-anchoring): the two conditionals together being the two days' one lesson, the commentators press: the community lived both clauses within a year—unbeatable at Badr, unhelped at the hill's minute—and the charter files both at one Desk: the help's presence and its withholding equally His administration (verse 126's source-clause: \"victory comes only from Allah\"), the variable never the Helper's capacity but the helped's conditions (verse 125's terms: the firmness and taqwa the columns march on).\n\n\"So in Allah let the believers put their trust\" seals the charter with the reliance-command—wa ala llahi falyatawakkali l-mu'minun: and upon Allah let the believers rely—and the seal's placement completes the passage's tawakkul-architecture, the scholars draw: the command's third sounding in the Uhud-curriculum (verse 122's identical clause at the wings' arrest; verse 159's decision-roof), here issued directly upon the two conditionals: the reliance commanded, that is, with both clauses in view: trust placed in the Helper as known through both days—the One whose help makes the few unbeatable and whose withholding no coalition supplies: the tawakkul thus schooled past its fair-weather counterfeit, the commentators note: the reliance that survives only the first conditional (trusting while helped) is confidence in weather; the believers' tawakkul holds through the second (the forsaken hour read as administration, the conditions repaired, the trust un-moved: Hamra al-Asad's next-morning march the community's own demonstration—verse 172's coming record).\n\nThe charter's permanent function, standing back, is the community's strategic creed compressed to two clauses and a command, the scholars press: every era's threat-assessment conducted under the first conditional (the coalitions' sizes irrelevant where the Help runs: the ghalib-clause absolute), every era's setback audited under the second (the forsaken hours traced to the lapsed terms, never to the Desk's depletion: verse 165's coming ruling \"it is from yourselves\"), and every era's planning roofed by the seal (the means worked, the counsel taken—verse 159's shura—and the weight rested where desertions cannot reach: verse 122's commentary); while the passage now turns to the trust's own guardian-clause in the community's inner life: the spoils' integrity and the prophet's incorruptibility—\"it is not appropriate for a prophet to illegally withhold spoils of war.\"",
  "161": "\"It is not appropriate for a prophet to illegally withhold spoils of war\" guards, mid-audit, the community's inner trust at its most sensitive register—and the guarding's occasion is fixed by the commentators from the record: murmurs after the field's confusions (a missing velvet cloak at Badr by one transmitted account; the spoils-anxieties Uhud's hill had just documented) touched the unthinkable—and the verse answers with the category-impossibility, the scholars unfold: wa ma kana li-nabiyyin an yaghull: it is not for a prophet to ghulul: the ghulul-word: the spoils' secret skimming: the taking before the lawful division—the office's very nature excluding the crime (the ma-kana grammar of verse 79's identical construction: \"it is not for a man whom Allah has given the Book... to say: worship me\"): prophethood and embezzlement not merely un-combined but un-combinable: the bearers of revelation's whole trust (the amanah at its summit) incapable, by the office's constitution, of the pouch's petty treason—the verse thus simultaneously acquitting the Prophet, peace be upon him, of the murmur and educating the community in the office's nature (the incorruptibility not his personal virtue only but the sending's own requirement: the message's channel necessarily clean).\n\n\"And whoever does so, it will be held against them on the Day of Judgment\" turns the guard to the ranks—wa man yaghlul ya'ti bima ghalla yawma l-qiyamah: and whoever skims will come, on the Day of Resurrection, with what he skimmed—and the clause's picture is the Sunnah's own gallery, the scholars set: the skimmer arriving at the gathering carrying his theft: the Prophet's fearsome exposition preserved entire: \"let me not find one of you coming on the Day of Judgment with a camel on his neck bleating... with a horse on his neck neighing... with sheep on his neck bleating\"—the embezzled things themselves borne, alive and crying, on their takers' necks before the assembly (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3073; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 1831); and the file's field-case published at Khaybar: the servant Mid'am struck by a stray arrow, the ranks crying \"congratulations, Paradise!\"—and the Prophet: \"No, by the One in whose Hand is my soul: the cloak he took from the spoils before their division is blazing upon him in fire\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 4234; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 115): the ghulul's smallest units—a cloak, and in the parallel case a sandal-strap or two—audited at the Day's full register.\n\n\"Then every soul will be paid in full for what it has done, and none will be wronged\" seals the guard with the settlement-formula (Surah 2, verse 281's near-last clause; verse 25's audit)—and its deployment over the spoils-file completes the passage's trust-architecture, the commentators draw: the community's wealth-integrity (the spoils the era's treasury) secured at both ends: the office incorruptible above (the prophet-clause), the ranks audited below (the Day's carried evidence), and the settlement exact for all (the full discharge, the no-wronging)—the verse's permanent jurisdiction running, the scholars press, wherever public wealth passes believing hands: the treasuries, the trusts, the offices of every era standing under the ghulul-file's terms (the Prophet's ruling on officials' \"gifts\": the hadith of Ibn al-Lutbiyyah—\"why does he not sit in his father's house and see whether gifts come to him?\"—Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 6979; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 1832): the public purse's every skimmed unit scheduled, by this verse, for the neck of its taker at the hall verse 158 named.",
  "162": "\"Are those who seek Allah's pleasure like those who deserve Allah's wrath?\" converts the spoils-guard's audit (verse 161) into the passage's sorting-question—and the question's two columns are the community's whole interior census, the scholars unfold: afamani ttaba'a ridwana llahi: is then the one who pursues Allah's pleasure: the ridwan-word: the divine good-pleasure this Book prices above Paradise's own furnishings (Surah 9, verse 72's ranking; verse 15's crowned inventory): the column of the ranks whose day—its obedience and its pardoned slips alike—was transacted toward the Pleasure (the intention-ledger of verse 145: the Hereafter's payroll); ka-man ba'a bi-sakhatin mina llah: like the one who returns with wrath from Allah: the ba'a-verb (the earned-return grammar of Surah 2, verse 90's \"wrath upon wrath\"): the counter-column carrying home, as its campaign's whole takings, the sakhat: the displeasure—the skimmer of verse 161's file (his spoils' units converting to the neck's fire), the whisperers of the aftermath (verse 154's concealed sentences), every rank whose day was transacted toward the world's payroll at the Pleasure's expense: the question's rhetorical form (the Book's constant instrument: Surah 2, verse 258) leaving the columns' inequality to answer itself—wa ma'wahu jahannam: and his refuge is Hell: wa bi'sa l-masir: and evil is the destination: the wrath-column's terminus posted in the question's own breath (the bitter-arrival idiom: verse 151's lodging; Surah 2, verse 126's \"what an evil destination\").\n\nThe question's placement is the audit-passage's studied pivot, the commentators draw: the Uhud-file had mingled the columns in one army—the hill's fifty containing both desires (verse 152's census), the night's one darkness covering the drowsy and the whispering (verse 154's X-ray), the spoils-murmurs touching the ranks' trust (verse 161)—and the sorting-question separates what the field mingled: the community taught that its body's one uniform clothes two pursuits, and that the Day's census (verse 158's hall) proceeds by the pursuits, never the uniforms (the label-jurisprudence this argument fixes everywhere: Surah 2, verse 111's demolished monopolies)—whence the next verse's grading-clause (\"they each have varying degrees in the sight of Allah\") will complete the census at its full resolution: not two blocks but a spectrum of ranks, each soul's degree filed at its pursuit's exact register.\n\nThe verse's permanent equipment, standing back, is the believer's own campaign-audit, the scholars press: every season's takings weighed at the question's scale—what did this venture return with: the Pleasure pursued, or the displeasure earned?—the ba'a-grammar's severity being its mercy: the returns are chosen cargo, loaded transaction by transaction (the intention's payroll elected at each deed: verse 145's commentary), and the columns exchangeable while the campaign runs (verse 155's pardoned runners re-boarding the Pleasure's column by the next morning's march): the question standing, in its interrogative permanence, over every homecoming—with what does this day return?",
  "163": "\"They ˹each˺ have varying degrees in the sight of Allah\" completes the sorting-question's census (verse 162's two columns) at its full resolution—and the completion's word is the doctrine, the scholars unfold: hum darajatun inda llah: they are degrees in Allah's sight: not merely holders of degrees but—the grammar's force—degrees themselves: the persons graded through and through: the two columns of verse 162 (the Pleasure's pursuers, the wrath's returners) each opening, at this verse, into its ascending and descending series: the believers ranked past one another (the ranks the Book publishes: the strugglers over the sitters by degrees—Surah 4, verse 95's coming daraja-clause; the earlier spenders over the later: Surah 57, verse 10; the prophets themselves ranked: Surah 2, verse 253) and the wrath's column likewise stratified (the Fire's floors as Paradise's storeys: the hypocrites' lowest depth—Surah 4, verse 145): the census's resolution being the passage's justice perfected, the commentators press: the Uhud-file's one army contained not two blocks but a whole spectrum—the twelve at the call (verse 153's standers), the pardoned runners (155), the drowsy sincere (154), the whisperers, the skimmers' temptations (161)—and the verse files every man of it at his exact degree: no rank flattened into another's, the day's every conduct-difference preserved in the grading (the full-discharge doctrine: Surah 2, verse 281's exact payment).\n\n\"And Allah is All-Seeing of what they do\" seals the census with the grading's instrument—wallahu basirun bima ya'malun: and Allah is Seeing of what they do—and the seal's selection is the resolution's guarantee, the scholars draw: degrees of such fineness (the hill's fifty sorted man by man; the intentions' payrolls read inside one uniform: verse 145) require the Basir's sight: the conduct seen entire—the deed, its manner, its motive, its cost (Surah 2, verse 265's identical seal over the soils' hidden difference)—the community thus taught its two standing postures before the grading, the commentators note: rest, for the under-credited (the unwitnessed stand at the pass, the swallowed panic, the quiet re-forming: all seen, all graded: no man's degree lost to the record's human gaps), and sobriety, for the over-credited (the uniform's prestige, the assembly's applause grading nothing: the Seer's file the only transcript).\n\nThe verse's permanent theology, standing back, is the Book's whole rank-doctrine compressed, the scholars press: existence under this Lord is graded—the flattening ideologies refused in both directions: no egalitarianism of outcomes that erases the strivers' purchased degrees (the racing commanded because the track is real: Surah 2, verse 148; verse 133's hastening), and no aristocracy of positions that fixes the grades by birth or badge (the degrees indexed to \"what they do,\" never to what they are called: the label-jurisprudence's final form)—the believer formed by the census carrying its double motion: the eyes lifted at the ranks above (the envy-free emulation of the two enviable: Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 73) and the ladder's every rung priced at conduct: the next degree purchasable this afternoon, by the deed the Basir is watching now; while the passage turns to the community's whole ladder's base: the favour that set every rung—\"Allah has done the believers a great favour by raising a messenger from among them.\"",
  "164": "\"Indeed, Allah has done the believers a ˹great˺ favour by raising a messenger from among them\" turns the audit-passage to its foundation-stone—and the turning's logic is the passage's studied mercy, the scholars unfold: after the census's degrees (verse 163) and before the wound's final accounting (verse 165's coming ruling), revelation re-anchors the community in the favour beneath all its files: laqad manna llahu alal-mu'minina idh ba'atha fihim rasulan min anfusihim: Allah has surely conferred favour on the believers in raising among them a messenger from their own selves: the mann-word: the conferral: the sending itself the summit-grant (Surah 2, verse 151's identical inventory; the community's debt-register headed, always, by this line)—and the min-anfusihim clause the grant's nearness-mercy (verse 151's commentary: the teacher from among the students: known forty years before the claim, imitable at close range, loved as kin): the clause carrying, at this passage's station, its specific consolation, the commentators press: the community bleeding from its worst week is reminded that its wounded commander—the one bled at their failure, calling at their backs (verse 153's frame), lenient past their desertion (verse 159's audit)—is himself the favour: the week's whole grief transacted around the grant's own person: Uhud re-read, one more time, as a season inside the mercy, never outside it.\n\n\"Reciting to them His revelations, purifying them, and teaching them the Book and wisdom\" itemises the favour's operations—the three-fold curriculum (Surah 2, verse 151's inventory; verse 129's Ibrahim-petition receipted): the recitation (the words delivered exact), the tazkiyah (the persons transformed: the purifying that turned the raiders into the ranks—and that the week itself had continued: verse 141's tamhis: the defeat as the curriculum's own assay), and the Book-and-wisdom (the revelation and the Sunnah: verse 48's pairing)—the operations' listing at this station being the audit's frame, the scholars draw: the community's failures (the hill, the passes) were failures inside a curriculum: students' lapses under a Teacher still teaching: the file processed as formation, never as abandonment.\n\n\"For indeed they had previously been clearly astray\" closes the favour-verse with the before-photograph—wa in kanu min qablu lafi dalalin mubin: though they had been, before, in manifest error—and the photograph's function is gratitude's calibration, the commentators press: the community's self-audit (the passage's whole business) conducted against the right baseline: not the angels' record (the ranks' failures measured from perfection would crush) but their own before: the Jahiliyyah's pit (verse 103's brink: the fiery hole's very lip)—from which the favour's operations had carried them to a community that, at its worst week, still held its Prophet alive, its ranks re-formable, its pardon published (verse 152), and its next morning's march musterable (verse 172): the distance from the pit the favour's true measure; and the believer formed by the verse carries its permanent audit-discipline: every era's and every soul's failures weighed with the before-photograph in the other hand—the mann remembered at the wound's own table (Surah 2, verse 231's \"remember Allah's favours upon you\")—while the accounting now closes at its cause: \"why is it when you suffered casualties... you protested 'How could this be?' Say: it is because of your disobedience.\"",
  "165": "\"Why is it when you suffered casualties ˹at Uḥud˺—although you had made your enemy suffer twice as much ˹at Badr˺—you protested, 'How could this be?'\" opens the audit's final accounting with the community's own protest quoted—and the protest's arithmetic is answered before its theology, the scholars unfold: awalamma asabatkum musibatun qad asabtum mithlayha: when a calamity struck you though you had struck its double: the ledger read whole (verse 140's equalised wounds now given their full figures): Uhud's seventy against Badr's seventy slain and seventy captured—the enemy's suffering at the community's hands double its own received: the qultum anna hadha: \"you said: how is this?\": the protest's form the passage's target, the commentators press: the \"how\" of entitlement, not inquiry: the community's unspoken theorem—the truth's army should not bleed—exposed at its own question (the same theorem the whole curriculum has been dismantling: verse 140's rotation-law, verse 142's examination, Surah 2, verse 214's shaken believers).\n\n\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ 'It is because of your disobedience'\" delivers the accounting's cause-clause—qul huwa min indi anfusikum: say: it is from your own selves—and the four-word ruling is the passage's whole jurisprudence sealed, the scholars draw: the day's turning sourced neither at the enemy's strength (three thousand had not availed while the hill held: verse 152's first hour) nor at the Helper's lapse (the promise kept: verse 152's opening finding; the charter's terms: verse 160) but at the ranks' own conduct: the order disputed, the post abandoned, the loved thing chased (verse 152's three verbs)—min indi anfusikum: the community handed, at its protest's peak, the ownership of its own wound: and the ruling's mercy is its empowerment, the commentators press: a wound sourced at the enemy leaves the wounded helpless (the adversary's strength beyond their editing); a wound sourced at one's own file is repairable by the file's owner: the ruling that convicts is the ruling that restores the keys—the community's later centuries reading their every setback at this verse's desk (the scholars' constant deployment of it over the ummah's declines: the causes sought first in the ranks' own departures from the terms).\n\n\"Surely Allah is Most Capable of everything\" seals the accounting with the capability-clause (Surah 2, verse 20's first sounding; verse 26's estate)—and its selection over this ruling completes the theology, the scholars note: the qadir-seal guards the cause-clause from its misreading: \"from your own selves\" never meaning the day escaped His governance (the next verse will file the same day \"by Allah's Will\": the two clauses one doctrine: His leave the frame, their conduct the cause within it—the decree-and-deed architecture this Book holds everywhere: verse 145's written terms walked by choosing feet); and the seal's comfort closing the accounting at its forward edge: the Capable-over-everything stands over the repair as over the wound: the conditions restorable, the schedule resumable (verse 125's columns awaiting their terms), the double-ledger's arithmetic—their double suffered at Badr—renewable by the same Power at the next field: the community formed by the ruling marching out, within the week, to its own demonstration at Hamra al-Asad.",
  "166": "\"So what you suffered on the day the two armies met was by Allah's Will\" files the day's frame-clause (verse 165's cause-clause now given its governing roof)—and the two clauses' co-existence is the verse's whole doctrine, the scholars unfold: wa ma asabakum yawma ltaqa l-jam'ani fabi'idhni llah: and what struck you on the day the two hosts met was by Allah's leave: the idhn-clause (the permission-grammar of verse 145's terms; verse 49's miracles): the day's every event—the first hour's sweep, the hill's emptying, the reversal, the seventy—transacted inside His leave: nothing on the field escaping the governance (the sparrow-fall jurisdiction of verse 5's unhidden estate)—and the clause's pairing with verse 165's ruling being the Book's decree-and-deed architecture at its clearest joint, the commentators press: \"it is from your own selves\" (the cause within the frame: the ranks' conduct the day's variable) and \"by Allah's leave\" (the frame around the cause: the conduct's consequences administered, scheduled, and purposed by the Governor): neither clause diluting the other—the community taught to hold both without collapse: the fatalism that reads the frame and drops the cause (the hypocrites' own move inverted: verse 154's \"do we have a say?\") and the self-sufficiency that reads the cause and drops the frame both barred at this verse's grammar.\n\n\"So that He might distinguish the ˹true˺ believers\" attaches the frame's first purpose—wa liya'lama l-mu'minin: and that He might know the believers: the knowing-as-manifestation clause (verse 140's commentary: the eternal knowledge un-added-to; the believers' reality displayed in the record)—and the purpose's naming at the frame-verse completes the day's teleology, the scholars draw: the leave was not bare permission but purposed administration: the day licensed for its harvest: the believers distinguished—the ranks' gold assayed visible (verse 141's tamhis; the twelve at the call, the pardoned returners, the next morning's musterers: the day's distinguishing-work already published in the passage's own frames)—and the purpose-clause running, by the verse's grammar, into the next verse's completion: \"and expose the hypocrites\": the day's second harvest: the one darkness sorting both columns (verse 154's X-ray)—the distinguishing and the exposing being one operation read from its two sides: the field as the community's great manifest, its every rank printed true.\n\nThe verse's permanent equipment, standing back, is the believer's suffering-frame, the commentators press: every strike received—communal or private—filed at the verse's two desks in order: the cause audited at one's own file first (verse 165's keys: the repairable conduct), and the frame confessed over the whole (the leave, the purposes: the distinguishing being wrought in the striking—Surah 2, verse 155's syllabus; the assay-doctrine of verse 141): the two-desk discipline producing the only posture that survives both weathers: accountable without despair, surrendered without passivity—the wound owned and the Owner trusted; while the exposure-clause now opens, in the next verse, the hypocrites' own file at its documented sentence: \"if we had known there was fighting, we would have definitely gone with you.\"",
  "167": "\"And expose the hypocrites. When it was said to them, 'Come fight in the cause of Allah or ˹at least˺ defend yourselves,' they replied, 'If we had known there was fighting, we would have definitely gone with you'\" completes the day's two-harvest purpose (verse 166's distinguishing) with the exposure's documented scene—and the scene is the march's own record, the scholars set: wa liya'lama lladhina nafaqu: and that He might expose those who played the hypocrite: the day's second manifest—and its exhibit the road's three hundred: Abdullah ibn Ubayy's column breaking away at the march (verse 121's commentary: the seven hundred left from a thousand), and the recorded exchange at the breaking: the believers' summons—ta'alaw qatilu fi sabili llahi awi dfa'u: come, fight in Allah's cause, or defend: the summons's two registers the callers' careful mercy, the commentators note: the cause's fighting offered first, the bare defence (the homes, the families: the city's own shield-line) offered as the floor beneath it—and the deserters' answer preserved verbatim: law na'lamu qitalan la-ttaba'nakum: had we known fighting, we would have followed you: the sentence's transparent bad faith its own exposure, the scholars press: three thousand of Quraysh camped at the mountain, and the column pleads ignorance of fighting—the excuse not believed even by its makers: the taqiyyah of desertion dressed as military assessment (the transmitted record adding Ibn Ubayy's real grievance: his counsel for the city-defence overruled—the shura's loser converting his outvoting into the ranks' thinning).\n\n\"They were closer to disbelief than to belief on that day—for saying with their mouths what was not in their hearts\" delivers the scene's classification—hum lil-kufri yawma'idhin aqrabu minhum lil-iman: they were nearer to disbelief that day than to belief—and the clause's calibrated grammar is its jurisprudence, the commentators draw: the nearness-language (aqrab: closer), not the boundary's crossing declared: the deserters filed at disbelief's approaches—the day's conduct dragging them toward the border (the heel-turning trajectory: verse 149's warned dragging)—while the final classification is left, as this Book leaves it, to the Knower and the endings (verse 154's chest-audit); and the nearness's stated cause being the hypocrisy's whole definition: yaquluna bi'afwahihim ma laysa fi qulubihim: saying with their mouths what is not in their hearts: the mouth-heart gap (Surah 2, verse 8's file: \"they say... yet they are not true believers\"): the column's pleaded ignorance the gap's documented specimen.\n\n\"Allah is All-Knowing of what they hide\" seals the exposure at its instrument—wallahu a'lamu bima yaktumun: and Allah knows best what they conceal—and the seal's function closes the day's two-harvest file entire, the scholars note: the field manifested what the Knower already knew (verse 166's knowing-as-display): the believers distinguished at the call, the hypocrites exposed at the road—the community's ranks printed true for its own reading (the three hundred's exit, the commentators observe, the day's disguised mercy: the column that would have whispered inside the lines—Surah 9, verse 47's \"they would have scurried around among you\"—self-removed before the meeting)—and the believer formed by the file carries its standing intelligence: the community's testing-days perform this printing in every era: the summons issued, the excuses recorded, the gaps exposed—the concealed known before the printing, and the printing itself the mercy that lets the ranks re-form around the true.",
  "168": "\"Those who sat at home, saying about their brothers, 'Had they listened to us, they would not have been killed'\" completes the hypocrites' exposure-file (verse 167's road-desertion) with their aftermath-speech—and the speech's anatomy is the file's final specimen, the scholars unfold: alladhina qalu li'ikhwanihim wa qa'adu: those who said of their brothers—while they sat: the qu'ud-clause the sentence's frame: the counsel issued from the cushions: the deserters of the road (Ibn Ubayy's three hundred) converting their desertion, at the graves' season, into vindicated wisdom: law ata'una ma qutilu: had they obeyed us, they would not have been killed: the counterfactual-theology of verse 156's mourning-formula now weaponised as faction-speech, the commentators press: there the \"if\" spoke grief's confusion; here it campaigns: the fallen seventy conscripted as the deserters' evidence, each grave a talking-point for the sitting—the sentence's double treason noted by the careful readers: the brothers abandoned at the road, then abandoned again at the graves: their blood spent, posthumously, on their deserters' politics.\n\n\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ 'Try not to die if what you say is true!'\" answers the campaign with the challenge that ends it—qul fadra'u an anfusikumu l-mawta in kuntum sadiqin: say: then avert death from your own selves, if you are truthful—and the challenge's logic is the counterfactual's own premises turned upon their holders, the scholars draw: the sitting-theology's claim was death's manageability: counsel prevents killing, prudence defers dying (the arithmetic of verse 154's concealed sentence)—very well: the claimants hold, by their own doctrine, death's keys: let them use them: avert your own deaths—the challenge unanswerable at either horn, the commentators note: attempted, it fails (the sitters' own terms arriving on schedule: verse 145's written hour; Ibn Ubayy's own bed awaiting its appointment within the decade); declined, it concedes (the theology confessed as funeral-rhetoric: potent over others' graves, powerless before its holders' own)—the challenge-form being this Book's standing instrument against unfalsifiable religious talk (Surah 2, verse 94's death-wish test; verse 61's declined mubahala): the claim staked, the claimants' feet watched.\n\nThe verse's placement closes the exposure-passage's arc (verses 166 to 168), the scholars press: the day's two harvests complete—the believers distinguished at the call, the hypocrites exposed at the road and now at the graves: the file's three specimens (the pleaded ignorance, the concealed arithmetic, the funeral-campaign) one theology throughout: death managed by counsel, life's whole horizon the skin (verse 154's anfusuhum)—and the passage's answer one throughout: the written term (145), the walked death-beds (154), the unavertable appointment (this verse): the community formed by the arc carrying its standing discernment: the aftermath-voices that convert the fallen into arguments for sitting are reading from this verse's exposed script—while the curtain now lifts, in the next verse, on the fallen themselves: \"never think of those martyred in the cause of Allah as dead: in fact, they are alive with their Lord, well provided for.\"",
  "169": "\"Never think of those martyred in the cause of Allah as dead\" lifts the curtain the whole aftermath-passage required—and the prohibition's address is its first mercy, the scholars unfold: wa la tahsabanna lladhina qutilu fi sabili llahi amwata: and do not reckon those killed in Allah's cause dead: the reckoning barred at the thought's own register (the husban-verb: the mental filing)—the community's seventy, conscripted by the sitters' campaign as evidence of waste (verse 168's grave-politics), re-filed by revelation at their true status: the prohibition's twin already issued at Surah 2, verse 154 (\"never say that those martyred... are dead\") and here escalated from the tongue's register to the mind's: not only unsaid but unthought—the community's whole interior relationship with its graves legislated.\n\n\"In fact, they are alive with their Lord, well provided for\" publishes the true filing—bal ahya'un inda rabbihim yurzaqun: rather, alive, with their Lord, provided for—and each clause's weight the scholars set: ahya'un: alive: the life real (Surah 2, verse 154's doctrine: \"but you do not perceive it\": the barzakh-life beyond the senses' reach, affirmed at revelation's word); inda rabbihim: with their Lord: the address the honour: the fallen not merely surviving but located—at the Presence (the indiyyah of nearness: the martyrs' post the Book's most honoured address); yurzaqun: provided for: the rizq continuing: the provision-verb's present tense the clause's quiet wonder—the men the sitters filed as cut off from provision (the stay-behind arithmetic: the amassing forfeited, verse 157's commentary) published as the community's best-provisioned: and the Sunnah unveils the provision's very form, the commentators set from the Sahih: the Prophet, peace be upon him: \"the souls of the martyrs are inside green birds, and they have lamps hung from the Throne; they roam Paradise wherever they wish, then return to those lamps\"—and their Lord asked them: do you desire anything?—and they, given all Paradise to roam, answered at last: only that our souls be returned to our bodies that we might be killed in Your cause again (Sahih Muslim, Hadith 1887): the request the interim-life's own audit: the state so surpassing that its holders' single wish is the road that led there, walked twice.\n\nThe curtain-verse's occasion binds it to Uhud's own fallen, the scholars note from the transmitted accounts: the passage descending over the seventy—the community's grief answered not with condolence's generalities but with intelligence from the other side (the report's family at the Sunan: Allah's word to the Uhud-martyrs' kin conveyed through this passage's descent)—and the verse's permanent function stands as the community's grave-theology entire, the commentators press: the fallen of the cause, in every century's ledger, filed at this verse's address: unmourned as waste (the sitters' campaign refuted at its premise: nothing was lost), unworshipped as idols (alive with their Lord: servants still, provided for as servants), and unforgotten as arguments for sitting: the graves that faction-speech spends being, by the published filing, the community's forward positions—its first ranks already at the Presence, rejoicing, as the next verse unveils, \"for those yet to join them.\"",
  "170": "\"Rejoicing in Allah's bounties and being delighted for those yet to join them\" continues the martyrs' unveiled interim (verse 169's curtain)—and the unveiling's two joys are its studied portrait, the scholars unfold: farihina bima atahumu llahu min fadlih: rejoicing in what Allah has given them of His bounty: the first joy the received portion: the green-bird roaming, the Throne-hung lamps, the Presence's address (verse 169's provision itemised)—the farah-word's usage noted by the careful readers: the rejoicing this Book elsewhere audits (the exulting in the world's portion) here sanctioned at its true object: joy in His fadl the believer's lawful exultation (Surah 10, verse 58: \"in Allah's bounty and mercy—in that let them rejoice\"); wa yastabshiruna billadhina lam yalhaqu bihim min khalfihim: and receiving glad tidings concerning those who have not yet joined them, behind them: the second joy the portrait's astonishment, the commentators press: the martyrs' attention turned backward—toward the living: the fallen, granted the Presence, spending their joy on the ranks still marching: alla khawfun alayhim wa la hum yahzanun: that no fear shall be upon them, nor shall they grieve: the covenant-formula (Surah 2, verse 38's primal clause; verse 62's charter) held by the martyrs as good news about their brothers: the interim-life's society revealed as the community's own, un-severed: the ranks' first arrivals watching the road behind, delighted at each comrade's approach.\n\nThe backward-joy's theology is the passage's deepest consolation, the scholars draw: the community's grief-question at its seventy—are they lost to us?—answered at both directions: not only are the fallen alive (verse 169's filing), but the bond is alive: the martyrs' own rejoicing runs on the connection's continuance (their delight indexed to the living's progress: the community's marches, holdings, and endings watched from the lamps)—death re-filed, one register deeper than verse 158's staggered doors, as the ranks' extension: the column's head already at the Presence, its body still on the road, the whole one formation (the gathering of verse 158 merely the column's closing-up); and the formula's content being the martyrs' specific chosen tidings, the commentators note: of all the Presence's news, their published delight is the no-fear-no-grief of those coming: the fallen's first concern their brothers' safety at the crossing—the community taught its martyrs' actual posture toward it: not reproach for the survivals, not demands of vengeance, but the elder ranks' glad watching.\n\nThe verse's permanent function, standing back, serves the community's every grieving generation, the scholars press: the fallen of the cause held, by this portrait, in the double joy the Book published—their portion received, their eyes on the road—and the living's part drawn by the portrait's own grammar: to march so as to gladden the watchers: the road walked toward ranks already rejoicing at the walker's approach (the reunion of verse 158's hall pre-celebrated from its far side)—while the next verse completes the interim's threefold joy: \"joyful for receiving Allah's grace and bounty, and that Allah does not deny the reward of the believers.\"",
  "171": "\"They are joyful for receiving Allah's grace and bounty\" completes the martyrs' interim-portrait (verses 169 and 170) with its third joy—and the completion's vocabulary is its crescendo, the scholars unfold: yastabshiruna bi-ni'matin mina llahi wa fadl: they rejoice at news of favour from Allah and bounty: the istibshar-verb repeated from verse 170 (the glad-tidings-receiving: the interim not a static bliss but a continuing arrival of news: portion upon portion, tiding upon tiding—the Presence's address a place of ongoing delivery), and the delivered items paired: the ni'mah and the fadl: the favour and the surplus: the covenanted reward and the Giver's excess beyond it (the fadl-doctrine of Surah 2, verse 105's infinite bounty; the multiplication past every stated figure: Surah 2, verse 261)—the martyrs' joy thus graded through the portrait's three verses, the commentators trace: the received portion (170's first clause), the brothers' watched approach (170's second), and now the tidings' continuing stream: the interim-life published as joy in motion.\n\n\"And that Allah does not deny the reward of the believers\" seals the portrait with the martyrs' final published delight—wa anna llaha la yudi'u ajra l-mu'minin: and that Allah does not waste the believers' wage—and the seal's selection is the passage's crowning teaching, the scholars press: of all the tidings the fallen rejoice at, the portrait's last entry is a doctrine: the wage-security of the believers as such—not the martyrs' own settlement only but the whole community's: the la-yudi'u clause (the non-wasting: Surah 2, verse 143's \"Allah would never discount your faith\"; verse 195's coming response: \"I will never deny any of you the reward of your deeds\") held by the interim's residents as their culminating joy: the fallen, from the Presence's vantage, seeing the ledgers whole—every unwitnessed stand at the passes credited, every swallowed panic filed, every rank's degree preserved (verse 163's census)—and rejoicing at the system itself: the martyrs' deepest published delight being the Auditor's integrity: the community they left served by accounts that lose nothing.\n\nThe three-verse portrait's closure, standing back, completes the aftermath-passage's whole answer, the commentators draw: the sitters' campaign (verse 168) priced the fallen as waste—counsel's refusers, provision's forfeiters, arguments for the cushions; and the curtain's lifting answered at every priced register: alive, not dead (169); provided, not cut off (169); connected, not severed (170); and joyful at the very accounting the sitters' arithmetic denied (this verse): the graves' whole file re-written from the graves' own side—and the believer formed by the portrait carries its permanent double equipment: the community's fallen held at the published address (the mourning schooled: the loss real, the lost un-lost), and the wage-security held as the marching ranks' own tiding: the doctrine the martyrs rejoice at being exactly the one the living march on—no believer's work wasted, at either side of the crossing; while the passage now returns to the road's living exemplars: \"those who responded to the call of Allah and His Messenger after their injury.\"",
  "172": "\"˹As for˺ those who responded to the call of Allah and His Messenger after their injury\" opens the aftermath's counter-file (the sitters exposed at 168; the responders now honoured)—and the file's occasion is the campaign the community remembers as Hamra al-Asad, the scholars set from the record: the morning after Uhud—the ranks bandaged, the seventy unburied hours—word came that Quraysh, marching home, weighed returning to finish Madinah; and the Prophet, peace be upon him, summoned the pursuit with a condition that made it the passage's own assay: none may march but those who stood yesterday: and the wounded rose—the transmitted scenes the file's flesh: the brothers of Banu Abd al-Ashhal, both injured, one carried part of the road by the other; the ranks that had bled through the passes re-forming, at eight miles' pursuit, around the commander who had called them from behind (verse 153's frame inverted: the called now running toward the caller)—alladhina stajabu lillahi war-rasuli min ba'di ma asabahumu l-qarh: those who responded to Allah and the Messenger after the wound had struck them: the qarh-word (verse 140's equalised wounds) fixing the response's price: obedience at the bandage's own hour.\n\n\"Those of them who did good and were mindful ˹of Allah˺ will have a great reward\" grades the file at its published wage—lilladhina ahsanu minhum wattaqaw ajrun azim: for those of them who did excellently and were mindful: a great reward—and the grading's two credentials complete the passage's whole formation-course, the commentators draw: ihsan and taqwa: the excellence and the mindfulness (the love-registry's summit at verse 134; the reinforcement-conditions at verse 125): the wounded march certified at both—the response itself the ihsan (the beautiful deed beyond the owed: no fresh obligation compelled the bandaged eight miles) and the taqwa its engine (the fear of Allah outweighing the fear of Quraysh: verse 175's coming sorting)—and the ajrun azim's grammar the file's seal: the great reward: the adjective this Book reserves for its heaviest payments (verse 179's closing clause) assigned to the community's sorest obedience: the pursuit that met no battle (Quraysh declining the field: the ru'b-forecast of verse 151 cashing at Hamra al-Asad's fires) yet banked the passage's richest wage: for the assay's whole object was the response, not the engagement.\n\nThe file's placement is the Uhud-curriculum's designed ending, the scholars press: the day's audit ran its severities—the disobedience named (152), the flight narrated (153), the cause owned (165)—and the curriculum closes at the repair demonstrated: the same ranks, within the day, walking the restored terms (the firmness and mindfulness the columns march on: verse 125's commentary): the community's worst week ending not at the wound but at the wounded march—the tamhis-doctrine (verse 141's assay) displaying its product: the fired metal moving; and the believer formed by the file carries its permanent repair-schedule: the lapse's morning-after being the response's own hour: the fallen formation re-dressed at the first summons, the bandages no exemption (the pardoned of 155 marching among the responders: the amnesty walked)—while the next verse preserves the marchers' own sentence at the warning's peak: \"Allah alone is sufficient for us, and He is the best Protector.\"",
  "173": "\"Those who were warned, 'Your enemies have mobilized their forces against you, so fear them,' the warning only made them grow stronger in faith\" preserves the Hamra al-Asad marchers' tested interior (verse 172's file)—and the test's mechanics are the verse's studied physics, the scholars unfold: alladhina qala lahumu n-nasu inna n-nasa qad jama'u lakum fakhshawhum: those to whom the people said: the people have gathered against you, so fear them: the warning's carriers fixed by the record (the riders crossing the pursuit's road with Quraysh's mustering-news—Abu Sufyan's own commissioned rumour, by the transmitted accounts: the enemy's psychological operation running ahead of his declined return); and the operation's designed payload—fakhshawhum: fear them—landing on the bandaged column with the verse's astonishing result: fazadahum imana: it increased them in faith: the fear-payload converted, at the believing interior, into faith's own increase: the physics the commentators marvel at: the same news that breaks unanchored ranks (the ru'b of verse 151 operating on the shirk-hollowed chest) compounds the anchored (the threat driving the reliant deeper into the Reliance: each pressure-unit transferred to the Support it presses them against)—the faith-increase doctrine documented at its field-case: iman growing, by the texts' own testimony, at exactly the hours designed to kill it.\n\n\"And they replied, 'Allah ˹alone˺ is sufficient ˹as an aid˺ for us and ˹He˺ is the best Protector'\" records the column's sentence—hasbuna llahu wa ni'ma l-wakil: Allah is sufficient for us, and how excellent a Trustee—and the sentence's pedigree is the community's own treasure, the scholars set from the Sahih: Ibn Abbas: \"Allah is sufficient for us, and how excellent a Trustee—Ibrahim said it when he was cast into the fire; and Muhammad, peace be upon him, said it when the people told them 'the people have gathered against you, so fear them,' and it only increased them in faith\" (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 4563): the one sentence at history's two furnaces: the friend of Allah on the catapult's arm and the wounded column on the pursuit-road—the hasbala's anatomy the commentators draw: hasbuna: our sufficiency: the threat's whole arithmetic answered at the Supply (verse 160's charter: the Helper's presence voiding every count); ni'ma l-wakil: the best Trustee: the affairs handed over at the excellence-grade (the wakalah of verse 159's tawakkul-seal): the sentence being the reliance-doctrine compressed to a breath—and issued, by its two furnace-provenances, as the community's standing equipment at every gathered-against hour.\n\nThe verse's permanent function, standing back, is the believer's threat-conversion manual, the scholars press: the gathered-against seasons certain (the operations of every era running the same payload: the mustered numbers announced, the fear commanded), and the column's two-step published as the response: the interior first (the payload converted: the pressure driven into the anchor—faith audited at the news's arrival: does the threat shrink the heart or deepen it?), the sentence second (the hasbala spoken as the handing-over's instrument: the Prophet's own prescription at the overwhelming: \"when a matter overwhelms you, say: Allah is sufficient for me, and how excellent a Trustee\"—the sentence's usage across the Sunnah's family)—while the next verse publishes the conversion's settlement: \"so they returned with Allah's favours and grace, suffering no harm.\"",
  "174": "\"So they returned with Allah's favours and grace, suffering no harm\" publishes the hasbala-column's settlement (verse 173's sentence now paid)—and the settlement's inventory is the verse's studied audit, the scholars unfold: fanqalabu bi-ni'matin mina llahi wa fadlin: so they turned back with favour from Allah and bounty: the return-verb (inqalabu) pointed by the careful readers: the enemy's designed inversion (verse 149's warned turning-back to disbelief) answered by the believers' actual one: the column turned back—with favour and bounty: the pursuit's ledger itemised from the record: the ni'mah of the community preserved (Quraysh declining the return: the ru'b of verse 151 at Abu Sufyan's own council—the operation's rumour-payload spent, its army marching home), the honour banked (the wounded pursuit itself re-writing Arabia's reading of Uhud: the community that marches the morning after its worst day is not a broken force—the strategic settlement the campaign purchased), and the fadl's material excess the commentators note from the accounts: the column trading at Badr's fair during the stand, the profits carried home: the bounty literal upon the spiritual; lam yamsas'hum su': no harm touched them: the settlement's third entry the operation's complete failure: the threatened annihilation arriving as—nothing: the gathered-against column returning unscratched.\n\n\"For they sought to please Allah. And surely Allah is ˹the˺ Lord of infinite bounty\" seals the settlement at its cause and its source—wattaba'u ridwana llah: and they pursued the pleasure of Allah: the ridwan-clause (verse 162's sorting-column: the Pleasure's pursuers) filing the column's whole campaign at its intention: the march not for vengeance's heat nor honour's recovery but the Pleasure—the settlement thus paid, the scholars press, on the intention-ledger's terms (verse 145's payroll: the Hereafter's desirers given of both worlds: verse 148's two-column payment re-enacted at Hamra al-Asad within the week of its revelation); wallahu dhu fadlin azim: and Allah is the Possessor of immense bounty: the fadl-seal (Surah 2, verse 105's title) closing the file at the Supply the hasbala drew on: the sufficiency-sentence honoured from a bounty without floor.\n\nThe settlement's function in the curriculum, standing back, is the Uhud-passage's demonstrated restoration, the commentators draw: the charter of verse 160 ran two conditionals—helped and unbeatable, forsaken and unhelped—and the week walked both: the hill's lapse under the second, and Hamra al-Asad under the first: the same ranks, the terms repaired (the response, the ihsan, the taqwa: verse 172's credentials), receiving the first conditional's full operation (no overcomer: the enemy declining even to meet them)—the curriculum thus closing its field-work at the proof the community most needed: the schedule intact, the conditions repairable, the repair paid at once and in both coins; and the believer formed by the file carries its complete sequence as issued equipment: the summons answered at the bandage's hour (172), the threat converted at the interior (173), the sentence spoken (the hasbala), and the return awaited in the settlement's three entries: favour, bounty, and the harm that never arrives—while the next verse unmasks the whole operation's true author: \"that warning was only from Satan, trying to prompt you to fear his followers.\"",
  "175": "\"That ˹warning˺ was only ˹from˺ Satan, trying to prompt you to fear his followers\" unmasks the gathered-against operation's true author (verses 173 and 174's rumour-campaign)—and the unmasking's attribution is the verse's studied intelligence, the scholars unfold: innama dhalikumu sh-shaytanu yukhawwifu awliya'ah: that was only Satan frightening ˹you with˺ his allies: the riders' news, the mustering-rumours, the commissioned panic (Abu Sufyan's operation: verse 173's commentary)—all of it filed at its source: the enemy's psychological apparatus being, at its root, Satan's own instrument: the takhwif—the frightening-by: the awliya-clause read at both its constructions, the commentators note: Satan frightening the believers with his allies (the mustered numbers inflated, the enemy's shadow lengthened: fear's whole cinema projected) and frightening his own allies into the campaigns (the two readings one operation: the apparatus running on manufactured fear at both ends)—the attribution's function being the fear's demotion, the scholars press: the threat that arrives source-labelled loses its payload: the community shown the rumour-mill's proprietor reads its products as advertisements, not intelligence (Surah 2, verse 268's identical unmasking: \"Satan threatens you with poverty\": the forecast without portfolio).\n\n\"So do not fear them; fear Me if you are ˹true˺ believers\" issues the fear-monopoly's decree—fala takhafuhum wa khafuni in kuntum mu'minin: so fear them not, and fear Me, if you are believers—and the decree's economy is the whole passage's theology sealed, the commentators draw: fear, in the believing interior, is a single allocation: assigned to the creatures, it governs for the operation's proprietor (the frightened community yields at the counsel-stalls: verse 149's warned obedience); assigned to Allah, it liberates from every lesser fear (the khashyah-monopoly of Surah 2, verse 150's qiblah-decree: \"do not fear them; fear Me\"—the identical construction: the community's one fear as its whole immunity): the hasbala-column's documented physics (verse 173's faith-increase at the fear-payload) being the decree walked—and the conditional's closing edge the decree's audit, the scholars press: in kuntum mu'minin: if you are believers: the fear's address as faith's own test (verse 139's identical conditional over the elevation): whom a heart fears most is what it believes in most: the allocations legible, at every gathered-against hour, in the community's counsel-rooms and its marching-columns.\n\nThe verse's permanent equipment, standing back, is the believer's fear-jurisprudence entire, the scholars draw: the era's frightening-operations constant (the mustered forces announced, the consequences projected, the allies' shadows cast: every age's takhwif running the one apparatus), and the verse's two-step issued against them all: the source-label first (the fear-products read at their proprietor: the panic that arrives unbidden auditing its own origin), the allocation second (the one Fear enthroned, the rest evicted: the Prophet's taught liberation—the servant who fears Allah alone finding all things fear him, while the creature-fearer is given over to his fears: the transmitted wisdom's family)—and the conditional carried as the standing self-audit: the fear-map of each season redrawn at the creed's own compass; while the passage now turns to the Prophet's own consolation at the racers toward disbelief: \"do not grieve for those who race to disbelieve—surely they will not harm Allah in the least.\"",
  "176": "\"˹O Prophet!˺ Do not grieve for those who race to disbelieve—surely they will not harm Allah in the least\" turns the passage's consolation to its bearer—and the addressed grief is the office's own documented wound, the scholars unfold: wa la yahzunka lladhina yusari'una fil-kufr: and let not those who race into disbelief grieve you: the racing-verb (the musara'ah of verse 133's commanded hastening toward forgiveness here inverted: the counter-race: the hypocrites' aftermath-campaigns, the deserters' compounding, the factions sprinting toward the old order at the wound's season)—and the grieved one the Prophet himself, peace be upon him: the hazn-prohibition addressing the mission's tenderest burden (Surah 18, verse 6's \"perhaps you will grieve yourself to death\"; verse 128's boundary over the same heart): the racers' spectacle—souls sprinting from the light he bled to bring them—landing on the office's compassion as grief upon grief; and the consolation's first clause the office's release: innahum lan yadurru llaha shay'a: they will never harm Allah at all: the race's whole yield voided at its imagined register (the harm-ceiling of verse 111 applied to the mission's Owner: the cause un-dented by its deserters: Surah 47, verse 38's replacement-doctrine)—the Prophet's grief unburdened at its root: the racing damages no one but the racers.\n\n\"It is Allah's Will to disallow them a share in the Hereafter, and they will suffer a tremendous punishment\" publishes the race's actual settlement—yuridu llahu alla yaj'ala lahum hazzan fil-akhirah: Allah intends to assign them no portion in the Hereafter—and the clause's theology held with the scholars' care: the iradah-clause read at this Book's constant architecture (the racers' own chosen sprint met by the Will's judicial assignment: the sealing-after-choosing of Surah 2, verse 7's commentary; verse 178's coming respite-doctrine): the no-portion decree the race's own destination ratified (the khalaq-forfeiture of verse 77's oath-traders: the Hereafter's share the one asset the racing spends)—wa lahum adhabun azim: and theirs is a tremendous punishment: the sentence's scale (the azim-register: verse 105's splitters) matching the race's compounding (verse 90's increase-file: the disbelief built upon disbelief).\n\nThe consolation's permanent function, standing back, is the caller's grief-jurisprudence, the commentators draw: the office's inheritors—the teachers, parents, and callers of every era watching their own racers sprint from the light—served by the verse's three-fold release: the grief bounded (felt, as the Prophet's was, but not governing: the hazn-prohibition guarding the mission's continuance), the harm-register corrected (the cause un-harmed: the racing's damage all self-inflicted—the deserter's exit subtracting nothing from the truth's standing: verse 144's heel-turners harming Allah nothing), and the settlement trusted to its Desk (the no-portion decree His assignment, not the caller's sentence: verse 128's withdrawn file standing)—the caller freed, by the three, for the callable: the grief that would have spent itself on the racers re-invested in the reachable; while the next verse files the racers' transaction at the chapter's own market-language: \"those who trade belief for disbelief will never harm Allah in the least.\"",
  "177": "\"Those who trade belief for disbelief will never harm Allah in the least, and they will suffer a painful punishment\" files the racers' transaction (verse 176's sprint) at the chapter's market-register—and the filing's verb is the argument's oldest instrument, the scholars unfold: inna lladhina shtarawu l-kufra bil-iman: those who purchased disbelief at the price of belief: the shira-grammar (Surah 2, verse 16's founding trade: \"who trade guidance for misguidance\"; verse 86's Hereafter sold; verse 90's souls) here at its starkest exchange: the iman itself tendered as payment—the racers of verse 176 audited at their counter: what the sprint spends is the faith: each stride into the kufr a unit of belief handed across (the apostasy-file's economics: verse 90's increase purchasing the turning-organ's own decay)—and the transaction's harm-audit repeated from verse 176 verbatim: lan yadurru llaha shay'a: they will never harm Allah at all: the repetition's insistence the consolation's completion, the commentators press: the trade damages neither the Owner (the estate un-needing: verse 97's closing) nor the cause (the mission's ranks re-filled past every exit: the replacement-doctrine) nor the community's standing (verse 139's altitude un-moved by the deserters' census): the purchase's whole cost falling, by the market's own law, on the purchaser: wa lahum adhabun alim: and theirs is a painful punishment: the settlement's grade shifted, the careful readers note, from verse 176's azim (the tremendous: the compounding racers' scale) to alim (the painful: the trade's personal invoice)—the two verses' paired sentences pricing the one file at its two registers: the race's public enormity and the racer's private receipt.\n\nThe market-filing's function at the passage's station is the consolation's jurisprudence completed, the scholars draw: the Prophet's grief (verse 176's address) was compassion's response to the racers as souls; the market-verse re-frames the same spectacle as transaction: the racers not victims of a seizure but parties to a purchase—the iman theirs to spend, the kufr theirs to buy, the counter open in both directions until the seal (the re-purchase standing at verse 89's door: the repentance that trades back)—the caller's compassion thus schooled, not extinguished, the commentators press: grief for the trader appropriate at the trade's foolishness (the asset-audit: the one currency that crosses, spent on the one acquisition that burns), and the file held at the market's own hope: traders re-trade: the community's history stocked with the bought-back (the qunut-list's conversions: verse 128's commentary).\n\nThe verse's permanent equipment, standing back, closes the consolation-passage's arc (verses 176 and 177) at the believer's own counter, the scholars note: the trade-grammar's mirror facing, as this argument's mirrors always face, the reader before the neighbour: the iman a currency spent or banked at every choosing (Surah 2, verse 16's commentary: every man trading daily), the racers' file differing from the ordinary believer's not in the market but in the direction and the rate—whence the audit carried home: which counter does this season's conduct stand at, and what is being tendered?—while the passage now turns the respite-doctrine upon the traders' apparent prosperity: \"those who disbelieve should not think that living longer is good for them.\"",
  "178": "\"Those who disbelieve should not think that living longer is good for them\" corrects the traders' last consolation (verse 177's purchasers reading their unpunished years as vindication)—and the corrected thought is history's oldest misreading, the scholars unfold: wa la yahsabanna lladhina kafaru annama numli lahum khayrun li'anfusihim: and let not the disbelievers reckon that Our granting them respite is good for their souls: the imla-word: the extending: the rope paid out—the racers of verse 176 sprinting unarrested, the traders of verse 177 prospering at their counters, Quraysh marching home unpunished from Uhud: the whole spectacle read, by its subjects and by watching doubters, as heaven's indifference or endorsement (the misreading this argument has met at every prosperity-station: Surah 2, verse 212's dazzling portion; Surah 3, verse 196's coming caution: \"do not be deceived by the prosperity of the disbelievers\")—and the correction's mechanism published without softening: innama numli lahum liyazdadu ithma: We only grant them respite that they may increase in sin: the respite's actual function in the hardened file: the extended years extending the ledger—each unpunished season another stratum of earning (the increase-doctrine of verse 90's compounding; Surah 2, verse 15's \"leaving them to continue wandering\"): the rope paid out being, for the persisting, the fall's own lengthening.\n\n\"They are only given more time to increase in sin, and they will suffer a humiliating punishment\" completes the correction at its terminus—wa lahum adhabun muhin: and theirs is a humiliating punishment: the muhin-grade (the humiliation answering the prosperity's pride: Surah 2, verse 90's identical pairing: the arrogance-invoice paid in disgrace's coin)—and the doctrine held with the scholars' care at its boundaries, the commentators press: the respite-reading applies to the persisting file (the hardened traders whose seasons compound), never as a universal decoder of delay: for the same extended years are, toward the open file, mercy's own instrument (the door of verse 89 held open precisely by the unarrested seasons; the qunut-list's conversions purchased by the respite: Khalid's decade, Ikrimah's)—the one imla serving two administrations: towards the returning, the space of return; towards the persisting, the rope: the difference never in the granted time but in the granted-to (Surah 2, verse 26's parable-sorting: one instrument, two receptions)—whence the believer's schooled reading of every unpunished prosperity: the delay decoded neither as endorsement (the corrected thought) nor as automatic doom (the open file's hope) but as the test still running: the season's meaning determined by the seasoned one's response.\n\nThe verse's permanent equipment, standing back, serves the community at its two chronic temptations, the scholars draw: envy's reading (the wicked's long noon read as heaven's favour: the reading Surah 2, verse 212 barred and the psalm-old complaint this verse settles: the prosperity re-priced as accumulating invoice) and impatience's reading (the community's own pressed seasons read against the enemy's easy ones: the alternated-days law, verse 140, holding both columns' schedules at one Desk)—the believer formed by the correction carrying its audit-question over every era's untouchable tyrannies and unpunished frauds: not \"why does heaven wait?\" but \"what is the waiting filling?\"—the ledgers, by this verse's mechanism, never idle; while the next verse turns from the respite's readers to its purpose in the community's own sifting: \"Allah would not leave the believers in the condition you were in, until He distinguished the good from the evil.\"",
  "179": "\"Allah would not leave the believers in the condition you were in, until He distinguished the good from the evil ˹among you˺\" publishes the whole Uhud-season's design-statement—and the statement's negation is its doctrine, the scholars unfold: ma kana llahu liyadhara l-mu'minina ala ma antum alayhi: Allah would not leave the believers upon what you are on: the pre-Uhud condition named at its problem: the community's ranks mingled—believer and hypocrite one indistinguishable uniform (the mixed columns of verse 154's night; the road's three hundred marching as ranks until the road tested them: verse 167)—and the mingling untenable by the mission's own nature, the commentators press: the commissioned community (verse 110's office) cannot operate unsorted: its counsels penetrated (Surah 9, verse 47's scurrying whisperers), its trust-architecture corrupted, its witness-function (Surah 2, verse 143) clouded—hatta yamiza l-khabitha mina t-tayyib: until He distinguishes the foul from the good: the mayz-verb: the sifting: Uhud's whole file re-stated as the sifting's instrument (the two harvests of verse 166: the believers distinguished, the hypocrites exposed)—the wound re-framed, one final time, as the community's necessary refining.\n\n\"Nor would Allah ˹directly˺ reveal to you the unseen, but He chooses whoever He wills as a messenger\" bars the sifting's imagined shortcut—wa ma kana llahu liyutli'akum alal-ghayb: and Allah would not acquaint you with the unseen: the community's wished-for decoder (the hypocrites named by list, the ranks pre-sorted by disclosure) declined at the doctrine's root, the scholars draw: the ghayb His (verse 44's epistemology; Surah 2, verse 255's knowledge-clause), and the test's whole architecture requiring its concealment (the sifting operates because the uniforms are opaque: ranks sorted by conduct under trial, never by roster leaked—the examination-doctrine of verse 142: the claims converted to records, not read off registers); wa lakinna llaha yajtabi min rusulihi man yasha: but Allah chooses of His messengers whom He wills: the exception's channel: the unseen's portions delivered by election—the messengers acquainted (the exposures this passage itself carried: verse 167's published excuses, verse 154's concealed sentences: the community's sifting served by revelation's own disclosures, at the Chooser's dosage)—the clause guarding both registers at once, the commentators note: against the diviners' claims (no soul's ghayb-access but by the sending: Surah 72, verses 26 and 27's guarded disclosure) and against the community's impatience (the sorting's pace His administration: the tests scheduled, the exposures dosed).\n\n\"So believe in Allah and His messengers. And if you are faithful and mindful ˹of Allah˺, you will receive a great reward\" closes the design-statement at its summons—fa'aminu billahi wa rusulih: so believe in Allah and His messengers: the response the whole architecture asks: the creed held through the sifting's seasons (the tests read as design, the exposures as mercy, the concealments as the examination's integrity); wa in tu'minu wa tattaqu falakum ajrun azim: and if you believe and are mindful, yours is a great reward: the ajrun-azim seal (verse 172's wage over the wounded marchers)—the believer formed by the statement carrying its permanent test-theology: the community's mingled seasons never abandonment but administration: the sifting scheduled, the shortcut barred, the messengers' disclosures sufficient—and the individual's part unchanged through every season's opacity: the belief and the taqwa that need no roster, being themselves what the sifting is sifting for.",
  "180": "\"And do not let those who ˹greedily˺ withhold Allah's bounties think it is good for them—in fact, it is bad for them!\" turns the respite-correction's grammar (verse 178's corrected thought) upon the community's own purse—and the paralleled construction is the passage's designed symmetry, the scholars unfold: wa la yahsabanna lladhina yabkhaluna bima atahumu llahu min fadlih: and let not those who are miserly with what Allah has given them of His bounty reckon it good for them: the bukhl-file: the withholding—the hoarded wealth first (the zakat unpaid: the file's fiqh-register by the commentators' majority), and the clause's breadth holding every withheld bounty (the knowledge sat upon: the concealment-covenant two verses ahead; the strength, the standing, the witness withheld from the cause's need): the misreckoning identical to the disbelievers' (verse 178's annama... khayrun: the same \"good for them\" corrected): the hoarder reading his growing pile as his growing good—bal huwa sharrun lahum: rather, it is bad for them: the correction flat: the pile itself the injury (Surah 2, verse 268's whisper-audit: the withholding sold as prudence; verse 195's self-destruction by the clutched hand).\n\n\"They will be leashed ˹by their necks˺ on the Day of Judgment with whatever ˹wealth˺ they used to withhold\" publishes the injury's form—sayutawwaquna ma bakhilu bihi yawma l-qiyamah: they will be collared with what they withheld on the Day of Resurrection—and the Sunnah unveils the collar entire, the scholars set from the Sahih: the Prophet, peace be upon him: \"whoever Allah gives wealth and he does not pay its zakat, his wealth will be made to appear to him on the Day of Judgment as a bald-headed serpent with two black spots over its eyes, which will encircle him on that Day, then seize him by his two jaws and say: I am your wealth; I am your treasure\"—and he recited this verse (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 1403): the hoard's conversion the punishment's exact justice, the commentators press: the withheld thing itself the instrument (the ghulul-file's carried camels: verse 161's necks; the collar here the miser's own pile worn)—what was clutched against Allah's claims returning as the clutcher's chain: the wealth that would not circulate in the world circling its owner at the Day.\n\n\"And Allah is the ˹sole˺ inheritor of the heavens and the earth. And Allah is All-Aware of what you do\" seals the file at the estate's final probate—wa lillahi mirathu s-samawati wal-ard: and to Allah belongs the inheritance of the heavens and the earth—and the seal's selection is the bukhl-file's dissolving argument, the scholars draw: the hoarding's whole premise (the pile as the holder's) audited at the estate's terminal fact: every holding reverts—the heavens' and earth's entire inventory probating, at the last, to the one Inheritor (Surah 19, verse 40's \"We will inherit the earth and all those on it\"): the miser thus exposed as the estate's most confused tenant: withholding from the Owner what is already deeded to Him, guarding against His claims a pile en route to His probate—the only editable variable being the route: circulated at His command, the wealth crosses as the holder's credit (Surah 2, verse 110's sent-forth ledger); clutched, it crosses as his collar; wallahu bima ta'maluna khabir: and Allah is Aware of what you do: the khabir-seal (Surah 2, verse 271's banking-clause) closing the file at the audit that reads the withholdings no assessor sees—the quiet shavings, the pleaded incapacities, the bounties' hidden registers—while the passage now turns to the withholding-file's most brazen specimen: the mockers who heard the loan-verse and answered, \"Allah is poor; we are rich.\"",
  "181": "\"Indeed, Allah has heard those ˹among the Jews˺ who said, 'Allah is poor; we are rich!'\" opens the mockers' file at its recorded blasphemy—and the sentence's occasion is fixed by the commentators from the transmitted accounts: when the loan-verse descended (\"who will lend to Allah a good loan\"—Surah 2, verse 245; the qard-courtship's grammar), voices among Madinah's Jewish establishment seized the metaphor for mockery: a Lord who borrows must be poor—and we, the solicited, rich: the sneer's authorship the accounts name (Finhas of Qaynuqa the transmitted speaker; the establishment's circles its market)—and the verse's opening verb the file's first sentence, the scholars unfold: laqad sami'a llahu qawla lladhina qalu: Allah has certainly heard the saying of those who said: the sami'a-clause: the mockery's first consequence its registration: the closed-circle wit (the sneer traded in the safe assembly: Surah 2, verse 76's managed disclosures) published to its speakers as heard—the audit-doctrine (verse 5's unhidden estate) operating at the tongue's most private registers.\n\n\"We have certainly recorded their slurs and their killing of prophets unjustly\" files the heard sentence with its companion-charge—sanaktubu ma qalu wa qatlahumu l-anbiya'a bighayri haqq: We will record what they said, and their killing of the prophets without right—and the pairing's jurisprudence is the verse's studied depth, the commentators press: the sneer and the prophet-blood entered on one line: the mockery of Allah's words filed with the murder of His messengers (the three-count indictment of verse 21; the file of Surah 2, verse 91)—the pairing's logic the argument's oldest: the tongue's contempt and the hand's violence one enmity at two organs (the fathers' blades, the sons' wit: verse 183 will hear them plead the fathers' own criteria)—and the recording-verb's tense the file's patience: sanaktubu: We shall write: the registration continuing, the invoice compiling (verse 178's respite-mechanism: the extended seasons extending the ledger)—thumma naqulu dhuqu adhaba l-hariq: then We will say: taste the torment of the burning: the sentence's deferred delivery at the Day's own desk: the mockers of the \"poor\" Lord addressed, at the last, by the Rich One's justice—the dhuqu-imperative (taste: the sensory verb answering the sneer's sensory smugness: the wealth fingered while the wit ran) serving the burning as the file's compiled total.\n\nThe file's permanent function, standing back, guards the community's whole relationship with revelation's figures of speech, the scholars draw: the loan-metaphor's courtship (Surah 2, verse 245's commentary: the Owner dignifying His servants' giving as lending) was mercy's own rhetoric—and the mockers' method the deviant hearts' standing one (verse 7's file): the tender figure pried from its plain frame and weaponised as absurdity: the method's every era's edition audited by this verse's registration-clause: the revelation's expressions handled, by the schooled believer, at their intended register (the anthropomorphic sneers and the literalist bludgeons both barred: the well-grounded's \"we believe in it—all of it is from our Lord\"), and the mockers' assemblies—ancient and modern—reminded of the file's first line: heard; while the next verse completes the sentence's justice-clause: \"this is for what your hands have done—and Allah is never unjust to His creation.\"",
  "182": "\"This is ˹the reward˺ for what your hands have done\" delivers the mockers' sentence its causation-clause (verse 181's compiled file: the sneer, the prophet-blood, the burning)—and the clause's grammar is the Day's own address, the scholars unfold: dhalika bima qaddamat aydikum: that is for what your hands sent forward: the qaddamat-verb: the sending-ahead (Surah 2, verse 110's ledger-grammar inverted: the good sent forth finding its Sender; here the crimes forwarded finding theirs): the burning of verse 181's imperative sourced, at its tasting, to its tasters' own dispatch—the hands' idiom the commentators weigh: the deeds attributed to the hands as agency's emblem (the tongues' sneers included: the whole conduct-file gathered under the working limb)—the sentence thus served, at the Day, with its invoice attached: no torment unexplained, no sufferer unshown his own consignment (the deeds-made-present doctrine: verse 30's presentation; Surah 18, verse 49's \"they will find everything they did present\").\n\n\"And Allah is never unjust to ˹His˺ creation\" seals the sentence with the justice-clause at its emphatic form—wa anna llaha laysa bizallamin lil-abid: and that Allah is not ever unjust to the servants—and the form's intensity is the seal's studied theology, the scholars press: the zallam-grade (the intensive: not merely \"not unjust\" but \"never, in any measure, toward any servant\") deployed, as at this argument's every severity (verse 108's identical station: the no-injustice clause after the Day's scenes; Surah 2, verse 281's solvency), at the exact point the sentence's weight might suggest excess: the burning's tasters addressed, in their sentence's own text, with the tribunal's incorruptibility: the file self-assembled (verse 181's recording), the invoice self-forwarded (this verse's hands), the Judge un-needing and un-tilted (verse 109's estate-argument: the Owner of all gains nothing by wronging any)—the pairing of the two clauses being the Book's complete penal theology in one breath, the commentators draw: every punishment a delivery (the sent-ahead arriving) and no punishment an imposition (the zallam-negation absolute).\n\nThe seal's permanent function, standing back, serves the community's own reading of every hard file, the scholars note: the sentences this passage has served—the collared misers (verse 180), the burning's tasters (181), the no-portion racers (176)—all stand under this verse's two clauses: the reader's recoil at any severity re-addressed to the severity's source: the hands that forwarded (the question never \"why does He punish so?\" but \"what did they send?\")—and the seal's mirror facing, as the argument's mirrors always face, the reader's own consignments: the qaddamat-ledger open at every hand this hour (Surah 2, verse 110's \"whatever good you send forth for yourselves, you will find it with Allah\": the same forwarding-system, the same finding, the cargo the only variable)—the believer formed by the clause dispatching accordingly; while the mockers' file now reopens at their pleaded criterion: \"Allah has commanded us not to believe in any messenger unless he brings us an offering consumed by fire.\"",
  "183": "\"Those ˹are the same people˺ who say, 'Allah has commanded us not to believe in any messenger unless he brings us an offering to be consumed by fire ˹from the sky˺'\" reopens the mockers' file at their pleaded criterion—and the plea's construction is evasion's most learned form, the scholars unfold: alladhina qalu inna llaha ahida ilayna: those who said: Allah covenanted with us: the claimed charter: a standing divine instruction—alla nu'mina lirasulin hatta ya'tiyana biqurbanin ta'kuluhu n-nar: that we believe no messenger until he brings us a sacrifice the fire consumes: the fire-from-heaven sign (the offering accepted by visible descent: the earlier scriptures' remembered episodes): the criterion pleaded, the commentators press, as piety's own caution—we are not resisters but covenant-keepers: the awaited prophet must present the ancestral credential—the plea's function transparent at its station: the recognised Messenger (Surah 2, verse 146's file) declined behind a test selected precisely because his mission's published signs (the Book's challenge, the confirmations: verse 184's coming inventory) did not include it: the demand-for-signs jurisprudence (Surah 2, verse 118's hearts-all-alike) at its scriptural dress: the criterion invented, the covenant unproduced (the Torah summoned at verse 93's method would find no such charter), and the delay indefinitely renewable.\n\n\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ 'Other prophets did in fact come to you before me with clear proofs and ˹even˺ what you demanded—why then did you kill them, if what you say is true?'\" answers the plea with the file's own records—qul qad ja'akum rusulun min qabli bil-bayyinati wa billadhi qultum: say: messengers came to you before me with the clear proofs and with that which you said: the answer's concession its blade, the scholars draw: grant the criterion—the fire-consumed offering was brought: the earlier messengers arrived with the bayyinat and with the demanded sign itself (the scriptures' own fire-answered altars)—falima qataltumuhum: then why did you kill them: the record's Zakariyyas and Yahyas (verse 181's paired charge; verse 21's indictment): the criterion's own certified passers slain by the criterion's pleaders' fathers—in kuntum sadiqin: if you are truthful: the truth-conditional (verse 168's challenge-form) closing the trap entire, the commentators note: the plea's two components cannot cohere: if the fire-sign compels belief, the fathers' blades against its bearers convict the house; if the blades were the house's way with the sign's bearers, the sign was never the criterion—the pleaded covenant exposed, at either horn, as the resistance's paperwork: the demand not the belief's condition but its indefinite deferral (the same anatomy at every demanded-sign station: the granted signs of the nations disbelieved anyway: Surah 17, verse 59's withheld wholesale granting).\n\nThe verse's permanent equipment, standing back, is the caller's manual against criterion-pleading, the scholars press: the method's every era's editions—the conversion conditioned on the un-offered proof, the compliance deferred behind the self-selected test, the \"I would believe if\"—audited at this verse's two instruments: the criterion's pedigree demanded (produce the covenant: verse 93's bring-and-read), and the pleader's record consulted (how fared the criterion's actual passers at these hands?): the sincerity of every demanded test measured, decisively, by the demanders' conduct toward the evidence already delivered—while the next verse turns the exposed rejection into the Messenger's own consolation: \"if you are rejected by them, so too were messengers before you.\"",
  "184": "\"If you are rejected by them, so too were messengers before you who came with clear proofs, divine Books, and enlightening Scriptures\" converts the mockers' exposed file (verses 181 to 183) into the office's consolation—and the consolation's instrument is the census, the scholars unfold: fa'in kadhdhabuka faqad kudhdhiba rusulun min qablik: then if they reject you, messengers before you were rejected: the takdhib-census (verse 146's company of the unfaltering here read at its bearers' own register): the rejection normalised at the office's whole history—the Prophet's wound at the criterion-pleaders' evasions (the recognised mission declined behind invented charters) dressed by the file of every predecessor so declined: the consolation's logic the sunan-doctrine (verse 137's passed patterns), the commentators press: the rejection that arrives on schedule, at the pattern's documented station, cannot indict the rejected (the fault-question re-addressed: the constant across the census being the rejecters' method, never the messengers' deficiency).\n\n\"Who came with clear proofs, divine Books, and enlightening Scriptures\" itemises the rejected cargo—ja'u bil-bayyinati waz-zuburi wal-kitabi l-munir: they came with the clear proofs, the Psalms-books, and the illuminating Scripture—and the three-fold inventory is the census's evidentiary clause, the scholars draw: the bayyinat (the signs: the demanded and the granted alike: verse 183's fire-answered altars included), the zubur (the inspired writings: the prophetic corpus's breadth), and the kitab munir (the light-giving Book: the legislation's illuminating registers): the predecessors' full credential-set listed to seal the consolation's point: the rejection operated against complete deliveries—no messenger's cargo was ever the shortfall (the criterion-pleaders' implicit charge dissolved at the inventory): the rejecters of every station rejecting, with the proofs entire on the table, from the file's own named motives (the envy, the revenue, the rank: Surah 2, verse 90's diagnosis; verse 183's deferral-machinery)—the office's inheritors thus schooled, the commentators note, past the caller's most corrosive misreading: the response-rate read as the delivery's audit (the crowds' declining taken as the message's failing): the census ruling the reading false at every era: the perfect deliveries rejected, the illuminating Books cast behind backs (Surah 2, verse 101), the fire-answered bearers slain—clarity never having guaranteed reception (Surah 2, verse 118's sorting: the signs sufficient for the sincere, insufficient for the settled).\n\nThe verse's permanent function, standing back, is the consolation-jurisprudence issued to every bearer of the truth's cargo, the scholars press: the rejection-seasons filed at the census (the company kept: the rejected of the office's whole roll—the Prophet's own usage of the predecessors' files at his wounds: the transmitted solaces of the sirah's hard years), the cargo audited at the inventory (the deliverer's duty the delivery's completeness: Surah 2, verse 119's boundary—the clear proofs presented, the illuminating Book opened: the reception's file at its own Desk: verse 128's withdrawn amr), and the endurance financed by the pattern's known endings (the rejecters' aqibah-column: verse 137's commanded fieldwork; the rejected's vindication: the census's every entry closed, in history's record, at the messengers' side)—while the passage now widens the consolation to its universal floor: the sentence that levels every file, mocker and messenger, at one tasting: \"every soul will taste death.\"",
  "185": "\"Every soul will taste death\" lays the universal floor beneath the whole passage's files (the mockers' respite, the misers' collars, the rejected messengers' consolation)—and the sentence's five words are the Book's most level ground, the scholars unfold: kullu nafsin dha'iqatu l-mawt: every soul is a taster of death: the kull-clause without exception's crack (the prophets included: verse 144's paper; the mockers included: verse 181's deferred tasting; the wealthy and the collared, the racers and the marchers): the dha'iqah-word the verse's studied figure, the commentators press: death tasted—the experience personal, unmediated, undelegatable (no soul tastes another's: the individual crossing of Surah 6, verse 94)—and a tasting, not a terminus: the taster persists through the taste (the barzakh's continuing files: verse 169's living martyrs; the grave's registers): death re-filed, by its own verse, as passage's flavour, not existence's end.\n\n\"And you will only receive your full reward on the Day of Judgment\" schedules the settlement past the tasting—wa innama tuwaffawna ujurakum yawma l-qiyamah: and you will only be paid your wages in full on the Day of Resurrection: the tawfiyah-clause (the full-discharge grammar: Surah 2, verse 281; verse 57) with its innama-restriction the verse's patience-doctrine, the scholars draw: the complete accounting deferred to its one session: the world's interim payments partial by design (the respited mockers unpunished, the marching believers under-credited: the passage's every apparent imbalance)—the full wage at the Day alone: whence the community's audit-discipline: no file read as closed at any worldly season's balance (verse 178's corrected decoding).\n\n\"Whoever is spared from the Fire and is admitted into Paradise will ˹indeed˺ triumph\" defines success at the settlement's own desk—faman zuhziha ani n-nari wa udkhila l-jannata faqad faz: whoever is drawn away from the Fire and admitted to the Garden has triumphed: the zahzahah-verb the definition's mercy (the drawing-away: the rescue's image—success's first component the escape), and the fawz-word the definition itself: the triumph: this Book's success-term assigned, exclusively, to the two-clause outcome (the Fire escaped, the Garden entered)—every other victory-register (the markets', the fields', the seasons') re-priced at the definition: the Prophet, peace be upon him, completing the verse's own arithmetic: \"a place in Paradise the size of a whip is better than the world and all it contains\"—and he recited this verse's closing clause (the transmission at Jami` at-Tirmidhi's chapters on this passage, graded sound; the whip-length comparison at Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 3250).\n\n\"Whereas the life of this world is no more than the delusion of enjoyment\" closes the floor-verse at the world's final pricing—wa ma l-hayatu d-dunya illa mata'u l-ghurur: and the world's life is nothing but the enjoyment of delusion: the mata-word (the traveller's kit: verse 14's pricing) compounded with the ghurur (the deception: the kit mistaken for the destination): the world not evil but deceptive—its enjoyments real (the mata conceded) and their promise false (the permanence implied, the sufficiency suggested: the delusion's whole cargo)—the believer formed by the verse carrying its three-clause equipment as the passage's distillate: the tasting certain (the preparations sized to it), the settlement scheduled (the interims un-decoded), and the triumph defined (the two clauses pursued past every deluding kit)—while the next verse issues the road's terms between here and the tasting: \"you will surely be tested in your wealth and yourselves.\"",
  "186": "\"You ˹believers˺ will surely be tested in your wealth and yourselves\" issues the road's terms between the floor-verse's two certainties (verse 185's tasting and settlement)—and the terms' grammar is the notice's solemnity, the scholars unfold: latublawunna fi amwalikum wa anfusikum: you will most surely be tested in your wealth and your selves: the doubled emphasis (the lam and the nun of certainty) the covenant's own signature: the testing not contingency but clause (Surah 2, verse 155's identical notice: \"We will certainly test you\"—the syllabus there itemised: the fear, the hunger, the losses of wealth, life, and harvest; here compressed to the two registers that hold everything: the amwal and the anfus: the possessions and the persons)—the community served its terms in writing, the commentators press, precisely so that no season's arrival reads as breach: the tested believer holding the notice knows the testing as the road's published toll, never its ambush (verse 142's examination-doctrine; the shaken companies of Surah 2, verse 214).\n\n\"And you will certainly hear many hurtful words from those who were given the Scripture before you and ˹from˺ the polytheists\" adds the terms' third register—wa latasma'unna mina lladhina utu l-kitaba min qablikum wa mina lladhina ashraku adhan kathira: and you will surely hear, from those given the Book before you and from the associators, much hurt: the adha-word (verse 111's priced annoyance: the harm-ceiling's surface) at its auditory register: the tongues' campaigns—the mockeries (verse 181's sneer the passage's own specimen), the slanders, the criterion-pleadings, the crookedness-projections (verse 99)—scheduled from both flanks (the scriptural establishment and the pagan): the hearing certain (latasma'unna: the same doubled emphasis): the community's ears served notice with its purses and persons—and the notice's mercy its realism, the scholars draw: the wounding words pre-announced lose their ambush-force (the believer who expects the campaign reads each specimen as the schedule keeping: the notice itself the armour's first layer).\n\n\"But if you are patient and mindful ˹of Allah˺—surely this is a resolve to aspire to\" closes the terms with the response-clause—wa in tasbiru wa tattaqu fa'inna dhalika min azmi l-umur: but if you are patient and mindful, that is of the resolve of affairs: the sabr-taqwa pairing (the passage's constant equipment: verse 120's insulation, verse 125's reinforcement-conditions, verse 200's coming charter) prescribed over all three registers (the wealth's losses borne, the selves' wounds held, the tongues' campaigns un-answered in kind)—and the closing phrase's honour the verse's studied seal, the commentators note: min azmi l-umur: of the affairs of firm resolve: the patient mindfulness classed among existence's weightiest undertakings (the azm-word of the resolved decision: verse 159's azamta; Luqman's identical clause over the same pairing: Surah 31, verse 17): the endurance under the terms dignified not as passivity but as resolve's own summit—the believer formed by the notice carrying its three-part equipment: the terms held in writing (no season's testing read as breach), the campaigns priced at their surface (the adha bounded: verse 111), and the response filed at its true rank: the patient, mindful bearing of the road's published toll being among the greatest things a soul resolves; while the passage now turns to the notice's dark counterpart: the covenant the Scripture's custodians were served—and cast behind their backs.",
  "187": "\"˹Remember, O Prophet,˺ when Allah took the covenant of those who were given the Scripture to make it known to people and not hide it\" opens the custodianship-covenant's file—and the covenant's two clauses are knowledge's whole trust-deed, the scholars unfold: wa idh akhadha llahu mithaqa lladhina utu l-kitab: and when Allah took the covenant of those given the Book: the mithaq-register (Surah 2, verse 83's covenant-grammar): the Scripture's receivers bound, at the receiving, by the deed's two terms: latubayyinunnahu lin-nas: you shall surely make it clear to the people: the bayan-duty (the publication: the Book's contents delivered to its addressees—the descriptions of the awaited Messenger foremost in the file's Madinan register: Surah 2, verse 146's recognised-and-concealed); wa la taktumunah: and you shall not conceal it: the kitman-bar (the argument's oldest charge: Surah 2, verse 42's \"do not hide the truth knowingly\"; verse 71's audit): the two terms one duty at two faces (the truth's two rights: to be published, and to be unhidden: Surah 2, verse 159's commentary)—the covenant's taking at the Scripture's own bestowal being the deed's studied jurisprudence, the commentators press: knowledge received is knowledge mortgaged: the custody never title (the Book held for its addressees—the people—not for its holders' vaults).\n\n\"Yet they cast it behind their backs and traded it for a fleeting gain. What a miserable profit!\" files the deed's documented breach—fanabadhuhu wara'a zuhurihim: but they cast it behind their backs: the nabdh-image (Surah 2, verse 101's identical posture: the Book of Allah cast behind the backs \"as if they did not know\"): the publication-duty inverted at its very gesture—the deed held before the eyes for delivery flung behind the spine for burial; washtaraw bihi thamanan qalila: and purchased with it a small price: the trade-grammar's return (verse 77's oath-sellers; Surah 2, verse 174's concealment-traders): the concealment monetised—the revenue-registers the commentators itemise from the files: the establishment's fees and standings, the rulings edited for patrons, the descriptions suppressed for the primacy they threatened (Surah 2, verse 79's pens at their market)—fabi'sa ma yashtarun: so wretched is what they purchase: the bi'sa-verdict (the miserable-bargain formula: Surah 2, verse 90's identical audit) closing the trade at its pricing: the deed's cargo—the Book's light, the covenant's standing, the Hereafter's share (verse 77's forfeited khalaq)—tendered for the qalil: the small: every concealment-revenue, whatever its era's figures, filed at the adjective.\n\nThe file's permanent jurisdiction, standing back, is the community's own custodianship served notice, the scholars press: the final Book's holders inherit the identical deed (the bayan-duty and the kitman-bar running on the custody, not the community: Surah 2, verse 159's curse and its bridle-hadith: \"whoever is asked about knowledge and conceals it will be bridled on the Day of Judgment with a bridle of fire\"—Jami` at-Tirmidhi, Hadith 2649; Sunan Abi Dawud, Hadith 3658)—the file's every breach-register auditable at the successor-community's own practice: the rulings shelved for audiences, the texts trimmed for patrons, the knowledge vaulted for rank (verse 180's withheld bounties at their gravest register: the hoarded Book)—and the deed's discharge the community's standing definition (verse 110's commission: the commanding and forbidding running on the publication; verse 104's callers): the custodians of every era standing, by this verse, between the two postures its file photographed: the deed held before the eyes for delivery, or flung behind the spine for the small price—while the next verse closes the traders' file at their strangest revenue: the credit taken for what they never did.",
  "188": "\"Do not let those who rejoice in their misdeeds and love to take credit for what they have not done think they will escape torment\" closes the traders' file (verse 187's covenant-breach) at its strangest revenue—and the audited disposition is hypocrisy's finished portrait, the scholars unfold: la tahsabanna lladhina yafrahuna bima ataw: do not reckon those who rejoice at what they have done: the farah-audit (the rejoicing this Book sanctions at Allah's bounty—verse 170's martyrs—here at its corrupt object: the misdeeds themselves enjoyed: the concealments savoured as cleverness, the breaches celebrated in the closed assemblies: Surah 2, verse 76's managed circles); wa yuhibbuna an yuhmadu bima lam yaf'alu: and love to be praised for what they did not do: the double-ledger's completion, the commentators press: the done concealed and enjoyed, the undone displayed and applauded—the file's occasion fixed in the Sahih: Marwan puzzled at the verse's severity (\"if everyone who rejoices at his deeds and loves praise for what he did not do is punished, we shall all be punished\")—and Ibn Abbas corrected the address from the record: the verse descended over the People of the Book: questioned by the Prophet, peace be upon him, they concealed the matter, showed him other than it, and departed having shown him that they had answered—seeking praise for the disclosure they never made, rejoicing at the concealment they did (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 4568; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 2778): the verse's photographed specimen being the covenant-file's own scene (verse 187's deed flung behind the back—then the flingers bowing to applause as its faithful custodians).\n\n\"They will suffer a painful punishment\" delivers the double-ledger's settlement—fala tahsabannahum bimafazatin mina l-adhab: so do not reckon them in a place of escape from the torment: the mafazah-word the verse's studied irony, the scholars note: the escape-ground (the fawz-root: verse 185's triumph-word): the double-keepers' whole craft was escape—the deed escaped by concealment, the blame escaped by display, the audit escaped by the managed impression—and the verse voids the craft at its object: no mafazah from the adhab: the impression-management's entire yield spent on the one audience it never reaches (the Knower of the concealed and the displayed alike: Surah 2, verse 77); wa lahum adhabun alim: and theirs is a painful punishment: the alim-grade closing the file at the trade's personal invoice (verse 177's identical settlement).\n\nThe verse's permanent jurisdiction, standing back, is the community's own reputation-economy served notice, the scholars press: the double-ledger's mechanics outliving their first specimens wherever conduct and credit trade separately—the piety displayed past its practice (Surah 2, verse 44's preachers), the credentials claimed past their earning, the institution's failures concealed and its unearned successes applauded: every register of the loved-praise-for-the-undone audited at Ibn Abbas's corrected address and at Marwan's honest tremor alike (the tremor itself the verse's intended reading, the commentators note: the file's first specimens named, its jurisdiction universal)—the believer formed by the audit carrying its two-sided discipline: the done owned (the misdeed unenjoyed: verse 135's remembering-and-returning), and the credit un-courted (the deed's one Audience sufficient: Surah 2, verse 271's unreceipted banking)—while the passage now lifts, from the traders' small ledgers, to the estate that dwarfs them: \"to Allah belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth.\"",
  "189": "\"To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth\" lifts the passage from the traders' small ledgers (verses 187 and 188) to the estate that dwarfs them—and the lifting's placement is its argument, the scholars unfold: wa lillahi mulku s-samawati wal-ard: and to Allah belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth: the mulk-clause (verse 26's sovereignty-prayer; Surah 2, verse 107's foundation) stationed directly upon the concealment-traders' file: the small-price purchasers (verse 187's qalil) and the praise-collectors (verse 188's managed credit) audited, in one clause, against the estate their trades ignored—the concealments transacted, the commentators press, as though some register lay outside the Owner's holdings (the managed impressions banked as though the reputation-market were sovereign territory): and the mulk-clause forecloses the whole geography: no vault for the flung deed (verse 187's behind-the-back a location inside the estate), no audience-hall outside His (verse 188's applause collected under His observation), no revenue-stream un-probated (verse 180's inheritance-clause: the estate's terminal probate)—the traders' entire economy revealed as conducted, at every counter, inside the Kingdom it discounted.\n\n\"And Allah is Most Capable of everything\" seals the estate-clause with the capability-formula—wallahu ala kulli shay'in qadir: and Allah is over all things powerful—and the pairing's function at this station is the passage's hinge, the scholars draw: the mulk and the qudrah (the ownership and the capability: Surah 2, verse 284's identical pairing at that surah's summation) joined as the two clauses every foregoing file requires: the ownership guaranteeing the audit's jurisdiction (nothing transacted outside the estate: the registers total), the capability guaranteeing its enforcement (the settlements deliverable: the collars of verse 180, the burning of verse 181, the no-escape of verse 188—each sentence backed by the power that no debtor's flight outruns)—and the seal's forward-face opening the chapter's finale, the commentators note: the verse stands as the threshold of the surah's closing movement (the night-reflection passage descending at the next verse: the creation's signs, the standing-sitting-reclining rememberers, the five-fold prayer, and the Lord's answering)—the estate-clause thus read at both its faces: backward as the traders' audit (the Kingdom they discounted), forward as the reflectors' text (the Kingdom they will read all night: verse 190's heavens and earth being this verse's own estate handed to the people of reason as their book of signs).\n\nThe verse's permanent function, standing back, is the transition it engineers, the scholars press: from the chapter's longest indictment-passages to its most luminous devotion-passage by way of one clause—the same estate that convicts the concealers convenes the contemplatives: the heavens and the earth, held in one Ownership, serving simultaneously as the traders' closing audit and the rememberers' opening page: the reader's own approach to the identical sky deciding, as this argument's sortings always decide (Surah 2, verse 26), which passage he walks into—while the finale now opens: \"indeed, in the creation of the heavens and the earth and the alternation of the day and night there are signs for people of reason.\"",
  "190": "\"Indeed, in the creation of the heavens and the earth and the alternation of the day and night there are signs for people of reason\" opens the chapter's luminous finale—and the verse's own reception in the Sunnah is its first commentary, the scholars set: the Prophet, peace be upon him, wept at its descent: Bilal, coming at dawn, found him weeping, and he answered: \"there was revealed to me this night a verse—woe to whoever recites it and does not reflect upon it\"—and he recited this verse (the transmission at Ibn Hibban's collection and the tafsir-books' family, graded in its supports); and Aishah's and Ibn Abbas's accounts fix its devotional station: the Prophet reciting these closing verses upon waking for the night-prayer, at the sky's own face (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 183's night-narration; Hadith 4569's placement of the recitation): the verse issued, by its bearer's own usage, as the night-watcher's text.\n\nThe verse's content is the chapter's evidence-doctrine at its distillation, the commentators draw: inna fi khalqi s-samawati wal-ardi wakhtilafi l-layli wan-nahar: indeed in the heavens' and earth's creation and the night's and day's alternation: the two registers (the standing structure and the running rhythm: Surah 2, verse 164's eight signs here compressed to their two heads: the built and the alternating)—la'ayatin li'uli l-albab: are surely signs for the possessors of the cores: the ulu l-albab: the people of the lubb: the kernel: reason at its stripped centre (the chapter's recurring address: verse 7's closing; Surah 2, verse 269's wisdom-holders)—the sorting-clause's grammar the finale's continuity with the whole argument, the scholars press: the signs present to all eyes (the estate of verse 189 overhead every night), operative upon the cores alone (Surah 2, verse 164's identical sorting: \"for people of understanding\"): the sky one text, the readerships two.\n\nThe verse's placement completes the chapter's designed symmetry, the commentators note: the surah opened at the Book's two registers (verse 7's precise and elusive verses, the deviant and the well-grounded readers) and closes at creation's parallel text (the heavens' signs, the reasoning and the heedless readers): the two Books—the revealed and the created—issued by one Author to one readership: the ulu l-albab of this verse being the rasikhun of that one (the well-grounded's \"all of it is from our Lord\" spoken over both texts)—and the believer formed by the verse carries its weeping-Prophet standard as the reading's own grade, the scholars press: the recitation without reflection the woe-earning waste (the flock's heard calls: Surah 2, verse 171); the reflection's practice itemised in the very next verse: the remembering in every posture, the pondering at the creation, and the prayer that rises from both—\"Our Lord! You have not created all of this without purpose.\"",
  "191": "\"˹They are˺ those who remember Allah while standing, sitting, and lying on their sides\" itemises the people of reason (verse 190's ulu l-albab) at their defining practice—and the three postures are the itemisation's completeness, the scholars unfold: alladhina yadhkuruna llaha qiyaman wa qu'udan wa ala junubihim: who remember Allah standing, and sitting, and upon their sides: the postures' census covering the body's whole repertoire (no fourth remains): the dhikr un-confined to the prayer-mat's minutes—the remembrance running through the day's standing labours, the assembly's sitting, the night's reclining (the practice's legal echo the commentators note: the prayer itself performed by the same descending series at incapacity—standing, then sitting, then on the side: Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 1117's ruling to Imran—the worship's postures and the remembrance's one continuum); and Aishah's summary of the Prophet's own practice the itemisation's living form: \"he used to remember Allah at all his times\" (Sahih Muslim, Hadith 373).\n\n\"And reflect on the creation of the heavens and the earth\" joins the second practice—wa yatafakkaruna fi khalqi s-samawati wal-ard: and reflect upon the heavens' and earth's creation—and the joining is the verse's studied anthropology, the scholars press: the dhikr and the fikr: the tongue's-and-heart's remembrance and the mind's reflection: the two faculties this Book refuses to divorce (the remembrance without reflection drying into formula; the reflection without remembrance chilling into speculation): the people of reason defined by the union—the sky read (verse 190's text) with the Author remembered: whence the reflection's issue is neither treatise nor theorem but the verse's own next word: prayer.\n\n\"˹And pray˺, 'Our Lord! You have not created ˹all of˺ this without purpose. Glory be to You! Protect us from the torment of the Fire'\" records the reflection's arrival—rabbana ma khalaqta hadha batila: our Lord, You have not created this in vain: the batil-negation the reflection's whole finding, the commentators draw: the cosmos's purposiveness read off its structure (the built heavens, the governed alternation: the design's seriousness excluding the pointless: Surah 38, verse 27's identical negation against the deniers' reckoning)—subhanaka: glory be to You: the tasbih at the finding (the transcendence-reflex of the well-grounded: verse 7's manner; Surah 2, verse 32's angels)—and then the finale's studied leap, the scholars marvel: faqina adhaba n-nar: so protect us from the torment of the Fire: the cosmology issuing directly into eschatology: the fa-connective's logic the reasoning's own chain: the creation purposive, therefore the accounting real (the purpose entailing the audit: a cosmos not in vain is a cosmos with a settlement: verse 185's scheduled Day), therefore the Fire real, therefore the plea—the people of reason arriving, by pure reflection at the night sky, exactly where revelation stands: the two Books (verse 190's commentary) delivering one reader to one door: the believer formed by the verse carrying its complete practice-chain as the finale's issued devotion: the remembrance in every posture, the reflection at every sky, and the reflection's honest terminus prayed at each arrival—the Fire's protection begged by the very minds the cosmos convinced.",
  "192": "\"Our Lord! Indeed, those You commit to the Fire will be ˹completely˺ disgraced!\" continues the night-watchers' prayer (verse 191's reflection arrived at its plea)—and the prayer's second movement is its studied deepening, the scholars unfold: rabbana innaka man tudkhili n-nara faqad akhzaytah: our Lord, whoever You admit to the Fire, You have surely disgraced him: the khizy-word the movement's chosen dread (the disgrace: the humiliation before the assembly: verse 194's coming plea against the Day's shaming): the reflectors' reading of the Fire's essence, the commentators press: beyond its pain the demotion—the admitted one dis-graced: stripped of the honour the creation's purposiveness (verse 191's finding) had offered every soul (the wasted purpose the disgrace's core: the cosmos's invited guest ejected from its whole design)—and the prayer's address-grammar the movement's humility: the admission filed at His committal (man tudkhil: whom You admit: the Judge's act confessed sovereign) while the disgrace's earning stands at the admitted's own file (the passage's constant justice: verse 182's hands; the zallam-negation)—wa ma liz-zalimina min ansar: and the wrongdoers will have no helpers: the no-helpers clause (the terminal formula: Surah 2, verse 270; verse 22's file) recited by the watchers as their own dread's fence: the prayer naming, at the night's honesty, the settlement no advocacy amends.\n\nThe movement's function in the prayer's architecture is the scholars' noted design: the night-watchers' liturgy (verses 191 to 194) descends and ascends by turns—the purposive cosmos (191's finding), the Fire's plea (191's close), this verse's deepened dread (the disgrace read into the torment), then the rising movements ahead (the heard caller, the begged forgiveness, the claimed promise)—the descent's depth being the ascent's spring, the commentators draw: the prayer that has looked into the khizy prays the coming movements at their true weight (the forgiveness of verse 193 begged by askers who priced its refusal; the promise of verse 194 claimed by claimants who read the alternative)—the liturgy thus teaching the community its whole prayer-anatomy: the pleas' power drawn from the dreads' honesty: the night-watcher neither despairing (the movements rise) nor presuming (they rise from this verse's floor).\n\nThe verse's permanent equipment, standing back, serves the believer's own night-liturgy, the scholars press: the Fire held in the prayer's vocabulary not as rhetoric's furniture but as the reflection's honest finding (verse 191's chain: the purposive cosmos entailing the real settlement)—the dread prayed, not suppressed (the taught pleas of the tradition's every register: Surah 2, verse 201's third clause; verse 16's second petition)—and the no-helpers clause carried as the dread's clarifying edge: the settlement's solitude (Surah 2, verse 48's closed doors) read at the night's quiet, while the doors stand open (the repentance's hours the watchers are using: the prayer itself the door walked)—the movement closing, by its own honesty, into the liturgy's rising turn: \"Our Lord! We have heard the caller to true belief...\"",
  "193": "\"Our Lord! We have heard the caller to ˹true˺ belief, ˹proclaiming,˺ 'Believe in your Lord ˹alone˺,' so we believed\" turns the night-watchers' liturgy (verses 191 and 192's descent) into its rising movement—and the movement's opening is the community's whole biography in one clause, the scholars unfold: rabbana innana sami'na munadiyan yunadi lil-iman: our Lord, we heard a caller calling to faith: the munadi—the caller: the Messenger, peace be upon him, by the commentators' reading (and the Quran itself the call's text: the two one summons): an amanu birabbikum: believe in your Lord: the call's whole content compressed to its tawhid-core—fa'amanna: so we believed: the fa-connective the biography's honour, the scholars press: the hearing followed by the believing at the conjunction's speed (no criterion-pleading between: verse 183's deferrals; no generation's delay: the \"we hear and obey\" of Surah 2, verse 285 as narrative): the watchers pleading, before their Lord, the one credential this argument has priced above every lineage: the call answered when it came.\n\n\"Our Lord! Forgive our sins, absolve us of our misdeeds, and allow us ˹each˺ to die as one of the virtuous\" ascends the movement's three petitions—faghfir lana dhunubana: so forgive us our sins: the ghafr (the covering: the record's shielding); wa kaffir anna sayyi'atina: and absolve us of our misdeeds: the takfir (the erasure: the expiation's deeper cleansing—the two petitions' pairing the scholars' noted gradation: the sins covered and the traces effaced); wa tawaffana ma'a l-abrar: and take us ˹in death˺ with the virtuous: the ending-petition the liturgy's summit, the commentators draw: the abrar—the virtuous (verse 198's coming accommodation): the watchers praying their deaths' company: the dying-with as the life's whole aim (the patriarchs' bequest: Surah 2, verse 132's \"do not die except in submission\"; the endings-doctrine: the deeds by their closings)—the three petitions' sequence the believer's complete file-management: the record cleared, the traces effaced, the ending secured.\n\nThe movement's fa-grammar is the liturgy's studied teaching, the scholars note: the believing (fa'amanna) flowing directly into the forgiveness-plea (faghfir): the faith itself presented as the pardon's ground (verse 16's identical anatomy: \"Our Lord! We have believed, so forgive us our sins\")—the watchers' theology exact: the belief no wage-claim (the credential pleaded, not the payment demanded) but the covenant-fact laid before the Covenant-Keeper: we answered Your caller; deal with us as the Answerer deals—and the believer formed by the movement carries its liturgy as issued equipment (the night-prayer's own transcript: the Prophet's recitation of these verses at the sky: verse 190's commentary): the biography confessed (the call heard, the faith given), the file's three petitions prayed in their order, and the company of the ending begged nightly—while the liturgy now rises to its final movement: the promise itself claimed: \"Our Lord! Grant us what You have promised us through Your messengers.\"",
  "194": "\"Our Lord! Grant us what You have promised us through Your messengers and do not put us to shame on Judgment Day\" completes the night-watchers' liturgy (verses 191 to 193) at its boldest movement—and the boldness is covenant-grammar, not presumption, the scholars unfold: rabbana wa atina ma wa'adtana ala rusulik: our Lord, and give us what You promised us upon Your messengers: the promise claimed at its channel (ala rusulik: upon Your messengers: the pledges carried by the sent ones—the victory, the establishment, the Gardens, the Pleasure: the covenant's published schedule)—the claiming's jurisprudence the commentators press: the watchers ask for what is already pledged: the petition's whole audacity resting on the Promiser's own instrument (the prayer teaching the community its covenant-literacy: the promises known, held, and prayed back to their Maker—the Prophet's own Badr-night the form's summit: \"O Allah, fulfil for me what You promised me\": Sahih Muslim, Hadith 1763's plea); wa la tukhzina yawma l-qiyamah: and do not disgrace us on the Day of Resurrection: the khizy-plea (verse 192's dread returned at the liturgy's close: the shaming before the assembly begged against)—the two petitions' pairing the movement's balance: the promise claimed and the shame feared: hope and dread at the liturgy's summit as at its floor (Surah 15, verses 49 and 50's twin announcement).\n\n\"For certainly You never fail in Your promise\" seals the liturgy with its ground—innaka la tukhlifu l-mi'ad: indeed You do not break the appointment—and the seal's function is the whole prayer's warrant, the scholars draw: the claiming-movement's audacity underwritten at the Promiser's attribute (verse 9's identical clause: \"surely Allah does not break His promise\": the watchers' liturgy closing where the chapter's opening prayer closed: the two prayers—verse 8's and this passage's—the surah's matched bookends: the deviation feared and the mercy begged there; the promise claimed and the shame feared here; both grounded at the unfailing appointment)—the liturgy thus complete at its four movements, the commentators map: the cosmos read (191), the Fire dreaded (192), the call confessed and the file prayed (193), the promise claimed (this verse): reflection, fear, faith, and hope: the night-watcher's whole interior traversed in four verses—and the traversal answered, the next verse will publish, at once: \"so their Lord responded to them.\"\n\nThe liturgy's permanent function, standing back, is the community's issued night-office, the scholars press: the passage's verses carried in the Prophet's own waking recitation (verse 190's commentary: the practice at Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 183) and in the community's tahajjud ever since: the four movements prayed as the tradition's inheritance—and the movement's covenant-literacy its standing discipline: the believer schooled to know the promises (the messengers' carried pledges studied as one's own file's clauses), to claim them at the Desk that issued them (the asking itself worship: the promise prayed being the promise honoured), and to hold the claiming inside the khizy-plea's humility: the same tongue that claims the appointment begging against the shame—the liturgy's boldness and brokenness one breath; while the answer now descends: \"I will never deny any of you—male or female—the reward of your deeds.\"",
  "195": "\"So their Lord responded to them: 'I will never deny any of you—male or female—the reward of your deeds. Both are equal in reward'\" publishes the liturgy's answer (verses 191 to 194's four movements)—and the answer's speed is its first mercy, the scholars unfold: fastajaba lahum rabbuhum: so their Lord responded to them: the istijabah at the prayer's own heels (the fa-connective: the night-watchers' claiming-movement answered in the passage's next breath: Surah 2, verse 186's nearness-doctrine enacted in the text's own sequence)—anni la udi'u amala amilin minkum min dhakarin aw untha: that I do not waste the deed of any doer among you, male or female: the non-wasting clause (verse 171's martyrs' rejoiced doctrine: \"Allah does not deny the reward of the believers\") here delivered in the first person and with the census-clause the occasion demanded, the commentators set from the record: Umm Salamah asked the Prophet, peace be upon him: why are we ˹women˺ not mentioned in the Quran as the men are?—and this verse descended (the transmission at Jami` at-Tirmidhi, Hadith 3023's family, graded sound): the answer's census explicit: male or female: the wage-registers un-gendered—ba'dukum min ba'd: you are of one another: the equality's ground stated as kinship's own grammar (the one soul's issue: Surah 4, verse 1's coming charter): the deed the only sorting-clause at the Desk (verse 163's degrees by conduct).\n\n\"Those who migrated or were expelled from their homes, and were persecuted for My sake and fought and ˹some˺ were martyred\" itemises the answered file's honour-roll—falladhina hajaru wa ukhriju min diyarihim wa udhu fi sabili wa qatalu wa qutilu: those who migrated, were driven from their homes, were hurt in My cause, fought, and were killed: the five-fold ladder the community's own biography, the scholars draw: the hijrah's uprooting, the expulsion's injustice (Surah 2, verse 191's answered wrong), the adha borne (verse 186's notice), the fighting and the falling (verse 169's living martyrs): the roll ascending through the cause's whole cost-registers—la'ukaffiranna anhum sayyi'atihim wa la'udkhilannahum jannatin tajri min tahtiha l-anhar: I will surely absolve them of their misdeeds and admit them into Gardens beneath which rivers flow: the two pledges at the doubled-emphasis grammar (the la and the nun: the covenant's own solemnity: verse 186's testing-notice answered by the settling-notice): the takfir begged at verse 193's second petition granted in the first person, the Gardens of the whole argument's furnishing deeded.\n\n\"As a reward from Allah. And with Allah is the finest reward!\" seals the answer at its source and its superlative—thawaban min indi llah: a reward from Allah's own presence: the min-indi clause (the payment from the Presence itself: verse 169's inda rabbihim: the martyrs' address the wage's warehouse); wallahu indahu husnu th-thawab: and with Allah is the best of rewards: the husn-superlative (verse 148's graded columns: the Hereafter's excellence-word) closing the liturgy's whole transaction, the commentators note: the night-watchers claimed the promise (194) and the answer over-delivers it: the deeds unwasted, the registers un-gendered, the misdeeds absolved, the Gardens deeded, and the payment sourced at the Presence—the community formed by the exchange carrying its two permanent charters: the prayer answered (the liturgy's efficacy published in the text's own sequence) and the wage-equality published (Umm Salamah's question the ummah's standing warrant: no register of the cause's costs—the migrations, the hurts, the fields—gendered at the Desk); while the passage now turns its last caution toward the ledgers that deceive: \"do not be deceived by the prosperity of the disbelievers throughout the land.\"",
  "196": "\"Do not be deceived by the prosperity of the disbelievers throughout the land\" issues the finale's last caution—and the caution's address is its studied mercy, the scholars unfold: la yaghurrannaka taqallubu lladhina kafaru fil-bilad: let not the moving-about of the disbelievers in the lands deceive you: the taqallub-word: the turning-about: the traffic—the caravans plying, the markets humming, the campaigns provisioned: Makkah's commerce running rich through the very seasons the believers bled (the Uhud-years' visible ledger: the persecutors prosperous, the persecuted pressed)—and the ghurur-verb (the deception: verse 185's delusion-word: the world's enjoyment as mata'u l-ghurur) naming the spectacle's operation: the prosperity read as verdict: the old misreading this passage has audited at every station (verse 178's corrected respite; Surah 2, verse 212's dazzled reading)—the address's singular grammar (la yaghurrannaka: let it not deceive you) the commentators weigh at both registers: the Prophet addressed, peace be upon him, that the community behind him be schooled (the protected head's address: Surah 2, verse 120's device), and every reader singly: the deception's pressure landing, in every era, on each watcher of the unpunished traffic one by one.\n\nThe caution's necessity is the finale's own realism, the scholars press: the night-watchers' liturgy just answered (verse 195's deeded Gardens) transacts in the unseen's registers—the promises carried by messengers, the wages warehoused at the Presence—while the disbelievers' ledger transacts in the seen's: the lands, the traffic, the figures: and the two ledgers' visibility-gap is faith's standing test (Surah 2, verse 3's first credential: the unseen believed): the caution thus posted at the finale's edge precisely where the test presses: the believer rising from the night-liturgy into the morning's markets, the answered prayer in his heart and the unanswered spectacle in his eyes—la yaghurrannaka: let the spectacle not re-price the prayer.\n\nThe verse's permanent equipment, standing back, is the community's spectacle-discipline entire, the commentators draw: every era's version of the taqallub—the tyrannies' skylines, the frauds' portfolios, the mockers' platforms turning-about through the lands unpunished—read at the caution's grammar: the traffic conceded as real (the Book never denies the prosperity: the mata's genuineness at verse 197's own concession) and its meaning voided (the verdict-reading barred: the prosperity neither favour's evidence nor the cause's audit: verse 178's mechanism—the respite filling ledgers)—and the deception's cure the next verse's arithmetic, the scholars note: the finale answering the spectacle not with its denial but with its pricing: \"it is only a brief enjoyment; then Hell will be their home\"—the caution and the pricing one instrument: the eyes schooled at the ledger the night-watchers already read: the two columns' terms (the qalil and the eternal) held against every morning's traffic.",
  "197": "\"It is only a brief enjoyment. Then Hell will be their home—what an evil place to rest!\" prices the spectacle the caution barred (verse 196's deceiving traffic)—and the pricing's two entries are the finale's complete audit, the scholars unfold: mata'un qalil: a small enjoyment: the concession-and-demotion in two words (the mata conceded: the prosperity real, the traffic genuine—the Book's constant honesty about the world's goods: verse 14's inventory, verse 185's kit; and the qalil demoting: small—the adjective this argument stamps on every trade's wrong side: the thamanan qalilan of the covenant-sellers, verse 187; the fleeting gains of Surah 2, verse 41): the disbelievers' whole turning-about (the lands, the figures, the seasons) filed at two words' measure—small against the scale the night-watchers read (the eternity's registers: verse 195's deeded Gardens), small against its own duration (the tasting's floor: verse 185's every soul), small at the probate (verse 180's one Inheritor); thumma ma'wahum jahannam: then their refuge is Hell: the thumma-connective the audit's second entry, the commentators press: the sequence's mercy-stripped brevity: the enjoyment, then the abode: the traffic's terminus published without intermediate softening (the respite-mechanism of verse 178 arriving at its filled ledger's delivery)—wa bi'sa l-mihad: and evil is the resting-place: the mihad-word (the cradle spread: Surah 2, verse 206's bitter idiom) closing the audit at the spectacle's final furniture: the prosperity's beds re-made, at the sequence's end, in the abode the traffic financed.\n\nThe two-verse instrument (196 and 197), standing back, is the community's issued spectacle-arithmetic, the scholars draw: the caution (let it not deceive you) and the pricing (small, then Hell): the deception dissolved not by the eyes' closing but by the ledger's completion—the visible column extended to its published terminus (the audit the world's figures always omit: the thumma-entry)—and the arithmetic's pedigree the commentators note across the Book: the identical instrument at Surah 2, verse 126 (Ibrahim's amended prayer: \"I will let them enjoy themselves for a little while, then I will condemn them to the torment of the Fire\") and Surah 31, verse 24: the mata-then-abode sequence the Quran's standing answer to prosperity's whole theatre.\n\nThe verse's permanent function, at the finale's edge, is the contrast it stages for the next verse's counter-column, the scholars press: the two homecomings set side by side at the chapter's close: the disbelievers' sequence (the small enjoyment, the evil cradle: this verse) and the mindful's (the Gardens' eternity, the Presence's accommodation: verse 198's nuzul)—the two-column display being the finale's last sorting-instrument (Surah 2, verse 26's one text, two receptions): every reader's own traffic and own night audited between the columns: which enjoyment is being banked, and which resting-place furnished—while the counter-column now publishes: \"but those who are mindful of their Lord will be in Gardens under which rivers flow... as an accommodation from Allah.\"",
  "198": "\"But those who are mindful of their Lord will be in Gardens under which rivers flow, to stay there forever\" publishes the counter-column the spectacle-audit staged for (verses 196 and 197's priced traffic)—and the column's opening is the sorting's own grammar, the scholars unfold: lakini lladhina ttaqaw rabbahum: but those who were mindful of their Lord: the lakin-adversative (the \"but\" that turns the ledger's page: the disbelievers' small-then-Hell sequence answered at its every entry): the muttaqin—the chapter's and the Book's watchword-people (Surah 2, verse 2)—entered at their settlement: lahum jannatun tajri min tahtiha l-anhar khalidina fiha: theirs are Gardens beneath which rivers flow, abiding forever: the two-clause answer to verse 197's two-clause audit, the commentators trace: against the mata'un qalil (the small enjoyment) the Gardens' estate; against the thumma-terminus (then Hell) the khulud (the forever): the columns' asymmetry total at both registers—the enjoyments' sizes and the tenures' terms.\n\n\"As an accommodation from Allah\" adds the column's most studied word—nuzulan min indi llah: as a hospitality-offering from Allah's own presence—and the nuzul-word is the verse's crowning courtesy, the scholars draw: the term of the guest's first provision: the welcome-fare set before the traveller at arrival (the host's opening honour before the residence's fullness): the Gardens themselves—the rivers, the eternity—filed, by this word, as the reception's first tray: the accommodation from the Presence (min indi llah: verse 195's warehouse-address) with the implied more behind it (the Pleasure above the estate: Surah 9, verse 72's ranking; the Vision the texts unveil): the mindful arriving, by the word's own grammar, not as tenants to a property but as guests to a Host—the hospitality-frame the commentators set against the counter-column's furniture: verse 197's bi'sa l-mihad (the evil cradle: the resting-place as sentence) answered by the nuzul (the resting-place as welcome).\n\n\"And what is with Allah is best for the virtuous\" seals the column at the pricing that closes the whole spectacle-file—wa ma inda llahi khayrun lil-abrar: and what is with Allah is better for the righteous—and the seal's two terms complete the finale's threads, the scholars press: ma inda llah: what is with Allah: the inda-vault (Surah 2, verse 110's banking; verse 195's min-indi payments): the unseen column's whole inventory named at its address—khayrun: better: the comparative the spectacle-discipline required (verse 196's deceiving traffic priced against the vault: the taqallub's entire visible ledger out-priced by the warehoused unseen)—lil-abrar: for the virtuous: the abrar-title the night-watchers' own begged company (verse 193's third petition: \"allow us each to die as one of the virtuous\"): the liturgy's askers named, at the finale's settlement, as the vault's heirs: the prayer's arc closed across five verses—the company begged, the company deeded; and the believer formed by the column carries the finale's whole arithmetic home: the mornings' traffic read at verse 197's two words, the nights' prayers banked at this verse's vault, and the abrar-company held as the file's one pursuit—while the chapter turns, in its last two verses, to the honour-roll across the communities and the four-command charter that seals the surah.",
  "199": "\"Indeed, there are some among the People of the Book who truly believe in Allah and what has been revealed to you ˹believers˺ and what was revealed to them\" opens the chapter's penultimate verse at its honour-roll—and the roll's occasion is the community's own funeral-record, the scholars set from the transmitted accounts: when the Negus of Abyssinia died—an-Najashi, the Christian king who had sheltered the first emigrants, recognised the recited Maryam-verses with tears, and believed—the Prophet, peace be upon him, announced his death to the community the day it occurred and led the funeral-prayer in absentia (Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 1245; Sahih Muslim, Hadith 951); and when voices murmured at the prayer for a foreign king, this verse descended by the commentators' transmitted connection: the honour-roll published over the Negus's file—wa inna min ahli l-kitabi laman yu'minu billahi wa ma unzila ilaykum wa ma unzila ilayhim: and indeed among the People of the Book are those who believe in Allah and what was sent down to you and what was sent down to them: the census-clause (the argument's constant guard: verse 113's \"not all alike\"; verse 75's trustworthy) at its warmest register: the believing column's creed itemised complete: the two revelations held together (the confirmation-doctrine walked: Surah 2, verse 121's true following carrying its readers across).\n\n\"They humble themselves before Allah—never trading Allah's revelations for a fleeting gain\" credentials the roll at the two registers the chapter's indictments ran on—khashi'ina lillah: humbling themselves to Allah: the khushu (the awed lowliness: the night-reciters' prostration: verse 113's portrait) against the establishment's pride (verse 188's loved praise); la yashtaruna bi'ayati llahi thamanan qalila: not purchasing with Allah's revelations a small price: the trade-bar (verse 187's covenant-sellers; verse 77's oath-traders) negated at the roll's holders: the two credentials together being the counter-portrait exact, the commentators press: where the indicted files ran on pride and revenue, the honoured column runs on humility and the un-sold Book—the Negus's own file the roll's sealed specimen: the king who priced his throne's politics below the recognised truth (the emigrants unsurrendered at the patriarchs' demand; the recognition wept, not managed).\n\n\"Their reward is with their Lord. Surely Allah is swift in reckoning\" settles the roll at the vault and the schedule—ula'ika lahum ajruhum inda rabbihim: those—their reward is with their Lord: the inda-vault (verse 198's address) holding the cross-community column's wage (the double-reward doctrine: Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 97's twice-paid believer of the earlier Book); inna llaha sari'u l-hisab: surely Allah is swift in reckoning: the swift-reckoning seal (Surah 2, verse 202's two-faced clause) closing the roll at both its faces, the scholars draw: toward the honoured—the settlement near (the Negus's prayer already prayed, his wage already vaulted); toward the murmurers of every era—the files' sorting His and fast: the community schooled, at its own funeral-row, in the census-justice the whole chapter legislated: the blocs never flattened (verse 113's guard), the credentials portable (verse 114's demonstration), and the honour-roll's entries written by the Reckoner alone—while the chapter now seals itself with the four-command charter: \"O believers! Patiently endure, persevere, stand on guard, and be mindful of Allah, so you may be successful.\"",
  "200": "\"O believers! Patiently endure, persevere, stand on guard, and be mindful of Allah, so you may be successful\" seals the Family-of-Imran chapter with its four-command charter—and the commands' ascent is the whole surah compressed, the scholars unfold: ya ayyuha lladhina amanu sbiru: O believers, be patient: the sabr-command at its base (the chapter's constant equipment: verse 120's insulation, verse 125's reinforcement-condition, verse 186's notice): the holding under the road's published tolls; wa sabiru: and out-endure: the musabarah-form the ascent's first rise, the commentators press: the patience made contest: the enduring measured against the adversary's (the enemy's persistence out-lasted: the campaigns' stamina answered stride for stride—falsehood's own documented perseverance, Surah 2, verse 175's \"how persistent,\" out-persevered by truth's); wa rabitu: and stand on guard: the ribat-command the ascent's summit: the frontier held—the murabit's station at the community's exposed edges (the posts the hill's fifty taught the price of: verse 152's audit)—and the Sunnah's widening of the word the charter's own depth, the scholars set from the Sahih: the Prophet, peace be upon him: \"shall I not direct you to that by which Allah erases sins and raises degrees? Perfecting ablution despite difficulties, many steps to the mosques, and waiting for prayer after prayer—that is the ribat, that is the ribat\" (Sahih Muslim, Hadith 251): the frontier-word deeded to the prayer-mat's own vigil: every believer stationed, the community's guard mounted at the mosque-thresholds as at the marches (the literal ribat's honour standing entire: \"a day's guarding in Allah's cause is better than the world and all it contains\"—Sahih al-Bukhari, Hadith 2892); wattaqu llah: and be mindful of Allah: the taqwa-command the charter's binding thread (the surah's watchword from Surah 2, verse 2's threshold): the three stations—the patience, the out-enduring, the guard—all held inside the one mindfulness.\n\n\"So you may be successful\" closes the charter and the chapter at the falah-clause—la'allakum tuflihun: that you may prosper: the success-word (Surah 2, verse 5's opening portrait; verse 104's commissioned party) sealing the two-hundred-verse argument at its harvest-term, the commentators draw: the chapter that opened at the Ever-Living's creed (verse 2), hosted the disputations and the narratives, audited Uhud's wound and answered the night-watchers' liturgy, closes at four imperatives and one hope: the community's whole formation-course graded into marching orders—and the charter's permanent function, standing back, is the surah's bequest entire, the scholars press: every era's community holding its Uhud-seasons and its delegation-seasons at the four commands' ascent: the patience for the tolls, the out-enduring for the campaigns, the guard for the frontiers (the marches' and the prayer-mats' alike), and the taqwa binding all—with the falah held, as the Book always holds it, at the la'alla's humility: the success hoped from the Bestower, never claimed from the labour: the Family of Imran ending, as the Fatihah's daily petition begins, with the community on the road, equipped, stationed, and asking."
};
